window.CODELAB_STUDY={"checked":"17-18 October 2026","edition":"3.0","courses":[{"id":"python","title":"Python","group":"Language foundations","summary":"Build programming logic, then write typed, tested automation and backend code.","duration":"Beginner path: 4-6 weeks","project":"Build a command-line task tracker with JSON persistence, validation, tests and a short README.","sources":[{"title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"py-setup","title":"Setup, modules and environments","body":"## What runs a Python program?\nAn interpreter reads your Python source and executes it. The editor helps you write the file; it is not the interpreter. Save a script as `main.py`, open its folder in a terminal, and run `python main.py` (or `py -3 main.py` on Windows).\n\n## Set up a repeatable project\n1. Create a folder for one project, not one folder shared by every experiment.\n2. Run `python -m venv .venv`. Activate `.venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1` on Windows or `source .venv/bin/activate` on Unix. Activation changes the shell's Python search path; it does not install a second operating system.\n3. Select that interpreter in VS Code. Use `python -m pip install package_name` so pip belongs to the same interpreter.\n4. Record dependencies in `pyproject.toml` or a requirements file; keep `.venv/` out of Git. A lockfile adds reproducible resolved versions. `uv` is an alternative environment/package tool.\n\n## Organise code before it grows\nA module is one `.py` file. A package groups modules. Import useful definitions, keep side effects out of imports, and put CLI startup behind `if __name__ == \"__main__\":`. Run a package entry point with `python -m package_name` when relative imports require package context.\n\nRuff can lint/format code; pre-commit runs selected checks before a commit. These tools catch mechanical issues, while tests check behaviour. Inside this site's browser Python engine, standard-library examples work without installing Python locally; project package installation belongs to your local environment.","exercise":"Create `math_tools.py` with an `add` function and a separate `main.py` that imports it. Add a `.gitignore` and explain how another person creates the environment.","interviews":[{"q":"Why use a virtual environment?","a":"It gives each project its own installed Python packages. Two projects can depend on different versions without overwriting each other. Recreate it from dependency metadata instead of committing the environment directory."},{"q":"Why use the __main__ guard?","a":"Importing a module should make its definitions available without unexpectedly starting a CLI, network request or server. The guard runs startup code only when that module is the entry point."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which command installs into the interpreter currently selected by python?","options":["python -m pip install requests","copy requests into every folder","git install requests","python main.py --pip"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The -m form asks that interpreter to run its pip module."},"skills":[7,10,24],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\nprint(sys.version_info.major)\nprint(Path(\"notes.txt\").suffix)","expected":"3\n.txt","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-values","title":"Values, variables, input and mutability","body":"## Names point to objects\n`age = 21` binds a name to an integer object. A name can later reference a different type because Python checks types at runtime. Learn `int`, `float`, `str`, `bool` and `None` before adding libraries. `type(value)` helps inspect a value; it does not replace a well-designed input contract.\n\n`input()` returns text, even when the user types digits. Convert explicitly with `int()` or `float()`, and handle invalid input. `print()` writes output. Comments begin with `#`; meaningful names explain more than a comment that merely repeats the next statement.\n\n## Operators and conditions\n`/` performs true division, `//` floor division, `%` remainder and `**` exponentiation. Floor division rounds down: `-3 // 2` is `-2`. Compare with `==`, `!=`, `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=`. `=` assigns; it does not compare. `and`, `or` and `not` combine conditions; `and` and `or` return operands, so they are not always booleans.\n\n## Mutable versus immutable\nIntegers, strings and tuples are immutable; lists and dictionaries are mutable. Assignment does not copy an object. If `b = a` and both names reference a list, `b.append(3)` changes the list seen by `a`. A shallow copy duplicates the outer list, not every nested object. Use `is` for identity, especially `value is None`; use `==` for value equality.\n\nEmpty containers, zero, `None` and empty strings are false in conditions. Do not confuse `0` with a missing value when zero is valid input. Prefer an explicit `is None` check in that situation.","exercise":"Read two integers and print their sum, integer quotient and remainder. Then demonstrate an alias bug with a nested list and repair it.","interviews":[{"q":"What is the difference between == and is?","a":"== asks whether two values compare equal. is asks whether both references point to the same object. Two independently created lists can be equal while having different identities."},{"q":"Does assigning a list copy it?","a":"No. Both names reference the same list. list.copy() creates a new outer list, while nested mutable objects can still be shared; use a deliberate deep copy only when that ownership is required."}],"quiz":{"question":"After a=[1]; b=a; b.append(2), what is a?","options":["[1, 2]","[1]","[2]","None"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Assignment creates an alias; append mutates the shared list."},"skills":[1,2],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"a = [1]\nb = a\nb.append(2)\nprint(a)\nprint(-3 // 2)","expected":"[1, 2]\n-2","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-control","title":"Conditions, loops and tracing","body":"## Turn a requirement into branches\nUse `if`, `elif` and `else` for mutually ordered decisions. Python uses indentation to define a block, so inconsistent indentation changes meaning or produces an error. Start with a small decision table: valid input, boundary input and invalid input. For a grade classifier, decide whether the top boundary includes equality before writing comparisons.\n\n## Repeat safely\n`for item in iterable` visits items. `range(start, stop, step)` excludes `stop`. `while condition` repeats until its condition becomes false; ensure some state changes towards termination. `break` exits the nearest loop; `continue` skips to its next iteration. A loop's `else` runs when it finishes without `break`, which can express a search that found nothing.\n\n## A tracing method that works in interviews\n1. Write the variable values before the first iteration.\n2. Record the current item, condition result and updated variables for each step.\n3. Check a loop invariant: for a running sum, the accumulator equals the sum of the items already processed.\n4. Check termination and the final output separately.\n\nAvoid changing a list's size while iterating over that same list. Build a new result, iterate over a copy, or use a comprehension. Do not hide an infinite loop behind a broad exception handler. An empty iterable is a useful test: a correct accumulator often needs to produce its identity value, such as zero for a sum.","exercise":"Write a prime checker using a loop through possible divisors. Test 1, 2, a square and a prime. Explain why checking only through the square root is enough.","interviews":[{"q":"How do break and continue differ?","a":"break terminates the nearest loop immediately. continue moves to that loop's next iteration; the loop itself remains active. In a while loop, update the control variable before a possible continue to avoid getting stuck."},{"q":"What is a loop invariant?","a":"It is a statement that holds before and after every iteration. Proving initialisation, preservation and the final consequence gives a structured argument for correctness."}],"quiz":{"question":"How many values are in range(2, 8, 2)?","options":["3","4","2","6"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The values are 2, 4 and 6; the stop value 8 is excluded."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"total = 0\nfor n in range(1, 6):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        continue\n    total += n\nprint(total)","expected":"9","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-strings","title":"Strings, slicing and text handling","body":"## Text is a sequence\nStrings support indexing, slicing and iteration. Index zero is the first character, negative indices count from the end, and `s[a:b]` excludes `b`. A slice creates a string; it does not mutate the original. Python strings contain Unicode characters, so do not assume every character is one byte or every visible symbol is one code point.\n\n## Useful transformations\n`strip()` removes leading/trailing whitespace; `split()` without an argument collapses runs of whitespace. `separator.join(parts)` joins strings efficiently. Use `lower()` or `casefold()` deliberately for case-insensitive matching, and f-strings for readable formatting. A `replace()` call returns a new string. Save that result if you need it later.\n\n## Parse before processing\nFor a comma-separated pair, split, trim and validate the expected field count before conversion. For real CSV files, use the `csv` module rather than splitting on commas: quoted fields can themselves contain commas or newlines. For JSON, use `json.loads()` and `json.dumps()` instead of string concatenation.\n\nRepeatedly building a long string with concatenation can create many intermediate objects. Collect fragments and join once when processing large inputs. For a palindrome, define whether punctuation and case count before choosing a two-pointer or reverse-and-compare implementation. Good text algorithms begin with a precise character and normalisation contract.","exercise":"Normalise a sentence by collapsing whitespace and reversing its word order. Then write a separate palindrome checker that ignores only ASCII spaces and case; document that exact contract.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is join useful?","a":"It combines a sequence of text fragments with a chosen separator in one operation. It communicates intent clearly and avoids repeatedly rebuilding a growing string inside a loop."},{"q":"Why not parse CSV with split(\",\")?","a":"CSV quoting allows a field to contain commas, quotes and line breaks. A CSV parser understands those rules; a plain split does not."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does \"  a   b  \".split() return?","options":["[\"a\", \"b\"]","[\"\", \"a\", \"\", \"\", \"b\", \"\"]","\"a b\"","[\"a   b\"]"],"answer":0,"explanation":"split() without a separator treats runs of whitespace as delimiters."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"text = \"  learn   one concept  \"\nwords = text.split()\nprint(\" \".join(reversed(words)))","expected":"concept one learn","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-collections","title":"Lists, tuples, sets, dictionaries and comprehensions","body":"## Choose by the operation\nUse a list for ordered items with duplicates, a tuple for a fixed record, a set for uniqueness/membership and a dictionary for key-to-value lookup. Modern Python dictionaries preserve insertion order; sets do not provide a sorted sequence. Dictionary keys must be hashable. An immutable tuple is hashable only when its contents are hashable too.\n\nList indexing is constant time; searching an unsorted list is linear. Appending is amortised constant time, while inserting at the front moves existing elements. Dictionary/set membership is expected constant time, with a worst case that is not guaranteed constant. `collections.deque` is usually a better queue than repeated `list.pop(0)`.\n\n## Build a frequency table\nStart an empty dictionary. For each value, increment `counts[value]` using a default of zero, or use `collections.Counter`. This replaces repeated rescans with one pass. Keep the distinction between a missing key and a key whose value is zero.\n\n## Comprehensions\n`[expression for item in source if condition]` transforms and filters. Dictionary and set comprehensions use the same idea. A generator expression produces values lazily rather than materialising every result. Prefer an ordinary loop when nested conditions or side effects make a comprehension hard to read.\n\nThe common grid trap is `[[0]*3]*3`: every row references the same list. Build each row independently with a comprehension. Copying containers without understanding which nested objects remain shared causes subtle state bugs.","exercise":"Count word frequencies without repeated list.count() calls. Build a 3-by-3 independent grid and prove that changing one cell leaves the other rows unchanged.","interviews":[{"q":"When should a set replace a list?","a":"When the operation is uniqueness or repeated membership checks and you do not need duplicates or positional indexing. A set changes the data contract, so it is not a drop-in replacement for every list."},{"q":"Why can a tuple contain a mutable list?","a":"Tuple immutability prevents rebinding its element slots. It does not freeze the objects referenced by those slots; a contained list can still change."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which creates three independent rows?","options":["[[0]*3 for _ in range(3)]","[[0]*3]*3","[0]*9","tuple([0]*3)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The comprehension evaluates a new inner list for every row."},"skills":[3,4],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"from collections import Counter\nwords = \"code test code learn\".split()\nprint(dict(Counter(words)))\nprint([n*n for n in range(5) if n % 2 == 0])","expected":"{'code': 2, 'test': 1, 'learn': 1}\n[0, 4, 16]","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-functions","title":"Functions, arguments, closures and type hints","body":"## Give a function one contract\nA function receives inputs, performs a small responsibility and returns a result. `print()` displays something; `return` passes a value to the caller. A function without an explicit return produces `None`. Keep calculation separate from input/output so the calculation is easy to test.\n\nPositional arguments are matched by order; keyword arguments make call sites explicit. `*args` collects positional arguments and `**kwargs` collects keyword arguments. Default values are evaluated once when the function is defined. A default list is therefore shared across calls: use `None` and create a new list inside the function when you need fresh state.\n\n## Scope and closures\nPython looks up names through local, enclosing, global and built-in scopes. A closure retains access to an enclosing function's variables. Use `nonlocal` when rebinding a name in that enclosing scope. Prefer explicit arguments and return values over hidden mutable globals.\n\n## Types help readers and tools\n`def total(values: list[int]) -> int:` expresses an input/output contract. `int | None` represents an optional value on supported Python versions. Generics keep the relationship between input and output types. A static checker such as mypy or pyright can catch mismatches before runtime, but annotations alone do not validate network or file input.\n\nUse runtime checks or a validation library at external boundaries. Avoid annotating everything as `Any`: that removes the checker precisely where you wanted help. Document what errors a function raises and whether it mutates its input.","exercise":"Implement a typed function that returns the average of a nonempty numeric list and raises ValueError for an empty list. Test that it never mutates the input.","interviews":[{"q":"What is the mutable-default-argument bug?","a":"A default object is created once at function definition. Mutating a default list can leak data between calls. Use a None default and construct the list inside the function."},{"q":"Do type hints make input safe at runtime?","a":"No. Static checkers analyse annotated code, but untrusted JSON can still have the wrong shape. Validate it when it enters the system and convert it to a trusted internal model."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does a function with no return statement return?","options":["None","0","False","An empty string"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Python returns None when execution reaches the end of a function."},"skills":[5,6],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"def add_item(item, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(item)\n    return items\nprint(add_item(\"a\"))\nprint(add_item(\"b\"))","expected":"['a']\n['b']","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-oop","title":"Classes, composition and dataclasses","body":"## Model state with behaviour\nA class defines how objects are constructed and what operations they support. `self` refers to the current instance. `__init__` initialises its state; it does not itself allocate the object. Put per-object lists on `self`, not in a shared class attribute unless sharing is intentional.\n\nEncapsulation means callers use a clear interface instead of depending on internal representation. Python's `_name` convention signals an internal detail; it is not an access-control boundary. A property can validate access while keeping a simple attribute-like interface.\n\n## Inheritance or composition?\nInheritance expresses substitutability: a specialised object should honour the base class's contract. Composition gives an object a collaborator, such as a report service that receives an email sender. Composition often makes tests easier because a small fake collaborator can replace a real network service.\n\nPolymorphism means callers can use the same operation across compatible objects. It does not require a large inheritance tree. Duck typing asks whether an object supports the required behaviour. Use abstract base classes or protocols when an explicit contract adds value.\n\n## Dataclasses\nA dataclass generates record conveniences such as an initializer, representation and value equality. Use `field(default_factory=list)` for a new list per instance. A frozen dataclass prevents ordinary attribute rebinding, but nested mutable values can still change. Useful dunder methods include `__repr__`, `__eq__`, `__len__` and iteration methods; implement only meaningful contracts.","exercise":"Create a Task dataclass and a TaskRepository interface. Implement an in-memory repository and test a service with it before adding JSON persistence.","interviews":[{"q":"Why prefer composition for a payment service?","a":"Payment processing, storage and notification are separate responsibilities. Passing collaborators into a service avoids inheriting unrelated behaviour and lets tests replace each external dependency."},{"q":"What does default_factory solve?","a":"It creates a fresh default value for each instance. A shared mutable default would let one task or account accidentally modify another instance's list."}],"quiz":{"question":"Where should an independent cart item list be created?","options":["In each instance, or with default_factory","As one shared class-level list","In a module-level global for all users","As a cached mutable default argument"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each cart needs its own list unless sharing is deliberately part of the design."},"skills":[8,9],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass, field\n@dataclass\nclass Task:\n    title: str\n    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)\na, b = Task(\"Learn\"), Task(\"Build\")\na.tags.append(\"python\")\nprint(a.tags, b.tags)","expected":"['python'] []","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-errors","title":"Exceptions and context managers","body":"## Handle the failure you understand\nAn exception interrupts ordinary control flow until a matching handler catches it. Catch specific exception types near the point where you can recover or add useful context. A broad `except: pass` hides programming errors and can make corrupt output look successful.\n\n`try` contains the risky operation, `except` handles selected failures, `else` runs when the try block succeeds, and `finally` runs during cleanup whether success or failure occurred. Raise a meaningful domain error when a caller needs to react differently, and use `raise NewError(...) from original` to preserve the cause.\n\n## Resource lifetime\nA file, lock or database transaction has a beginning and an end. A context manager expresses that lifetime with a `with` block. Its exit logic runs even if the body raises. `with open(path, encoding=\"utf-8\") as file:` closes the file reliably; manually scattering close calls across branches is fragile.\n\nA custom context manager implements `__enter__` and `__exit__`, or uses `contextlib.contextmanager` with a yield. Returning true from `__exit__` suppresses the exception, which should be a deliberate choice, not a default.\n\nFor retries, separate transient failures from invalid input. Do not retry a validation error or an operation with unsafe duplicate side effects. In web applications, log internal context securely but return a stable public error contract without stack traces or secrets.","exercise":"Read JSON from a file and report missing-file and invalid-JSON errors separately. Keep parsing in a function so a unit test can pass invalid text without creating a real file.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is catching Exception everywhere harmful?","a":"It makes unrelated failures look recoverable and can hide broken invariants. Catch a specific failure where you can recover, translate it at an application boundary, and let unexpected faults reach monitoring."},{"q":"Why use a context manager?","a":"It gives a resource a visible lifetime and guarantees exit logic when the block ends, including through exceptions. This is useful for files, locks, transactions and client sessions."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which block is intended for cleanup on both success and failure?","options":["finally","else","elif","match"],"answer":0,"explanation":"finally runs when control leaves the try statement, including during exception propagation."},"skills":[11,12],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import json\ntry:\n    json.loads(\"not JSON\")\nexcept json.JSONDecodeError:\n    print(\"Invalid JSON\")\nfinally:\n    print(\"Cleanup complete\")","expected":"Invalid JSON\nCleanup complete","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-lazy","title":"Iterators, generators and decorators","body":"## Separate a sequence from iteration\nAn iterable can provide an iterator. An iterator returns successive values through `next()` and raises `StopIteration` when exhausted. A list can produce fresh iterators; a generator object is normally consumed once. This matters when a pipeline is accidentally traversed a second time.\n\nA function containing `yield` creates a generator. Its local state is suspended between yielded values. That lets you process a large file one record at a time rather than loading the entire file. Laziness saves memory only when later stages also avoid collecting everything into a list.\n\n## Decorators wrap behaviour\nA decorator receives a function and returns a replacement callable. `@log_call` above a function is equivalent to rebinding it as `function = log_call(function)`. A wrapper can time execution, apply a cache or enforce a policy, but the caller's contract should remain clear.\n\nUse `functools.wraps` to preserve useful metadata. Handle arbitrary positional and keyword arguments if the wrapper promises to be general. Decorator order matters because each wrapper receives the result of the previous wrapping step. Framework route and fixture decorators are ordinary applications of this mechanism.\n\nAvoid caching functions that depend on hidden mutable state, current permissions or side effects without a carefully chosen key and invalidation rule. A faster wrong answer is still wrong.","exercise":"Write a generator that yields nonempty cleaned lines from text. Wrap a pure function with a decorator that counts calls, and verify that its return value is unchanged.","interviews":[{"q":"Can a consumed generator be iterated again?","a":"The same generator object continues from its current state and stays exhausted after completion. Call the generator function again to create a fresh iterator."},{"q":"What does a decorator actually change?","a":"It replaces the function name with another callable, usually a wrapper that delegates to the original. That wrapper can add behaviour before or after the call while preserving its public contract."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which expression is lazy?","options":["(x*x for x in range(100))","[x*x for x in range(100)]","list(range(100))","sorted(range(100))"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A generator expression produces values on demand."},"skills":[13,14],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"def squares(n):\n    for i in range(n):\n        yield i*i\ng = squares(3)\nprint(next(g))\nprint(list(g))\nprint(list(g))","expected":"0\n[1, 4]\n[]","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-async","title":"Async work, HTTP clients and concurrency","body":"## Choose concurrency for the workload\nConcurrency overlaps progress; parallelism executes work simultaneously. Asyncio is useful when tasks spend time waiting for network or other awaitable I/O. CPU-heavy Python code does not become faster merely because its function is declared async. Use an appropriate process pool or native library for CPU-heavy work; threads can help with blocking I/O.\n\nIn a conventional GIL-enabled CPython build, the global interpreter lock limits simultaneous Python bytecode execution by threads. Free-threaded builds change that assumption, so state the interpreter/build when discussing it. Either way, shared mutable state still needs a concurrency design.\n\n## Async step by step\n1. Define an `async def` function that awaits nonblocking operations.\n2. Start independent tasks with `asyncio.gather` or a task group.\n3. Bound concurrency with a semaphore; launching ten thousand external calls is not a free optimisation.\n4. Apply deadlines, propagate cancellation and close clients using an async context manager.\n\nHTTPX supports async HTTP requests. Requests is a common synchronous HTTP client. Set explicit connection/read timeouts, validate status and response shape, and retry only appropriate transient failures with backoff and jitter. Never run a blocking requests call directly inside an async endpoint's event loop.\n\nFor a batch report, validate each result and keep a deterministic fallback. A temporary model-provider outage should become a visible partial result, not an unbounded retry storm.","exercise":"Simulate three slow jobs with asyncio.sleep and gather them. Then limit concurrent jobs with a semaphore. Explain how you would add a timeout and handle one failed API call.","interviews":[{"q":"Does async mean parallel CPU execution?","a":"No. Async tasks cooperate around await points, usually to overlap I/O waits. CPU-bound code that does not yield blocks that event loop; use a suitable worker strategy for it."},{"q":"Why reuse an HTTP client?","a":"A client can pool connections and share configuration such as timeouts and headers. Reuse reduces repeated connection setup, while a context manager gives the pool a clear shutdown point."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is the best reason to add a semaphore to API calls?","options":["Bound concurrent requests and resource use","Make CPU instructions execute on every core","Remove the need for timeouts","Guarantee every response is valid"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Concurrency limits protect both your process and the downstream service."},"skills":[15,17,20],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import asyncio\nasync def job(n):\n    await asyncio.sleep(0)\n    return n*n\nasync def main():\n    print(await asyncio.gather(job(2), job(3)))\n# This browser already runs an event loop: use top-level await.\n# In a normal .py script, replace this line with asyncio.run(main()).\nawait main()","expected":"[4, 9]","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-files","title":"Files, formats, logging and CLIs","body":"## Treat file data as an external boundary\nUse pathlib for paths rather than manually joining separators. Choose an encoding, usually UTF-8, when reading or writing text. Text mode decodes characters; binary mode preserves bytes. Parse JSON and CSV with their standard modules. YAML needs an additional parser; use its safe-loading interface for untrusted input.\n\nFor a task tracker, read the old state, validate it, write the new content to a temporary file in the same directory, then replace the destination. This reduces partial-write corruption. Concurrent writers still need a lock or a stronger storage model. A successful write is not automatically a guarantee against sudden power loss; durability may require flushing and filesystem synchronisation.\n\n## Make useful operational output\nLogging records events with severity, time and context. DEBUG supports diagnosis, INFO records normal lifecycle events, WARNING marks an unusual condition and ERROR records a failed operation. Do not log passwords, tokens or entire private documents. A request or job identifier lets you connect related events.\n\n## A command-line interface\nargparse gives you named options, help text and type conversion. Parse arguments at the program entry point and pass explicit values into your logic. Environment variables are useful for deployment configuration, but defaults and validation still matter. Distinguish a missing secret from a non-secret default setting.\n\nAutomate a bounded task first: a file renamer with a dry-run preview, a JSON summary generator or a CSV cleaner. Print a useful result and choose a nonzero exit code when the operation fails.","exercise":"Design a JSON task tracker with add/list/complete commands. Include a dry-run option for migrations and recover cleanly from an invalid state file without silently overwriting it.","interviews":[{"q":"Why write a temporary file before replacement?","a":"It keeps readers from seeing a half-written destination during a normal successful replacement. It does not by itself solve concurrent updates, backups or every crash-durability requirement."},{"q":"Why avoid print for server diagnostics?","a":"A logging system can add severity, timestamps and structured context and route records to monitoring. print remains useful for CLI output that is deliberately part of the program's result."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which should never be written to ordinary logs?","options":["A full API secret","An operation name","A non-sensitive request ID","An error category"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Logs are copied and retained widely; secrets belong in protected configuration."},"skills":[16,18,25],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import csv, io, json\nrows = list(csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(\"name,score\\nAsha,80\\nRavi,90\\n\")))\nprint(json.dumps({r[\"name\"]: int(r[\"score\"]) for r in rows}))","expected":"{\"Asha\": 80, \"Ravi\": 90}","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-validation","title":"Validate data with clear boundary models","body":"## Static types and runtime data are different\nA JSON object can have missing fields, incorrect types or values outside the allowed range. Type hints describe what your Python code expects; they do not prove that a network response satisfies it. Validate data once at the boundary, then pass a well-defined internal model to the rest of the application.\n\nPydantic models declare fields, defaults and constraints and produce structured validation errors. A response model can also restrict which fields leave your service. Treat coercion rules deliberately: an API accepting the text \"12\" as an integer may be convenient, while a finance or identifier contract may require strict types.\n\n## A report input contract\nRequire a nonempty project ID, a list of tasks with enumerated statuses, nonnegative durations and a recognised report date. Reject impossible values with a message tied to the relevant field. Separate syntactic validation (the field is a date) from business validation (the caller can access that project).\n\nKeep settings validation separate from request validation. Read environment configuration at startup so missing required configuration fails clearly, rather than halfway through a user's request. Avoid including secret values in formatted validation errors or logs.\n\nThe example below uses only the standard library so it runs offline here. A project using Pydantic would express the same input contract in a model. Install that third-party package in the project's own environment; it is not bundled into the browser Python engine.","exercise":"Specify a ProjectSummary input model with project_id, completed_tasks and total_tasks. Reject completed counts larger than total, and return field-specific error information.","interviews":[{"q":"Where should runtime validation happen?","a":"At trust boundaries such as HTTP bodies, environment settings, file imports and model responses. Validate before business logic relies on the data; avoid scattering inconsistent checks throughout every function."},{"q":"Does schema validation enforce tenant permissions?","a":"No. A well-formed project ID can still belong to another tenant. Authorisation must verify the authenticated user's relationship to the requested object."}],"quiz":{"question":"A request passes schema validation. What still needs checking?","options":["Whether the caller can access the requested object","Whether JSON uses braces","Whether strings exist in Python","Whether the editor has syntax colours"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Data shape and access rights are separate concerns."},"skills":[19],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"def validate_report(data):\n    if not isinstance(data.get(\"project_id\"), str) or not data[\"project_id\"].strip():\n        raise ValueError(\"project_id is required\")\n    done, total = data[\"done\"], data[\"total\"]\n    if type(done) is not int or type(total) is not int or not 0 <= done <= total:\n        raise ValueError(\"expected 0 <= done <= total\")\n    return data\nprint(validate_report({\"project_id\":\"p1\", \"done\":2, \"total\":3}))","expected":"{'project_id': 'p1', 'done': 2, 'total': 3}","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-tests","title":"Test behaviour with pytest and small fakes","body":"## What a useful test proves\nA test chooses inputs, performs an operation and asserts an observable result. Start with a normal case, a boundary and a failure case. For a payment calculation, test zero items, a rounding boundary and invalid quantities, rather than only repeating the implementation's arithmetic inside the assertion.\n\npytest discovers test functions and uses ordinary assert statements. Parametrisation runs the same behaviour contract across explicit input/expected-output pairs. Fixtures provide setup and cleanup, such as a temporary directory or a reusable repository. A fixture should have a clear scope and avoid sharing mutable data accidentally across tests.\n\n## Isolate external work\nReplace an HTTP client with a fake that returns a known response or raises a timeout. Assert the feature's fallback, retry limit or returned error. Do not make a unit test depend on a live model provider, current network conditions or a real payment.\n\nUse integration tests for real boundaries such as database transactions, migrations and HTTP routing. A small end-to-end suite checks critical user journeys across components. Coverage counts executed code, not correctness: one assertion can still miss an important business invariant.\n\nFor async tests, use a compatible async test setup and test cancellation/timeouts explicitly. Keep tests deterministic by controlling randomness, clocks and external responses. When a bug is found, preserve a minimal case that would fail before the fix.","exercise":"Write explicit tests for your average function, including empty input and a negative value. Add a fake report provider that times out and assert the deterministic fallback text.","interviews":[{"q":"What should be mocked in a unit test?","a":"Usually external boundaries such as network calls, email, clocks or nondeterministic providers. Avoid mocking every internal function, because that can make tests describe an implementation instead of its behaviour."},{"q":"Why can 100% line coverage still miss a bug?","a":"It proves the lines were executed, not that meaningful cases and assertions were chosen. Different inputs, concurrency interleavings and business invariants may remain untested."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which test is most deterministic?","options":["A fake provider returning a fixed timeout","A call to a live paid API","A test depending on today's exchange rate","A test waiting for a random email"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A controlled fake removes network, billing and external-state variability."},"skills":[23],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"def average(values):\n    if not values:\n        raise ValueError(\"empty input\")\n    return sum(values) / len(values)\nassert average([2, 4]) == 3\ntry:\n    average([])\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(\"Both explicit cases passed\")","expected":"Both explicit cases passed","stdin":""}}]},{"id":"html","title":"HTML","group":"Web foundations","summary":"Structure meaningful pages, forms and content before adding styling.","duration":"1-2 weeks","project":"Build an About Me page with a portrait, navigation, a skills table and an accessible contact form.","sources":[{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"},{"title":"W3Schools HTML topic reference","url":"https://www.w3schools.com/html/"},{"title":"freeCodeCamp curriculum","url":"https://www.freecodecamp.org/learn/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"html-start","title":"Document structure, elements and attributes","body":"## A page is a tree of elements\nHTML describes the meaning and structure of a document. A tag such as `<p>` starts an element, its text is content, and `</p>` ends it. Attributes add information to the opening tag. Void elements such as `<img>` and `<input>` do not wrap child content. Nest elements correctly: a browser may repair invalid markup in surprising ways.\n\n## Build the smallest real document\nStart with `<!doctype html>` so the browser uses standards mode. Put the language on `<html lang=\"en\">`. The `<head>` contains metadata such as UTF-8 encoding, viewport information and the tab title; the `<body>` contains the visible document. A title in the head is different from the main heading in the body.\n\nUse `<h1>` for the main page heading and h2/h3 for subsections. Heading levels express structure, not font size. Paragraphs use `<p>`. Use `<strong>` for importance and `<em>` for emphasis; styling alone does not communicate those meanings.\n\n## IDs and classes\nAn `id` identifies one element in a document and can be a link target or a form label target. A `class` can be shared by many elements for styling and selection. Keep IDs unique. Escape literal `<`, `>` and `&` where needed in text using character references such as `&lt;` and `&amp;`.\n\nCreate a valid page first, open it in a browser, then inspect its DOM in DevTools. HTML validation and visual appearance are separate checks.","exercise":"Create an About Me document with a meaningful title, one main heading, two subsections and a paragraph containing an escaped less-than symbol. Inspect the DOM tree.","interviews":[{"q":"What is the difference between an element and an attribute?","a":"An element defines a piece of document structure, such as a paragraph or image. An attribute configures that element, such as an image source, alternative text or a language value."},{"q":"Why declare the viewport?","a":"Mobile browsers need an appropriate layout viewport to render a responsive design at device width. The viewport declaration enables that behaviour; responsive CSS still has to supply a suitable layout."}],"quiz":{"question":"Where does the browser-tab title belong?","options":["Inside title in head","Inside every paragraph","As the first class name","Inside the image alt attribute"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The title element in the document head supplies the page title."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"html","code":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"><title>My learning desk</title></head>\n<body><h1>My learning desk</h1><h2>Today</h2><p>Learn HTML &amp; build one page.</p></body>\n</html>","expected":"A heading, a subsection and a paragraph appear in the preview.","stdin":""}},{"id":"html-content","title":"Links, images, lists and tables","body":"## Use the right content element\nUse `<a href=\"...\">` for navigation and `<button>` for an action. A relative link points within your site; an absolute URL names a complete location. A fragment such as `#contact` jumps to an element ID. Write descriptive link text so a reader understands the destination without surrounding text.\n\nAn image needs a source and a text alternative that serves the image's purpose. A decorative image usually needs `alt=\"\"`, while an informative diagram needs a useful explanation. Set dimensions or an aspect ratio to reserve space and reduce layout shifts. Use responsive image techniques when one file would be unnecessarily large for small screens.\n\n## Lists express relationships\nUse `<ul>` when order does not matter, `<ol>` when sequence matters and `<li>` for each item. A description list uses `<dl>`, `<dt>` and `<dd>` for term/description pairs. Do not imitate a list using repeated paragraphs and decorative dashes when the semantic relationship is a list.\n\n## Tables are for tabular data\nUse a caption, table head/body groups and header cells. A column header can use `<th scope=\"col\">`; a row header can use `scope=\"row\"`. Keep the data comprehensible in reading order and provide horizontal overflow on narrow screens when needed. Do not use tables merely to position a page's sidebar.\n\nWhen a chart communicates a value, include the values or a data table as an alternative. Colour alone should not be the only way to identify a series or status.","exercise":"Add a project list, a contact fragment link and a skills table with a caption and headers. Write alternative text for an informative portrait and explain when an empty alt would be correct.","interviews":[{"q":"When should a button be used instead of a link?","a":"Use a link to navigate to a location or resource. Use a button to perform an action such as saving, submitting or opening a dialog. Native elements provide expected keyboard and browser behaviour."},{"q":"Why add table headers and a caption?","a":"They explain what the data means and help assistive technology associate values with the correct rows or columns. A visually bold cell alone does not provide that relationship."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which element represents an ordered sequence?","options":["ol","ul","div","span"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An ordered list communicates that the sequence of items matters."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"html","code":"<h1>Practice plan</h1>\n<ol><li>Read the rule</li><li>Solve an example</li><li>Review mistakes</li></ol>\n<table><caption>Weekly targets</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Questions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Arrays</th><td>10</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">SQL</th><td>5</td></tr></tbody></table>","expected":"A numbered plan and a labelled two-column table.","stdin":""}},{"id":"html-semantic","title":"Semantic pages, divs and accessible structure","body":"## Structure expresses purpose\nA div is a generic block container and a span is a generic inline container. They are useful when no more specific element fits. A meaningful page also has landmarks: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>` and `<footer>`. An article is independently meaningful content; a section groups a thematic part, normally with a heading.\n\n## Build a page skeleton\n1. Put a skip link before repeated navigation so keyboard users can reach main content quickly.\n2. Use a navigation landmark for the important site links and a main landmark for the primary content.\n3. Keep heading levels in a logical hierarchy. Use CSS to change visual size instead of choosing a wrong heading level.\n4. Use native interactive controls. A clickable div does not automatically gain focus, button semantics or Enter/Space handling.\n\n## Accessibility is behaviour\nTest the whole path using Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter and Space. Ensure visible focus and sufficient contrast. A hidden control should not remain unexpectedly focusable. For custom widgets, use ARIA only when native HTML cannot express the required role and state; incorrect ARIA can make a useful page worse.\n\nScreen-reader testing complements automated checks. A tool can find a missing label, but it cannot prove that a checkout instruction or an image alternative is helpful. Write specific error messages and associate them with the relevant field. Accessibility improves predictable interaction for everyone, including users on a small screen or with a temporary injury.","exercise":"Rebuild your About Me page with landmarks, a skip link and a logical heading order. Complete its form using only the keyboard and note every unexpected focus movement.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is semantic HTML useful beyond SEO?","a":"It communicates document structure and expected interaction to browsers and assistive technology. A native button, labelled form and clear heading hierarchy provide behaviour that generic containers do not."},{"q":"Should every div receive an ARIA role?","a":"No. Start with appropriate native elements. Add ARIA only when required semantics are missing, and implement the associated keyboard and focus behaviour as well."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the best default for a Save action?","options":["A button with meaningful text","A div with a click listener only","A paragraph with a green colour","An image without an accessible name"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A native button already has keyboard focus and activation behaviour."},"skills":[27,31],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"html","code":"<a href=\"#main\">Skip to content</a>\n<header><h1>Learning notebook</h1><nav aria-label=\"Main\"><a href=\"#main\">Notes</a> <a href=\"#contact\">Contact</a></nav></header>\n<main id=\"main\"><article><h2>Why I practise</h2><p>Small deliberate exercises build understanding.</p></article></main>\n<footer id=\"contact\">Contact: learner@example.invalid</footer>","expected":"A semantic page with a skip link, navigation, article and footer.","stdin":""}},{"id":"html-forms","title":"Forms, labels and validation","body":"## A form is an input contract\nA form groups controls and submits named values. The action identifies the destination and the method describes submission semantics. GET is useful for a search whose state belongs in a URL; POST sends a request body for a submission. A secure connection is still required for private data.\n\nAssociate a visible `<label>` with its input through `for` and a matching unique ID, or wrap the input in its label. A placeholder is not a replacement for a label: it disappears as the user types. The `name` controls the key used in form data; the ID is mainly for document relationships and selection.\n\n## Common controls\nUse text/email/password/number/date input types according to the value, a textarea for longer text, a select for a fixed list and radio buttons for one choice in a group. Checkboxes represent independent choices. Related controls can be grouped with fieldset and legend. In a form, set `type=\"button\"` on an action that must not submit; the default button type can otherwise surprise you.\n\n## Validate without trusting the browser\nAttributes such as required, minlength, min, max and pattern improve immediate feedback. The backend must validate again because a caller can send an HTTP request without your form. Show a useful field-specific error and retain valid entered data. Do not accept a price, role or account owner simply because a hidden form field supplied it.\n\nThe offline preview demonstrates native validation only; it does not send email or create an account.","exercise":"Create a contact form with a name, email, topic selection and message. Add visible labels, native constraints and a non-submit Clear button. Describe the checks a real backend must repeat.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is client-side validation insufficient?","a":"It helps usability, but callers can bypass it or modify requests. The server must enforce data constraints, permissions and business rules independently."},{"q":"What is the difference between an input name and id?","a":"The name determines the field key included in form submission. The id identifies an element in the document and can connect its label, error message or fragment link."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which label association is correct?","options":["label for=\"email\" with input id=\"email\"","label class=\"email\" with any input","A placeholder with no label","Two unrelated IDs with matching colours"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The for value and the input ID create an explicit label relationship."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"html","code":"<h1>Contact practice</h1>\n<form onsubmit=\"event.preventDefault(); document.getElementById('result').textContent='Valid demo form; nothing was sent.'\">\n<p><label for=\"name\">Name</label><input id=\"name\" name=\"name\" required minlength=\"2\"></p>\n<p><label for=\"email\">Email</label><input id=\"email\" name=\"email\" type=\"email\" required></p>\n<p><label for=\"message\">Message</label><textarea id=\"message\" name=\"message\" required></textarea></p>\n<button type=\"submit\">Check form</button><p id=\"result\" role=\"status\"></p></form>","expected":"Invalid inputs trigger browser feedback; a valid form displays a local demo message.","stdin":""}},{"id":"web-browser","title":"Page loading and DevTools diagnosis","body":"## Follow a page request\nThe browser resolves the host, establishes a connection and requests the document. It parses HTML into a DOM and CSS into style information. Scripts may modify that structure. The browser calculates styles and layout, paints content and composites layers. These activities overlap with downloading resources; a slow page is not explained by one universal number.\n\n## Find the bottleneck\nOpen the Network panel, reload and inspect the waterfall. Separate waiting for the server from transferring a large file. Look for unnecessary requests, oversized images and render-blocking resources. Cache behaviour changes repeat visits, so compare a controlled cold load with a repeat load.\n\nThe Performance panel shows main-thread work, long tasks and layout/paint activity. A large JavaScript loop can freeze interaction even after all files finished downloading. Memory tools help find retained objects and detached DOM nodes. Check whether a suspected leak persists after ordinary garbage collection rather than treating every temporary allocation as a leak.\n\n## Measure a change\nRecord a baseline, make one relevant change and repeat the same interaction under similar conditions. Core Web Vitals address different parts of experience: loading, visual stability and interaction responsiveness. Lab results help diagnose, while real-user measurements reveal actual device/network conditions.\n\nSource maps connect bundled code to original sources. They improve diagnosis, but deployment policy should control access and avoid publishing secrets in source files. A production minifier does not make embedded credentials private.","exercise":"Inspect this app with Network and Performance tools. Identify one request's timing phases and one user interaction. Write a before/after measurement plan for lazy-loading a large optional component.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a page remain unresponsive after downloading?","a":"JavaScript and rendering work can still occupy the main thread. A long synchronous task blocks input handling even when the network panel is idle."},{"q":"How would you investigate a layout shift?","a":"Find which element moved and when, then inspect late-loading images, fonts or injected content. Reserve dimensions and compare the same loading path after the change."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which tool best shows long main-thread tasks?","options":["The browser Performance panel","Only the page title","A screenshot of the footer","A Git branch name"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Performance timeline exposes CPU work and interaction delays."},"skills":[40,41],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"css","title":"CSS","group":"Web foundations","summary":"Style readable, responsive interfaces with a predictable cascade and layout.","duration":"2-3 weeks","project":"Style your About Me page, then build an original responsive landing page with keyboard-visible controls.","sources":[{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"},{"title":"W3Schools CSS topic reference","url":"https://www.w3schools.com/css/"},{"title":"freeCodeCamp curriculum","url":"https://www.freecodecamp.org/learn/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"css-cascade","title":"Selectors, cascade, units and colours","body":"## A rule selects and declares\n`p { color: navy; }` selects paragraphs and declares a property value. Use element selectors for broad defaults, classes for reusable components and IDs sparingly for styling. Combinators express relationships; pseudo-classes such as `:hover` and `:focus-visible` express state.\n\nWhen rules conflict, the cascade considers origins/importance, layers, specificity and order. More selectors do not automatically make a better design. Keep specificity low and avoid solving every conflict with `!important`. Some properties inherit from an ancestor; many layout properties do not.\n\n## Choose units deliberately\nPixels are useful for fine borders, while rem scales with the root font size and em depends on the relevant font size. Percentages depend on the containing context. Viewport units relate to the viewport, but mobile browser chrome makes dynamic viewport units useful for some full-height layouts. `clamp()` can bound a fluid value.\n\nDefine theme colours and spacing as custom properties so changes have one source of truth. Ensure contrast, and do not use colour alone to communicate an error. Keep focus visible when changing button styling.\n\nA practical debugging order is: inspect the element, check which rule wins, inspect computed values, then inspect the box and its containing layout. Guessing by repeatedly adding selectors makes the stylesheet harder to maintain.","exercise":"Create reusable text, button and card classes with custom properties. Intentionally create a conflicting rule, then explain from DevTools why one declaration wins.","interviews":[{"q":"Why avoid excessive selector specificity?","a":"It makes later overrides difficult and ties styles to a particular DOM shape. Small reusable classes with a deliberate cascade are easier to maintain and theme."},{"q":"How do rem and em differ?","a":"rem is based on the root element's font size. em is based on the relevant element's font size, with details depending on the property, so nested contexts can scale differently."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should you inspect first when a colour rule is ignored?","options":["The winning cascade and computed styles","The GitHub repository description","The database index","The screen brightness only"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DevTools shows overridden declarations and the value actually applied."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"css","code":":root { --accent: #157c52; }\nbody { font: 1rem/1.7 system-ui; padding: 2rem; }\nh1 { color: var(--accent); font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 5vw, 3rem); }\nbutton { padding: .7rem 1rem; color: white; background: var(--accent); border: 0; border-radius: .5rem; }\nbutton:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #d68b00; outline-offset: 3px; }","expected":"The preview has a fluid green heading and a visibly focusable button.","stdin":""}},{"id":"css-layout","title":"Box model, display, Flexbox and Grid","body":"## Every rendered box has layers\nContent is surrounded by padding, then border, then margin. With content-box, width describes content width; with border-box, the declared width includes padding and border. Set border-box consistently to simplify component sizing. Margin collapse can affect adjacent block margins; padding does not collapse in the same way.\n\n`display` chooses a layout participation model. Block elements normally start a new line; inline content flows in a line; flex and grid containers lay out their children with their own rules. `display:none` removes a box from layout; hiding visibility can preserve its occupied space.\n\n## Flexbox for one dimension\nUse Flexbox for rows or columns such as a toolbar. The main axis follows flex-direction; justify-content acts on that axis, while align-items acts on the cross axis. Gaps separate children. A child with long content may need `min-width:0` to shrink rather than overflow.\n\n## Grid for rows and columns\nGrid defines tracks and lets items align across two dimensions. `repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr))` gives three shrinkable equal columns. An auto-fit/minmax pattern can create a card grid that adapts to available width. Use Grid for a sidebar/content page and Flexbox within a toolbar.\n\nDo not fix an overflow bug by hiding all page overflow. Find the element whose intrinsic size or minimum size exceeds its container. Tables and code samples may need their own scroll region.","exercise":"Build a dashboard with a sidebar, a main area and responsive cards. Put a long unbroken code string in one card and repair the resulting overflow without clipping the entire page.","interviews":[{"q":"When would you choose Grid over Flexbox?","a":"Grid is useful when rows and columns should align together. Flexbox is useful for distributing items along one main axis, such as a navigation row; both can be combined."},{"q":"Why does min-width:0 fix some flex/grid overflows?","a":"A child can otherwise keep an automatic minimum based on its content. Allowing it to shrink lets wrapping or a deliberate inner scroll area handle long content."}],"quiz":{"question":"With border-box, what does width include?","options":["Content, padding and border","Only margin","The entire browser window","Only the text characters"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Border-box includes padding and border in the declared width, but not margin."},"skills":[28],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"css","code":"* { box-sizing: border-box; }\nbody { margin: 0; padding: 1.5rem; font: 1rem system-ui; }\nmain { display: grid; gap: 1rem; max-width: 50rem; margin: auto; }\np, h1 { padding: 1rem; margin: 0; background: #e3f4eb; border-radius: .5rem; }\n@media (min-width: 40rem) { main { grid-template-columns: 2fr 1fr; } h1 { grid-column: 1 / -1; } }","expected":"The example switches between one and two columns as the preview width changes.","stdin":""}},{"id":"css-responsive","title":"Responsive design, positioning and stacking","body":"## Start with a usable narrow layout\nMobile-first design means the base rules work at a small width. Add columns when content has enough space, not merely at a device brand's width. Let text wrap, size images within their containers and use comfortable interactive targets. Test keyboard interaction and zoom as well as a resized window.\n\nMedia queries respond to conditions such as viewport width or reduced-motion preference. Container queries respond to an element's containing layout, which is useful for components reused in different page regions. Fluid sizing with percentages, max-width and clamp can reduce the number of breakpoints.\n\n## Positioning is relative to a context\nStatic is ordinary flow. Relative positioning offsets a box while retaining its flow space and can establish a containing block. Absolute positioning removes a box from normal flow and positions it against its containing block. Fixed positioning relates to a fixed containing context, commonly the viewport. Sticky positioning combines flow participation with a scroll threshold and depends on the relevant scrolling ancestors.\n\n`z-index` does not rank every element in the entire document independently. Stacking contexts group descendants; properties such as transforms and opacity can create one. A large z-index inside a lower context may still sit below another context.\n\nFor a mobile topic drawer, preserve a close control, focus behaviour and page scrolling. Do not cover the code editor with a fixed footer unless sufficient space is reserved for the footer's actual height.","exercise":"Turn a two-column tutorial page into a narrow-screen layout with a topic drawer. Test at 360px, with long titles and at 200% zoom. Explain the containing block for an absolutely positioned badge.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can z-index:999999 fail to bring a modal above a header?","a":"The modal may be inside a stacking context below the header's context. Inspect the ancestor contexts instead of repeatedly increasing one descendant's number."},{"q":"How do media and container queries differ?","a":"A media query normally responds to environment conditions such as viewport width. A container query responds to a selected ancestor container's dimensions or supported style conditions, making a component more reusable."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a good breakpoint rule?","options":["Change layout when the content needs more space","Use one phone model as the only test","Prevent all text from wrapping","Always force three columns"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Content-driven breakpoints adapt better to varied screens, text lengths and zoom."},"skills":[29],"minutes":15},{"id":"css-modern","title":"Transitions, custom properties and modern CSS","body":"## Animate a meaningful change\nA transition interpolates between supported property values when state changes. An animation uses named keyframes for a sequence. Prefer transforms and opacity for many motion effects, and measure when a complex effect affects responsiveness. Animation should clarify a state change rather than delay the user's next action.\n\nRespect `prefers-reduced-motion` and avoid essential information that can be understood only while something moves. Keep loading and validation states clear even when motion is disabled.\n\n## Maintainable modern rules\nCustom properties hold reusable design values and participate in inheritance and the cascade. They can change by theme or component context. Cascade layers let a stylesheet declare the relative priority of groups such as base, components and utilities before specificity is considered within them.\n\n`:has()` selects an element based on a relative selector condition, such as a field group containing invalid input. Use it as a precise tool, not as an excuse to make the DOM and stylesheet inseparable. Container queries allow a card to respond to its allocated width instead of assuming that viewport width predicts its space.\n\nUse progressive enhancement: begin with readable content and working controls, then add modern presentation where supported. A CSS feature should improve the experience without making the underlying information disappear in a less capable environment.","exercise":"Add hover/focus transitions to a card, then disable nonessential motion under prefers-reduced-motion. Create a theme using custom properties and explain the fallback appearance.","interviews":[{"q":"Why use design tokens through custom properties?","a":"They give colours, spacing and typography shared names and a controlled override mechanism. A theme can change a small set of values instead of editing dozens of unrelated rules."},{"q":"Why honour reduced-motion preferences?","a":"Some motion can be uncomfortable or disruptive. The interface should preserve all information and actions while reducing nonessential movement for users who request it."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the best fallback strategy?","options":["Readable working content before optional effects","Hide all content until animation loads","Require hover to reveal every action","Use motion as the only error signal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Progressive enhancement preserves the core experience when optional presentation is unavailable."},"skills":[32],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"css","code":":root { --surface: #ecf8f1; --accent: #146b48; }\nbody { font: 1rem system-ui; padding: 2rem; }\nmain { padding: 2rem; border-radius: 1rem; background: var(--surface); }\nbutton { background: var(--accent); color: white; padding: 1rem; border: 0; transition: transform .15s ease; }\nbutton:hover { transform: translateY(-3px); }\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { button { transition: none; } button:hover { transform: none; } }","expected":"A themed card with a small optional button transition.","stdin":""}},{"id":"css-utilities","title":"Tailwind, Bootstrap and a reusable design system","body":"## Frameworks encode styling choices\nTailwind uses utility classes to compose a design directly in markup. Bootstrap supplies a collection of layout utilities and predesigned components. Neither removes the need to understand HTML semantics, the box model or responsive behaviour. Choose a consistent approach for the project instead of mixing several conflicting systems.\n\n## A utility workflow\nStart with shared colour, spacing and typography tokens. Compose a component from a small set of utilities, add responsive and interaction variants, then extract a component when its behaviour or repeated pattern deserves a name. A reusable component should expose meaningful variants such as size or intent rather than arbitrary unrelated class strings.\n\nWhen building class names dynamically, understand what the build tool scans. A runtime-generated class that is absent from scanned source may not be generated. Prefer complete known class names or the framework's supported mechanisms.\n\n## Design for states\nA button needs normal, focus, disabled and loading states. A form field needs valid, invalid and help-message states. A dashboard needs loading, empty, successful and error states. A design system is not just a colour palette; it is a set of accessible, consistent decisions and contracts.\n\nThe offline example uses plain CSS equivalents so it does not download a framework. In a real Tailwind or Bootstrap project, install/configure the selected major version according to its documentation; build setup can differ between versions.","exercise":"Define primary/secondary button variants, two card densities and a form-error style. Implement them first in plain CSS, then describe how you would map them to utilities without changing their semantics.","interviews":[{"q":"Does a utility CSS framework replace CSS knowledge?","a":"No. It gives convenient names and conventions for CSS decisions, but debugging alignment, overflow, specificity and responsive behaviour still requires understanding those decisions."},{"q":"What makes a design system more than a component folder?","a":"It includes shared tokens, accessible interaction contracts, documented variants and rules for states and composition. Consistency is a behaviour and maintenance concern as well as a visual one."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which state must an interactive design system include?","options":["Visible keyboard focus","Only hover on a mouse","Only a static screenshot","Only the brand colour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Keyboard users need a visible indication of the active control."},"skills":[30],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"javascript","title":"JavaScript","group":"Web foundations","summary":"Learn the browser language, from variables and arrays to DOM events and async APIs.","duration":"4-6 weeks","project":"Build a calculator, persistent task list, offline quiz and API-powered weather interface with loading/error states.","sources":[{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"},{"title":"JavaScript.info tutorial","url":"https://javascript.info/"},{"title":"The Odin Project learning paths","url":"https://www.theodinproject.com/paths"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"js-values","title":"Variables, operators, conditions and loops","body":"## Values and bindings\nUse const when a binding should not be reassigned and let when reassignment is part of the algorithm. const does not freeze an object. Avoid var in new beginner code so block-scoping rules remain predictable. JavaScript values include numbers, strings, booleans, null, undefined, objects and other specialised types such as BigInt.\n\nUse strict equality `===` for comparisons unless coercion is a deliberate part of the contract. The plus operator can add numbers or concatenate text, so convert input explicitly. `Number(\"\")` is zero; a conversion alone does not prove that required input was present. Number values use floating-point arithmetic, so money calculations need a deliberate representation and rounding policy.\n\n## Flow control\nUse if/else or switch for choices, for/while for controlled repetition and for...of to visit iterable values. for...in enumerates property keys and is not the default array-value loop. `break` exits and `continue` skips to the next iteration.\n\nNullish coalescing `??` supplies a default for null or undefined. The `||` operator also treats zero, false and an empty string as false, which may overwrite valid values. Optional chaining can safely access a missing nested property, but it should not silently hide a missing required field.\n\nTrace the initial value, each update and the stopping condition. Test empty inputs, zero and negative values before trusting a loop.","exercise":"Write a calculator function accepting two numeric values and an operator. Reject missing/invalid inputs and division by zero; keep DOM handling separate from calculation.","interviews":[{"q":"Can a const array be changed?","a":"Its binding cannot be reassigned, but the array object can still be mutated through methods such as push. Immutability requires a separate design or enforcement mechanism."},{"q":"When is ?? safer than || for a default?","a":"When false, zero or an empty string are legitimate values. ?? substitutes only for null or undefined, while || substitutes for every falsy operand."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does 0 ?? 10 evaluate to?","options":["0","10","null","undefined"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Zero is not null or undefined, so the left operand is retained."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"const values = [0, 2, 4];\nconsole.log(values.map(n => n * 3));\nconsole.log(0 ?? 10);\nconsole.log(0 || 10);","expected":"[0,6,12]\n0\n10","stdin":""}},{"id":"js-arrays","title":"Arrays, objects and useful transformations","body":"## Choose a transformation by its result\nmap creates one transformed output per input item. filter keeps matching items. find returns the first matching item or undefined. some/every answer boolean questions. forEach performs side effects and returns undefined; it is not a replacement for map when you need a result array. reduce combines items into an accumulator and benefits from a clear initial value.\n\nObjects group named properties. Use destructuring to read meaningful fields and spread to make shallow copies. A shallow copy shares nested objects, just as in Python. Use Map when key/value operations require arbitrary key types or a dedicated map interface; use Set for uniqueness.\n\n## Sorting is a common trap\nArray.sort mutates its array and compares strings by default. For numeric ascending order, pass `(a,b) => a-b`. Clone first when the original order must remain unchanged. A comparator must be consistent; returning only true/false is not a correct general numeric comparison.\n\n## Use data to drive the UI\nStore tasks as records with stable IDs, titles and completion flags. Derive the visible filtered list rather than keeping several independently mutated copies. For a quiz, keep question data separate from the current answer state. This makes saving, testing and rendering easier.\n\nTreat arrays from external JSON as untrusted until their shape is checked. An apparently successful fetch can still return a missing field or an unexpected object.","exercise":"Build a pure transformation that filters completed tasks, sorts them by title and returns only their IDs. Verify the original task array remains unchanged.","interviews":[{"q":"How do map and forEach differ?","a":"map returns a new array of transformed values. forEach is intended for side effects and returns undefined, so assigning its result does not give you transformed items."},{"q":"Why clone before sorting in state code?","a":"sort mutates the array. A shallow array copy lets you reorder a new container while preserving the previous state's array identity and order; nested objects are still shared."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which sorts numbers without mutating nums?","options":["[...nums].sort((a,b)=>a-b)","nums.sort()","nums.forEach(x=>x)","nums.sort((a,b)=>a>b)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A copy preserves the original, and the numeric comparator avoids string ordering."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"const nums = [10, 2, 5];\nconsole.log([...nums].sort((a,b) => a-b));\nconsole.log(nums);\nconsole.log(nums.filter(n => n > 3).map(n => n*2));","expected":"[2,5,10]\n[10,2,5]\n[20,10]","stdin":""}},{"id":"js-functions","title":"Functions, closures, this and prototypes","body":"## Functions are values\nA function can be passed as an argument, returned from another function or stored in an object. Arrow functions provide concise syntax and capture this from the surrounding scope. Ordinary function calls determine this from how they are invoked, with strict-mode details affecting unbound calls. An arrow is therefore not a universal replacement for an object method.\n\n## Scope and closures\nLexical scope depends on where code is defined. A closure retains access to its defining environment even after the outer call finishes. Use this for private state or a configured callback. Avoid unintentionally retaining large objects through long-lived closures.\n\nlet/const are block-scoped. Their bindings exist before the declaration is evaluated but cannot be accessed in the temporal dead zone. A loop with let creates useful per-iteration bindings for closures, unlike a single shared var binding.\n\n## Objects delegate through prototypes\nWhen a property is not found on an object, JavaScript can look along its prototype chain. Class syntax builds on that object model; it does not turn JavaScript into a different runtime. Prefer composition for independent behaviours instead of long inheritance chains.\n\nES modules use import/export and explicit dependencies. Browser modules have their own scope and strict semantics. Keep a module's public API small and avoid relying on execution order of unrelated global script files when designing a new application.","exercise":"Write a counter factory with increment/read functions that share private state. Create two counters and prove that their state is independent. Then explain a method losing its receiver when passed as a callback.","interviews":[{"q":"What is a closure?","a":"A callable that retains access to its lexical environment. For example, a counter function can continue reading and updating a variable created by its factory after that factory returns."},{"q":"Why can an arrow function be wrong as an object method?","a":"It captures this from its surrounding scope rather than receiving the object as this from method-call syntax. Use the function form that matches the intended receiver contract."}],"quiz":{"question":"What determines a closure's outer scope?","options":["Where the function is defined","Only where it is called","The current HTML colour","The last imported CSS file"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Closures follow lexical scope, which is determined by definition context."},"skills":[33],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"function makeCounter(){\n  let count = 0;\n  return () => ++count;\n}\nconst a = makeCounter(), b = makeCounter();\nconsole.log(a(), a(), b());","expected":"1 2 1","stdin":""}},{"id":"js-dom","title":"DOM selection, events and accessible interactions","body":"## The DOM is the live document\nquerySelector finds the first matching element; querySelectorAll returns a static collection of matches. Create elements, set properties and append them to update the page. Use textContent for plain text. Assigning untrusted text to innerHTML can create an injection vulnerability; sanitisation is required when HTML rendering is an intentional feature.\n\n## Events carry context\naddEventListener attaches behaviour without overwriting every other listener. The event target is where the event began; currentTarget is the element whose listener is running. Many events bubble to ancestors, so a container can delegate actions for dynamically added children. Use closest with a bounded container check when selecting an action element.\n\npreventDefault cancels a default browser action when cancelable; stopPropagation affects event propagation. They solve different problems. Do not use either as a universal fix for a confusing click path.\n\n## A task-list workflow\nKeep tasks in data, add one through a form submit event, render stable IDs, update completion through a button/checkbox, then persist the data. Handle empty and invalid states. A keyboard user should complete every action, and a live status region can announce a save or validation result.\n\nDo not attach a fresh global listener every time you render without removing the previous one. Duplicate listeners can cause double saves and confusing behaviour. Store state once and derive visible output from it.","exercise":"Build a task list with add, complete and delete. Use a labelled form, event delegation and textContent for task names. Verify a task name containing HTML stays text.","interviews":[{"q":"How do target and currentTarget differ?","a":"target is the element where the event originated. currentTarget is the element whose listener is currently executing, which may be an ancestor during bubbling."},{"q":"Why is textContent safer for a user-provided title?","a":"It inserts text without interpreting it as HTML. innerHTML parses markup, so untrusted content needs a suitable sanitisation policy when HTML is genuinely required."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which inserts an untrusted task title as plain text?","options":["element.textContent = title","element.innerHTML = title","eval(title)","new Function(title)()"],"answer":0,"explanation":"textContent does not interpret the title as HTML or executable code."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"html","code":"<h1>Local task demo</h1><form id=\"f\"><label for=\"task\">Task</label><input id=\"task\" required><button>Add</button></form><ul id=\"list\"></ul>\n<script>document.getElementById(\"f\").addEventListener(\"submit\",e=>{e.preventDefault();const input=document.getElementById(\"task\");const li=document.createElement(\"li\");li.textContent=input.value;document.getElementById(\"list\").append(li);input.value=\"\";});</script>","expected":"Each submitted title is appended as text; HTML-looking input is not executed.","stdin":""}},{"id":"js-async","title":"Promises, async/await and explicit error handling","body":"## A promise represents a future outcome\nA promise is pending, fulfilled or rejected. then transforms a fulfilled result; catch handles rejection. Returning a promise from a callback chains its result. Forgetting to return/await work can make an operation appear complete before its real work finishes.\n\nAn async function always returns a promise. await pauses that async function's continuation; it does not block the browser thread while a genuinely asynchronous operation waits. try/catch handles a rejection only when the promise is awaited within the try or returned through a suitable chain.\n\n## Choose sequencing deliberately\nAwait one operation before another when the second depends on the first. Use Promise.all for independent work when any failure should reject the group. Use Promise.allSettled when every outcome must be inspected. Neither automatically limits how many operations are started; use a bounded queue for large batches.\n\n## Make failure a visible state\nAn interface needs loading, success, empty and error states. Translate technical failures into a useful action while preserving diagnostic context for logs. A typed result or discriminated status can make expected failures explicit instead of throwing for every branch. Do not catch an error merely to return fake success.\n\nfinally is useful for releasing a loading state, but it should not overwrite a useful return value or mask the original failure. Cancel work that is no longer relevant when the underlying API supports cancellation.","exercise":"Simulate two independent data calls, one failing. Compare all and allSettled, then create UI state objects that distinguish loading, empty, error and successful data.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does try/catch sometimes miss a promise rejection?","a":"If asynchronous work is started without awaiting or returning it through the handled chain, the try block ends before the rejection occurs. Await inside the try when that is the intended control flow."},{"q":"When is allSettled preferable to all?","a":"When independent outcomes all matter, such as importing several unrelated files. Inspect every settled result and report partial failure rather than ignoring rejected items."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does an async function return?","options":["A promise","Always a string","Always undefined","A browser tab"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Even a directly returned value is wrapped in the async function's promise."},"skills":[34,39],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"async function load(){\n  const results = await Promise.allSettled([Promise.resolve(7), Promise.reject(new Error(\"unavailable\"))]);\n  console.log(results.map(r => r.status));\n}\nreturn load();","expected":"[\"fulfilled\",\"rejected\"]","stdin":""}},{"id":"js-event-loop","title":"Event loop, microtasks and workers","body":"## One thread can schedule many waits\nThe browser runs JavaScript tasks on a main thread that also participates in rendering and input handling. A task runs to completion before another ordinary task can interrupt its JavaScript execution. A long loop therefore delays interaction even if a timer was scheduled to fire sooner.\n\nPromise continuations are microtasks. After a task completes, the event loop drains relevant microtasks before moving on to later tasks and rendering opportunities. A timeout delay is a minimum scheduling threshold, not an exact execution time. Repeatedly adding microtasks can also starve other work.\n\n## Move the right work away\nA Web Worker can execute code off the main thread and communicate by messages. It does not have direct access to the document DOM. Data is cloned or transferred according to the messaging API; transferring a buffer changes ownership rather than magically making every object shared.\n\nUse a worker for expensive parsing or a code runner and terminate it when cancellation or a time limit requires it. Node.js has its own event-loop phases and worker-thread APIs; do not assume every browser-specific ordering statement transfers unchanged.\n\nWhen diagnosing lag, identify the long task first. Adding async to a CPU loop does not make the loop nonblocking. Break work into suitable chunks or use a worker, and keep cancellation and error messages part of the design.","exercise":"Predict the example output before running it. Then describe how this app can stop an infinite JavaScript loop without freezing its interface.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does setTimeout(fn,0) not run immediately?","a":"The callback becomes eligible for a later task after the current work finishes and relevant scheduling rules are satisfied. A zero delay is not an interruption of the current call stack."},{"q":"Can a worker directly modify a button in the page?","a":"No. It communicates with the main thread through messages. The main thread can validate the result and update the DOM."}],"quiz":{"question":"What normally runs first after synchronous code: a promise continuation or a timer task?","options":["The queued promise microtask","The timer always","They execute simultaneously on the DOM","Neither can run"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Relevant microtasks are drained before later timer tasks are taken."},"skills":[35],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"console.log(\"A\");\nPromise.resolve().then(() => console.log(\"B\"));\nconsole.log(\"C\");\nreturn Promise.resolve();","expected":"A\nC\nB","stdin":""}},{"id":"js-browser","title":"Fetch, cancellation, streams and browser storage","body":"## Fetch is only the beginning of validation\nfetch resolves to a Response for many HTTP error statuses; check response.ok or the expected status before reading a successful payload. Parsing JSON can fail, and parsed data can still have the wrong shape. A weather interface should distinguish invalid search input, no result, a rejected request and a provider outage.\n\nAbortController can cancel a supported request. When a user changes a search quickly, cancel stale work or ignore results that no longer match the current request. Otherwise an older, slower response can overwrite the latest search. Streams let a client process chunks before the whole response is available; chunk boundaries do not necessarily match JSON or line boundaries.\n\n## Choose storage by the data\nlocalStorage stores strings synchronously within an origin. Use JSON serialisation for structured data, handle quota errors and keep writes small. IndexedDB supports larger structured asynchronous storage. Cookies travel with matching HTTP requests and can have server-set HttpOnly/Secure/SameSite controls. Browser JavaScript-readable storage is exposed to successful script injection.\n\nOrigins include scheme, host and port. GitHub Pages, localhost and different localhost ports have separate storage. Offline browser saving does not automatically become cloud sync. Export/import or a configured backend is needed to move progress.\n\nDesign migrations for saved data: include a format version, validate imported keys and avoid silently replacing current work with an incompatible file. Never place a server API secret in frontend code just because it is minified.","exercise":"Design a weather search state machine with cancellation and a cached last successful result. For an offline-only task app, specify storage keys, a versioned export format and quota-failure behaviour.","interviews":[{"q":"Why check response.ok after fetch?","a":"An HTTP response such as 404 or 500 can still fulfil the fetch promise. Treating every fulfilled response as successful can turn an error page into an unexpected parsing or application failure."},{"q":"Why does saved progress differ between localhost ports?","a":"The port is part of the origin. Browser storage is isolated by origin, so moving to another port requires a deliberate transfer or a shared local/cloud backend."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is included in a web origin?","options":["Scheme, host and port","Only the page heading","Only the repository name","Only the browser brand"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Changing scheme, host or port changes the storage/security origin."},"skills":[42,43],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"const text = JSON.stringify({version:1, tasks:[{id:1, title:\"Revise\"}]});\nconst restored = JSON.parse(text);\nconsole.log(restored.version, restored.tasks[0].title);","expected":"1 Revise","stdin":""}}]},{"id":"typescript","title":"TypeScript","group":"Frontend engineering","summary":"Use types to express contracts, then validate data where it crosses runtime boundaries.","duration":"1-2 weeks after JavaScript","project":"Share typed API contracts between a task dashboard and its backend without trusting unvalidated JSON.","sources":[{"title":"TypeScript handbook","url":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/intro.html"},{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"ts-types","title":"Types, unions, narrowing and generics","body":"## TypeScript checks JavaScript before execution\nTypeScript adds a static type system and compiles to JavaScript. Most type annotations are erased; they do not turn external JSON into safe runtime values. Start with strict checking and allow inference where the value is clear.\n\nUse object types/interfaces for shapes, unions for alternatives and literal types for restricted states. An optional property can be absent, which is different from asserting that it always exists. Prefer unknown for untrusted data and narrow it before use; any opts out of useful checking.\n\n## Represent states that cannot be confused\nA discriminated union gives each variant a common tag, such as status: loading, error or ready. A switch on that tag lets the checker narrow the available fields. This prevents a component from accidentally reading data from an error state.\n\nGenerics preserve a relationship between types: an identity function can return the same type it receives. They are not a reason to make every function abstract. Utility types such as Pick, Omit, Partial and Readonly derive useful shapes, but Readonly is a static view, not a deep runtime freeze.\n\n## A careful boundary\nReceive a payload as unknown, validate its shape with code or a schema, then expose a typed internal value. A cast such as `payload as Invoice` asserts your belief; it does not prove that a field exists. Keep missing, loading and failed states visible in the model.","exercise":"Model a request as a discriminated union of loading/error/ready. Write a function that renders every variant and explain how an exhaustive check catches a newly added variant.","interviews":[{"q":"How do unknown and any differ?","a":"unknown requires narrowing before most operations, so it preserves a safety boundary. any permits unchecked operations and can spread that loss of checking through other code."},{"q":"What is a good use of generics?","a":"To express a meaningful type relationship, such as returning the same element type that an input array contains. A generic with no useful relationship often adds complexity without improving the contract."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does a type assertion do at runtime?","options":["It performs no automatic validation","It validates every field","It encrypts the object","It converts every string to a number"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Type assertions are erased and do not prove the runtime shape."},"skills":[36],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"typescript","code":"type Load<T> =\n  | { status: \"loading\" }\n  | { status: \"error\"; message: string }\n  | { status: \"ready\"; data: T };\nfunction label(state: Load<string[]>): string {\n  if (state.status === \"ready\") return `${state.data.length} tasks`;\n  if (state.status === \"error\") return state.message;\n  return \"Loading\";\n}","expected":"Compile in a TypeScript project. Each branch sees only the fields valid for its status.","stdin":""}},{"id":"ts-contracts","title":"Shared contracts, runtime schemas and monorepos","body":"## One contract, several consumers\nA frontend and backend should agree on field names, nullable values, error shapes and pagination. A shared package can export TypeScript types and runtime schemas. Keep this package free of server-only secrets and dependencies so importing a request type cannot accidentally bundle database code into the browser.\n\nTypes reduce accidental disagreement in one codebase, but callers outside that codebase can still send arbitrary values. Use a runtime validator such as Zod at request/response boundaries and derive types from the schema when that reduces duplication. Separate a database entity from a public response so sensitive fields never become public merely through object spreading.\n\n## Evolve safely\nPrefer additive optional fields when backward compatibility requires it. Define when old clients stop being supported and how breaking changes are versioned. Generated OpenAPI clients can help with contracts across different languages, but generated code still needs integration checks.\n\n## Repository structure\nA monorepo stores related packages together. It needs explicit package boundaries, build ordering and compatible tooling. Path aliases are a developer convenience, not a complete runtime module-resolution policy. A frontend build may resolve an alias that Node does not understand without configuration.\n\nTest the actual serialised request and response, not only that two source files import the same type. Dates, big integers and undefined values have important JSON representation rules that a static type name can hide.","exercise":"Define separate internal User and public UserSummary contracts. Add runtime validation for a task creation body and design one backwards-compatible response change.","interviews":[{"q":"Why not return a database user object directly?","a":"It may contain password hashes, internal flags or fields never intended for clients. Create an explicit response contract and select only the allowed fields."},{"q":"What does sharing types fail to guarantee?","a":"It does not validate external callers, deployed version compatibility or actual serialisation. Runtime schemas and integration tests are still needed at the boundary."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which belongs in a browser-safe shared contract package?","options":["Public request/response schemas","A private database password","A server signing key","A production session cookie"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shared frontend imports must contain public contracts, never server secrets."},"skills":[37,38],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"git","title":"Git, Linux & Tooling","group":"Engineering workflow","summary":"Track changes, collaborate through branches and build a reproducible project.","duration":"Git basics: 3-5 days","project":"Publish a portfolio repository with a clear README, useful commit history and a passing CI build.","sources":[{"title":"Pro Git book","url":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2"},{"title":"Node.js Learn","url":"https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs"},{"title":"Docker Get started","url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"git-basics","title":"Working tree, staging and useful commits","body":"## Git records snapshots\nThe working tree contains files you edit. The index or staging area selects the changes for the next commit. A commit records a snapshot with metadata and parent history. GitHub hosts repositories and collaboration features; Git itself also works offline.\n\n## A normal change cycle\n1. Inspect `git status` and `git diff` before editing and before committing.\n2. Stage a coherent change with `git add`, then inspect the staged diff.\n3. Commit with a message that explains the outcome. Several unrelated changes are harder to review and undo together.\n4. Fetch/pull according to the team's workflow, resolve conflicts deliberately and push the intended branch.\n\nUse .gitignore for build output, local environments and secrets. Ignoring a file does not remove a secret already committed in history; rotate exposed credentials and follow a deliberate cleanup process. A README should explain the project, setup, execution, testing and limitations without inventing results.\n\nRemote-tracking references describe fetched remote state. They are not a live view that changes without network communication. Check the branch and remote before a push. Avoid a force push on shared history unless the team has explicitly agreed to that operation.\n\nGitHub Pages serves static assets. A browser app can use it, but it does not run your Python/Node backend or native compiler merely because the files are in the repository.","exercise":"Create a practice repository, commit an About Me page, add a README and ignore a local settings file. Inspect the staged diff before each commit and explain what is and is not being published.","interviews":[{"q":"How do add, commit and push differ?","a":"add selects changes in the local index, commit records a local snapshot, and push transfers relevant commits/references to a remote. A commit can exist and be useful without any network connection."},{"q":"Does adding a secret to .gitignore remove an earlier leak?","a":"No. The earlier commit can still contain it. Revoke or rotate the credential, then handle history cleanup according to the repository's collaboration policy."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which command reviews exactly what is staged?","options":["git diff --cached","git push --all","git init --force","git checkout --delete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The cached/staged diff compares the index with the current commit."},"skills":[],"minutes":15},{"id":"git-branches","title":"Branches, merging, rebasing and conflict resolution","body":"## A branch is a movable reference\nA branch name points to a commit. New commits advance the current branch. Use a feature branch to keep work reviewable without mixing it into a stable branch prematurely. A pull request is a collaboration/review workflow around proposed changes, not a different kind of Git commit.\n\nMerging integrates histories and can preserve their structure with a merge commit. Rebasing replays commits onto another base and creates new commit identities. Rebasing unpublished local work is common; rewriting shared history requires coordination because other people may depend on the old identities.\n\n## Resolve a conflict from intent\n1. Read the conflicting versions and understand both changes.\n2. Decide the desired combined behaviour, rather than choosing every line from one side automatically.\n3. Edit the file, remove conflict markers, stage it and continue the operation.\n4. Run the checks relevant to the combined result.\n\nUse `git log --oneline --graph` to understand history. `git restore` and reset have different effects on working tree/index/history, so inspect their scope before using them. Revert makes a new commit reversing an earlier change and is often suitable for a shared branch.\n\nA clean merge does not prove semantic correctness. Two changes can combine without textual conflicts while breaking an API contract or duplicating a side effect.","exercise":"Create two branches that edit the same lesson title, merge them and resolve the conflict intentionally. Then describe why a revert is often easier to collaborate on than rewriting a published main branch.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does rebasing change commit IDs?","a":"A commit includes its parent and other metadata. Replaying the change onto a new parent creates a new commit object even when the patch looks similar."},{"q":"Can a conflict-free merge still break a feature?","a":"Yes. Textual compatibility does not prove behavioural compatibility. For example, one change may rename a field while another adds a consumer that still expects its old meaning."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is generally appropriate for undoing a bad shared commit?","options":["A reviewed revert commit","Deleting every teammate's branch","A hidden force push without discussion","Editing the remote object database manually"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A revert preserves shared history while recording an explicit reversal."},"skills":[45],"minutes":15},{"id":"tooling-build","title":"Build tools, linting and repeatable commands","body":"## Separate source, dependencies and output\nA package manifest declares scripts and dependencies; a lockfile records resolved versions. Install the project's expected versions rather than relying on a global tool that happens to be installed. A reproducible CI command should work in a fresh checkout.\n\nVite provides a development server and production build workflow. Bundling groups dependencies for delivery; tree shaking can remove unused exports when the module structure and side-effect information permit it. Source maps support debugging. Minification reduces representation size but does not protect embedded secrets.\n\n## Quality gates have different jobs\nFormatting keeps style consistent. Linting catches selected mistakes and enforces conventions. Type checking catches certain contract errors. Tests check behaviour. A build proves that production output can be produced; it does not prove every user journey works.\n\nDefine named scripts such as dev, lint, typecheck, test and build so teammates and CI use the same commands. Keep generated output separate from source. Deployment should promote a reviewed build, and environment configuration should identify the intended backend without exposing private server credentials.\n\n## Terminal fluency\nLearn your current directory, relative/absolute paths, quoting and exit codes. Linux pipes connect one process's output to another's input; redirection writes or reads streams. Use focused search commands and inspect a path before deleting files. Shell syntax differs between PowerShell and Bash, so copy a command only after identifying its intended shell.","exercise":"Add documented lint, test and build scripts to a sample project. Explain what each check catches, which output folder is deployable and why a frontend environment variable cannot hold a private API secret.","interviews":[{"q":"Why commit a lockfile?","a":"It records the resolved dependency graph so a clean install can reproduce the reviewed versions. It supports reproducibility, while updates still need testing and security review."},{"q":"Does a successful build prove the app works?","a":"It proves the build pipeline completed. Runtime configuration, permissions, network failures and user workflows can still be wrong, so meaningful tests and deployment checks remain necessary."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is safe to expose in a frontend bundle?","options":["A public non-secret API base URL","A database administrator password","A private signing key","A payment webhook secret"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Frontend code and its configuration are delivered to users and must not contain server secrets."},"skills":[44,46],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"react","title":"React","group":"Frontend engineering","summary":"Build component-based interfaces with explicit state, accessible forms and tested data flows.","duration":"4-6 weeks after JavaScript","project":"Build a notes dashboard with search, routing, validation, loading/error states and three end-to-end tests.","sources":[{"title":"React Learn","url":"https://react.dev/learn"},{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"react-components","title":"JSX, props, composition and list keys","body":"## A component describes UI from inputs\nA React component is a function that returns a description of an interface. JSX is syntax transformed by a build tool; the browser does not interpret raw JSX as ordinary JavaScript. Begin in a configured React project, such as one created with a compatible Vite setup.\n\nProps are inputs passed by a parent. Treat them as read-only and derive output from them. Compose a page from small components with clear responsibilities: a TaskCard displays one task, a TaskList maps tasks and a dashboard owns relevant state. children allows a component to wrap content without knowing every detail of that content.\n\n## Render lists with stable identity\nUse a stable data ID as a key among siblings. A key helps React match an item across renders; it is not automatically passed as a normal prop. Array indices become unsafe when items can be inserted, deleted or reordered because identity can move to a different item.\n\nRender conditional UI deliberately. A loading state, an empty list and a failed request are different states. Avoid mixing an accidental numeric zero into JSX through a loose `count && ...` expression.\n\nComposition normally scales better than a component with dozens of flags. Extract a component for meaningful repeated structure or behaviour, while keeping simple local markup readable. Do not copy props into state unless there is a specific lifecycle reason and a clear update policy.","exercise":"Build NoteCard and NoteList components. Insert a new note at the start and verify that each note keeps its own editing state through a stable key.","interviews":[{"q":"Why are stable keys important?","a":"They let React preserve the intended identity of sibling elements across insertions and reordering. An index key can make state follow a position rather than the actual data item."},{"q":"What is composition in React?","a":"Combining focused components through props and children instead of inheriting large component classes or adding unrelated flags. It keeps responsibilities and reusable structure explicit."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the best list key when notes can be reordered?","options":["note.id","The current array index","Math.random() on every render","The note card colour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A stable unique data ID preserves the intended item identity."},"skills":[47,50],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"jsx","code":"function NoteCard({ note, children }) {\n  return <article><h2>{note.title}</h2><p>{note.body}</p>{children}</article>;\n}\nfunction NoteList({ notes }) {\n  return <section>{notes.map(note => <NoteCard key={note.id} note={note} />)}</section>;\n}","expected":"In a React project, each note renders with stable sibling identity.","stdin":""}},{"id":"react-state","title":"State, events, reducers and context","body":"## State is a snapshot for a render\nuseState stores a value across renders. Calling its setter schedules a new render; it does not mutate the variable already captured by the current render. When the next value depends on the previous one, a functional update such as `setCount(c => c + 1)` avoids relying on a stale captured value.\n\nUpdate objects and arrays immutably at the level being changed. Copy the relevant container and replace the changed item. A spread is shallow, so nested objects need their own deliberate update. Derive a filtered list or total from existing state instead of maintaining duplicate state that can disagree.\n\n## Put state where it belongs\nKeep state local until another component genuinely needs to coordinate with it. Lift shared state to the nearest useful owner. Context distributes a value to a subtree; it does not automatically optimise every consumer. Split unrelated concerns if changes cause excessive rerenders.\n\nuseReducer expresses related transitions through actions, which can make a complex form or workflow easier to test. Redux Toolkit is useful when shared client state needs consistent reducers, tooling and explicit transitions; it is not required for every small page. Server data caching has a different lifecycle from local UI state.\n\nEvent handlers should perform the user's action and update state predictably. Avoid an effect that simply copies one derivable state value into another; it creates extra renders and synchronisation bugs.","exercise":"Build a task filter with local search state and derived visible tasks. Then model a three-step form with reducer actions and explain which state, if any, needs context.","interviews":[{"q":"Why use a functional state update?","a":"It computes the next state from the current queued state, which is useful when multiple updates or callbacks depend on a previous value rather than the render's captured snapshot."},{"q":"When should state become global?","a":"When a meaningful set of distant consumers needs coordinated ownership. Start with local state and lift it deliberately; global state adds coupling and should not become a default dumping ground."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is best for a filtered list?","options":["Derive it from tasks and filter state","Store independent copies for every filter","Mutate the original list during render","Refetch all tasks on every keystroke without cancellation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Derived output avoids several state copies drifting apart."},"skills":[61],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"jsx","code":"import { useState } from \"react\";\nexport function Counter() {\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>;\n}","expected":"Each activation increments the component state and rerenders the label.","stdin":""}},{"id":"react-effects","title":"Effects, refs, custom hooks and cleanup","body":"## Effects synchronise with external systems\nAn effect is appropriate for a subscription, timer, external widget or other system outside React's render calculation. It is usually unnecessary for deriving a value from props/state or handling a specific button action. Keep render logic pure so React can call it safely.\n\nDependencies describe reactive values used by the effect. Omitting a dependency can retain a stale closure; adding unrelated unstable objects can rerun the effect repeatedly. Fix the design rather than suppressing the linter without understanding the relationship.\n\n## Cleanup is part of the contract\nReturn cleanup that disconnects the subscription, clears the timer or cancels/invalidates pending work. Development checks can intentionally run an extra setup/cleanup cycle; code should behave correctly through it rather than assuming setup occurs only once.\n\nuseRef keeps a mutable value without causing a render and can reference a DOM node. A ref is not a replacement for state when the displayed UI must update. Custom hooks extract reusable stateful logic with the same hook rules; call hooks consistently at the top level of a component or another hook.\n\nMemoisation can reduce repeated calculations or stabilise identities where it matters, but it is not a correctness tool. Measure the expensive path first. A stale value caused by incorrect dependencies remains wrong even when useMemo makes it fast.","exercise":"Create a custom hook that subscribes to the browser online/offline events and cleans up both listeners. Test mounting, unmounting and remounting without duplicate notifications.","interviews":[{"q":"When is useEffect unnecessary?","a":"When the value can be calculated from current props/state or when the work belongs directly to a user action handler. Effects are for synchronising with external systems, not every update."},{"q":"Why does an effect need cleanup?","a":"The component can rerun its effect or unmount. Without cleanup, old listeners, requests or timers remain active and can duplicate work or update stale state."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which value should normally be state rather than a ref?","options":["A counter displayed in the UI","An interval handle used only for cleanup","A DOM element reference","A mutable diagnostic flag not rendered"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Displayed reactive data needs a render update when it changes."},"skills":[48,49],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"jsx","code":"import { useEffect, useState } from \"react\";\nfunction useOnline() {\n  const [online, setOnline] = useState(navigator.onLine);\n  useEffect(() => {\n    const update = () => setOnline(navigator.onLine);\n    addEventListener(\"online\", update); addEventListener(\"offline\", update);\n    return () => { removeEventListener(\"online\", update); removeEventListener(\"offline\", update); };\n  }, []);\n  return online;\n}","expected":"A reusable hook tracks connection hints without leaving duplicate listeners.","stdin":""}},{"id":"react-data","title":"Server state, queries and optimistic updates","body":"## Remote data has its own lifecycle\nAn API result can be loading, stale, fresh, failed or invalidated. A query library such as TanStack Query or SWR manages caching, deduplication and refetching around a query key. A query key must include the meaningful inputs, such as tenant ID, filters and page cursor, without exposing private secrets.\n\nChoose freshness rules deliberately. Refetching on focus can be useful for a dashboard but does not replace an access-control check on the server. A mutation should invalidate or update relevant cached data after success. Do not maintain a second unsynchronised copy of the same server list in global client state.\n\n## Optimistic does not mean guaranteed\nAn optimistic update shows an expected result before the server confirms it. Keep enough previous state to roll back and handle a conflicting server response. For a payment or stock decision, the server remains authoritative; a frontend animation cannot prove success.\n\n## Merge realtime events carefully\nSocket.io can deliver updates to a connected client, but reconnects and duplicates require a policy. Include stable event/object IDs, apply idempotent updates, and reconcile with a fresh snapshot after a gap. Do not simply append every received event forever.\n\nAxios is an HTTP client convenience; it does not replace data ownership or cache design. Whether using it or fetch, centralise base URL/error handling and apply request cancellation when the user moves to a different resource.","exercise":"Design query keys for tasks filtered by tenant, status and page. Describe an optimistic rename, including the rollback and how a later realtime event is merged.","interviews":[{"q":"How is server state different from UI state?","a":"Server state is owned remotely and can change independently; it needs freshness, invalidation and request coordination. A local open-dialog flag has a different ownership and lifecycle."},{"q":"Why refetch after a socket reconnect?","a":"Events may have been missed while disconnected. A snapshot or replay protocol reestablishes a known state; assuming that every past event arrived can leave a silently incomplete cache."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must a task-list query key include when results vary by tenant?","options":["The tenant identity and relevant filters","Only the component colour","Only a constant word tasks","A private API secret"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The cache identity must distinguish different result sets without exposing secrets."},"skills":[62,64],"minutes":15},{"id":"react-forms","title":"Forms, validation, error boundaries and loading UI","body":"## Treat a form as a workflow\nControlled inputs render from state and update it through events. An uncontrolled input keeps its current value in the DOM and can be read through form APIs or refs. Either approach needs labels, constraints, helpful errors and a clear submit state.\n\nFor larger forms, React Hook Form can manage field registration and validation with a schema such as Zod. Keep server errors separate from basic client validation. On failure, retain entered data and focus or link to a useful error summary. Prevent accidental duplicate submission, and still make the backend operation idempotent where required.\n\n## Isolate different kinds of failure\nAn error boundary catches supported render-time descendant errors and shows a fallback region. It does not automatically catch every event-handler error or every asynchronous rejection. Handle those at their own boundaries. Place error boundaries where one failed panel should not destroy the entire dashboard.\n\nSuspense coordinates loading for supported data/code sources. It is not a universal replacement for every loading boolean around an arbitrary effect fetch. Design loading, empty, error and retry states explicitly.\n\nAn optimistic UI should announce progress accessibly and provide a recovery path. Do not hide a failed save behind a permanent success toast or erase the draft just because a request started. A useful form acknowledges the server's final outcome.","exercise":"Design a note editor that preserves its draft on a failed save. Specify client validation, server errors, duplicate-submit prevention and a panel-level render fallback.","interviews":[{"q":"Does an error boundary catch every async error?","a":"No. Its main role is supported errors in descendant rendering/lifecycles. Event handlers and arbitrary async work need explicit error handling in their own control flow."},{"q":"Why keep form data after a failed request?","a":"The user's work remains valuable even if the network or server fails. Preserve it, show the failure clearly and allow a deliberate retry rather than forcing the user to retype."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should a failed save do?","options":["Preserve the draft and offer a useful retry","Erase the form and claim success","Hide every error permanently","Trust that disabling a button prevents all server duplicates"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A recoverable interface retains user work and reports the actual outcome."},"skills":[51,52],"minutes":15},{"id":"react-design","title":"Design systems, charts, drag-and-drop and localisation","body":"## Reuse interaction contracts\nA design system combines tokens, accessible primitives, component variants and documentation. Radix-style primitives or shadcn/ui can provide a starting point, but the application still owns labels, focus management, semantics and tested composition. Do not assume a copied component is automatically accessible in every configuration.\n\n## Make dashboards answer a question\nChoose a chart by the comparison: bars compare categories, lines show ordered trends and a table provides exact values. Recharts can render a React dashboard, but you must supply meaningful axes, units, legends and an accessible data alternative. Distinguish revenue from payment count and label the period/time zone.\n\nDrag-and-drop, for example with dnd-kit, needs a non-drag alternative and keyboard support. When a Kanban task moves, update both the visible state and the server contract, handle failure and avoid allowing a client to bypass a permission or invalid status transition.\n\n## Localisation is more than translation\nUse Intl formatting for dates, numbers and currencies rather than concatenating a symbol onto an arbitrary number. Decide where time zones matter; a due date and an exact event timestamp have different meanings. Text length, plural forms and right-to-left layout can affect component design.\n\nKeep visual variants focused: size, emphasis, state and density should have documented meanings. A shared component should not become a collection of unrelated exceptions to avoid creating a second component.","exercise":"Design an invoice dashboard with a labelled revenue trend, an exact data table and an INR amount formatted with Intl. Specify a keyboard alternative for moving a task between columns.","interviews":[{"q":"How would you make a chart usable without colour perception?","a":"Use labels, distinguishable series styles and an exact data alternative. Colour can reinforce meaning, but it should not be the only channel carrying that meaning."},{"q":"What is needed for accessible drag-and-drop?","a":"A keyboard/non-drag way to perform the same move, meaningful announcements and predictable focus. The server must still validate the action and permissions."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the best way to format a currency amount?","options":["Intl.NumberFormat with an explicit locale/currency","Always prepend a dollar sign","Round every number to an integer","Store the formatted text as the only numeric value"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Locale-aware formatting separates numeric data from its presentation."},"skills":[65,66,69],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"javascript","code":"const money = new Intl.NumberFormat(\"en-IN\", {style:\"currency\", currency:\"INR\"});\nconsole.log(money.format(123456.5));","expected":"An INR amount using Indian digit grouping; the exact currency glyph/spacing can vary by locale data.","stdin":""}},{"id":"react-quality","title":"Component tests, end-to-end tests and performance","body":"## Test what the user can do\nTesting Library encourages querying by accessible role/label and asserting observable behaviour. Test a form submission, validation error or keyboard action rather than a private state variable. A test that passes while the user cannot perform the action is not useful evidence.\n\nPlaywright checks a real browser journey across routing, persistence and rendering. Choose a few critical journeys: create a task, recover from a failed save, and resume a saved exam. Use deterministic test data and controlled network responses. Prefer locator conditions over arbitrary fixed sleeps.\n\n## Performance starts with measurement\nInspect Core Web Vitals and the relevant interaction. Use bundle analysis to identify large optional dependencies, code splitting to defer them and appropriate image sizes to reduce transfer/decoding cost. Avoid rerender optimisation until you know which component or calculation is expensive.\n\nMemoisation can help, but incorrect dependencies or unstable identities can remove the benefit. Rendering a very large list may need pagination or virtualisation rather than one useMemo. Record a baseline and retest the same path after changing it.\n\nRun lint, types, component tests and selected browser tests in CI. A green suite does not prove complete correctness, so keep the tests focused on meaningful contracts and add a regression case when a real bug is found. Include loading, empty, failed and permission-denied states.","exercise":"Write acceptance criteria for three notes-app journeys and identify which checks belong in component tests versus browser tests. Profile one slow interaction and propose a measurable fix.","interviews":[{"q":"Why query a button by role/name in a UI test?","a":"It follows the interface a user or assistive technology can identify. It also exposes missing accessible names and is usually less coupled to decorative DOM details than a long CSS selector."},{"q":"Why can useMemo fail to solve a slow page?","a":"The bottleneck may be a large DOM, a network request or another component. Memoisation only avoids selected recalculations when dependencies remain stable; measure the actual expensive path first."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which browser assertion best describes user behaviour?","options":["A saved draft survives reload","An internal helper has a particular private name","A CSS class contains exactly seven characters","A timeout of three seconds elapsed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Persistence across reload is a real observable contract."},"skills":[53,67,68],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"next","title":"Next.js","group":"Frontend architecture","summary":"Choose where code runs, how data is cached and how each route fails or loads.","duration":"2-3 weeks after React","project":"Build a small product catalog with server-rendered product pages, a client cart and explicit cache/revalidation behaviour.","sources":[{"title":"Next.js App Router","url":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started"},{"title":"React Learn","url":"https://react.dev/learn"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"next-routing","title":"App Router, layouts and route states","body":"## Routes are an architecture\nThe App Router organises route segments and their UI boundaries. A page represents a route's content, a layout provides shared structure, and nested segments express hierarchy. Route groups can organise files without becoming a visible URL segment. Dynamic segments represent data-dependent paths.\n\nDefine loading, not-found and error states at useful boundaries. A product page should distinguish a missing product from a provider outage; an error page should offer a meaningful recovery action. A shared layout should not depend on a fragile assumption that every child route loads successfully.\n\n## Server and client components\nServer Components can access server-side resources and keep server-only dependencies out of the client bundle. Client Components enable browser interaction, state and relevant hooks. A client boundary does not mean every byte renders only in the browser; understand the framework's rendering/hydration model instead of equating the label with a complete deployment strategy.\n\nValues crossing the server/client boundary must satisfy supported serialisation rules. Never pass private keys or unrestricted database objects into client props. Keep the interactive boundary as small as practical so a static product description does not require a large client bundle.\n\nFramework conventions evolve. The exact file/API names for request interception and runtime behaviour should be checked against the installed major version; the invariant is to make routing, trust boundaries and fallback UI explicit.","exercise":"Sketch a catalog route tree with a shared layout, product detail page, loading state, not-found state and a small interactive Add to cart component. Mark which code needs browser APIs.","interviews":[{"q":"Why keep a client boundary small?","a":"Only the parts that need browser interaction should bring their client-side dependencies and state. Keeping static/data-access work server-side can reduce shipped JavaScript and avoid exposing server-only details."},{"q":"What should not cross into client props?","a":"Secrets, privileged credentials and unrestricted internal records. Select a public, serialisable view model and enforce access before constructing it."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which usually requires a client component boundary?","options":["A button using local React state","Reading a private database password into a server-only function","A static heading with no interaction","A server-side access check"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Interactive state and browser-specific behaviour belong on the client side of an appropriate boundary."},"skills":[54,55,60],"minutes":15},{"id":"next-rendering","title":"SSR, SSG, ISR and data caching","body":"## Choose by freshness and audience\nStatic generation prepares content ahead of requests. Server rendering produces content for a request. Incremental regeneration/revalidation can refresh cached generated content. Client rendering fetches/updates data in the browser. One application can use several strategies for different routes.\n\nA public product description can benefit from pre-rendering and controlled revalidation; a private account dashboard needs request-appropriate access and freshness. Do not cache tenant-specific content under a public shared key. Avoid assuming that a framework version's default fetch caching matches another version's defaults.\n\n## Design invalidation before mutation\nName what is cached, where it is cached, how long it is valid and what event invalidates it. A product edit might invalidate a product tag and a category listing. The browser, framework server, CDN and database cache can each have separate lifecycles.\n\n## Prevent stale and duplicate work\nFetch at the layer that owns the data contract. Avoid request waterfalls when independent requests can run together. Keep error/loading handling attached to the route or component that needs it. Measure which cache actually served a response before guessing why stale data appeared.\n\nUse a before/after test: publish a product change, inspect the detail page and listing through the actual deployed cache path, and verify the documented freshness policy. A local development refresh is not proof of production invalidation correctness.","exercise":"Choose rendering and caching policies for a public product page, search results and a private order history. For each, name its invalidation event and the data that must never be publicly cached.","interviews":[{"q":"How is ISR different from rebuilding every page on every request?","a":"It allows generated/cached content to be refreshed according to a revalidation policy. The exact behaviour depends on the framework version and cache configuration, so define and test the intended freshness contract."},{"q":"What is the main danger of caching a private dashboard by URL alone?","a":"Different users or tenants may share that URL but have different permitted data. An insufficient cache key or an inappropriate shared cache can expose one user's information to another."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must a cache policy specify?","options":["Key, scope, freshness and invalidation","Only the colour of a loading spinner","Only the repository owner","A claim that all data is permanent"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A useful policy identifies what is cached, for whom and how it becomes fresh again."},"skills":[56,57],"minutes":15},{"id":"next-mutations","title":"Server actions, route handlers and streaming","body":"## Every mutation is a trust boundary\nA server action or route handler executes on the server, but client-provided arguments remain untrusted. Authenticate the caller, authorise the exact object, validate input and enforce business rules. Hiding a button in the interface does not enforce those rules.\n\nUse a route handler/API when non-browser clients, webhooks or a stable HTTP contract need it. A server action can simplify a tightly coupled UI mutation. Both need a consistent error strategy, duplicate-request handling and a post-success cache update/invalidation policy.\n\n## Stream useful parts early\nStreaming lets a response deliver ready content before slower regions finish. Suspense boundaries can isolate delayed regions in supported rendering flows. Design a meaningful skeleton or partial state; do not show a success message before the operation is committed.\n\nFor AI token streaming, account for connection closure, buffering proxies and cancellation. The browser should distinguish a partial answer from a completed answer and preserve the useful partial text if the connection fails.\n\nKeep long-running jobs outside a short request when the runtime's limits make completion unreliable. Return a job identifier, persist status and let the interface observe progress. Choose runtime capabilities deliberately; a restricted edge environment may not support every Node package or database driver.","exercise":"Design a product edit action that checks tenant ownership and validates price. Specify cache invalidation, duplicate submission behaviour and the UI shown if a later report-generation job fails.","interviews":[{"q":"Are server-action arguments trusted because the function runs on the server?","a":"No. They come from a caller and must be validated and authorised like any other request. Server execution location does not prove caller permission."},{"q":"When is a separate HTTP route useful?","a":"When webhooks, mobile apps or other clients need a stable endpoint, or when an explicit HTTP resource contract makes the boundary clearer than a UI-specific action."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should happen before updating an object?","options":["Authenticate, authorise and validate on the server","Only hide the Edit button","Trust a role sent in a hidden field","Mark the UI successful before receiving any result"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Server-side enforcement is required regardless of the frontend workflow."},"skills":[58,59],"minutes":15},{"id":"next-url-state","title":"URL state, navigation and shareable filters","body":"## Some state belongs in the address\nSearch terms, sort order, page/cursor and selected categories often belong in URL parameters so a view can be refreshed, bookmarked and shared. A temporary open tooltip normally does not. Private drafts and secrets should not be put into the URL because URLs appear in history, logs and shared links.\n\n## Back and forward must mean something\nPush a history entry for a meaningful navigation change; replace one for a correction that should not add another step. Preserve enough state to render the route from its URL rather than depending on a previous page's in-memory object. Validate parameters and provide defaults for unknown values.\n\nWhen a filter changes, reset or reconcile pagination so a cursor from an unrelated result set is not reused. For search-as-you-type, debounce network work and cancel stale requests. Debouncing a remote request does not require delaying a local draft save.\n\n## Preserve user work\nBefore leaving an editor, save its current draft and show a clear restore path. For an assessment, save answers and keep the original deadline; navigating away should not secretly grant more time. A submitted section should remain locked when returning through history.\n\nTest direct deep links, reload, browser Back, browser Forward and opening a new tab. A route that works only after a sequence of clicks is an incomplete navigation contract.","exercise":"Design a catalog URL with category, sort and search parameters. Define when to push versus replace history, then test a direct link and Back/Forward after changing filters.","interviews":[{"q":"Why keep filters in the URL?","a":"It makes the view reproducible through refresh, sharing and browser history. The renderer can reconstruct the relevant query instead of relying on a previous screen's memory."},{"q":"What must stay out of query parameters?","a":"Private credentials and sensitive drafts. URLs can be copied, logged and stored in history, so they are not an appropriate secret store."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which state is usually suitable for a shareable URL?","options":["A public category filter","A password reset secret copied into unrelated links","An unsaved private invoice note","A private server API key"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A non-sensitive filter helps reconstruct a view without leaking private data."},"skills":[63],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"data-science","title":"Data Science Basics","group":"Python applied skills","summary":"Understand array shapes, clean tables and evaluate data before adding models.","duration":"2-3 weeks after Python","project":"Clean a small sales CSV, produce a grouped summary and keep the transformation in a tested script.","sources":[{"title":"NumPy fundamentals","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.html"},{"title":"Pandas getting started","url":"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/index.html"},{"title":"scikit-learn user guide","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"data-numpy","title":"NumPy arrays, shapes and vectorised operations","body":"## Arrays describe shape and type\nA NumPy ndarray stores a regular collection with a shape and dtype. A vector has one dimension, a table commonly has two and a batch can add more. The number of axes is not the same as the number of elements. Shape mistakes are common in ML and embedding code.\n\nVectorised operations express work over arrays and can use efficient native implementations. They do not make an algorithm with an unnecessarily huge intermediate array memory-free. Inspect both time and allocation size.\n\n## Broadcasting\nBroadcasting aligns dimensions from the trailing side; compatible dimensions are equal or one of them is one. A per-column mean can therefore be subtracted from a table without writing a Python loop. Confirm which axis represents rows, features or batches before reducing with mean/sum.\n\nSlicing can return a view sharing storage with the original. A mutation through a view may change the source; make an explicit copy when independent ownership is required. Dtype conversion also matters: integer arithmetic, floating precision and missing-value representation have different rules.\n\nFor embeddings, a common matrix is number-of-documents by embedding-dimensions. Validate dimensions, normalisation and finite numeric values before similarity calculations. The runnable example below demonstrates the arithmetic with standard Python because NumPy is not bundled into this browser's core Python runtime.","exercise":"Represent three documents with two features each. Compute per-column means, subtract them and explain the expected array shapes. Then describe when a NumPy slice needs copy().","interviews":[{"q":"Why inspect array shape before a model call?","a":"It establishes which axis represents batch/items and which represents features. Incorrect shapes can fail immediately or, worse, broadcast into a plausible but meaningless computation."},{"q":"Does vectorisation always reduce memory?","a":"No. It can speed computation while creating large temporary arrays. Estimate intermediate shapes and use chunking or a different algorithm when memory use becomes excessive."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does shape (100, 8) commonly represent for embeddings?","options":["100 items with 8 dimensions each","8 items with 100 dimensions by definition","A scalar value","A guarantee of 100% accuracy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The meaning is a contract, but rows-as-items and columns-as-dimensions is a common layout."},"skills":[21],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"rows = [[2, 4], [4, 8], [6, 12]]\nmeans = [sum(row[i] for row in rows)/len(rows) for i in range(2)]\ncentered = [[value-means[i] for i,value in enumerate(row)] for row in rows]\nprint(means)\nprint(centered)","expected":"[4.0, 8.0]\n[[-2.0, -4.0], [0.0, 0.0], [2.0, 4.0]]","stdin":""}},{"id":"data-pandas","title":"DataFrames, cleaning, grouping and joins","body":"## A table has a schema, not just rows\nA DataFrame associates labelled columns and rows with typed values. Begin by inspecting column names, dtypes, missing values and row counts. Preserve a raw input copy or reproducible source so cleaning does not destroy the evidence needed to diagnose a mistake.\n\n## A deliberate cleaning pipeline\nParse dates and numeric values explicitly, standardise category labels and decide how to handle missing values. Dropping every incomplete row may bias the dataset. Distinguish an unknown value from a real zero, and record the policy used.\n\nFiltering selects rows; groupby splits rows by key and computes aggregates. A join/merge combines tables through keys and has the same cardinality risks as SQL joins. If one customer key matches several rows on both sides, the output can multiply. Check expected uniqueness and before/after row counts.\n\nSeparate data validation from presentation. Keep monetary values and timestamps in suitable internal forms, then format for the report. Test transformation functions on tiny explicit examples including duplicates, missing fields and unexpected categories.\n\nPandas is a third-party project dependency, not included in this app's core browser engine. The example uses standard-library records to show the grouping logic; apply the same contract in a DataFrame inside your local project environment.","exercise":"Design a sales-cleaning pipeline that validates order IDs, parses amounts and groups revenue by category. Specify how refunds, missing categories and duplicate order rows should affect the report.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a merge unexpectedly increase row count?","a":"The join key may not be unique on one or both sides. A many-to-many match produces combinations, so inspect cardinality and validate the intended key relationship."},{"q":"Should missing numeric values always become zero?","a":"No. Missing can mean unknown, unrecorded or inapplicable. Replacing it with zero changes the meaning and can bias totals or averages; choose a documented policy."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should be checked before joining customer and order tables?","options":["Key meaning, uniqueness and expected cardinality","Only the font of the CSV","Only the current theme","Whether every missing value can be guessed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The join contract determines whether matches and row multiplication are correct."},"skills":[22],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"sales = [{\"category\":\"books\",\"amount\":100}, {\"category\":\"tools\",\"amount\":70}, {\"category\":\"books\",\"amount\":50}]\ntotals = {}\nfor row in sales:\n    totals[row[\"category\"]] = totals.get(row[\"category\"], 0) + row[\"amount\"]\nprint(totals)","expected":"{'books': 150, 'tools': 70}","stdin":""}},{"id":"data-notebooks","title":"Notebooks, reproducibility and useful plots","body":"## Explore without hiding execution order\nA notebook combines code, results and explanation, which is useful for exploration. Its state can become misleading if cells are run out of order or depend on values created in a deleted cell. Restart the kernel and run all cells to verify that the notebook is reproducible from its visible contents.\n\nMove reusable transformations into tested modules as the work stabilises. Keep a notebook as a readable investigation or report rather than the only place where production logic exists. Pin important dependencies and record input data versions, random seeds and assumptions.\n\n## Choose a plot by the question\nUse a bar chart for category comparison, a line for an ordered trend, a scatter plot for a relationship and a histogram for a distribution. Label units and population/time range. A truncated axis or an inappropriate aggregation can exaggerate an effect even when every plotted value is real.\n\nMatplotlib provides a flexible plotting foundation; higher-level tools can simplify common charts. Include exact values or an accessible table when the chart alone is insufficient. Avoid using a beautiful chart to conceal missing data, a tiny sample or an unsuitable comparison.\n\nFor a job portfolio, show the question, cleaning decisions, results and limitations. A clear, reproducible small analysis is more defensible in an interview than a large notebook whose outputs cannot be regenerated.","exercise":"Plan a notebook that analyses a week of study activity. Include the input schema, a restart-and-run-all check, one useful plot and two limitations of the data.","interviews":[{"q":"What is hidden notebook state?","a":"A result depends on the kernel's past execution rather than only the visible top-to-bottom code. Restarting and running all cells exposes missing steps and order dependencies."},{"q":"Why move stable logic into modules?","a":"Modules make reusable functions easier to test, import and run outside the notebook. The notebook can then explain the analysis without becoming an unstructured production codebase."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a good reproducibility check?","options":["Restart the kernel and run all cells in order","Keep only screenshots of outputs","Delete the input data description","Rely on variables from yesterday's session"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A clean run verifies that visible code and recorded inputs can recreate the results."},"skills":[26],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"node","title":"Node.js & APIs","group":"Backend engineering","summary":"Design resource APIs, secure their boundaries and make retries and failures predictable.","duration":"4-6 weeks","project":"Build a tenant-scoped project API with login, validated requests, payment events and a background report job.","sources":[{"title":"Node.js Learn","url":"https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs"},{"title":"Express routing","url":"https://expressjs.com/en/guide/routing/"},{"title":"OWASP Cheat Sheet Series","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/"},{"title":"Razorpay webhook validation","url":"https://razorpay.com/docs/webhooks/validate-test/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"api-resources","title":"Servers, REST resources and pagination","body":"## A server owns a request boundary\nNode runs JavaScript outside the browser. Express layers routing and middleware over HTTP handling. A route matches a method and path; route parameters identify resources, query parameters refine a request and a request body carries structured input. Validate all three.\n\n## Design resources instead of random action names\nFor tasks, use a collection such as `/projects/:projectId/tasks` and a specific resource such as `/tasks/:taskId`. GET reads, POST commonly creates or requests a non-idempotent operation, PUT expresses replacement semantics, PATCH a partial update and DELETE removal. Choose status codes and error shapes consistently. Method semantics do not replace permission checks.\n\nFiltering and sorting must have bounded, documented choices. Limit page size and include a deterministic tie-breaker. Offset pagination is simple but can become expensive and inconsistent as rows move. Cursor pagination continues after a stable ordering key, often a tuple of timestamp and unique ID; encode the required sort/filter context so a cursor is not reused for a different query.\n\n## Make the contract reviewable\nOpenAPI describes paths, schemas and responses and can generate clients. Document nullable fields, limits, auth requirements and error responses. Define a versioning policy for breaking changes. A published type is only one part of the contract; test actual serialisation and deployed compatibility.\n\nDo not return an unbounded database collection. Even a small dataset grows, and a public endpoint must control memory, latency and response size.","exercise":"Design list/create/read/update/delete endpoints for project tasks. Specify a maximum page size, sort order, cursor shape and three error responses.","interviews":[{"q":"When is cursor pagination preferable to offset?","a":"It is useful for large or changing ordered datasets, where continuing after a stable key avoids scanning a large offset and reduces some shifting-page anomalies. It still needs a deterministic ordering and clear consistency expectations."},{"q":"Does a REST-looking URL make an API secure?","a":"No. Resource naming improves the contract, but the server must still authenticate, authorise the exact object, validate input and enforce quotas/business rules."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should a list endpoint always define?","options":["Bounded page size and deterministic order","An unlimited response by default","A private database password in the URL","A random order with no continuation contract"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bounds protect resources and a stable order makes pagination meaningful."},"skills":[70,73,74],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"node","code":"app.get(\"/tasks\", requireUser, async (req, res, next) => {\n  try {\n    const limit = Math.min(100, Math.max(1, Number(req.query.limit) || 20));\n    const rows = await taskService.list({tenantId:req.user.tenantId, limit});\n    res.json({items:rows});\n  } catch (error) { next(error); }\n});","expected":"Project snippet: the authenticated tenant is server-derived and the result size is bounded.","stdin":""}},{"id":"api-middleware","title":"Middleware, validation and stable errors","body":"## Middleware order is behaviour\nAn Express application passes a request through ordered middleware and matching routes. Typical layers attach a request ID, apply relevant body parsing and security limits, authenticate, validate, authorise and execute a handler. An error handler translates unexpected faults into a stable public response while logging safe diagnostic context.\n\nBody parsing has limits and content-type expectations. A webhook requiring a raw-body signature must preserve the exact bytes at the correct route; parsing and reserialising JSON first can invalidate the signature. Middleware that is useful for one route is not automatically correct for every route.\n\n## Give clients one error contract\nUse a consistent structure with a machine-readable type/code, human-readable summary and suitable status. RFC 9457 problem details is one standard format. Field errors can identify invalid input, while a request ID links a public response to protected logs. Never return stack traces, database credentials or raw secret-bearing provider responses to users.\n\n## Harden without confusing controls\nValidate supported fields, constrain request sizes and rate-limit sensitive operations. Helmet-style headers, a deliberate CORS policy and route-level quotas address different risks. CORS controls browser response access; it does not authenticate every possible HTTP caller.\n\nAsync failures must reach the application's error boundary according to the installed Express version and handler style. Test a rejected database call and malformed JSON, not only the happy route.","exercise":"Specify middleware order for a login endpoint and a payment webhook. Define one public error JSON shape and explain which diagnostic fields stay only in server logs.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does middleware order matter?","a":"Each layer observes or changes the request before later layers run. Parsing, authentication or signature verification in the wrong order can change semantics or remove the bytes required for a security check."},{"q":"Why not return a stack trace in an API error?","a":"It can reveal internal paths, query details and implementation information. Return a stable public error and a request identifier while keeping sensitive diagnostic context in protected logs."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must a raw-body webhook verifier use?","options":["The original request bytes","Pretty-printed JSON created later","The frontend success message","Only the request path"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Signatures depend on the exact bytes defined by the provider contract."},"skills":[71,75,76,78],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-idempotency","title":"Idempotency and retry-safe operations","body":"## Retries are normal\nA network timeout does not prove that an operation failed. The server may have committed the change while the response was lost. Retrying a payment or order creation blindly can therefore duplicate a side effect.\n\nAn idempotent operation has the same intended effect when repeated with the same relevant input. An idempotency key lets a server recognise a retried request. Scope the key to the authenticated actor/tenant and operation, store the request fingerprint and result, and define a retention policy. Reusing a key with different input should not silently create an unrelated operation.\n\n## Make the key effective under concurrency\n1. Enforce uniqueness at the database boundary.\n2. Acquire/insert the key record and perform the business change in a suitable transaction or coordinated workflow.\n3. Store the committed result so a retry can receive the same outcome.\n4. Handle in-progress, failed and expired records explicitly.\n\nA frontend disabled button reduces accidental clicks but does not solve retried HTTP requests, multiple tabs or duplicated provider events. Likewise, a process-local Set is not enough when several server instances handle the same key.\n\nFor external services, use their idempotency support where available and still track your own business operation. Exactly-once delivery is a strong claim; practical systems often combine at-least-once delivery with idempotent processing and reconciliation.","exercise":"Design retry-safe invoice payment creation. Include key scope, request fingerprint, database uniqueness and behaviour when two identical requests arrive concurrently.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a timeout be ambiguous?","a":"The request may have completed on the server even though the client did not receive its response. The client must not infer that no side effect occurred merely from a transport failure."},{"q":"Why is an in-memory idempotency map insufficient?","a":"It disappears on restart and is not shared across server instances. A durable uniqueness/transaction mechanism is needed to coordinate the operation across concurrency and failures."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should happen when one idempotency key is reused with different input?","options":["Reject or explicitly handle the contract violation","Silently charge again","Ignore all authentication","Use the newest body without any record"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The key identifies one operation; a different request fingerprint must not be treated as the same successful retry."},"skills":[72],"minutes":15},{"id":"node-runtime","title":"Event-loop work, streams and memory","body":"## Fast I/O does not make CPU work free\nNode can coordinate many I/O operations through its event-loop architecture. A large synchronous JSON transformation, compression step or loop can still block request progress. An async function that performs a CPU-heavy loop without yielding remains blocking work.\n\nUse worker threads for appropriate CPU-heavy JavaScript tasks and job workers for operations that can complete outside the request. Bound concurrency instead of starting every task immediately. Keep cancellation, timeouts and shutdown part of the worker contract.\n\n## Stream with backpressure\nA stream processes data in chunks. A producer must respect the consumer's ability to accept more data; otherwise buffering grows until memory is exhausted. Use supported stream piping/pipeline patterns and propagate errors rather than manually appending every chunk into one huge string.\n\n## Diagnose memory behaviour\nDistinguish temporary allocations, retained objects and operating-system/container limits. A cache without eviction, an ever-growing event listener collection or a request reference stored globally can retain data. Heap snapshots and runtime metrics help locate the retaining path.\n\nSet appropriate request/body limits and keep large uploads off the main process when object storage is more suitable. A larger heap can delay a failure but does not repair an unbounded data structure. Measure latency percentiles, active work and memory under a realistic load, including slow consumers.","exercise":"Plan a CSV export for a large table without loading all rows into one array. Specify pagination/cursor use, streaming, backpressure, cancellation and an error path.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a Node API stall despite no slow database query?","a":"Synchronous CPU work or heavy garbage collection can occupy the event loop. Profile the process and inspect long tasks instead of assuming every delay is a network problem."},{"q":"What is backpressure?","a":"A mechanism that makes a producer respect downstream capacity. Without it, a fast source can buffer an unbounded amount while a slow consumer reads, exhausting memory."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a likely memory leak?","options":["An unbounded map retaining completed requests","A bounded temporary array that becomes unreachable","A correctly removed event listener","A closed stream with no remaining references"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Retaining completed request objects indefinitely prevents their memory from being reclaimed."},"skills":[79],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-tests","title":"API tests, fakes, Postman and database evidence","body":"## Test at the boundary that owns the risk\nA unit test can exercise a service with a fake mail or model provider. An HTTP integration test verifies routing, parsing, authentication and serialised responses. A database integration test checks actual constraints, transactions and queries. An end-to-end test checks a small critical user journey across those parts.\n\nVitest and Jest are test runners with different project ecosystems/configurations. Choose the one used by the actual repository rather than claiming both are installed. A Postman collection can document and exercise requests, but a manual successful request is not a complete automated regression suite.\n\n## Use explicit adversarial cases\nTest missing authentication, another tenant's object ID, invalid body fields, duplicate idempotency keys, a provider timeout and a database failure. Assert both the public outcome and the relevant persisted invariant. A response of 200 is not enough if the database contains two payments.\n\nAn in-memory substitute is useful for fast tests, but it may differ from PostgreSQL/MongoDB in isolation, SQL syntax, constraints or indexes. Keep selected tests against the target engine for the contracts that depend on it. Reset fixtures without touching a real user's database.\n\nMake time, randomness and external responses controlled. Run the meaningful suite in CI and preserve a small regression case for a bug after fixing it. Do not mirror every internal helper in a test that would pass with the same mistake on both sides.","exercise":"Write five API acceptance cases for task creation, including tenant isolation and a failed notification. Identify which need a real database and which can use a fake provider.","interviews":[{"q":"Why are in-memory database tests not sufficient for every guarantee?","a":"A substitute can have different transaction, constraint and query semantics from the production engine. Use the target database to test engine-dependent invariants and keep fakes for appropriately isolated behaviour."},{"q":"What should a duplicate-payment test assert?","a":"It should inspect the persisted business effect as well as the response: one logical payment/order transition, a durable idempotency record and a consistent retry result."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which test provides the best tenant-isolation evidence?","options":["A verified tenant A user is denied tenant B's object through the actual API/data path","A hidden button is absent in one screenshot","A private helper has a particular name","A request returns any JSON"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The protected object boundary must be tested with an authenticated cross-tenant attempt."},"skills":[77],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-auth","title":"Authentication, sessions, OAuth and tenant RBAC","body":"## Identity and permission are separate\nAuthentication establishes who the caller is. Authorisation establishes whether that caller may perform this operation on this object. A valid login token does not prove access to every project ID. Derive tenant identity from verified server-side context and include it in the data-access condition.\n\nPasswords need an appropriate password-hashing scheme with a salt and configurable work factor; bcrypt is one established option with input/parameter considerations. Do not use a fast general hash as a password database design. Support rate limits and a safe reset flow.\n\n## Sessions and tokens\nA signed JWT is not automatically encrypted. Verify its signature, allowed algorithm, issuer/audience and expiry according to your system's contract. Plan refresh, revocation/logout and key rotation. Browser storage choices have trade-offs: HttpOnly cookies reduce JavaScript token exposure but require CSRF-aware session design; localStorage is readable by injected script.\n\nOAuth 2.0 concerns delegated authorisation; OpenID Connect adds an identity layer. A browser/mobile authorisation-code flow commonly uses PKCE, along with state/nonce checks where applicable. Do not treat an arbitrary frontend email string as proof that a user signed in through Google.\n\n## Object-level access\nCheck role permissions and tenant/object ownership at the service/query layer. A task update should select by both task ID and the caller's permitted tenant, then enforce allowed status changes. Test one tenant trying another tenant's IDs, including nested resources, downloads and search endpoints.","exercise":"Design an admin/member/client permission matrix for a project portal. Write the access condition for reading one invoice and specify how logout affects an already issued session/token.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is hiding an admin button insufficient?","a":"A user can call the endpoint directly. The server must verify the authenticated identity, role and relationship to the requested object on every protected operation."},{"q":"Are JWT payloads secret?","a":"Ordinary signed JWT payloads are encoded, not encrypted. Do not place secrets in them; verify signatures and claims, and use a deliberate session, expiry and revocation strategy."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which tenant ID should a protected query trust?","options":["One derived from verified server-side identity","Any tenant ID from the request body","A CSS class in the browser","The first tenant in the database"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Caller-controlled identifiers cannot establish access rights."},"skills":[86,87,88],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-payments","title":"Payment webhooks, signatures and replay protection","body":"## The browser is not the payment authority\nA checkout success callback improves the interface but is not proof that money settled. The backend creates the provider order using a server-authoritative amount and links it to your own pending business operation. Never accept a final price or paid flag just because the client supplied it.\n\n## Verify the event boundary\nRead the provider's current signature contract. For a typical HMAC webhook, compute the digest from the exact raw request body and the configured webhook secret, then compare using a timing-safe method. Check identifiers and expected business context, and keep the secret out of source code and ordinary logs.\n\n## Process a duplicate safely\nProviders retry deliveries. Store a unique event/delivery identifier and make the business transition idempotent. Verify the allowed state transition, update payment/order state transactionally and record a useful audit event. A valid signature proves origin/integrity under the shared-secret model, not that processing this same event twice is safe.\n\nOutgoing webhooks need similar discipline: stable event IDs, a signed body, timestamps where appropriate, delivery logs, bounded retries and a way to inspect/retry failed deliveries. Receivers should understand the retry policy.\n\nKeep invoices and payment records reconciliable. Partial refunds, cancelled orders and delayed events are separate states, not one boolean that every code path can overwrite.","exercise":"Draw a pending-to-paid order flow using a verified webhook. Include signature failure, duplicate event, out-of-order event and a reconciliation job for a missing callback.","interviews":[{"q":"Why verify a webhook signature on the server?","a":"The endpoint is publicly reachable. Signature verification checks the event bytes against the configured provider secret so an arbitrary caller cannot simply claim a payment succeeded."},{"q":"Why is signature verification not enough?","a":"A genuine event can be duplicated, delayed or irrelevant to the current business state. Idempotency, expected identifiers, allowed transitions and reconciliation are still required."}],"quiz":{"question":"Who should decide the final checkout amount?","options":["The backend using authoritative product/pricing data","The client-provided total alone","A hidden HTML field alone","A success toast"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The client is an untrusted input source; pricing must be recomputed/verified on the server."},"skills":[89,92],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-integrations","title":"Email, uploads, PDF invoices and provider fallback","body":"## An integration is a failure boundary\nEmail, media storage, SMS, AI and PDF services can time out, reject input or become unavailable. Define the success contract and decide which failures block the user action. A saved task should not disappear because its notification email failed.\n\nNodemailer works with configured SMTP delivery; services such as SendGrid/Resend expose their own APIs. A password-reset link needs a short-lived, single-use server-validated token and a response that avoids unnecessary account disclosure. Delivery acceptance is not the same as the recipient reading the email.\n\n## Uploads and generated documents\nUse file size/type limits and server-authorised object names. Cloudinary or S3-compatible storage can hold media; a signed upload/download URL should have a scope and expiry. Do not trust a supplied filename or allow a user to select another tenant's object path.\n\nPDFKit can create a PDF programmatically; browser-based rendering tools can turn controlled HTML into a document. Escape untrusted fields and restrict external resource access. An invoice should snapshot the legally/business-relevant values at issuance rather than silently changing when a current product title or price changes.\n\n## A graceful fallback\nIf an AI summary provider fails, return a clearly labelled deterministic summary from validated task counts. Preserve the non-AI workflow. Record the provider error safely and retry according to a bounded policy rather than locking the entire portal behind one optional integration.","exercise":"Design a weekly project report that saves independently of email delivery. Specify an invoice snapshot, upload ownership check and a deterministic fallback when the text-generation provider is down.","interviews":[{"q":"Why store an invoice/order snapshot?","a":"The issued document must preserve the relevant price, tax, address and item details from that transaction. Reading only current product data later can rewrite the apparent historical transaction."},{"q":"What is a useful graceful fallback?","a":"A feature remains usable with a clear deterministic result when an optional provider fails. For example, a report can show verified task counts instead of inventing an AI narrative or returning fake success."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should happen if a notification email fails after a task is saved?","options":["Retain the task and retry/report notification separately","Delete the task silently","Claim the email was read","Retry forever inside the original request"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The core transaction and optional delivery have different lifecycles and failure policies."},"skills":[90,91],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-jobs","title":"Queues, schedules and state machines","body":"## Long work needs a durable lifecycle\nAn HTTP request can enqueue a report job and return an identifier. A worker processes it, stores a result and updates status. BullMQ and Celery are examples of queue tools in different ecosystems. Queue choice does not eliminate duplicate delivery or partial failures.\n\nDefine queued, running, succeeded, failed and cancelled states with allowed transitions. Include retry count, last error category and a visibility/lease strategy appropriate to the queue. A dead-letter queue or failed-job store makes persistent failures inspectable rather than silently dropping them.\n\n## Make recurring work safe\nA scheduler such as cron identifies when work should begin; it does not prove that one instance ran exactly once. Several application replicas may trigger the same schedule, a machine can be down at the expected time and a retry can overlap. Use a unique logical period/job key, a durable ownership mechanism and a catch-up policy.\n\n## Model business transitions explicitly\nFor an order, allowed transitions might include pending to paid and paid to shipped, with separate cancellation/refund paths. Centralise the rule rather than letting every route assign an arbitrary status string. Apply transitions with the relevant data change transactionally where possible.\n\nMonitor queue depth, age and failure rate. A worker that never crashes but falls hours behind is still failing the product's timeliness contract. Bound provider retries and apply backoff/jitter instead of amplifying an outage.","exercise":"Define the report job state machine and retry policy. Then prevent two weekly schedulers from producing two reports for the same tenant and calendar week.","interviews":[{"q":"Why do workers need idempotency?","a":"A job can be delivered again after a crash, lost acknowledgement or retry. The handler must recognise the business operation and avoid duplicating durable side effects."},{"q":"Why use an explicit state machine?","a":"It makes valid transitions and their preconditions reviewable. It prevents unrelated routes from assigning impossible states such as shipping an unpaid/cancelled order."}],"quiz":{"question":"What best prevents duplicate weekly reports across replicas?","options":["A durable unique tenant-and-period job key","A global variable in one process","Only disabling a frontend button","Assuming cron never repeats"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The logical job identity must be coordinated durably across processes."},"skills":[93,94,97],"minutes":15},{"id":"api-realtime","title":"WebSockets, Socket.io rooms and SSE","body":"## Pick the direction of communication\nWebSockets provide a persistent bidirectional channel. Socket.io adds its own connection/reconnection protocol and features; it is not interchangeable with a raw WebSocket client in every case. Server-Sent Events send a server-to-browser stream over HTTP and are useful for notifications or token output when client messages can use ordinary requests.\n\n## Authenticate the connection and each action\nA socket room is a routing mechanism, not a permission boundary. Derive permitted rooms from the verified identity and enforce tenant membership. Validate incoming event payloads and recheck permissions for sensitive actions. Do not let an arbitrary client join a tenant room by naming its ID.\n\n## Design recovery\nConnections drop. Include event IDs/versioning where needed, handle duplicates, reconcile a snapshot after a gap and specify when the UI is stale. Use heartbeat/idle policies that work through the actual proxy/load balancer. Unbounded per-client queues can consume memory when a client reads slowly.\n\nFor SSE, send correctly framed events and handle reconnect/last-event identifiers according to your retention strategy. A streaming response should not buffer the entire result accidentally at a proxy. Cancellation must propagate when the client stops watching a costly generation.\n\nRealtime is not durability. Persist important changes first and use the stream to notify clients about committed state. A missed notification must not mean the underlying payment or task update vanished.","exercise":"Choose Socket.io or SSE for task collaboration and for one-way AI token output. Define room ownership, duplicate handling and how the UI recovers after a 30-second disconnect.","interviews":[{"q":"Are Socket.io rooms an access-control system?","a":"No. They organise delivery. The server must authenticate the connection, derive allowed room membership and enforce object/tenant permissions on actions."},{"q":"When is SSE a good fit?","a":"When the server mainly pushes a stream to the browser, such as notifications or tokens, while client commands can use regular HTTP. Bidirectional low-latency collaboration may justify a WebSocket-based design."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should happen before broadcasting an important task update?","options":["Persist and authorise the actual change","Only update one client's local array","Trust any room name from the client","Discard the event if no one is connected"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The durable business change must remain correct independently of notification delivery."},"skills":[95,96],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"python-backend","title":"Python Web Backend","group":"Backend engineering","summary":"Choose Flask, Django or FastAPI deliberately, then validate, authorise and persist requests.","duration":"3-5 weeks after Python","project":"Build a task API with tested CRUD, typed input, a database migration and a safe deployment configuration.","sources":[{"title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"},{"title":"FastAPI tutorial","url":"https://fastapi.tiangolo.com/tutorial/"},{"title":"OWASP Cheat Sheet Series","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"py-web-choice","title":"Flask, Django and FastAPI: choosing a framework","body":"## Frameworks organise the HTTP boundary\nFlask is a small framework that lets you choose many surrounding components. Django supplies a broader integrated stack including ORM, migrations, forms/admin and authentication facilities. FastAPI focuses on typed API declarations and validation/OpenAPI integration. Choose according to the product and team rather than a claim that one is universally best.\n\n## The shared request lifecycle\nA server receives a request, a router selects a handler, middleware/dependencies establish context, input is validated, a service performs the operation and the response is serialised. Configuration, logging, error handling and access checks are part of that lifecycle in every framework.\n\nStart with a small task resource. Implement list/create/read/update/delete and explicit error cases. Keep application logic independent enough to test without an actual HTTP server, then add request-level integration tests.\n\n## Project environment\nCreate a virtual environment, record dependencies and use environment-based configuration. A development server is not automatically the complete production deployment design. Configure the appropriate application server, reverse proxy, timeouts, static/media handling and shutdown behaviour.\n\nDo not add authentication by inventing a token format or hashing scheme. Use maintained framework/security components and understand their session/CSRF/permission contracts. Browser examples in this study desk explain the flow; a real Python web project needs its framework packages installed locally.","exercise":"Choose a framework for a small JSON API and for an admin-heavy content application. Give two concrete reasons for each choice and list the components you must still configure.","interviews":[{"q":"Why might Django fit an admin-heavy application?","a":"It provides an integrated ORM, migrations, admin and common web facilities. That can reduce assembly work, while still requiring deliberate configuration, permissions and deployment."},{"q":"Does choosing an async framework make every handler nonblocking?","a":"No. Blocking database drivers, HTTP clients or CPU work can still block an event loop. The handler and its dependencies must use a compatible execution strategy."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is common to all three framework choices?","options":["Server-side validation and authorisation remain necessary","No database design is needed","Every external call is automatically safe","A development server is always enough for production"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Framework conveniences do not remove the underlying trust and data boundaries."},"skills":[],"minutes":15},{"id":"fastapi-routing","title":"FastAPI routes, validation and dependencies","body":"## Declare a clear endpoint\nA FastAPI handler can receive path, query and body values through an explicit declaration. Pydantic models express request/response shapes and constraints. Generated OpenAPI/Swagger documentation makes the declaration inspectable, but clients still need correct examples, error contracts and access requirements.\n\n## Dependency injection\nDepends can provide a database session, authenticated user or validated settings. A dependency has a lifecycle and can be overridden in tests. Keep the dependency graph understandable; hiding every business rule in nested dependencies makes errors hard to trace.\n\n## Async boundaries\nUse async endpoints with compatible nonblocking operations. A synchronous blocking call inside async code can still occupy the event loop. Choose the framework's supported sync execution path or an appropriate worker for blocking work, and close sessions/clients reliably.\n\n## Response contracts\nReturn public fields through an explicit response model. Never rely on a caller to ignore a private password hash or internal tenant flag. Validate ownership before returning a resource, even if its identifier is a valid integer/UUID.\n\nTest a valid request, missing/invalid fields, unauthorised access and a failed dependency. Override a provider or session in tests so the result does not depend on an external network or an uncontrolled production database.","exercise":"Specify a create-task endpoint with a Pydantic body, authenticated-user dependency and response model. Add tests for invalid title, another tenant and a database exception.","interviews":[{"q":"What does dependency injection improve?","a":"It makes collaborators and lifecycles explicit and provides a controlled way to replace them in tests. It should clarify ownership rather than hide all business logic behind magic."},{"q":"Why use a response model?","a":"It documents and restricts the public output shape. This helps prevent accidental exposure of internal fields and makes the serialised API contract testable."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which value should a protected FastAPI dependency supply?","options":["A verified authenticated user context","A trusted role copied directly from the request body","A private key sent back to the browser","An unvalidated global account ID"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The handler needs identity established by server-side verification."},"skills":[80,81,82,83,85],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python-project","code":"from fastapi import FastAPI, Depends\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\napp = FastAPI()\nclass TaskInput(BaseModel):\n    title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=120)\n@app.post(\"/tasks\")\ndef create_task(body: TaskInput, user=Depends(require_user)):\n    return task_service.create(user.tenant_id, body.title)\n# require_user and task_service are project collaborators to implement/test.","expected":"Project sketch: validate the input and derive tenant context from a verified dependency.","stdin":""}},{"id":"py-orm","title":"SQLAlchemy sessions, transactions and Alembic","body":"## An ORM is a data-access tool\nSQLAlchemy maps Python operations and models to database work. It does not remove SQL concepts, transaction design or query-plan costs. A session coordinates unit-of-work/identity behaviour and must have a clear lifetime; sharing one mutable session across unrelated requests is not a safe default.\n\n## Make the transaction visible\nGroup related changes in an explicit transaction. Commit after the business invariant is satisfied and roll back on failure. A session flush can send changes to the database without being the same as a final commit. External calls inside a long database transaction can hold locks and connections unnecessarily.\n\n## Migrations are reviewed changes\nAlembic records schema changes. Inspect generated migrations, test them against representative data and plan backfills. Renaming a field in Python does not automatically prove that a generated database drop/add is the intended safe migration.\n\nAvoid N+1 queries when rendering related objects. Choose suitable eager-loading or explicit queries and measure query count and plans. Authorisation belongs in the data-access conditions as well as the route contract.\n\nFor a breaking schema change, use expand/contract: add compatible fields, deploy code that can handle both forms, backfill safely, switch readers and remove the old form later. A rollback plan includes the data consequences, not only reverting application source.","exercise":"Design a create-project transaction that also creates its initial membership. Explain session lifetime, rollback on the second insert failing and how you would migrate a required field without a long outage.","interviews":[{"q":"How are flush and commit different?","a":"Flush sends pending changes through the database session so constraints and generated values can be observed. Commit completes the transaction; a flushed change can still be rolled back."},{"q":"Why inspect autogenerated migrations?","a":"A tool infers structural differences but not the intended business migration, data preservation or deployment order. Review destructive operations, backfills and compatibility before applying them."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should happen if a required second insert fails?","options":["Roll back the related transaction","Keep half the business operation and claim success","Ignore the exception","Commit the first change without considering the invariant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The transaction should preserve the business invariant across related changes."},"skills":[84],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"databases","title":"SQL & DBMS","group":"Systems foundations","summary":"Query data, design schemas and understand transactions, indexes and operational trade-offs.","duration":"4-6 weeks","project":"Model users, projects, invoices and payments, then test constraints, query plans and a stock update under concurrency.","sources":[{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/"},{"title":"MongoDB data modelling","url":"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/"},{"title":"pgvector project documentation","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"sql-query","title":"SELECT, filtering, joins and aggregates","body":"## A query describes a result\nA relational table has named columns and rows constrained by a schema. SELECT chooses output expressions, FROM/JOIN establish input relations, WHERE filters rows, GROUP BY forms groups, HAVING filters grouped results and ORDER BY controls presentation. Without ORDER BY, do not depend on a particular row order. LIMIT bounds output.\n\n## Joins are relationships\nINNER JOIN returns matching combinations; LEFT JOIN retains unmatched rows from the left with nulls on the right. RIGHT/FULL joins express other preservation choices where supported. A one-to-many join can multiply rows, so COUNT(*) after a join may not mean the number of distinct customers. State which entity you are counting.\n\n## Aggregates and NULL\nCOUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) excludes null values of that expression. SUM/AVG operate on their supported non-null inputs; an empty result has aggregate-specific behaviour. Use COALESCE deliberately rather than silently treating every unknown value as zero. NULL comparisons require IS NULL/IS NOT NULL rather than = NULL.\n\nUse parameterised queries for external values. String concatenation is not a safe query-construction strategy. INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE change data; review WHERE clauses and use transactions when several changes must remain consistent.\n\nThe example runs in this app's bundled SQLite engine. Database-specific syntax and features should be checked against the actual database used in your project.","exercise":"Write queries for customers with no orders, total paid amount by customer and customers whose total exceeds a threshold. Explain the difference between filtering orders before grouping and filtering totals after grouping.","interviews":[{"q":"How do WHERE and HAVING differ?","a":"WHERE filters individual input rows before grouping. HAVING filters groups after aggregation, such as customers whose sum exceeds a threshold."},{"q":"Why can a LEFT JOIN act like an INNER JOIN accidentally?","a":"A WHERE condition requiring a non-null right-side value can discard the unmatched null-extended rows. Put the predicate in the join or account for nulls according to the intended result."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which counts all result rows including rows with NULL fields?","options":["COUNT(*)","COUNT(nullable_column)","SUM(*)","WHERE NULL = NULL"],"answer":0,"explanation":"COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(expression) excludes null values of that expression."},"skills":[98],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE study_sales(customer TEXT, amount INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO study_sales VALUES ('Asha',100),('Ravi',70),('Asha',50);\nSELECT customer, SUM(amount) AS total\nFROM study_sales\nGROUP BY customer\nHAVING SUM(amount) >= 100\nORDER BY total DESC;","expected":"One row: Asha, total 150.","stdin":""}},{"id":"sql-advanced","title":"Subqueries, CTEs, windows and upserts","body":"## Break a query into named steps\nA subquery provides an intermediate relation/value. A common table expression names a query within a statement, often making a multistep transformation easier to review. A CTE is not a universal performance optimisation; planner behaviour and materialisation rules depend on the database/version.\n\n## Window functions preserve rows\nGROUP BY normally collapses rows into aggregates. A window function computes across a related window while retaining each row. Use PARTITION BY to separate groups and ORDER BY to define sequence. The window frame matters: a running total is different from the total of an entire partition.\n\nROW_NUMBER assigns an order-based sequence; RANK leaves gaps after ties; DENSE_RANK does not. Add deterministic tie-breakers when the result needs one stable row. For top-N per category, compute the window rank in a subquery/CTE and filter in an outer query.\n\n## Recursive and write patterns\nA recursive CTE can traverse a hierarchy, but it needs termination and cycle handling appropriate to the data. An upsert combines an insert with conflict handling under a unique constraint; choose which fields may update and preserve business rules.\n\nUse EXPLAIN to inspect a complex query and compare it with explicit expected rows on a tiny dataset. A concise query can still be expensive or wrong when joins multiply rows before the window calculation.","exercise":"Produce a running total of study minutes per person and the top two scores per topic. Explain tie handling and test with equal scores and an empty input table.","interviews":[{"q":"How is a window function different from GROUP BY?","a":"It calculates over related rows without necessarily collapsing them. That lets a result include each original row beside a rank, running total or partition aggregate."},{"q":"Why add a tie-breaker to ROW_NUMBER ordering?","a":"If equal ordering values remain, the database may choose any order among them. A stable unique secondary key makes pagination or selecting one representative row deterministic."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which function is suited to retaining rows with a running total?","options":["SUM(...) OVER (...)","Only GROUP BY with no detail columns","DELETE FROM","DROP TABLE"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A window aggregate can retain detail rows while calculating an ordered running sum."},"skills":[99],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE study_scores(id INTEGER, person TEXT, points INTEGER);\nINSERT INTO study_scores VALUES (1,'Asha',10),(2,'Asha',20),(3,'Ravi',15);\nSELECT id, person, points,\n SUM(points) OVER (PARTITION BY person ORDER BY id ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS running\nFROM study_scores ORDER BY person,id;","expected":"Asha running totals 10 and 30; Ravi running total 15.","stdin":""}},{"id":"db-schema","title":"Keys, ER diagrams and normalisation","body":"## Model the relationships first\nAn entity is a kind of thing you store; a relationship describes how instances connect. Cardinality asks one-to-one, one-to-many or many-to-many; optionality asks whether a link is required. Draw these before creating tables. A membership table often models users belonging to projects or tenants with roles.\n\nA primary key identifies a row. A foreign key enforces a relationship to a referenced key. Unique and check constraints enforce additional rules close to the data. Application checks improve error messages but cannot reliably replace database constraints under concurrent requests.\n\n## Reduce update anomalies\nFirst normal form is commonly taught through atomic values and a consistent relational structure. Second normal form removes partial dependencies on part of a candidate key; third normal form addresses inappropriate transitive dependencies. BCNF requires determinants of nontrivial functional dependencies to be superkeys. State the relevant dependencies before claiming a schema is normalised.\n\nDuplicating a customer's current address across many editable rows can create inconsistencies. A historical order snapshot is a different requirement and can intentionally preserve a transaction-time address. Normalisation is not a rule to delete meaningful historical data.\n\nDenormalise deliberately for a measured access pattern, with a clear source of truth and update/rebuild policy. A schema should encode tenant ownership and prevent invalid cross-tenant relationships where feasible, not only store a tenant label that queries forget to use.","exercise":"Draw an ER model for clients, projects, tasks, invoices and payments. Mark cardinality, required links, primary/foreign keys and one unique business rule. Explain a deliberate invoice snapshot.","interviews":[{"q":"Why enforce uniqueness in the database?","a":"Two requests can both pass a prior application existence check. A database uniqueness constraint arbitrates concurrent attempts at the actual write boundary."},{"q":"When is duplicated data justified?","a":"When it serves a defined requirement such as historical snapshots or a measured read model. Specify its source, consistency/rebuild policy and why normalised access is insufficient."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does a foreign key primarily enforce?","options":["A referenced relationship between rows","A frontend colour rule","A password hashing algorithm","A guarantee that every query is fast"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Foreign keys enforce referential integrity; performance and permissions need separate design."},"skills":[100,101,102,104],"minutes":15},{"id":"db-prisma","title":"Prisma models, migrations and ORM boundaries","body":"## Map an application contract onto a real database\nPrisma expresses models and relations and generates a client for typed data access. It can improve developer feedback, but the database still owns constraints, transactions and query execution. Generated types do not remove runtime validation or authorisation.\n\n## A migration workflow\nChange the schema, generate a migration in development, inspect its SQL and test it against representative data. Apply reviewed migrations through the deployment process rather than making production schema changes manually and hoping source catches up.\n\nFor a required new field, design defaults/backfills and compatible deployment order. Renames and destructive changes need particular attention. A migration that succeeds on an empty database can fail or lock a large production table.\n\n## Query intentionally\nSelect the fields needed for the public response and scope by verified tenant/object permissions. Inspect query counts when loading related records; an ORM can still generate N+1 behaviour or large joins. Use transactions for related changes and a supported safe raw-query mechanism when the ORM does not express the necessary operation clearly.\n\nDo not concatenate untrusted input into a raw SQL string. Keep generated code and client versions compatible with the schema/deployment. Integration tests should verify serialised API behaviour and constraints using the target database, not only a mocked ORM method.","exercise":"Model Project and Task with a relation and tenant ownership. Plan a migration adding an optional deadline, then a later validated requirement, without breaking the previous deployed client.","interviews":[{"q":"Does an ORM prevent every SQL injection?","a":"No. Unsafe raw queries or string-built fragments can still introduce injection. Use the ORM's safe parameterisation facilities and validate allowed structural choices."},{"q":"Why inspect generated migration SQL?","a":"The tool sees structural differences but cannot know your safe deployment/backfill intent. Review data loss, lock duration, constraints and compatibility with running application versions."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the safest raw-query pattern?","options":["Parameterised values through the supported API","Concatenate a user search string into SQL","Trust every query fragment from the browser","Disable constraints during ordinary requests"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Parameterisation separates data values from SQL structure."},"skills":[103],"minutes":15},{"id":"db-indexes","title":"Storage, indexes, plans, WAL and vacuum","body":"## Rows live in pages, not magic\nA storage engine groups data into pages and maintains structures to find and update it. An index is an additional access structure, often a B-tree for ordered equality/range access. It consumes storage and adds write maintenance; adding an index to every column is not a free optimisation.\n\n## Composite indexes follow access patterns\nColumn order matters because the index has an ordering. Consider equality filters, ranges and the requested sort together. A covering/index-only strategy can reduce table access under the database's rules; partial indexes target a subset of rows. Use the target engine's planner behaviour rather than a universal slogan about every composite index.\n\nEXPLAIN shows a proposed plan; EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes and measures it, so do not run it blindly on destructive statements. Compare estimated and actual rows and inspect where work multiplies. Nested-loop, hash and merge joins have different cost profiles depending on input size, ordering and available memory.\n\n## Writes and maintenance\nWrite-ahead logging records information needed for crash recovery before relevant changes become durable. In PostgreSQL, MVCC leaves old row versions that vacuum can reclaim when safe. Stale statistics can mislead the planner; analyse/maintenance are part of operating the database.\n\nMeasure a representative query before and after one index change. Record read benefit, write cost and data distribution. A plan from a tiny empty development table does not predict a large production workload reliably.","exercise":"For a task query filtered by tenant and status and ordered by creation time/ID, propose an index. Explain how you would compare actual plans and what write/storage costs you would measure.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can an index make writes slower?","a":"Every relevant write may also update the index structure and its pages/log records. Additional indexes trade storage and write work for selected read paths."},{"q":"Why can a planner choose a sequential scan despite an index?","a":"It may estimate that a large fraction of rows is needed or that index/table lookups cost more. Data distribution, statistics, table size and the exact query all affect the choice."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must you remember about EXPLAIN ANALYZE?","options":["It executes the statement to measure it","It never touches data under any circumstance","It automatically adds every useful index","It replaces backups"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Execution makes it useful but requires care, particularly with data-changing statements."},"skills":[105,106,107,113,114],"minutes":15},{"id":"db-transactions","title":"ACID, isolation, locks and race-free updates","body":"## A transaction groups a business invariant\nAtomicity prevents a partially committed transaction. Consistency means the transaction must preserve the database's declared/business invariants, not that the database invents them. Isolation controls how concurrent work interacts. Durability concerns committed data surviving the failures promised by the configured system.\n\n## The last-item problem\nReading stock=1 in two requests and later writing stock=0 twice can oversell. Use an atomic conditional update such as decrementing only where stock is sufficient and checking the affected-row result. Coordinate the order/payment workflow deliberately; a client-provided stock flag is not authoritative.\n\n## Isolation is a contract\nRead committed, repeatable-read and serializable behaviours differ by engine. Learn anomalies such as nonrepeatable reads, phantoms and write skew through specific interleavings. Serializable transactions can require retries; the application must retry the entire suitable transaction, not an arbitrary middle statement.\n\nMVCC lets readers observe versions according to a snapshot policy. In PostgreSQL, ordinary readers and writers often avoid blocking each other, but locks and conflicts still exist. Lock resources in a consistent order, keep transactions short and handle deadlock/serialization errors explicitly.\n\nDo not keep a database transaction open across an unbounded model or payment-provider call. Use a state machine, durable job/outbox or a well-defined reservation workflow when external side effects cannot join one database transaction.","exercise":"Write a race-free stock decrement and describe two concurrent buyers. Then draw a write-skew example and explain whether a stronger isolation level or a constraint/lock is appropriate.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is read-then-write stock checking unsafe?","a":"Several requests can read the same available value before either writes. An atomic conditional update or properly coordinated transaction must enforce the invariant at the write boundary."},{"q":"Does MVCC mean no locks ever exist?","a":"No. It changes how versions and snapshots serve readers, but writes, explicit locks, schema changes and isolation conflicts still require coordination and can block or abort work."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which best prevents stock from going negative?","options":["An atomic decrement conditioned on sufficient stock","A frontend disabled button only","An earlier unlocked read followed by an unconditional write","Trusting the requested quantity without validation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The condition and update must be enforced together under concurrency."},"skills":[108,109,110,111,112],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"sql","code":"CREATE TABLE study_stock(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, quantity INTEGER CHECK(quantity >= 0));\nINSERT INTO study_stock VALUES (1,1);\nUPDATE study_stock SET quantity=quantity-1 WHERE id=1 AND quantity>=1;\nSELECT changes() AS first_success;\nUPDATE study_stock SET quantity=quantity-1 WHERE id=1 AND quantity>=1;\nSELECT changes() AS second_success;\nSELECT quantity FROM study_stock;","expected":"The first update changes one row, the second changes zero, and stock remains 0.","stdin":""}},{"id":"db-operations","title":"Pools, migrations, backups, replicas and partitioning","body":"## Connections are bounded resources\nA connection pool reuses a controlled number of database connections. Size it against database capacity and the number of application instances, not just one process. Serverless scaling can multiply connections unexpectedly; a compatible pooler such as PgBouncer may help, with mode/transaction limitations that must match the application.\n\n## Change schemas without breaking running code\nUse expand/contract migrations and paced backfills for large data. Deploy compatible code, observe it, migrate data and remove old structures later. Set appropriate lock/statement timeouts and test rollback/recovery plans.\n\n## Backups must be restorable\nLogical backups describe database objects/data; physical backups capture storage-level state. Point-in-time recovery also depends on a correct log/archive strategy. A backup job reporting success is not proof: rehearse a restore and verify the data and recovery time.\n\nReplicas can scale reads and improve recovery options, but replication lag means a just-written value may not be visible on every replica immediately. Decide which reads require read-your-writes consistency and route them appropriately.\n\nPartitioning divides data within a database according to a rule; sharding distributes ownership across database nodes. Range/hash choices affect pruning, hotspots and operations. Do not shard merely because a project might grow: first measure indexes, queries, connection use and operational limits.","exercise":"Specify a database recovery objective and restore rehearsal. Design a read-after-write path with replicas and a safe migration/backfill plan for a large task table.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is a successful backup job insufficient evidence?","a":"The backup may be incomplete, inaccessible or too slow to restore. A rehearsal verifies the actual recovery path, required credentials/logs and the promised recovery time/data-loss window."},{"q":"What can replica lag break in a UI?","a":"A user writes successfully and then reads from a lagging replica, seeing an older value. The application needs a deliberate consistency/routing policy for such paths."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should precede sharding?","options":["Evidence that simpler query/index/operation improvements are insufficient","A guess that every app needs it immediately","Removing backups","Making every query unbounded"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sharding adds coordination and operational complexity, so it should solve a measured need."},"skills":[115,116,117,118,119],"minutes":15},{"id":"db-documents","title":"MongoDB documents, Mongoose and model choice","body":"## Model access patterns\nA document database stores structured documents, often with nested fields and arrays. Embedding related data can make a bounded read convenient; referencing can avoid unbounded duplication and support independent lifecycles. Choose based on how data is read/updated and its size/growth, not a slogan that documents have no schema.\n\nMongoose supplies application-side schemas, validation and mapping conventions. Database validation, indexes and transaction guarantees remain separate decisions. An application schema alone does not prevent another client from writing an incompatible document.\n\n## Useful query discipline\nIndex the actual filters and sorts, paginate large collections and project only needed fields. Aggregation pipelines transform and combine data through stages; inspect cardinality and memory implications. A dynamic product specification may fit document modelling, while invoices/payments may require strong relational constraints or carefully designed document transactions.\n\nPrevent operator/structure injection by validating request shape and building queries from allowed fields. Do not spread arbitrary client objects into a database filter. Atomic conditional updates can enforce a stock rule, but a multi-document business invariant still needs a coordinated transaction/workflow.\n\nRelational, document and key-value models all have useful workloads. Explain your chosen consistency requirements, relationships and access patterns in an interview rather than presenting MERN or PERN as a complete architectural argument.","exercise":"Model products with category-specific specifications and bounded variants. Decide which data is embedded versus referenced, and specify a safe filter/pagination contract.","interviews":[{"q":"When is embedding a good choice?","a":"When related data is naturally read/updated together, remains bounded and has a compatible lifecycle. Independent growth or many-to-many reuse may favour references and explicit query/consistency design."},{"q":"Does MongoDB mean no schema is needed?","a":"No. The application still has a data contract, and validation/index/consistency choices remain important. Flexible storage does not make invalid or unbounded documents a good design."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is risky in a public search endpoint?","options":["Spreading arbitrary client objects directly into a database filter","Allowlisting supported filter fields","Bounding page size","Selecting only public output fields"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unvalidated structure can alter query semantics and expose unsupported operators or fields."},"skills":[120,121],"minutes":15},{"id":"db-cache-search","title":"Redis, text search, vectors and analytical workloads","body":"## A cache needs an ownership policy\nCache-aside reads the cache, loads from the source on a miss and stores a value with a chosen expiry. Redis can support cached values, counters and other structures, but an expiring key does not automatically make invalidation correct. Choose keys that include tenant/user scope when the value differs by permission.\n\nPrevent cache stampedes when many requests miss together through bounded coordination or suitable stale/refresh strategies. Define whether a failed cache should fall back to the source. A cache must not become an undocumented second source of truth.\n\n## Search has several contracts\nFull-text search ranks lexical terms and language-aware matches; PostgreSQL can be a useful starting point before a separate search engine is justified. Vector search compares embeddings and uses an index such as pgvector's supported structures. Semantic similarity does not prove factual correctness or permission to reveal a document.\n\nFor search engines, keep a freshness/reindex policy and apply access filters. Evaluate actual queries before changing technology. A faster retrieval of the wrong tenant's data is a security failure.\n\n## OLTP versus OLAP\nTransactional workloads optimise many bounded reads/writes and invariants. Analytical workloads scan/aggregate larger data sets, often benefiting from column-oriented storage and different data preparation. Sending every dashboard aggregation to the busiest transactional table can hurt request latency; measure before designing a separate warehouse/read model.","exercise":"Define a tenant-scoped cached dashboard key, expiry and invalidation event. Compare exact keyword search, semantic search and a reporting aggregation for the same product catalog.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is TTL not a complete invalidation policy?","a":"A stale value can remain until expiry, and important changes may need immediate invalidation. Scope, source of truth and update/rebuild behaviour must be designed alongside the timeout."},{"q":"How do OLTP and OLAP differ?","a":"OLTP focuses on bounded transactional operations and invariants; OLAP focuses on large scans and aggregations for analysis. Their storage/query patterns and latency trade-offs can differ."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must a private search cache key account for?","options":["The relevant tenant/user permission scope","Only the text colour","Only the public page title","A universal key shared across all private accounts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Different access scopes must not share a cached private result accidentally."},"skills":[122,123,124,125],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"os","title":"Operating Systems","group":"Systems foundations","summary":"Explain the processes, memory and I/O underneath your programs with concrete traces.","duration":"2-3 weeks alongside coding","project":"Profile a small service, diagnose one resource limit and document graceful shutdown and concurrency behaviour.","sources":[{"title":"Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces","url":"https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/"},{"title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"},{"title":"Node.js Learn","url":"https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"os-processes","title":"Processes, threads, lifecycle and signals","body":"## A process owns an execution context\nA process has an address space and operating-system-managed resources. Threads within a process share much of that address space while having their own execution state/stacks. Sharing can make communication cheaper, but it also permits races. A crash or memory error in a native thread can affect the whole process.\n\n## Lifecycle\nA simplified process moves among ready, running and blocked/waiting states, then terminates. A scheduler chooses runnable work. A process blocked on I/O is not necessarily consuming a full CPU core. On Unix-like systems, fork creates a process context and exec replaces the program image; exact behaviour differs across operating systems.\n\nSignals communicate events. SIGTERM requests termination and can be handled for graceful shutdown; SIGKILL forces termination and cannot be handled by the target. A service should stop accepting new work, finish or safely release in-flight work within a deadline, close resources and exit. Forced termination remains a fallback, not a normal flush strategy.\n\n## Concurrency versus parallelism\nConcurrency means overlapping progress; parallelism means simultaneous execution. One event loop can coordinate many waiting operations without doing several CPU instructions at once. Several threads/processes can run in parallel on suitable hardware and runtime configurations.\n\nContext switches save/restore execution state and may disturb caches. More threads are not automatically faster. Measure runnable work, I/O waiting, CPU use and scheduling overhead for the actual workload.","exercise":"Draw the lifecycle of a web request that waits on a database. Specify a graceful shutdown sequence for a worker and what happens if its deadline expires.","interviews":[{"q":"How do processes and threads differ?","a":"Processes normally have separate virtual address spaces; threads in one process share memory and many resources. Threads can communicate through shared objects but need coordination to protect invariants."},{"q":"Why handle SIGTERM before a forced kill?","a":"It gives the service a bounded opportunity to stop new work, release/requeue jobs and close resources. A forced kill cannot run the target's cleanup logic."}],"quiz":{"question":"A process waiting on a network response is commonly in which simplified state?","options":["Blocked/waiting","Always running at full CPU","Already terminated","A database index"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Waiting for I/O is different from being ready to execute CPU work."},"skills":[126,127,129,133],"minutes":15},{"id":"os-scheduling","title":"FCFS, SJF, Round Robin and priority scheduling","body":"## Measure the same quantities\nArrival time is when work becomes ready, burst time is required CPU service and completion time is when it finishes. Turnaround = completion - arrival. Waiting = turnaround - burst in the simplified single-burst model. Response time measures when the first service begins, not when the process finishes.\n\nFCFS serves arrival order and can make a short task wait behind a long one. SJF chooses the shortest available burst; its preemptive relative chooses shortest remaining work. These methods need burst estimates. Priority scheduling chooses by a priority policy and can starve low-priority work unless the design addresses it, for example through ageing.\n\nRound Robin gives each ready process a time quantum. A very large quantum approaches FCFS; a very small one increases switching overhead. Real operating systems use more complex policies than this teaching model.\n\n## Worked trace\nAll arrive at 0: P1 needs 5, P2 needs 3, P3 needs 1. FCFS P1/P2/P3 has waits 0,5,8: average 13/3. SJF P3/P2/P1 has waits 0,1,4: average 5/3. For Round Robin quantum 2, the sequence is P1(0-2), P2(2-4), P3(4-5), P1(5-7), P2(7-8), P1(8-9).\n\nAlways state arrival times, tie-breaking, preemption and context-switch assumptions before comparing algorithms. An average alone can hide a very poor response time for one class of work.","exercise":"Calculate completion, turnaround, waiting and response times for the Round Robin trace. Then change P3 arrival to time 3 and recompute under an explicitly stated policy.","interviews":[{"q":"How do waiting and turnaround differ?","a":"Turnaround spans arrival to completion. In a simple one-burst model, waiting is turnaround minus CPU burst time; response time is the delay until first service."},{"q":"What is the Round Robin quantum trade-off?","a":"A smaller quantum can improve initial responsiveness but increases switching overhead. A larger one reduces switches and increasingly behaves like FCFS for the ready queue."}],"quiz":{"question":"For arrival 2, burst 4 and completion 9, what is waiting time in the single-burst model?","options":["3","7","4","9"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Turnaround is 9-2=7; waiting is 7-4=3."},"skills":[128],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"arrival, burst, completion = 2, 4, 9\nturnaround = completion - arrival\nprint(turnaround, turnaround - burst)","expected":"7 3","stdin":""}},{"id":"os-synchronisation","title":"Critical sections, locks, semaphores and deadlock","body":"## Interleavings can violate an invariant\nA read-modify-write increment is several steps. Two workers can both read 0 and each write 1, losing an update. A critical section is the code region whose shared-state invariant must be protected. A mutex permits one owner; a semaphore tracks a count of permits and can bound concurrent access to a resource pool.\n\nLocks need a clear scope and release path. Keep protected work small, avoid an external network wait while holding a lock and do not assume the language's runtime lock makes every business operation atomic. Read/write locks and fine-grained locks can help specific workloads but add design complexity.\n\n## Deadlock requires four conditions\nThe classic conditions are mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevention breaks a necessary condition, such as a global lock order. Avoidance checks whether granting resources keeps the system in a safe state; an unsafe state is not automatically a current deadlock. Detection/recovery allows deadlocks to occur and then handles them.\n\n## A concrete lock-order rule\nIf one operation locks A then B and another locks B then A, both can wait indefinitely. Require both to acquire in the same order and release reliably. Timeouts help detection/recovery but do not by themselves prove a race-free design.\n\nTest concurrency with controlled barriers/interleavings where possible. A test that happens to run without collision once is weak evidence that a race cannot occur.","exercise":"Trace the lost-update interleaving for two increments. Then describe a lock-order rule for transferring between two accounts and how a failed operation releases both locks.","interviews":[{"q":"How do a mutex and a semaphore differ?","a":"A mutex protects exclusive ownership of a critical region/resource. A semaphore represents permits and can allow a bounded number of concurrent users; correct ownership/release semantics still matter."},{"q":"Does a timeout prevent deadlock by itself?","a":"It can bound waiting or trigger recovery, but the locking design still needs a correctness policy. Consistent ordering or an appropriate avoidance strategy addresses the circular dependency itself."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is one classic deadlock condition?","options":["Circular wait","A sorted input array","A unique HTML ID","An empty task queue"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Circular wait is one of the four necessary conditions in the classic model."},"skills":[130,131,132],"minutes":15},{"id":"os-memory","title":"Paging, virtual memory, stack/heap and garbage collection","body":"## Virtual addresses are a mapping\nVirtual memory gives a process an address-space abstraction. Paging maps fixed-size virtual pages to physical frames or other backing state; a translation cache such as a TLB accelerates common mappings. A page fault invokes the OS to resolve a missing/protected mapping and is not always a fatal program error. Excessive paging can cause thrashing and poor progress.\n\nSegmentation models logical variable-sized regions; it is conceptually different from fixed-size paging. Real systems combine hardware/OS mechanisms, so do not reduce all address translation to a single classroom diagram.\n\n## Stack and heap\nCall stacks organise frames and control flow; heap allocation supports objects with other lifetimes/ownership patterns. The exact placement of a language value depends on its implementation and optimisation. Deep recursion can exhaust stack resources even when the algorithm's input data would fit elsewhere.\n\n## Managed memory still leaks\nGarbage collectors reclaim unreachable objects, not every object you no longer need logically. A cache, listener or global reference can keep obsolete data reachable. V8 uses generational collection techniques; CPython commonly combines reference counting with cyclic garbage collection, with build/version details that matter.\n\nContainer/cgroup memory limits add another boundary. A process may be killed for exceeding its limit even when the host has free memory. Compare heap metrics with total resident/native allocations and the configured limit before increasing one runtime heap setting.","exercise":"Explain a page fault, a stack overflow and a retained-listener leak as three different failures. Design a bounded cache and a memory measurement plan for a Node or Python service.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a garbage-collected program leak memory?","a":"Objects can remain reachable through a cache, closure, listener or global reference after they stop being useful. The collector cannot infer that the application no longer wants reachable data."},{"q":"Why can a container die while the host has free RAM?","a":"The container/process can have its own enforced memory limit. Its runtime heap is only part of total memory, so native buffers and other allocations can exceed that boundary."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is a page fault?","options":["An event requiring the OS to resolve a memory mapping/access condition","Always a syntax error","A guarantee that RAM is physically broken","A CSS page break"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A page fault can be part of ordinary virtual-memory operation or can indicate an invalid access."},"skills":[134,135,136,137,138],"minutes":15},{"id":"os-io","title":"File descriptors, file systems and durable writes","body":"## Open resources have identities and limits\nA file descriptor is a process-visible handle for an open resource such as a file, socket or pipe on Unix-like systems. A descriptor table and OS limits bound how many can be open. Leaking sockets/files can cause too-many-open-files errors even when CPU and memory look acceptable.\n\nBlocking I/O waits in the calling execution context. Nonblocking I/O reports readiness/progress without requiring that thread to wait for every operation. Readiness mechanisms such as epoll or kqueue help event loops coordinate many resources; they do not make arbitrary CPU work parallel.\n\n## File-system concepts\nA filename maps through directory structures to underlying file metadata/data. Inodes, hard links, symbolic links, permissions, mounts and volumes explain why a path and a stored object are not always the same concept. A container's writable layer and a persistent mounted volume have different lifecycles.\n\n## A successful write is not every durability guarantee\nData may first enter language/runtime buffers and OS page caches. Flushing those layers and calling filesystem synchronisation where required serves different purposes. Atomic rename/replacement can prevent ordinary readers seeing a partial destination, but a crash-durability design must also consider the filesystem and directory metadata guarantees.\n\nUse context managers/finally blocks to close resources. Bound concurrent uploads/downloads and inspect descriptor counts under a sustained workload, including cancelled and failed requests.","exercise":"Design safe persistence for a local notes file: encoding, temporary file, replacement, concurrent writers, backup and crash recovery. Explain why closing descriptors and ensuring durable data are related but distinct.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a service exhaust file descriptors?","a":"It may leak open files/sockets or start too much concurrent I/O. Inspect ownership and cleanup paths, including failures, before treating a higher limit as the complete fix."},{"q":"Does write() returning mean data is safe against power loss?","a":"Not universally. Buffers and OS caches can still hold it. Durability depends on explicit synchronisation and the storage/filesystem guarantees required by the application."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which resource can consume a file descriptor?","options":["A network socket","Only a plain-text .txt file","Only a Python integer","Only a Git commit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Descriptors can represent sockets, files, pipes and other OS-managed open resources."},"skills":[139,140,141,142],"minutes":15},{"id":"os-linux","title":"Linux shell, permissions, containers and service logs","body":"## Navigate and inspect before changing\nLearn pwd, cd, ls, path quoting and file permissions. Pipes connect streams; redirection changes stream destinations. Tools such as grep/rg, sed, awk, find and xargs transform/search data, but filenames with spaces/newlines require correct quoting or null-delimited handling. Inspect an absolute path before destructive operations.\n\nUse ps/top/htop for processes, lsof for open resources and strace where appropriate for system-call diagnosis. A process existing is not proof that its service is healthy; check the relevant health endpoint and actual work.\n\n## Least privilege\nUsers/groups and read/write/execute permissions determine access. Directory execute permission affects traversal. Run services with a suitable non-administrator account and grant only required paths/operations. chmod 777 is not a general deployment repair.\n\n## Containers share the host kernel\nNamespaces isolate selected views/resources and cgroups apply resource controls. A container is not simply a full virtual machine and is not an automatic boundary against every possible escape or host exposure. Docker images/layers package a filesystem/configuration; Compose coordinates related local services. Use mounted volumes for intended persistence.\n\nOn systems using systemd, service units declare startup/restart policy and journalctl helps inspect logs. Handle signals and configure restart limits so a broken process does not spin forever without an actionable error.","exercise":"Write a non-destructive diagnosis checklist for a service returning 503: process, port, logs, dependencies, permissions and limits. Explain which data needs a persistent volume.","interviews":[{"q":"How is a container different from a virtual machine?","a":"A container generally isolates a process environment while sharing the host kernel. A VM typically virtualises hardware to run a guest kernel, with different isolation and resource trade-offs."},{"q":"Why is chmod 777 a poor default fix?","a":"It grants broad access without identifying the required owner and operation. Diagnose the path/identity mismatch and assign the minimum permissions needed."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should persist outside a disposable container layer?","options":["The application data that must survive replacement","Every temporary build cache by default","A private key baked into a public image","Only the container name"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Persistent data needs an explicit volume or external storage lifecycle."},"skills":[143,144,145,146,147,148],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"networks","title":"Computer Networks","group":"Systems foundations","summary":"Trace a request through addressing, transport, HTTP and failure recovery.","duration":"2-3 weeks","project":"Draw and measure one browser-to-API request, then define timeout, retry, cache and access policies.","sources":[{"title":"MDN HTTP guides","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides"},{"title":"Operating Systems Three Easy Pieces","url":"https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"net-models","title":"LAN/WAN, OSI, IP addresses and subnets","body":"## Layers are a debugging map\nA local network connects nearby devices; a WAN spans broader networks. OSI is a seven-layer teaching model: physical, data link, network, transport, session, presentation and application. TCP/IP groups responsibilities differently. Use the models to ask where a failure occurs rather than treating every protocol as a perfect one-layer box.\n\nAn IP address identifies an interface in an addressing/routing context. IPv4 uses 32 bits and IPv6 128. A MAC address participates in local link delivery; routers move packets between networks using routing information. NAT translates addresses/ports at a boundary and is not a complete firewall policy by itself.\n\n## Read CIDR notation\nA prefix length says how many leading bits identify the network. An IPv4 /26 leaves 6 host bits, so it spans 64 addresses. Under the usual network/broadcast teaching assumptions that gives 62 ordinary host addresses; special cases such as point-to-point /31 links have different rules.\n\nDHCP can supply addressing/configuration to a client. DNS maps names to relevant records; it is not the same as assigning a machine's local address. Private address ranges need routing/NAT/tunnels as appropriate to communicate beyond their networks.\n\nWhen a request fails, check link connectivity, local address, route, name resolution, transport connection and application response in order. A successful ping alone does not prove an HTTPS endpoint works.","exercise":"For an ordinary IPv4 /27 subnet, calculate its address count and usual host count. Draw the roles of a switch, router, DNS resolver and application server.","interviews":[{"q":"How do IP and MAC roles differ?","a":"IP supports addressing/routing across networks. MAC addresses support local link delivery within the relevant link-layer context; a remote destination is reached through next-hop links, not one global Ethernet segment."},{"q":"Why is the OSI model useful in an interview?","a":"It gives a structured way to locate a problem: physical/link connectivity, addressing/routing, transport, encryption/protocol and application behaviour. It is a map, not a substitute for the actual protocol details."}],"quiz":{"question":"How many total addresses are in an IPv4 /27 block?","options":["32","27","64","128"],"answer":0,"explanation":"32-27=5 host bits, giving 2^5=32 total addresses."},"skills":[149,150],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import ipaddress\nnetwork = ipaddress.ip_network(\"192.168.1.0/27\")\nprint(network.num_addresses)\nprint(len(list(network.hosts())))","expected":"32\n30","stdin":""}},{"id":"net-transport","title":"TCP, UDP, ports and HTTP versions","body":"## Transport connects application endpoints\nA port identifies a transport/application endpoint within an address/protocol context. TCP supplies an ordered reliable byte stream with connection state, acknowledgements and congestion control. Its handshake establishes connection parameters; application message framing still has to be defined above the stream.\n\nUDP sends datagrams without TCP's built-in reliable ordered stream contract. It is useful when the application or a higher protocol wants different latency/recovery trade-offs. UDP does not mean an application can never add reliability or encryption.\n\n## Compare failure and latency\nTCP retransmission recovers loss but ordering can make later bytes wait behind missing earlier bytes. Congestion control tries to avoid overwhelming the path. A fast local server can still feel slow across a high-latency or lossy connection.\n\nHTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 have different transport/multiplexing behaviour. HTTP/2 multiplexes streams over a TCP connection; transport-level loss can still affect progress. HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP and provides a different stream/recovery design with integrated modern security mechanisms.\n\nDo not choose a protocol only from a slogan that one is fast. Consider intermediaries, browser support, connection setup, loss patterns and the actual workload. WebSockets and SSE are application communication choices layered on these capabilities.","exercise":"Explain TCP versus UDP for a file download and a realtime voice application. Then describe why several HTTP/2 streams can still be affected by one lost TCP segment.","interviews":[{"q":"Does TCP preserve application message boundaries?","a":"No. It presents an ordered byte stream. The application protocol must define lengths, delimiters or another framing mechanism to reconstruct messages."},{"q":"Does using UDP guarantee low latency?","a":"No. Network congestion, application buffering and the higher-level protocol still matter. UDP offers datagrams with fewer built-in delivery guarantees; the application chooses what to add."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which statement is accurate?","options":["HTTP/3 uses QUIC over UDP","HTTP/2 eliminates every form of head-of-line delay","UDP cannot carry encrypted traffic","TCP automatically frames JSON messages"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HTTP/3 changes transport through QUIC; the other statements overgeneralise."},"skills":[151,152,161],"minutes":15},{"id":"net-dns-tls","title":"DNS resolution and TLS trust","body":"## Resolve a name before connecting\nA client normally asks a resolver for records. Cached answers can avoid further work; otherwise the resolution process follows the relevant authoritative hierarchy. A/AAAA records supply addresses, CNAME expresses an alias and other records serve other purposes. TTL influences cache lifetime; a DNS edit does not instantly erase every cached answer.\n\n## Establish an encrypted authenticated channel\nTLS negotiates security parameters and proves the server controls a suitable credential for the requested identity under a trusted certificate chain. The client checks names, validity and trust. The padlock does not prove that the site's business claims are honest or that its application has no bugs.\n\nKeep certificate renewal and hostname configuration operationally visible. A certificate error is not repaired safely by disabling verification in every client. For internal services, use an appropriate trust configuration rather than copying an insecure bypass.\n\n## Diagnose the phases separately\nMeasure DNS, TCP/QUIC connection establishment, TLS negotiation, server processing and body transfer. A slow name lookup and a slow database query require different fixes. Reused connections and caches change repeat-request timings, so test under clear conditions.\n\nDNS and TLS also affect deployment: a new domain, CDN or proxy can introduce stale records, wrong certificate names or an unexpected origin. Verify the actual deployed route rather than only a local hosts-file shortcut.","exercise":"Draw a first visit to a new HTTPS hostname. Mark the DNS resolver/cache, connection, certificate check and HTTP request. Explain what a stale DNS cache can and cannot affect.","interviews":[{"q":"What does a valid TLS certificate establish?","a":"It supports an authenticated encrypted connection to the named endpoint under the client's trust model. It does not certify every application behaviour or business statement on that site."},{"q":"Why can a DNS change appear inconsistent temporarily?","a":"Resolvers and clients can retain earlier answers according to cache/TTL behaviour. Different request paths may therefore use different cached records until they refresh."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should you do with a certificate-name mismatch?","options":["Fix the hostname/certificate configuration","Disable verification globally","Put a private key in the browser","Assume the database is corrupt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Verification errors identify a trust/configuration problem; bypassing them broadly removes the protection."},"skills":[153,154],"minutes":15},{"id":"net-http","title":"HTTP semantics, caching, CORS and cookies","body":"## Requests and responses have meaning\nHTTP methods, status codes and headers form a contract. Safe methods should not request state-changing semantics; idempotency describes repeated intended effect. A 2xx response, redirect, client error and server error need different handling. A frontend should not parse every response body as a successful domain object.\n\n## Cache deliberately\nCache-Control specifies relevant reuse rules; ETag can identify a representation for conditional requests. A 304 response allows reuse of a validated cached representation. no-cache and no-store are different: one concerns reuse without validation, the other forbids storing under its rules. Shared versus private cache scope matters for user-specific data.\n\n## Browser boundaries\nCORS controls whether browser script may read a cross-origin response. Some requests trigger a preflight. A non-browser caller is not authenticated merely because the CORS allowlist is narrow. For credentialed browser access, configure explicit origins and the relevant cookie/credential policy; a wildcard is not a universal fix.\n\nCookies match domain/path and other policy constraints. Secure restricts transmission to secure contexts under its rules; HttpOnly blocks ordinary JavaScript cookie access; SameSite affects cross-site sending. These are complementary controls, not replacements for authorisation or CSRF analysis.\n\nLearn common application protocols around this model: SMTP carries email, FTP is a file-transfer protocol family and DNS has its own record/query semantics. Their names alone do not imply modern secure deployment defaults.","exercise":"Design cache headers for public static assets and a private progress endpoint. Explain a preflight failure separately from an expired login session and a server-side permission denial.","interviews":[{"q":"How do no-cache and no-store differ?","a":"no-cache permits storage but requires suitable validation before reuse. no-store directs caches not to store the response under its rules. Neither is an object-level permission check."},{"q":"Why is CORS not authentication?","a":"It is enforced by browsers for script access to responses. Other HTTP clients can still call an endpoint, so the server needs real authentication and authorisation."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which cookie flag blocks ordinary JavaScript reads?","options":["HttpOnly","A CSS class","Cache-Control: public","The route name login"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HttpOnly limits script access; transport, cross-site sending and permissions need separate controls."},"skills":[155,156,157,158],"minutes":15},{"id":"net-streams","title":"WebSocket and SSE connections through proxies","body":"## A persistent connection changes operations\nA WebSocket begins through a handshake and then supports bidirectional messages. An SSE response streams framed server events over HTTP. Both need authentication, capacity limits and a recovery policy; keeping one connection open does not guarantee every important event is delivered durably.\n\n## Intermediaries matter\nA reverse proxy can buffer output, enforce idle timeouts or reject an upgrade. Test through the deployed proxy/load balancer, not only directly against a development server. Configure heartbeat and timeout behaviour without creating excessive traffic or an unbounded connection count.\n\n## Reconnection is an application contract\nUse event IDs, sequence/version information or a snapshot resynchronisation path. Duplicate delivery should be harmless. If the server retains only a short replay window, explain what the client does after a longer offline period.\n\nFor streamed AI text, distinguish receiving chunks from completing a valid answer. A network chunk can split a UTF-8 sequence or logical event; use a streaming decoder/parser. When the user stops generation or leaves, propagate cancellation so expensive provider work does not continue without a consumer.\n\nBound per-client buffers and slow-consumer behaviour. A service can look healthy under fast local clients and exhaust memory with a few stalled readers. Persist business changes independently so a dropped connection cannot erase the real task or payment state.","exercise":"Specify a notification stream that recovers after a disconnect, including replay limits and duplicate IDs. Name one proxy setting that can make a locally working stream appear frozen in production.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can SSE appear to deliver everything at once?","a":"A server or intermediary may buffer chunks instead of forwarding the stream promptly. Verify framing, flush behaviour and proxy configuration through the actual deployment path."},{"q":"Why should a stop button cancel the backend job?","a":"Otherwise the UI stops showing output while the server/provider may keep doing costly work. Cancellation needs to cross the browser, server and provider/worker boundaries."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is a safe reconnect strategy?","options":["Replay by event ID or reconcile a fresh snapshot","Assume no events were missed","Append every duplicate forever","Disable authentication after reconnect"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Recovery must restore a known state despite gaps and duplicates."},"skills":[159,160],"minutes":15},{"id":"net-resilience","title":"Latency, timeouts, retries and circuit breakers","body":"## Separate latency sources\nDNS, connection setup, TLS, server queueing, database/provider work and transfer all contribute. Measure percentiles and component timings. An average can hide a slow tail that dominates user complaints.\n\nEvery outbound call needs bounded connect/read/overall behaviour appropriate to the client. A timeout is not proof that a side effect did not happen. Retry only failures that are safe under the operation's idempotency contract and useful under the remaining deadline.\n\n## Backoff and budgets\nExponential backoff increases delay between attempts; jitter avoids synchronised clients retrying together. Limit attempt count and total elapsed time. Respect relevant 429/Retry-After signals. Several layers each retrying independently can multiply load, so coordinate the retry budget.\n\n## Fail fast when a dependency is unhealthy\nA circuit breaker can stop repeated calls after a failure threshold and later allow limited probes in a half-open state. It needs useful metrics and recovery behaviour; it is not a substitute for fixing the dependency. A fallback should preserve a clear reduced capability instead of returning fabricated success.\n\nLoad tests should include a slow or unavailable dependency, not just a healthy local server. Observe queue growth, pending requests and recovery after the dependency returns. A service that recovers by sending every queued request at once can trigger another outage.","exercise":"Design a report-provider call with an overall deadline, per-attempt timeout, safe retry conditions, jitter and a deterministic fallback. Explain which requests must not be retried blindly.","interviews":[{"q":"Why add jitter to backoff?","a":"Many clients can fail at the same time. Randomised delay spreads their retries instead of creating another synchronised traffic spike against the recovering service."},{"q":"What is a retry budget?","a":"A limit on attempts and/or total time/load spent retrying. It keeps one user action or outage from causing unbounded multiplied work across several system layers."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which operation most needs idempotency before retrying?","options":["Creating a payment after an ambiguous timeout","Reading a public static document","Calculating a local sum","Formatting a date"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The payment might already have succeeded, so a blind retry can duplicate its effect."},"skills":[162,163,164,165],"minutes":15},{"id":"net-operations","title":"Proxies, load balancers, CDNs and network tools","body":"## Place the network components deliberately\nA reverse proxy receives client traffic and forwards it to an application. A load balancer distributes requests/connections and checks backend health. Layer-4 decisions operate around transport information, while layer-7 systems can use application/HTTP information. Sticky routing can affect realtime/session designs but should not hide a missing shared-state policy.\n\nCDNs place cached content closer to users. Define cache keys, freshness, private-data exclusions and invalidation. A CDN can speed the wrong response just as effectively as the right one. Validate origin and edge behaviour separately after deployment.\n\n## Diagnose with focused tools\ncurl -v shows request/response and connection details. dig investigates DNS, ss inspects sockets and traceroute can help reason about a path, with important filtering/interpretation limits. A browser Network panel reveals preflights, redirects, status and timing that a screenshot cannot.\n\n## Bound exposure\nFirewalls and security groups restrict allowed traffic. Private subnets can reduce direct exposure, but identity, patching and service permissions are still needed. DDoS resilience combines provider/network controls, resource limits and application design; a login rate limit alone does not protect every layer.\n\nUse separate health/readiness checks when the platform supports that distinction. An application can be alive but not ready to accept work because a required dependency or migration is unavailable.","exercise":"Draw browser, CDN, load balancer, API and private database. For a 502 response, state which logs/health checks you would inspect and why a direct local 200 is insufficient.","interviews":[{"q":"How do liveness and readiness differ conceptually?","a":"Liveness asks whether the process should keep running; readiness asks whether it should currently receive traffic. A live process may be temporarily unready due to startup or a dependency condition."},{"q":"Why test through the actual proxy/CDN path?","a":"Intermediaries can alter caching, routing, headers, timeouts and streaming. A direct backend success does not prove the user-facing route has the same behaviour."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which tool is directly useful for inspecting HTTP headers and redirects?","options":["curl -v","Only a CSS formatter","A database diagram alone","A code-theme selector"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An HTTP client with verbose output exposes the actual request/response path."},"skills":[166,167,168,169],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"devops","title":"DevOps & Cloud","group":"Production engineering","summary":"Build reproducibly, deploy safely, observe failures and understand resource costs.","duration":"3-4 weeks","project":"Containerise one project, run CI, deploy a reviewed build and demonstrate a rollback plus a restore rehearsal.","sources":[{"title":"Docker Get started","url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/"},{"title":"Pro Git book","url":"https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2"},{"title":"MDN HTTP guides","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"ops-delivery","title":"Docker, CI and safe releases","body":"## Build an artefact you can promote\nA container image packages the application and its runtime dependencies into layers. A multi-stage build can keep build tools out of the final runtime image. Pin useful versions, avoid baking secrets into layers and run with an appropriate non-root identity when possible. Compose helps coordinate local dependencies with explicit configuration/volumes.\n\n## Continuous integration\nA workflow should install locked dependencies, run meaningful lint/type/test checks and produce a build. It needs limited permissions and protected handling of untrusted pull requests. A failing required check should block the release path instead of being silently ignored.\n\nUse development, preview/staging and production environments with explicit configuration. Prefer promoting the same reviewed build rather than rebuilding untracked differences for each environment. Validate the deployed page/API and its critical assets after publication.\n\n## Release safely\nFeature flags separate deployment from enabling behaviour. A canary exposes a controlled portion of traffic and watches the relevant metrics. A rollback must account for database compatibility, background workers and external side effects, not only the web image tag.\n\nKeep a small runbook: what success looks like, which signal triggers rollback, who/what performs it and how data remains compatible. A pipeline completing is useful evidence, but the user's actual journey is the final operational check.","exercise":"Design CI and release steps for a task API and frontend. Include a container build, migration compatibility check, smoke test, feature flag and measurable rollback trigger.","interviews":[{"q":"Why use a multi-stage Docker build?","a":"It can separate compilers/build dependencies from the smaller runtime artefact. This reduces unnecessary runtime contents, while dependency trust and configuration still need deliberate controls."},{"q":"Why can an application rollback fail after a schema change?","a":"The old application may no longer understand the new schema or data. Backward-compatible migration order and a tested data strategy are needed before assuming a source/image rollback is safe."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the strongest deployment practice?","options":["Promote a reviewed tested artefact and verify the live path","Rebuild manually from untracked local files","Ignore failed checks and hope","Publish private environment files with the static site"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reproducible artefacts and live verification connect reviewed source to actual behaviour."},"skills":[170,171,172,175],"minutes":15},{"id":"ops-iac","title":"Infrastructure as code and secret management","body":"## Configuration is part of the system\nInfrastructure as code describes resources such as networks, services and databases through reviewable configuration. Terraform/Pulumi can plan and apply changes, but a generated plan still needs review: a mistaken replacement can destroy data or change exposure.\n\nState tracks the relationship between definitions and deployed resources. Protect it because it can contain sensitive values and operational authority. Coordinate concurrent changes through the tool's appropriate locking/backend design.\n\n## Secrets have lifecycles\nUse per-environment credentials with limited privileges. Keep them out of Git, frontend bundles, container layers and ordinary logs. A .env file is a local configuration convenience, not encryption or a complete secret-management platform. Production platforms can inject protected configuration or use a dedicated secret manager/workload identity.\n\nRotate credentials deliberately, revoke exposed ones and test that services reload/use the intended version. A secret removed from the latest commit may remain in history, caches or a published artefact. Avoid copying production secrets into preview environments.\n\nReview infrastructure changes by intended outcome: which identities can read/write, which ports become reachable, which data persists and how rollback/restore works. A low-diff configuration edit can still have a large real-world effect.","exercise":"Describe a staging/production secret policy and a rotation plan for a database credential. Review a hypothetical infrastructure plan that replaces a database and explain the required recovery evidence.","interviews":[{"q":"Why protect infrastructure state files?","a":"They describe deployed resources and can contain sensitive attributes or authority-related information. Treat their access, locking and backups as part of the system's security and recovery design."},{"q":"Why use different secrets per environment?","a":"It limits the impact of a leak and prevents a compromised preview/test environment from automatically gaining production access. Permissions and rotation can then be managed independently."}],"quiz":{"question":"Where must a private server key never be placed?","options":["A publicly delivered frontend bundle","A protected server secret store","A carefully scoped deployment secret","A restricted runtime environment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Every visitor can inspect frontend assets, regardless of minification."},"skills":[173,174],"minutes":15},{"id":"ops-cloud","title":"Compute, storage, IAM and deployment models","body":"## Learn one provider through the primitives\nCompute runs work; a managed database stores/query data; object storage holds files; identity/access management determines who can perform operations. A virtual network/subnet/security-group design controls connectivity. Understanding these primitives transfers better than memorising console screenshots.\n\n## Object storage\nUse bucket/object keys with explicit tenant ownership and access policy. Presigned URLs delegate a bounded operation for a time window; they are capabilities, not harmless public labels. Validate upload size/type and do not let a caller choose another tenant's object name. Backups and retention need an explicit policy.\n\n## Functions or long-running containers?\nServerless functions can fit bursty bounded requests but have platform-specific execution, connection and cold-start limits. Long-running containers can fit workers, persistent streams or other service lifecycles. Choose the deployment model that matches the operation rather than forcing a long job into a short request.\n\nKubernetes provides concepts such as pods, deployments and services for orchestration, but it adds operational machinery. Learn the concepts and use it when the system/team requirements justify it; a small application does not automatically need a cluster.\n\nFor this site, static GitHub Pages hosting serves files while a local launcher provides optional native operations. A static host cannot execute your Python backend just because its source file is uploaded. Hosting plans, quotas and prices change, so verify them before selecting a provider for a new project.","exercise":"Choose compute/storage/network primitives for a private report API and a public image catalog. Decide which operation fits a function and which needs a worker, with explicit timeout and access assumptions.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is a presigned URL sensitive?","a":"It grants a particular operation under its scope/expiry, so someone who obtains it may use that capability. Keep its permissions narrow and avoid exposing a broader or longer-lived URL than needed."},{"q":"When might a long-running worker fit better than a function?","a":"When work exceeds request/runtime limits, needs a durable queue lifecycle or requires long-lived processing. The choice depends on the platform's actual limits and operational contract."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does IAM primarily govern?","options":["Which identities can perform which resource operations","Only the CSS theme","Only array sorting","The number of React components"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Identity/access policy controls authority over cloud resources."},"skills":[176,177,178,180,181],"minutes":15},{"id":"ops-cost","title":"Cost per user, quotas and capacity planning","body":"## Cost follows a workload\nIdentify the unit that grows: requests, stored bytes, database connections, egress, background jobs or model tokens. Estimate cost per useful user action instead of comparing only a provider's headline free tier. A low request count can still be expensive if each request triggers several long model calls or transfers a huge file.\n\n## Set measurable budgets\nDefine per-tenant limits, maximum input/output sizes and concurrency. Cache appropriate repeated work, but keep private-data scope and correctness intact. Rate limiting prevents some abuse; it does not replace capacity planning for legitimate spikes.\n\nMeasure latency, saturation and errors as load increases. A system near a database connection limit may fail suddenly rather than slow smoothly. Include downstream limits and retry amplification in the model.\n\n## A concrete estimate\nIf one report uses 1,000 input and 200 output tokens, record those quantities independently because pricing can differ. Multiply by report frequency and active tenants, add retries and cache-hit assumptions, then compare the estimate with observed usage. Do not hard-code today's provider price as an eternal constant.\n\nUse alerts and spending controls where supported, review anomalous usage and keep a graceful reduced-capability path. Saving money by silently discarding required data or bypassing access checks is not an acceptable optimisation.","exercise":"Estimate monthly work for 200 tenants generating four reports each, with 10% retries and a documented cache policy. List the quantities you need before calculating an actual bill.","interviews":[{"q":"Why track cost per useful action?","a":"It connects infrastructure/model spending to product usage and exposes expensive fan-out or retries hidden by aggregate request counts."},{"q":"Why include retries in capacity estimates?","a":"Failures can multiply downstream work. A service sized only for healthy first attempts can overload its dependencies precisely when they are already struggling."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which quantity can make an AI feature unexpectedly expensive?","options":["Repeated large input/output token usage and retry fan-out","Only the page background colour","Only the file extension of a README","Only the number of navigation links"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Actual model work and retry patterns drive usage, latency and cost."},"skills":[179],"minutes":15},{"id":"ops-observability","title":"Logs, metrics, tracing and actionable alerts","body":"## Different signals answer different questions\nStructured logs explain events with safe fields such as request/job IDs, component and error category. Metrics summarise rates, latency distributions and saturation over time. Traces connect spans across services and dependencies so one slow request can be followed end to end.\n\nOpenTelemetry provides instrumentation conventions/tooling for traces and related signals. Propagate correlation context across HTTP, queues and model calls without logging private prompts indiscriminately. A trace should show where time went and which operation failed, not become a secret dump.\n\n## Measure a distribution\nAverage latency can look fine while a minority of users wait much longer. Percentiles such as p95/p99 describe the tail but need sufficient sample size and a correctly aggregated metric. Track error rate, queue age, CPU/memory/connection saturation and dependency health alongside latency.\n\nError trackers such as Sentry group failures and associate them with releases/source maps. Scrub sensitive fields and ensure deployed source maps match the release being diagnosed.\n\n## Alerts need an action\nAlert on user-visible symptoms or impending resource exhaustion with a runbook and an owner. Too many noisy alerts train people to ignore them. A useful alert states the affected capability, evidence, likely diagnosis path and immediate mitigation. Test that the alert actually fires in a controlled failure exercise.","exercise":"Design signals for a report API: one structured log, three metrics, a trace with database/provider spans and an alert that has a concrete mitigation.","interviews":[{"q":"How do logs and traces differ?","a":"Logs record discrete events; traces link timed spans in one distributed operation. Correlation IDs let you move between them, while metrics reveal aggregate trends."},{"q":"What makes an alert actionable?","a":"It identifies a meaningful symptom/threshold, relevant context, an owner and a response path. An alert with no useful decision or mitigation mainly creates noise."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which metric can reveal a slow minority hidden by the mean?","options":["A suitable tail latency percentile","Only the total source line count","Only the logo file size","Only the number of Git branches"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tail latency measurements expose delays that an average can conceal."},"skills":[182,183,184,185,186],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"security","title":"Application Security","group":"Production engineering","summary":"Protect identities, data and side effects through explicit trust boundaries and tests.","duration":"Study throughout every project","project":"Threat-model one project, test cross-tenant access and webhook replay, and document dependency/secret controls.","sources":[{"title":"OWASP Cheat Sheet Series","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/"},{"title":"MDN HTTP guides","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides"},{"title":"Razorpay webhook validation","url":"https://razorpay.com/docs/webhooks/validate-test/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"security-risks","title":"OWASP risks and threat modelling","body":"## Start with assets and boundaries\nList valuable data/actions, the identities that may access them and where untrusted input enters. Draw browser, API, database, storage and external-provider boundaries. For each, ask how an attacker could read another user's data, change a price, replay a request or exhaust resources.\n\n## OWASP Top 10:2025 as a review map\nThe named 2025 list groups risks into broken access control, security misconfiguration, software supply-chain failures, cryptographic failures, injection, insecure design, authentication failures, software/data integrity failures, security logging/alerting failures and mishandling of exceptional conditions. It is an awareness map, not proof that a checklist guarantees security.\n\nConnect each group to your stack: tenant-filtered queries for access, non-debug production configuration, reviewed dependencies, appropriate secret/password protection, parameterised queries, abuse-resistant workflows, session controls, verified artefacts/events, useful monitoring and bounded fail-safe error paths.\n\n## Design before patching symptoms\nA permission check at one route cannot repair a data model that has no reliable ownership information. An encrypted connection cannot repair an endpoint that returns another tenant's invoice. Prioritise the actual business invariant and enforce it at the relevant boundary.\n\nTest negative paths on systems you own: cross-account IDs, malformed fields, duplicate events and unavailable dependencies. Record what the test proves and its limits; a single successful scan does not establish permanent safety.","exercise":"Create a threat model for a client portal with invoices and uploaded documents. For each major asset, name the allowed identities, likely abuse and one concrete test/control.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is threat modelling more useful than blindly adding middleware?","a":"It identifies the actual assets, trust boundaries and abuse paths. Controls can then enforce relevant invariants instead of creating an impressive but incomplete list of packages."},{"q":"Does passing an OWASP checklist prove the application is secure?","a":"No. It improves coverage of common risk classes, but the real design, configuration, dependencies and changing behaviour still need analysis and tests."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a direct broken-access-control example?","options":["Tenant A can download tenant B's invoice by changing an ID","A page uses a different font","A static image loads slowly","A local variable is named total"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The caller is accessing an object outside the authorised relationship."},"skills":[187],"minutes":15},{"id":"security-injection","title":"SQL/NoSQL injection, XSS and CSP","body":"## Separate code structure from data\nSQL injection occurs when untrusted input changes query structure. Parameterised values protect that boundary; dynamic table/column/order choices need an allowlist or safe structural construction. An ORM can still be misused through unsafe raw queries.\n\nNoSQL queries also have structure and operators. Validate the expected field types and construct supported filters explicitly rather than accepting an arbitrary client object. Command execution adds another boundary: avoid invoking a shell for data arguments and restrict the executable/argument contract.\n\n## Render text as text\nCross-site scripting lets attacker-controlled content execute in a user's web context. Use safe text insertion or framework escaping by default. When rich HTML/Markdown is an intentional feature, use a maintained sanitiser with a deliberate policy and validate URL schemes. Do not execute model-generated or imported code merely because it appears in a lesson.\n\nA Content Security Policy can reduce the impact of selected injection paths, but it is not a replacement for correct encoding/sanitisation and it must match the real application. Avoid weakening the entire policy to silence one blocked resource without investigating it.\n\n## Verify behaviour\nTest an HTML-looking task title and an unsupported query shape in a controlled local test. The expected outcome is inert text or a validation error, not a page script or a widened database filter. Keep legitimate content working while closing the unsafe interpretation boundary.","exercise":"Review a task-title renderer, a raw SQL search and an imported Markdown preview. Specify safe handling for each and a regression test that demonstrates inert untrusted input.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is input filtering alone a weak universal XSS defence?","a":"The safe representation depends on the output context, and many legitimate characters are useful. Use context-appropriate encoding/safe APIs and a maintained sanitiser when HTML is intentionally supported."},{"q":"Can a CSP replace output sanitisation?","a":"No. It can constrain selected execution/resource behaviours, but application-safe rendering remains necessary. An overly broad or bypassable policy does not repair unsafe HTML construction."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the best default for rendering a user note?","options":["Insert it as text","Evaluate it as JavaScript","Treat every URL scheme as safe","Concatenate it into an event-handler attribute"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plain text avoids an unnecessary executable interpretation boundary."},"skills":[188,189],"minutes":15},{"id":"security-access","title":"CSRF, IDOR and multi-tenant isolation","body":"## The browser can send ambient credentials\nA session cookie may be attached to a request under browser policy even when a different site initiated the action. CSRF protection considers which state-changing requests can be induced and which tokens/origin/SameSite controls apply. Use framework-supported protection and do not change state through a supposedly safe GET endpoint.\n\nSameSite reduces some cross-site request scenarios, but it is not a universal replacement for analysing the session design. HttpOnly addresses script access to a cookie, not whether an authenticated request is authorised for a particular object.\n\n## IDOR is an object relationship failure\nAn opaque or random ID can reduce guessing but is not access control. Every invoice/task/download/search result needs an authorisation condition based on verified identity and the object's ownership/relationship. Enforce it in service/query boundaries so a second route cannot accidentally bypass a first route's check.\n\n## Tenant isolation includes derived paths\nScope cache keys, object-storage paths, background jobs, exports, search indexes and realtime rooms. A correctly filtered main list is insufficient if a PDF export or semantic search can retrieve another tenant's data.\n\nTest tenant A versus tenant B through read, update, delete, nested resources and direct file access. Verify error behaviour without disclosing unnecessary information about whether another tenant's object exists. Log denied sensitive operations without putting the protected data into the log.","exercise":"Write a tenant-isolation test matrix for tasks, invoices, uploads, search, exports and socket rooms. Explain why UUIDs alone do not satisfy any row of that matrix.","interviews":[{"q":"Why are random IDs not authorisation?","a":"A user can obtain an ID through sharing, logs or another leak. The server must verify the relationship between the caller and the object regardless of how guessable the identifier is."},{"q":"Where can tenant leaks occur beyond API routes?","a":"Shared caches, object storage, background jobs, search indexes, exports and realtime room membership can all cross the same boundary if tenant scope is missing."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is the correct basis for an invoice access decision?","options":["Verified identity plus the invoice's permitted ownership/relationship","Only whether its ID is hard to guess","Only whether the button was visible","Only whether the request used HTTPS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Transport and identifier design do not establish object-level authority."},"skills":[190,191,192],"minutes":15},{"id":"security-events","title":"Webhook replay, API limits and fail-safe errors","body":"## Verify, then apply a bounded operation\nA valid webhook signature authenticates the signed bytes under the provider contract. Replay protection also needs a stable event/operation identity and an idempotent state transition. Timestamp checks can help where the signing protocol supports them, but clocks and retry windows need a defined policy.\n\nValidate content type, body size, expected event type and relevant business identifiers. Preserve raw bytes for the signature operation. Reject invalid events without performing a partial side effect, and retain safe delivery evidence for investigation.\n\n## Limits protect several layers\nRate limits can apply by account, tenant, endpoint or another verified key. A login limit and a report-generation quota address different costs. Bound concurrency, request size, output size and execution time as well as request count.\n\nSecurity headers and CORS are additional controls with their own contracts. Do not mark an operation successful simply because one optional downstream call timed out. Fail safely: return a stable error or a clearly labelled reduced capability, release resources and preserve a retry/recovery path.\n\nFor a native local code runner, same-origin/token checks protect the API boundary, while the program still executes with the computer user's permissions. Calling it a sandbox without OS isolation would give the user a false safety model.","exercise":"Design a webhook handler that rejects an invalid signature, ignores a duplicate event safely and logs a bounded error if the database is unavailable. Add independent rate and size limits.","interviews":[{"q":"How do signature verification and replay protection differ?","a":"A signature checks the event content/origin under a signing scheme. Replay protection prevents a previously valid event from causing a duplicate or invalid business effect."},{"q":"Why limit output and concurrency as well as request count?","a":"One request can consume excessive memory, CPU or storage. Different resource dimensions need independent bounds and recovery behaviour."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which should an invalid webhook signature cause?","options":["No business side effect and a clear rejection","A paid flag update before verification","An unlimited retry loop","A success response with a hidden failure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Verification must precede the protected business transition."},"skills":[193,194],"minutes":15},{"id":"security-supply","title":"Dependency trust, lockfiles and secure maintenance","body":"## Dependencies become part of your application\nA package can execute during installation/build or at runtime. Review why it is needed, its origin/maintenance and the authority it receives. A lockfile records resolved versions but does not prove that those versions are safe.\n\nUse dependency audits and update tooling as inputs to a review process. Prioritise reachable risks and apply/test a suitable update. Avoid blindly disabling a finding or replacing an entire toolchain without understanding compatibility. Pin CI actions appropriately for the repository's trust policy and keep workflow permissions narrow.\n\n## Build provenance matters\nKnow which reviewed source and dependencies produced a deployed artefact. Keep release metadata and verify the published path. Untrusted pull-request code should not receive deployment secrets simply because it runs inside a familiar CI platform.\n\n## Maintenance is continuing work\nTrack runtime/framework support windows, model/provider deprecations and migration plans. Re-run meaningful tests after a dependency change and preserve a rollback path. A copied tutorial's old dependency versions are not automatically a suitable production baseline.\n\nKeep source licenses/notices when bundling external code and distinguish original teaching content from imported reference modules. Secret scanning can catch selected patterns, but it does not replace reviewing changed files and rotating credentials if a leak occurs.","exercise":"Create a dependency update checklist for a project: why the package exists, current risk, compatible version, relevant tests, license notices and deployment rollback.","interviews":[{"q":"What does a lockfile guarantee and not guarantee?","a":"It records a resolved dependency graph for reproducibility. It does not guarantee that every dependency is trustworthy, vulnerability-free or correctly configured."},{"q":"Why restrict CI secrets on untrusted changes?","a":"Build/test code can execute commands and read its environment. Giving unreviewed code privileged secrets can turn a routine pull-request check into credential exposure."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a useful dependency-update outcome?","options":["A reviewed compatible update with relevant tests and a rollback path","Ignoring every audit permanently","Publishing all CI secrets for debugging","Assuming a familiar package name proves its integrity"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Maintenance needs evidence of compatibility and controlled deployment, not only a version-number change."},"skills":[195],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"ai-foundations","title":"AI & Model APIs","group":"AI engineering","summary":"Use models as measured components with explicit input, output, cost and fallback contracts.","duration":"3-4 weeks after Python and APIs","project":"Build a report generator with validated output, a deterministic fallback and a small evaluation dataset.","sources":[{"title":"Claude API and prompt design","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"title":"scikit-learn user guide","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"},{"title":"NumPy fundamentals","url":"https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/basics.html"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"ai-ml","title":"AI, learning paradigms and a baseline model","body":"## Put the terms in a useful hierarchy\nArtificial intelligence is a broad field of computational systems performing tasks associated with intelligent behaviour. Machine learning fits patterns from data; deep learning uses multilayer neural models. Generative models produce outputs such as text or images. An application calling a model API is not thereby training a new model.\n\nSupervised learning uses labelled examples; unsupervised learning seeks structure without the same explicit target labels. Self-supervised learning creates prediction signals from data itself. Reinforcement learning concerns actions and reward feedback through an environment. These are learning setups, not promises of accuracy.\n\n## Training and inference\nTraining adjusts parameters using data and an objective. Inference uses a trained model to produce an output. A neural network can be understood as parameterised transformations whose loss guides updates; gradient-based optimisation changes weights to reduce an objective, not to guarantee that every generated statement is true.\n\n## Generalise, do not memorise the test\nSeparate training, validation and held-out test data according to the real deployment setting. For time-dependent or grouped data, a random row split can leak future or related information. Fit preprocessing only on the appropriate training data. Repeatedly tuning against the test set makes it part of the training process in practice.\n\nStart a product problem with a deterministic rule or simple classical ML baseline. If a labelled classifier and a few well-chosen features solve the problem reliably, an LLM may add unnecessary cost and unpredictability. Compare on the same representative held-out cases.","exercise":"Define a ticket-priority problem, a deterministic baseline and a possible supervised model. Specify labels, split strategy, leakage risks and the decision threshold used by the product.","interviews":[{"q":"What is overfitting?","a":"A model performs well on the patterns/noise of its training data but fails to generalise to new relevant examples. Evaluate on a properly separated dataset that matches the deployment setting."},{"q":"Why build a simple baseline before an LLM feature?","a":"It establishes whether the added complexity improves a real outcome. A cheaper deterministic or classical model can be easier to test, explain and operate for a bounded classification task."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is data leakage?","options":["Using future outcome information as a training feature","Holding out a test set correctly","Fitting preprocessing only on training data","Measuring a simple baseline"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The model receives information that would not be available at the real decision time."},"skills":[196,197,198,199,201],"minutes":15},{"id":"ai-metrics","title":"Confusion matrices, precision, recall and a scikit-learn pipeline","body":"## Count the errors that matter\nFor a binary positive class, true positives are correctly flagged positives, false positives are incorrect flags and false negatives are missed positives. Precision = TP/(TP+FP); recall = TP/(TP+FN). F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall when defined. Specify a policy for zero denominators rather than hiding undefined cases.\n\nAccuracy can mislead with an imbalanced class. If only one in a hundred events is positive, a system predicting negative for everything has high accuracy and zero positive recall. Choose metrics and thresholds based on the cost of misses versus false alarms.\n\n## A reproducible classical pipeline\nIn a local scikit-learn project, split data appropriately, combine preprocessing and estimator in a Pipeline, fit on training data, choose parameters/threshold using validation and report the held-out results. Use suitable cross-validation for the data structure. Keep feature construction, label definition and dataset version explicit.\n\nInspect error examples, not only one aggregate score. Report per-group or per-category coverage where relevant and verify that a model did not exploit a leaking identifier. Compare with the same baseline and test population.\n\nThe runnable example computes metrics with the standard library. scikit-learn itself is a separate local project dependency and is not downloaded by this offline lesson.","exercise":"For TP=12, FP=3 and FN=8, calculate precision, recall and F1. Then choose which metric matters most for a support escalation feature and explain the trade-off.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can accuracy be misleading?","a":"A dominant negative class can make an always-negative classifier appear accurate while missing every useful positive. Inspect class-specific precision/recall and the real costs of mistakes."},{"q":"Why put preprocessing inside a training pipeline?","a":"It keeps transformations fitted on the correct training folds and applied consistently to new data, reducing leakage and mismatched train/inference processing."}],"quiz":{"question":"With TP=8 and FP=2, what is precision?","options":["0.8","0.2","4","10"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Precision is TP/(TP+FP), so 8/(8+2)=0.8: eight of ten positive predictions are correct."},"skills":[200,202],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"tp, fp, fn = 12, 3, 8\nprecision = tp/(tp+fp)\nrecall = tp/(tp+fn)\nf1 = 2*precision*recall/(precision+recall)\nprint(round(precision, 3), round(recall, 3), round(f1, 3))","expected":"0.8 0.6 0.686","stdin":""}},{"id":"ai-llms","title":"Tokens, attention, generation and embeddings","body":"## Tokens are a model-specific unit\nA tokenizer splits input/output into units used by a model. Token count affects context capacity, latency and billing under the provider's contract; it is not always equal to word or character count. The context window is a bounded working input/output budget, not an unlimited reliable memory.\n\nTransformers use attention to combine information from token representations according to learned relationships. Pretraining builds broad predictive capabilities; instruction tuning and preference-based methods shape assistant behaviour. These processes do not turn generated text into a verified database lookup.\n\n## Generation is conditional prediction\nSampling controls such as temperature/top-p influence output selection. Lower randomness does not guarantee truth or perfect repeatability across providers, model revisions and infrastructure. Models can hallucinate, reflect stale knowledge or respond sensitively to prompt wording. Evaluate the actual task instead of treating confidence in wording as evidence.\n\n## Embeddings are vectors\nAn embedding maps input to a numeric representation useful for a chosen similarity task. Compare compatible vectors from the same intended model/space and dimension. Cosine similarity measures direction after accounting for magnitude; a dot product has different behaviour unless normalisation makes them equivalent for ranking.\n\nAn embedding similarity is not a probability that a statement is correct. Keep text, metadata and source identifiers so retrieval can return inspectable evidence instead of only a score.","exercise":"Compute cosine similarity for two small vectors and explain what a high score can and cannot prove. Design a context budget for a report that includes instructions, data and an output allowance.","interviews":[{"q":"Does temperature zero guarantee factual correctness?","a":"No. It changes sampling behaviour, not whether the model has reliable evidence. Validate outputs and use grounded retrieval or deterministic logic where correctness requires it."},{"q":"Why must embedding model/dimension be tracked?","a":"Vectors from incompatible spaces cannot be meaningfully mixed just because they are arrays. Indexes, stored vectors and query embeddings need a compatible representation and migration policy."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does a high embedding similarity establish?","options":["Similarity under that representation/metric","Guaranteed factual truth","Permission to reveal the document","A valid payment"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Similarity is a retrieval signal, not truth, authority or access control."},"skills":[203,204,205,206,207,208],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import math\na, b = [1, 0, 1], [1, 1, 0]\ndot = sum(x*y for x,y in zip(a,b))\nscore = dot/(math.sqrt(sum(x*x for x in a))*math.sqrt(sum(y*y for y in b)))\nprint(round(score, 3))","expected":"0.5","stdin":""}},{"id":"ai-providers","title":"Model selection, local models and a provider interface","body":"## Select against the task\nCompare candidate models using a representative evaluation set, latency distribution, output constraints and total cost. A small model may be sufficient for classification, while a harder synthesis task may justify a stronger model. Brand or size alone is not a task evaluation.\n\nOpen-weight models can run locally through tools such as Ollama when hardware, model size and licensing permit. Downloaded weights, memory requirements and capabilities are separate from this site's bundled Python engine. This app does not silently include a full local LLM.\n\n## Put a narrow interface around providers\nDefine an internal operation such as generate_report(input, budget) returning validated output plus usage/status metadata. Keep provider-specific message formats, retries and credentials inside an adapter. A thin abstraction is easier to maintain than pretending every model/provider has identical features.\n\nClaude/Groq and other services can differ in supported modalities, structured output and streaming/tool semantics. Keep API keys on the server. For images/documents, validate type/size and privacy policy before sending data outside the system; a model accepting a file does not imply the user intended every private file to leave.\n\n## Preserve a deterministic path\nIf generation fails or validation rejects the output, return a clearly labelled template based on verified counts. Keep the rest of the product working and record the provider failure safely. Do not quietly invent a successful model response.","exercise":"Design a provider-neutral report interface with a timeout, validated result, usage metadata and a fallback. Explain how you would compare a local model and an external API on the same cases.","interviews":[{"q":"What should a provider abstraction hide?","a":"Transport/message details and provider-specific integration should be localised. It should not hide meaningful capability limits, cost, errors or validation evidence from the product."},{"q":"Does local inference automatically make a feature private and safe?","a":"It can reduce external data transfer, but local storage, logs, permissions, model licensing and tool access still need a deliberate design."}],"quiz":{"question":"Where should a paid model API key be stored?","options":["In protected server-side configuration","In a public React environment variable","In a GitHub Pages HTML file","Inside a query parameter shared with users"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A static/browser bundle is public to its users and cannot keep a server secret."},"skills":[209,210,211,212,219,220],"minutes":15},{"id":"ai-prompts","title":"Prompt structure, schemas and prompt versioning","body":"## A prompt is an input contract\nState the task, relevant context, constraints and expected output. Give examples when they clarify edge cases, and delimit data so the model can distinguish it from instructions. Do not assume that a retrieved document or user-supplied text is trusted control logic.\n\n## Ask for a verifiable result\nFor a report, specify fields such as status, completed_count and evidence_ids. Use structured-output/schema support where available, then parse and validate the result at runtime. A syntactically valid JSON object can still contain incorrect values, nonexistent references or an impermissible action.\n\nDecide how to handle invalid output: reject, apply a bounded repair attempt or use a deterministic fallback. Do not loop indefinitely asking the same model to fix itself. Keep the original input/output and error category only within the permitted privacy/logging policy.\n\n## Change prompts like code\nStore a versioned template with the model/configuration and evaluation results. Review changes against a golden set containing ordinary, edge and adversarial cases. A prompt that improves a few demonstrations can regress important categories silently.\n\nKeep instructions specific without demanding unsupported certainty. If evidence is insufficient, the output should say so. Let deterministic code compute totals and enforce permissions; reserve the model for the part that benefits from language interpretation or generation.","exercise":"Write a project-report prompt whose numeric totals come from code and whose narrative cites task IDs. Define its output schema, validation rules and failure fallback.","interviews":[{"q":"Why validate after requesting JSON?","a":"Formatting constraints do not prove field values, reference IDs or business rules are correct. Runtime checks must verify the actual output before the application relies on it."},{"q":"Why version prompts?","a":"Prompt changes can alter behaviour like code changes. Versioning connects a deployed result to its template/model and allows comparison, regression testing and rollback."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which fact should deterministic code compute when possible?","options":["The exact count of completed tasks","A fabricated source citation","A guessed payment state","An unverified account owner"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Exact structured facts should come from authoritative data and validated computation."},"skills":[213,215,217],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"import json\ndef valid_report(text, total):\n    data = json.loads(text)\n    if set(data) != {\"completed\", \"summary\"}:\n        raise ValueError(\"unexpected fields\")\n    if type(data[\"completed\"]) is not int or not 0 <= data[\"completed\"] <= total:\n        raise ValueError(\"invalid completed count\")\n    if not isinstance(data[\"summary\"], str):\n        raise ValueError(\"summary must be text\")\n    return data\nprint(valid_report('{\"completed\":2,\"summary\":\"Two tasks finished.\"}', 3))","expected":"{'completed': 2, 'summary': 'Two tasks finished.'}","stdin":""}},{"id":"ai-streaming","title":"Streaming, token budgets and cancellation","body":"## Stream an operation, not an illusion of success\nA provider can emit incremental events/tokens. The server forwards an appropriate stream and the browser decodes/fragments it into a visible partial result. Show whether the answer is still generating, complete, stopped or failed. Receiving some text is not proof that a required structured result completed successfully.\n\n## A budget spans the whole operation\nCount prompt/context input and reserved output separately. Set maximum output, request deadline and per-tenant usage controls. Provider pricing and caching rules vary, so use current configuration/usage data instead of a permanent price copied from a tutorial.\n\nPrompt caching can help repeated supported input, but cache identity and privacy scope matter. Trimming context should preserve the task's necessary evidence, not arbitrarily discard the most inconvenient source. The smallest model that passes the task evaluation can be a good cost/latency choice.\n\n## Cancel through every boundary\nWhen the user stops, propagate abort/disconnect to the server/provider where supported and close resources. Preserve useful partial output with a stopped/partial label instead of claiming it is a final verified answer. Handle slow readers and proxy buffering with bounded queues.\n\nMeasure time to first useful output and time to a validated final result separately. A fast first token can coexist with a slow, expensive or ultimately invalid operation.","exercise":"Design a streaming report UI with Stop, partial-output preservation and a final validation step. Allocate a context/output budget and name the usage/cancellation signals to record.","interviews":[{"q":"Why measure more than time to first token?","a":"The user needs a useful and ultimately valid result. A fast opening token can hide long completion latency, high cost or a failed structured output."},{"q":"What should a stopped stream display?","a":"The retained partial content should be clearly labelled stopped/incomplete. The backend should cancel remaining work where possible and release resources."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is an appropriate final success condition?","options":["The required complete output passed validation","One token arrived","A spinner appeared","The provider returned any bytes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A complete validated outcome is different from the first partial stream event."},"skills":[214,218],"minutes":15},{"id":"ai-coding","title":"AI-assisted coding with specs, tests and review","body":"## Make the task reviewable\nGive a coding assistant the repository context, intended behaviour, constraints and acceptance criteria. Ask for a small coherent change and identify the relevant validation path. A vague request to make everything perfect provides little basis for reviewing the result.\n\n## Review generated code as code\nRead the diff, trace data ownership and trust boundaries, and test explicit cases. Look for plausible but nonexistent APIs, missing error paths, unbounded work, data loss and secret exposure. A confident explanation is not evidence that the implementation ran.\n\nUse tests to encode a real contract, not to repeat every implementation detail. Preserve a failing regression case when a bug is found. Check the actual built/deployed artefact where packaging or runtime behaviour matters.\n\n## Know when to constrain authority\nSecurity-sensitive logic, payment transitions, authentication and destructive data changes need careful human review and established components. Do not paste secrets or private data into a tool without an appropriate data-sharing decision. Respect source licenses when importing code.\n\nAI can help plan, explain, refactor and diagnose, but the engineer remains responsible for the final system's behaviour and evidence. A useful portfolio describes what was built, why the trade-offs were chosen and which checks actually passed, without inventing usage numbers or project features.","exercise":"Write acceptance criteria for autosaving a study note across reload and a storage quota failure. Review a hypothetical assistant change that catches every error and still displays Saved.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can generated tests still miss the bug?","a":"They may encode the same mistaken assumption as the generated implementation. Use explicit expected outcomes, independent examples and boundary cases tied to the user requirement."},{"q":"What evidence should accompany a coding change?","a":"A focused diff, the resulting behaviour, relevant tests/checks and meaningful limitations. For packaging/deployment changes, verify the actual produced or live artefact too."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is strongest evidence that autosave works?","options":["A draft survives an immediate reload and a meaningful failure case is handled","A comment says autosave is robust","The button is green","The assistant sounds confident"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Observable behaviour and failure handling are evidence; presentation alone is not."},"skills":[221,222,223,224],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"rag","title":"Retrieval & Evaluation","group":"AI engineering","summary":"Build source-grounded answers and measure retrieval and output quality before expanding features.","duration":"3-4 weeks after model API basics","project":"Build a small private-document search/report pipeline with citations, access filters and a versioned evaluation set.","sources":[{"title":"pgvector project documentation","url":"https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector"},{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/"},{"title":"Claude API and prompt design","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"title":"scikit-learn user guide","url":"https://scikit-learn.org/stable/user_guide.html"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"rag-ingestion","title":"Ingest, clean, chunk and retain source metadata","body":"## Retrieval starts before the model call\nA basic RAG pipeline ingests documents, extracts/cleans text, creates chunks, embeds/indexes them, retrieves candidates, optionally reranks, assembles context and generates a source-grounded answer. A failure in extraction or permissions cannot be repaired just by choosing a larger generation model.\n\n## Keep provenance\nFor PDFs, HTML and office files, preserve document ID, version, page/section, tenant/access metadata and extraction status. Tables and multi-column layouts need inspection: plain text extraction can scramble relationships. Keep a recoverable source link rather than storing only an anonymous paragraph.\n\n## Chunk by meaning\nPrefer a structure-aware boundary such as a section, paragraph or coherent table region. Token size and overlap trade context continuity against duplication and retrieval precision. Very small chunks can lose meaning; very large ones can waste context or mix unrelated subjects. Evaluate actual questions rather than declaring one universal chunk size.\n\nNormalise obvious extraction noise without destroying important headings, units or qualifiers. Treat embedded instructions in documents as data, not instructions for an agent. Reject or flag unreadable sources rather than silently pretending they were indexed correctly.\n\nBuild a small inspection tool that shows retrieved chunks beside their original location. It is often easier to improve answers by fixing a broken source/chunk than by adding another layer of prompt text.","exercise":"Design ingestion records for a PDF with tables and a DOCX with headings. Specify chunk IDs, source locators, tenant metadata and how you detect an extraction failure.","interviews":[{"q":"Why keep page/section metadata?","a":"It supports verifiable citations, debugging and updates. A user and evaluator need to trace an answer back to the relevant source rather than trusting an anonymous retrieved string."},{"q":"Why is there no universal best chunk size?","a":"Documents and tasks differ in structure, evidence length and query granularity. Size/overlap must be evaluated against actual retrieval and answer outcomes."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which ingestion output is most useful?","options":["Text plus source/version/location/access metadata","Only a vector with no source ID","Only a filename with no content","A claim that every PDF parsed perfectly"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Retrieval, updates, permissions and citations all need provenance."},"skills":[225,226,227],"minutes":15},{"id":"rag-search","title":"Vector indexes, hybrid search and reranking","body":"## Retrieve candidates under a representation\nA vector database/index stores embeddings with metadata and supports a similarity query. pgvector can fit a PostgreSQL-centred application; other systems may fit different scale/operation needs. Use the same compatible embedding representation for indexed and query vectors.\n\nCosine, dot product and distance metrics have different meanings and normalisation assumptions. Approximate nearest-neighbour indexes trade recall, latency and resource use. HNSW uses a graph-based search structure; IVF partitions candidate space and searches selected partitions. Index parameters and data distribution affect the actual result.\n\n## Lexical and semantic signals complement each other\nKeyword ranking such as BM25 can match exact identifiers or rare terms; embeddings can match related meanings. Hybrid search combines signals/ranks. Raw scores from unrelated systems are not automatically comparable; a rank-fusion method or calibrated combination is more deliberate than simply adding arbitrary numbers.\n\nA reranker can examine query/candidate pairs more deeply and reorder a smaller candidate set. It adds latency and cost, so measure the gained quality per operation. Keep the candidate stage broad enough that the right passage can reach the reranker.\n\nEvaluate recall and latency with real filters and permissions enabled. A benchmark over public unfiltered documents does not prove that private tenant-scoped queries work correctly.","exercise":"Compare keyword, vector and hybrid retrieval for an exact invoice number and a conceptual help question. Define candidate count, reranking budget and the recall/latency measurements.","interviews":[{"q":"Why might keyword search beat embeddings for an identifier?","a":"An exact rare code or invoice number is a strong lexical signal. Semantic similarity can blur those distinctions, so hybrid or exact matching may be important."},{"q":"Can a reranker recover a passage never retrieved?","a":"Not if it only sees the candidate set. Candidate recall constrains later ranking, so inspect retrieval failures before tuning a final generation prompt."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is an approximate vector index trade-off?","options":["Recall versus latency/resource use","Permission checking versus no permission checking","Truth versus a guaranteed false answer","Only text colour"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Approximate search parameters affect which neighbours are found and the work required."},"skills":[228,229,230,231],"minutes":15},{"id":"rag-grounding","title":"Context assembly, citations and access-aware retrieval","body":"## Context is a bounded evidence set\nChoose relevant passages, preserve their source identifiers and fit them into the token budget with enough surrounding meaning. Duplicated or contradictory chunks waste space and can confuse the output. Ordering can affect whether important evidence is used, so test the actual assembly policy.\n\n## Ground claims in inspectable sources\nAsk the system to cite source IDs/locations that exist in the retrieved context. Validate those references and check that the cited passage actually supports the claim. A syntactically valid citation can still be irrelevant. When evidence is missing, the answer should say so rather than invent a source.\n\n## Apply permissions before exposure\nRetrieval must use the caller's permitted tenant/documents and enforce access in the actual data path. Filtering only the final answer is too late if private text was already sent to a provider or logged. Cache keys, reranker inputs and context assembly must preserve the same access scope.\n\nUse a stable public reference format rather than leaking an internal filesystem path unnecessarily. Document redaction and provider-data handling belong to the system design, not to a hopeful prompt line saying keep it private.\n\nTest with two tenants containing similar document titles and with a question that has no supported answer. Verify both the retrieval set and the generated output; a correct-looking answer can conceal an access leak in the context.","exercise":"Design an answer record with text, evidence IDs and an insufficient-evidence status. Add a cross-tenant retrieval test and a citation-support test.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is filtering only the final answer insufficient?","a":"Private content may already have reached retrieval logs, a reranker or an external model. Access scope must be enforced before those exposures, not only at display time."},{"q":"Does a valid-looking citation prove support?","a":"No. The reference may exist but not support the associated claim. Validate both source identity and the relationship between the passage and the claim."}],"quiz":{"question":"When should document access filtering happen?","options":["Before private content is retrieved/exposed downstream","Only after the model writes an answer","Only when a user complains","Only in a CSS rule"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Permissions must protect the actual retrieval and downstream data path."},"skills":[232,233,234],"minutes":15},{"id":"rag-freshness","title":"Index updates, query rewriting and advanced trade-offs","body":"## An index has a version and freshness policy\nWhen a source changes, identify affected chunks, remove stale ones and index the new version. A deleted or access-revoked document must stop being retrievable according to the product's policy. Changing embedding models can require re-embedding and a new compatible index; do not mix dimensions/spaces accidentally.\n\n## Query transformation\nRewriting, expansion or decomposition can improve a difficult query, but it can also change intent or expose more data. Retain the original question and evaluate the transformed retrieval. Bound the number of subqueries and downstream calls.\n\n## Choose extra machinery from evidence\nGraph-based retrieval can help with explicit relationships; agentic retrieval can choose multiple search steps; long context can avoid some chunk selection for suitable bounded corpora. Each adds different costs, failure modes and access-control obligations. A long context window does not guarantee reliable use of every passage.\n\nMeasure the baseline first. If failure comes from stale documents, broken extraction or missing keywords, adding an agent loop may make the system slower without fixing the cause. Keep a rollback path to a simpler retrieval policy.\n\nTrack document, chunker, embedding model, index and prompt versions together. An answer should be diagnosable against the data/index state used at that time, within the allowed retention/privacy policy.","exercise":"Design reindexing after a document edit, deletion and embedding-model switch. Compare a simple hybrid retriever with a multi-step agent for three representative questions.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can an embedding-model change require reindexing?","a":"The new representation can have a different dimension or semantic space. Query and stored vectors must remain compatible; version and migrate them deliberately."},{"q":"Why preserve the original query after rewriting?","a":"It lets you detect intent drift and diagnose whether transformation improved retrieval or introduced a different question. The original user request remains the task boundary."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is the first fix for an answer based on a deleted document?","options":["Repair deletion/freshness propagation and access checks","Add more prompt adjectives only","Increase output temperature","Hide all source links"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The underlying stale retrieval must be corrected rather than disguised."},"skills":[235,236,237],"minutes":15},{"id":"eval-methods","title":"Golden datasets, retrieval metrics and answer rubrics","body":"## Define success before adding features\nA golden dataset contains representative questions, expected evidence/outcome, edge cases and adversarial inputs. Version it and keep the held-out evaluation policy clear. A tiny set of impressive demos does not establish broad reliability.\n\n## Retrieval and answer metrics differ\nRecall@k asks what fraction of known relevant items appears in the top k. Precision@k asks what fraction of the top k is relevant under the chosen fixed-k convention. Reciprocal rank is 1/rank of the first relevant result, or zero if none; MRR averages that quantity across queries. These require relevance labels and do not directly measure whether a generated answer is faithful.\n\nFor answers, define faithfulness to evidence, relevance to the question and completeness of required points. Exact match fits strict outputs, while a rubric fits acceptable paraphrases. An LLM judge can help scale scoring but has biases, prompt sensitivity and potential disagreement with humans.\n\n## Inspect error categories\nSeparate missing evidence, poor ranking, unsupported claims, wrong arithmetic, invalid format and access leaks. A single average score can hide a catastrophic failure category. Use deterministic checks for schema, IDs and arithmetic whenever possible.\n\nCompare versions on the same dataset and budgets. Report sample size and uncertainty rather than claiming that a perfect score on ten examples proves production correctness.","exercise":"Create ten questions for a small document collection with relevance labels. Compute recall@3 and reciprocal rank for one query, then write a three-point answer-faithfulness rubric.","interviews":[{"q":"Why separate retrieval recall from answer quality?","a":"The retriever may find the right passage while the generator ignores or misstates it, or the generator may guess a correct answer without valid evidence. The stages need distinct diagnostics."},{"q":"What is a limitation of LLM-as-judge?","a":"It can prefer style, share model biases, vary with prompts and disagree with humans. Calibrate it against labelled examples and use deterministic checks where possible."}],"quiz":{"question":"If the first relevant result is at rank 4, what is reciprocal rank?","options":["0.25","4","1","0.75"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reciprocal rank is 1 divided by the first relevant rank."},"skills":[238,239,240,241,242],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"relevant = {\"b\", \"d\"}\nretrieved = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\nrecall = len(set(retrieved) & relevant)/len(relevant)\nrr = next((1/(i+1) for i,item in enumerate(retrieved) if item in relevant), 0)\nprint(recall, rr)","expected":"0.5 0.5","stdin":""}},{"id":"eval-operations","title":"Human review, regression gates and feedback loops","body":"## Calibrate automated scores\nSample outputs for human review across ordinary, rare and high-risk categories. Use a written rubric, compare reviewer disagreement and revise ambiguous labels. A judge score is useful only when it correlates with the decisions the product actually needs.\n\n## Put evaluations in the release path\nRun a suitable fixed suite when prompts, models, retrieval/index policies or tool contracts change. Gate on important categories, not only an average. Keep cost/latency budgets beside quality thresholds, and investigate a regression before silently lowering a threshold.\n\n## Learn from production carefully\nUser corrections, negative feedback and escalations can identify new cases. Validate them, protect private data and add representative examples to the dataset under a controlled process. Feedback volume is not automatically an unbiased quality measure because some users/categories report more than others.\n\nTools such as Langfuse, LangSmith, Ragas or promptfoo can help collect traces or run evaluation workflows. A small local harness can also be sufficient at first. Choose tooling after the dataset, rubric and release decision are clear.\n\nKeep versioned records of prompts/models/indexes and the suite result associated with a release. When a model is deprecated or swapped, rerun the relevant suite and compare actual outcomes. A new model label does not establish that your application improved.","exercise":"Define a CI gate with one access-leak must-pass category, a faithfulness threshold and a latency budget. Specify how one real user correction becomes a privacy-safe regression example.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can an average quality gate be dangerous?","a":"An improvement on easy cases can hide a severe regression on a small high-risk category. Keep category-specific requirements for failures such as data exposure or unsupported payment actions."},{"q":"When is a small evaluation harness enough?","a":"When it can reproducibly run the representative cases, apply meaningful checks and support the release decision. Tooling should serve that contract rather than replace it."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should a model migration trigger?","options":["Re-run task-specific evaluations and compare budgets","Assume the newest label is always better","Delete all old failure cases","Disable access tests to save time"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The application's real quality/cost/latency contract must be verified after a model change."},"skills":[243,244,245,246],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"agents","title":"Agents & AI Products","group":"AI engineering","summary":"Build bounded tool workflows, safe authority boundaries and observable user-facing AI features.","duration":"3-4 weeks after retrieval/evaluation","project":"Build a read-only project assistant first, then add one validated action with review, audit and cancellation.","sources":[{"title":"Claude API and prompt design","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"},{"title":"Model Context Protocol introduction","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/getting-started/intro"},{"title":"OWASP Cheat Sheet Series","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"agent-loop","title":"Workflows, tools, planning and memory","body":"## A tool loop is an application protocol\nThe model proposes a tool call, deterministic code validates/executes an allowed operation and the result returns as data for the next step. The loop needs explicit completion, maximum steps, time and cost limits. A tool call string is not authority to execute any arbitrary command.\n\nUse a fixed workflow when the required steps are known. An agent can be useful when selecting the next bounded step genuinely depends on intermediate results. More autonomy also increases the number of possible failure paths, so justify it against a simpler baseline.\n\n## Design narrow tools\nA get_project_summary tool is easier to validate and authorise than a generic run_anything tool. Define input schemas, output/error shapes and idempotency for side effects. Derive tenant/user context outside model-controlled arguments and enforce it during execution.\n\nConversation state, short summaries and long-term stores have different lifecycles. A summary can omit facts; a stored memory can become stale or contain untrusted content. Keep source/version/access metadata and let the user correct relevant state.\n\nPlanning can decompose a task and revise after a failure, but a plan remains a proposal bounded by the user request and tool permissions. Frameworks such as LangGraph or LlamaIndex can organise state, yet plain code may be simpler for a small controlled workflow.","exercise":"Design a read-only project assistant with three tools, structured errors, a five-step limit and a final evidence-based answer. Compare it with a fixed report pipeline.","interviews":[{"q":"When is a fixed workflow preferable to an agent?","a":"When the steps and decision rules are already known. A deterministic pipeline is easier to test, bound and debug; use an agent when dynamic choice provides measurable value."},{"q":"Why make tools narrow?","a":"A small contract reduces ambiguous inputs and excessive authority. It lets deterministic code validate permissions, resource limits and expected outcomes for each operation."}],"quiz":{"question":"Who must enforce a tool's tenant permission?","options":["The deterministic tool/server boundary","Only the model's polite wording","Only the frontend icon","An unverified tenant argument supplied by the model"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Model-controlled arguments cannot establish authority over private data."},"skills":[216,247,248,249,250,254,255],"minutes":15},{"id":"agent-mcp","title":"MCP, human review and bounded authority","body":"## Standardise the connection, not the permission decision\nModel Context Protocol provides a standard way for clients/hosts to connect with tool/data capabilities through compatible servers. It can reduce custom integration work, but installing or discovering a server does not automatically authorise every action it exposes.\n\nReview the server's origin, requested permissions, authentication and data flow. A tool description is a contract to inspect, not an instruction that outranks the user. Apply the same tenant/object controls as any other API and validate returned data.\n\n## Separate preparation from consequence\nA system can prepare a draft invoice, show the exact recipient/amount/content and require the appropriate human review before a consequential send/payment/deletion. The review should concern a concrete proposed action, not an abstract request to trust the agent forever.\n\n## Bound the run\nSet step/time/token limits, restrict accessible tools/resources and provide Stop. Side-effect tools need suitable isolation, idempotency and an audit trail. A kill switch must stop further actions, while already committed external effects require their own recovery process.\n\nUse a read-only capability first and test adversarial tool results. Add write authority only when its validation, review and rollback/reconciliation policy are clear. A protocol's existence does not make arbitrary remote instructions or plugins safe.","exercise":"Design a draft-and-review invoice workflow through a narrow tool interface. Specify what is shown for review, which permissions are checked and what Stop can and cannot undo.","interviews":[{"q":"Does MCP make every connected tool trusted?","a":"No. It standardises interaction, while trust, authentication, permissions and validation remain responsibilities of the host/application and server design."},{"q":"What should a useful approval screen show?","a":"The exact consequential action, target, relevant content/amount and expected effect. A person needs a concrete reviewable proposal, not a vague request for unlimited future authority."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a meaningful agent run limit?","options":["A bounded step/time/cost budget with a stop path","An unlimited loop until the model feels finished","A hidden global credential","A claim that tools never fail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Explicit limits bound cost and side effects when a loop fails to converge."},"skills":[251,252,253],"minutes":15},{"id":"agent-security","title":"Prompt injection, SSRF, output safety and audits","body":"## Tool outputs and documents are untrusted data\nAn attacker can place instructions in a webpage, retrieved file or tool result. That is indirect prompt injection when the system confuses those instructions with the user's authorised task. Preserve the hierarchy between trusted control instructions and external content; a quotation in a document is not a new user command.\n\n## Protect side-effect boundaries\nValidate tool arguments independently of model text. A URL-fetching tool needs an allowed destination/network policy to reduce SSRF risks such as reaching internal services. A file tool needs scoped paths. A payment/message tool needs object authority and the appropriate review/idempotency policy.\n\nNever render or execute model output as trusted HTML, SQL or shell code by default. Sanitize intended rich content, parameterise data operations and keep execution an explicit bounded capability.\n\n## Data and audit policy\nKnow which prompts/documents leave the system, how providers retain them and what personal data should be redacted. An audit trail can record request identity, model/prompt version, retrieved source IDs, proposed/executed tool calls and outcomes, with access and retention controls.\n\nBias can enter through training data, the retrieval corpus, labels and evaluation sampling. Review error distribution across relevant groups/tasks and avoid claiming fairness from one aggregate metric. Security and fairness reviews need actual system evidence, not a reassuring prompt sentence.","exercise":"Threat-model a research assistant that can fetch URLs and draft messages. Include a malicious webpage instruction, an internal-network URL, unsafe Markdown and private data in logs.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is a document telling an agent to ignore the user not authoritative?","a":"It is external task data controlled by another party, not a trusted instruction source. The system must keep it from changing tool authority or the user's intended objective."},{"q":"What is SSRF in an agent tool context?","a":"A tool can be induced to fetch destinations the attacker cannot access directly, such as internal services. Destination/network validation and a narrow fetch capability are needed outside the model."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should happen to model-generated HTML intended for display?","options":["Treat it as untrusted and apply a suitable rendering policy","Execute every embedded script","Grant it server credentials","Use it to redefine tool permissions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Generated content crosses an untrusted output boundary even when it looks well formatted."},"skills":[256,257,258,259,260,261],"minutes":15},{"id":"agent-interface","title":"Streaming chat, trust UX and multimodal input","body":"## Make the system's state visible\nAn assistant UI needs generating, stopped, failed and completed states; conversation history; and a useful retry/regenerate policy. Preserve drafts and partial output. A Stop action should propagate cancellation, not only hide a spinner.\n\nFrontend AI SDKs can help manage streaming hooks and tool-call rendering. The application still owns validation, access, error handling and the distinction between a proposed and executed action. A tool card should show a meaningful operation/outcome rather than expose private implementation details unnecessarily.\n\n## Perceived latency and trust\nSkeletons and partial results can reduce uncertainty while waiting. Do not use optimistic UI to claim a payment, message send or validated final answer before it happened. Show sources where they support verification, allow corrections and use edit-before-send for consequential generated content.\n\nGenerative UI can select from a constrained component schema rather than emitting arbitrary executable page code. Validate component names/props and preserve accessibility. A model choosing a chart does not remove the need for correct units and a data alternative.\n\nVoice/image inputs introduce capture permissions, privacy and unsupported-format cases. Use explicit controls, indicate recording/upload state and handle failure without discarding the user's remaining text. A speech transcript can be wrong and should be correctable before a consequential action.","exercise":"Sketch a chat/report interface with Stop, partial text, source cards and an editable draft before sending. Define a safe schema for one generated chart component.","interviews":[{"q":"Why distinguish a proposed tool action from an executed one?","a":"Users need to know what the system intends versus what has actually happened. Confusing them can create false confidence about a send, payment or data change."},{"q":"How can generative UI remain bounded?","a":"Let the model select among known components with validated props. Keep arbitrary script/HTML execution out of the rendering contract and test accessible states."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a trustworthy streaming state label?","options":["Stopped · partial answer retained","Paid successfully before confirmation","All sources verified because one token arrived","Sent while only a draft exists"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The label should describe the actual completed/partial state."},"skills":[262,263,264,265,266,267],"minutes":15},{"id":"agent-production","title":"Queues, fallback, per-tenant budgets and tracing","body":"## Place AI where its latency fits\nA quick bounded classification may fit a request; a long report may belong in a durable queue. Return a job ID and observable state when completion outlives the request. Define idempotency, cancellation and retry behaviour for each stage.\n\n## Keep the non-AI product functional\nIf the model/provider is down, preserve core CRUD/payment/task workflows and offer a clear deterministic fallback for optional summaries. A fallback should not fabricate evidence or hide that a capability was reduced.\n\n## Cost controls are resource controls\nLimit input size, output tokens, concurrent jobs and per-tenant usage. Cache only under a correct permission/freshness key. Trim prompts deliberately and choose a smaller model when it passes the required evaluation, rather than changing models solely from a headline price.\n\n## Trace the operation\nRecord safe metadata such as prompt/model/index version, tokens, stage latency, retries and outcome. Correlate browser request, queue job, retrieval and provider call. Apply redaction/retention rules so observability does not become a private-document archive by accident.\n\nTest an outage, a slow reader, an over-budget tenant and a cancelled job. Observe whether queue age, resource use and recovery remain bounded. A healthy provider benchmark does not prove production behaviour when dependencies fail.","exercise":"Define a queued weekly report with per-tenant limits, a deterministic outage response and a trace spanning retrieval/provider/output validation. State what metadata is logged and what content is excluded.","interviews":[{"q":"Why separate a long report from the request path?","a":"A durable job lifecycle can survive request timeouts and provide explicit progress/retry/cancellation. It avoids relying on a short-lived HTTP context to own lengthy work."},{"q":"What should AI observability avoid?","a":"Unnecessary secrets, private documents and personal data in broadly accessible logs. Capture useful operational metadata with scoped access and retention instead of logging everything by default."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a good per-tenant control?","options":["Bounded token/job/concurrency budgets","One unlimited shared provider key exposed to browsers","No accounting until the bill arrives","Dropping access checks to reduce latency"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Independent quotas constrain cost and resource impact without sacrificing data boundaries."},"skills":[268,269,270,271],"minutes":15},{"id":"agent-lifecycle","title":"Model migrations, routing, semantic caches and load tests","body":"## Version the whole behaviour\nA deployed AI feature depends on prompt, model, retrieval/index and tool/schema versions. Record them together with evaluation results and a changelog. When a provider deprecates a model, plan a migration and rerun the actual task suite before switching traffic.\n\n## Route from evidence\nA smaller model can handle easier cases and escalate under a defined policy. A model's self-reported confidence is not automatically calibrated. Evaluate the routing rule, error cases and escalation cost as a system, not just the strongest model alone.\n\n## Semantic caching has correctness risks\nEquivalent-looking questions can differ in tenant, permissions, date, source version or a critical numeric detail. Include the appropriate scope/freshness context and choose a conservative reuse policy. Measure wrong hits as well as hit rate; a high hit rate is not a success if answers are stale or leaked.\n\n## Load the complete dependency graph\nTen times the user traffic can multiply retrieval, reranking, model and queue work. Include token lengths, slow-provider responses, retries, concurrent streams and cancellation. Watch p95/p99 latency, queue age, memory, connection limits and spending.\n\nKeep a fallback/rollback plan that remains compatible with stored data and running jobs. A model upgrade is a product release with measurable quality, latency, cost and safety consequences, not merely a string replacement in configuration.","exercise":"Plan a model migration with a shadow/canary comparison and rollback. Define semantic-cache exclusion cases and a load scenario that includes retries plus long responses.","interviews":[{"q":"Why can a semantic-cache hit be wrong?","a":"The wording may be similar while permissions, time, source version or a numeric detail differ. Cache equivalence must preserve the task's relevant context, not only embedding similarity."},{"q":"What must be evaluated in model routing?","a":"The combined routing/escalation policy, including missed hard cases, latency, cost and category-specific errors. Evaluating only the strongest fallback model misses routing mistakes."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should accompany a model identifier change?","options":["Task evaluations, budget comparison and rollback evidence","A claim that all newer models are better","Deletion of the old golden dataset","Removal of source versioning"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The deployed system must still satisfy its own quality/cost/latency contract."},"skills":[272,273,274,275,276],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"dsa","title":"DSA with Python","group":"Placement foundations","summary":"Learn invariants and patterns, then solve explicit judged tasks instead of memorising a solution list.","duration":"Foundation: 2-3 months; deeper practice is ongoing","project":"Keep a topic notebook with a brute-force approach, improved invariant, complexity and regression cases for every solved pattern.","sources":[{"title":"MIT introduction to algorithms","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/resources/lecture-notes/"},{"title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"dsa-complexity","title":"Big-O, input contracts and a solving method","body":"## Complexity describes growth\nTime complexity counts how work grows with input size; space complexity counts relevant storage growth. Big-O is an asymptotic upper bound, not an exact runtime in milliseconds. State whether the analysis is worst-case, expected or amortised, and whether output/input storage is included.\n\nA scan is O(n), a pair of nested full scans is O(n²), binary search is O(log n) under its ordering/random-access assumptions and comparison sorting commonly needs O(n log n). Two consecutive scans add to O(n), while a loop that halves a search region is not linear merely because it contains a loop.\n\n## Solve from a contract\n1. Restate input/output, indexing, empty cases and numeric limits.\n2. Produce a correct small brute-force method and explicit examples.\n3. Identify repeated work or structure: sorting, a monotone predicate, frequencies, overlapping subproblems or graph connectivity.\n4. State an invariant for the improved method and trace a boundary case.\n5. Analyse time/space and test against a small independent oracle.\n\nPython can express most interview algorithms clearly. Language runtime and constants still matter under a particular time limit, so do not assume one language is universally required or sufficient for every platform.\n\nThe existing PDF roadmap targets are practice suggestions, not a claim that every company asks the same stages/questions. Use the staged judged tasks and reference library as resources while building actual topic coverage.","exercise":"Compare an O(n²) pair-sum scan with a hash-based solution. State the duplicate/index policy, expected versus worst-case assumptions and explicit empty/no-answer cases.","interviews":[{"q":"How do expected and worst-case complexity differ?","a":"Expected complexity depends on a distribution/randomness or data-structure assumptions; worst-case bounds the most expensive valid input. State the assumption instead of labelling every hash lookup universally O(1)."},{"q":"Why keep a brute-force solution?","a":"It supplies a simple correctness baseline for small inputs and can serve as an independent oracle when testing the more complex optimised implementation."}],"quiz":{"question":"Two separate O(n) scans have which total asymptotic time?","options":["O(n)","O(n²)","O(log n)","O(1)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Adding 2n linear work still grows linearly after constant factors are ignored."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"def pair_sum(nums, target):\n    seen = set()\n    for value in nums:\n        if target-value in seen:\n            return True\n        seen.add(value)\n    return False\nprint(pair_sum([2, 7, 11], 9))\nprint(pair_sum([], 9))","expected":"True\nFalse","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-search-sort","title":"Linear/binary search and five sorting methods","body":"## Search the right structure\nLinear search inspects candidates one by one. Binary search repeatedly discards half an ordered search space or uses a monotone predicate. Define a half-open or closed interval invariant and update it consistently; many errors come from mixing conventions.\n\nFor lower_bound, keep `[left,right)` with every index before left known smaller than the target and every index from right known not smaller. On termination, left is the first candidate position. A binary search on an answer needs a proved monotone feasibility predicate, not merely a numeric input.\n\n## Sorting comparison\n| Method | Typical/worst teaching bound | Key idea |\n|---|---|---|\n| Bubble | O(n²), with possible early exit | Swap adjacent inversions |\n| Selection | O(n²) | Select the next minimum |\n| Insertion | O(n²), useful on nearly sorted small data | Insert into a sorted prefix |\n| Merge | O(n log n), extra merge storage | Sort halves and merge |\n| Quick | Average O(n log n), worst O(n²) | Partition around a pivot |\n\nPython's sort/sorted provide a stable library sort; a key function maps values to comparison keys. Stable means equal keys preserve relative order. In-place mutation and extra space are separate properties from stability.\n\nCounting inversions extends merge sort: when a right-half value is taken before remaining larger left values, count those cross inversions. Keep the original comparison contract clear for equal values.","exercise":"Implement lower_bound and test empty input, repeated targets and insertion at either end. Then count inversions with both a pair scan and a merge-based method on small random arrays.","interviews":[{"q":"What does a stable sort preserve?","a":"The relative order of items with equal sort keys. It does not mean the sort uses constant memory or that it is always the fastest choice."},{"q":"What must be true for binary search on an answer?","a":"The feasibility predicate must be monotone over the search domain, with defined boundary behaviour. Without that property, discarding half the domain can remove a valid answer."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does lower_bound return?","options":["The first position whose value is not less than the target","Always the last equal element","The array maximum","Only an existing exact match"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is an insertion/candidate position, which may equal the array length."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"from bisect import bisect_left\nnums = [1, 2, 2, 5]\nprint(bisect_left(nums, 2))\nprint(bisect_left(nums, 4))","expected":"1\n3","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-array-patterns","title":"Hashing, prefix sums, two pointers and windows","body":"## Match the invariant to the input\nA frequency map avoids repeated scans for counts or complements. A prefix sum turns a range sum into a difference of cumulative totals; seed the empty prefix correctly. For counting subarrays with a target sum, count earlier prefix frequencies instead of assuming a positive-only sliding window.\n\nTwo pointers can move inward on a sorted pair-sum problem or maintain a read/write boundary for in-place compaction. Explain why moving one pointer cannot discard a needed answer. Sorting first may change original indices, so preserve them when output requires original positions.\n\n## Sliding windows\nA fixed-size window updates by adding one value and removing the outgoing value. A variable window needs a condition under which extending/shrinking changes validity predictably. Negative numbers break several sum-based monotonic assumptions. A longest-substring-without-repeats window tracks the last occurrence/frequency and never moves its left boundary backwards.\n\nPrefix XOR uses an analogous relation for XOR-range problems, but do not substitute arithmetic sum reasoning blindly. For exact pattern search, KMP keeps a prefix-function state so mismatches avoid rescanning already understood characters; overlapping matches require falling back to the proper prefix after a match.\n\nTest duplicates, all-zero values, negative values where allowed, empty strings and overlapping patterns. Write the window's valid state before writing its update order.","exercise":"Solve target-sum subarray counting with a frequency map and compare with a brute-force oracle. Give a counterexample to a positive-only sum window when negatives are allowed.","interviews":[{"q":"Why seed a prefix frequency with zero once?","a":"It represents the empty prefix before the array. A subarray beginning at index zero is counted by matching the current prefix against that initial zero."},{"q":"When can a sliding-window sum method fail?","a":"When its validity is not monotone under extension/shrinking, such as several target-sum problems with negative values. Choose a prefix-map or another method that matches the actual contract."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which method naturally handles negative values for counting subarrays with sum k?","options":["Prefix sums with a frequency map","Always shrink only when the sum exceeds k","Sort the array and ignore positions","Use only the maximum element"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Earlier prefix differences identify valid contiguous sums without a positive-only monotonicity assumption."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"nums, k = [1, -1, 0], 0\nfreq, prefix, answer = {0:1}, 0, 0\nfor value in nums:\n    prefix += value\n    answer += freq.get(prefix-k, 0)\n    freq[prefix] = freq.get(prefix, 0)+1\nprint(answer)","expected":"3","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-linear-structures","title":"Linked lists, stacks, queues, deques and heaps","body":"## Choose operations and ownership\nA singly linked node points to a next node; a doubly linked node also tracks a previous link. Reversal must save the next link before overwriting it. A dummy node can simplify head deletion/merge cases. Floyd's slow/fast pointers detect a cycle under the linked traversal model; finding its entry needs the second phase, not only the meeting point.\n\nA stack is LIFO and supports nested matching, DFS and monotonic patterns. A queue is FIFO and supports BFS. Use collections.deque for efficient end operations instead of repeated list.pop(0). A monotonic stack/deque discards dominated candidates while preserving the invariant needed by later queries.\n\n## Heaps order candidates\nPython heapq is a min-heap interface. Keep a size-k heap for selected streaming top-k tasks or merge sorted streams by the next available element. A heap is not a fully sorted list, and removing an arbitrary value is not automatically constant time.\n\nFor streaming median, balance a max-oriented lower half and min-oriented upper half with explicit size/order invariants. For sliding-window maximum, store indices in a decreasing deque so expired values can be removed by position.\n\nTrace empty/single-node lists, deletion at the head, repeated equal values and an expired maximum. Separate object identity from equality when reasoning about a cycle.","exercise":"Reverse a linked list with three pointers, then solve next-greater-element using a monotonic stack. Explain why each element is pushed/popped only a bounded number of times.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does a monotonic stack often give linear time?","a":"Although one iteration can pop several values, each value is pushed once and popped at most once. The total number of stack operations across the full scan is linear."},{"q":"Why store indices rather than only values in a sliding-window deque?","a":"Indices identify expiry and preserve the relationship to positions, including duplicates. Values alone cannot always distinguish an old equal maximum from a current one."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the usual Python queue choice for BFS?","options":["collections.deque","Repeated list.pop(0)","A sorted copy on every step","A string with concatenated node IDs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"deque supports efficient appends and removals at opposite ends."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"from collections import deque\nqueue = deque([1, 2])\nqueue.append(3)\nprint(queue.popleft())\nprint(list(queue))","expected":"1\n[2, 3]","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-trees","title":"Trees, BSTs, tries and ancestor queries","body":"## A traversal defines processing order\nDFS traversals of a binary tree include preorder (node/left/right), inorder (left/node/right) and postorder (left/right/node). BFS visits levels using a queue. Define whether height/diameter counts edges or nodes before implementing it; a one-node tree exposes that convention immediately.\n\nA strict BST has all keys in a left subtree below the node and all in a right subtree above it. Checking only immediate children is insufficient; propagate allowed bounds. Deletion and duplicate policies need explicit contracts.\n\n## Common tree patterns\nPostorder can return a useful summary, such as subtree height, while updating a global/returned diameter. LCA reasoning depends on whether the tree is a general binary tree, a BST or a rooted parent array. Binary lifting precomputes 2^j ancestors to answer kth-ancestor queries in logarithmic steps.\n\nA trie stores prefixes along character edges and supports prefix membership/counts with a clear terminal-marker/count policy. Empty words, duplicate insertions and deletion counts need explicit handling.\n\nRecursion depth can exceed a runtime limit on a long chain. An iterative stack can be appropriate even when the recursive invariant is easiest to explain. Test a balanced tree, a chain, missing children, repeated labels where allowed and a query above the root.","exercise":"Explain why checking only each BST node's direct children fails, then create a counterexample. Build a small ancestor table and answer k=0, k=1 and above-root queries.","interviews":[{"q":"Why do BST validation bounds need to come from ancestors?","a":"A node can satisfy its parent comparison while violating a higher ancestor's range. Propagating lower/upper bounds enforces the property across the entire subtree."},{"q":"How does binary lifting help ancestor queries?","a":"It precomputes jumps of lengths 1,2,4,... and decomposes k into binary bits. Queries then follow only logarithmically many jumps, with a defined missing-ancestor value."}],"quiz":{"question":"For a diameter measured in edges, what is a single-node tree's diameter?","options":["0","1","2","Undefined for every valid tree"],"answer":0,"explanation":"There are no edges on its longest path."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"def inorder(tree):\n    if tree is None:\n        return []\n    value, left, right = tree\n    return inorder(left) + [value] + inorder(right)\nprint(inorder((2, (1,None,None), (3,None,None))))","expected":"[1, 2, 3]","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-graphs","title":"BFS, DFS, shortest paths, DSU and graph structure","body":"## State the graph contract\nDefine directed/undirected edges, weights, self-loops, parallel edges and unreachable vertices. An adjacency list is often appropriate for sparse graphs. Mark visited at the correct point so cycles do not create unbounded repeated work.\n\nBFS finds shortest edge-count paths in an unweighted graph. DFS explores components and supports ordering/low-link reasoning. Dijkstra assumes nonnegative weights; Bellman-Ford can handle negative edges and detect a reachable negative cycle. Floyd-Warshall considers intermediate vertices for all-pairs shortest paths and needs a separate policy for negative cycles.\n\n## Other useful structures\nDSU maintains connectivity through representative roots, usually with path compression and size/rank heuristics. Topological sorting applies to directed acyclic graphs; a cycle means no valid full order. A lexicographically smallest topological order needs an ordered candidate structure.\n\nStrongly connected components group mutually reachable directed vertices. Undirected bridges need low-link reasoning and correct edge identity for parallel edges. Bipartite matching can use augmenting paths; a failed greedy pair choice does not prove that no larger matching exists.\n\nFor every algorithm, write its assumptions and complexity in terms of V/E. Test disconnected graphs, isolated vertices, a self-loop where permitted, duplicate/parallel edges and an unreachable negative cycle. A graph picture alone is not an input specification.","exercise":"Choose algorithms for unweighted routes, nonnegative weighted routes and graphs with negative edges. Explain a duplicate-edge bridge case and a greedy matching counterexample.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is Dijkstra not generally valid with negative edges?","a":"Its finalisation argument depends on nonnegative extension costs. A later path through a negative edge can invalidate an already treated shortest distance."},{"q":"Why must a bridge algorithm distinguish parallel edge IDs?","a":"One edge to the parent is the DFS tree edge, but another parallel edge is a valid back connection. Treating every parent-neighbour edge as the same can falsely report a bridge."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which algorithm directly finds shortest edge-count paths in an unweighted graph?","options":["BFS","Arbitrary DFS order","Selection sort","A stack that ignores visited vertices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"BFS explores increasing distance layers in the unweighted setting."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"from collections import deque\ngraph = [[1,2],[3],[3],[]]\ndistance = [-1]*len(graph); distance[0]=0\nqueue=deque([0])\nwhile queue:\n    node=queue.popleft()\n    for nxt in graph[node]:\n        if distance[nxt] == -1:\n            distance[nxt]=distance[node]+1;queue.append(nxt)\nprint(distance)","expected":"[0, 1, 1, 2]","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-dp","title":"Dynamic programming: state, transitions and order","body":"## Store the repeated subproblem\nDynamic programming is useful when a problem has overlapping subproblems and an appropriate optimal/substructure relation. Define exactly what one state means before writing a table. Then specify base cases, transitions, evaluation order and how the final answer is extracted.\n\nMemoisation computes states on demand through recursion; tabulation chooses an explicit order. Space optimisation is valid only when old values are no longer needed. For 0/1 knapsack, iterating capacity in the wrong direction can accidentally reuse an item and turn it into a different problem.\n\n## Recognise families\nGrid DP moves among cells, string DP compares prefixes/substrings and tree DP combines child summaries. LIS needs a precise subsequence-versus-substring distinction. LCS and edit distance have different transitions and objectives. Interval DP chooses a split or last action inside a range, as in matrix-chain cost or burst balloons.\n\nDigit DP processes a numeric bound with position, tightness, leading-zero and relevant history state. Define how zero itself is counted. A missing leading-zero policy often double-counts shorter numbers or imposes a false adjacency constraint.\n\nTest a small brute-force oracle, boundary dimensions and an impossible state. Use a suitable infinity/sentinel and avoid treating an unreachable state as a valid zero-cost solution. Reconstructing one solution requires predecessor/choice information beyond merely returning the optimal value.","exercise":"Define the state and transition for 0/1 knapsack and for palindrome minimum cuts. Explain iteration direction and test against exhaustive subsets/partitions on small inputs.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does state meaning come before code?","a":"It determines which information is sufficient for a subproblem and which transitions are valid. A table with an unclear meaning is difficult to initialise, order or prove correct."},{"q":"Why can a one-dimensional knapsack loop direction change the problem?","a":"Updating capacities upward can reuse a value written for the same item, allowing that item multiple times. Descending capacity preserves the previous-item dependency for 0/1 selection."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a subsequence property?","options":["Elements keep relative order but need not be contiguous","Elements must occupy adjacent positions","Elements must be sorted by original index and value automatically","Every character must be unique"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A subsequence preserves order; a substring/subarray is contiguous."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"weights, values, capacity = [2,3], [4,5], 3\ndp = [0]*(capacity+1)\nfor weight,value in zip(weights,values):\n    for cap in range(capacity,weight-1,-1):\n        dp[cap] = max(dp[cap], dp[cap-weight]+value)\nprint(dp[capacity])","expected":"5","stdin":""}},{"id":"dsa-design","title":"Backtracking, greedy proof, bits and range-query structures","body":"## Explore and undo\nBacktracking maintains a partial solution, chooses a candidate, checks/prunes constraints, recurses and restores state. Subsets, permutations, N-Queens and word search differ in candidate/duplicate rules. A prune must be proved safe; an aggressive guess can discard a valid answer.\n\nGreedy algorithms make a locally chosen step whose global correctness needs an argument, often an exchange argument or a maintained frontier invariant. Choosing the earliest finish works for maximum count of nonoverlapping intervals, but weighted interval profit needs a different method such as DP with compatible predecessors.\n\n## Bit reasoning\nUse shifts/masks for flags and binary states, with clear integer-width/sign assumptions. `x & (x-1)` clears one set bit for a positive integer. XOR has useful cancellation properties but does not sort values or preserve ordinary addition semantics.\n\n## Dynamic range queries\nA prefix sum is excellent for static ranges but costly to rebuild after every update. A Fenwick tree supports prefix sums and point additions in O(log n); range sums are differences of prefixes. A segment tree combines an associative summary over intervals and can support different queries/updates with a defined identity. Do not mix point-update and lazy range-update contracts accidentally.\n\nExplain the invariant and compare with a simple list scan on small random operation sequences. A complex data structure earns its place when the query/update workload requires it.","exercise":"Generate subsets without duplicates, give a greedy exchange argument for unweighted interval scheduling and compare Fenwick versus segment-tree operations on one update/query workload.","interviews":[{"q":"What makes a greedy solution convincing?","a":"A correctness argument showing the local choice preserves an optimal solution or invariant. Passing a few examples does not establish that the choice is always safe."},{"q":"When is a Fenwick tree useful?","a":"For workloads such as point additions and prefix/range sum queries where both updates and queries need logarithmic time. It is not a universal replacement for every range aggregate or update type."}],"quiz":{"question":"For positive x, what does x & (x-1) do?","options":["Clears the lowest set bit","Always doubles x","Sorts the bits","Always returns x+1"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Subtracting one flips the lowest set bit and lower zeros; AND removes that one set bit."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python","code":"x, count = 13, 0\nwhile x:\n    x &= x-1\n    count += 1\nprint(count)","expected":"3","stdin":""}}]},{"id":"native-languages","title":"C, C++ & Java","group":"Compiled language basics","summary":"Use the normal editor with a real installed compiler and explicit stdin/stdout contracts.","duration":"Choose one language, then practise daily","project":"Write and test a small command-line program; use local compilers only for code you intend to run on your computer.","sources":[{"title":"GNU C introduction and reference","url":"https://www.gnu.org/software/c-intro-and-ref/manual/"},{"title":"Standard C++ learning resources","url":"https://isocpp.org/get-started"},{"title":"Dev.java learning guides","url":"https://dev.java/learn/"},{"title":"MIT introduction to algorithms","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/resources/lecture-notes/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"c-basics","title":"C: values, arrays, pointers and safe input","body":"## Compile before execution\nC source is translated and linked into a native program. Learn compiler diagnostics separately from runtime errors and wrong output. This site's local launcher detects GCC/Clang when installed; GitHub Pages itself cannot run the computer's compiler.\n\n## Storage and bounds\nDeclare suitable types and match format specifiers. A C array has a fixed bound in its declared storage; passing it to many functions decays to a pointer, so its length often needs a separate argument. A pointer is an address-like reference under the language's rules, not a guarantee that dereferencing it is valid.\n\nAvoid out-of-bounds access, use-after-free and uninitialised reads. Allocate dynamically with a clear ownership/free policy, check allocation failure and keep size arithmetic within range. Undefined behaviour can appear to work in one build and fail in another; a successful sample run is not proof of safety.\n\n## Input/output contract\nCheck scanf conversion counts for a small numeric exercise, or read bounded lines and parse with a deliberate error policy. Never use an unbounded string read into a fixed array. Print exactly the requested result; extra prompts break automated judging.\n\nFor JSON-I/O tasks, C needs a real parser or an explicitly implemented supported subset; the generic starter intentionally does not pretend arbitrary JSON is one scanf away. Choose a plain-input task or implement the contract fully.","exercise":"Write a C program that reads two integers, checks the conversion count and prints their sum. Test invalid input and explain the valid numeric range.","interviews":[{"q":"Why pass array length to a C function?","a":"A pointer parameter does not generally carry the original array bound. The function needs an explicit valid length or another well-defined termination contract to avoid reading outside the object."},{"q":"What is undefined behaviour?","a":"The language does not prescribe the program's behaviour for that operation. Apparent success on one compiler/input is not a guarantee and can change with optimisation or environment."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should a judged program print?","options":["Exactly the specified output","Extra friendly input prompts by default","The whole source code","A success claim before reading input"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Automated judging compares the required output contract, so extra prompts can make an otherwise correct calculation fail."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"c","code":"#include <stdio.h>\nint main(void) {\n    long long a, b;\n    if (scanf(\"%lld %lld\", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;\n    printf(\"%lld\\n\", a + b);\n    return 0;\n}","expected":"42","stdin":"20 22\n"}},{"id":"cpp-basics","title":"C++: STL containers, references and RAII","body":"## Use ownership-aware library types\nC++ offers native performance and a rich standard library. vector manages a dynamic contiguous array, string manages text, unordered_map offers expected hash-based lookup and map offers ordered-key operations. Choose by the required access/ordering contract and state the complexity assumptions.\n\nReferences alias existing objects. Pass a large read-only input by const reference where appropriate; copying can be intentional when the algorithm needs independent ownership. Iterator/reference invalidation depends on the container and operation, so do not retain a vector element reference across arbitrary reallocating pushes.\n\n## RAII\nAn object's lifetime owns cleanup through its destructor. Standard containers, smart pointers and lock guards reduce scattered manual resource management. RAII is not a substitute for a sensible ownership graph, and shared ownership can still create cycles.\n\nUse long long or another suitable type when sums exceed int range and verify multiplication/overflow assumptions before computing. A comparator must define a valid ordering, not a random or contradictory rule. Standard sort is not a stable sort unless the selected operation provides that property.\n\nThe local runner uses a C++17 contract with detected GCC/Clang. The example uses only standard headers; platform-specific headers and external JSON libraries are not automatically installed by the app.","exercise":"Read n integers into a vector, compute their sum and sort a copy. Explain ownership, overflow assumptions and which operations can invalidate references.","interviews":[{"q":"What does RAII achieve?","a":"It ties resource acquisition/release to object lifetime so destructors perform cleanup on normal scope exit and exception unwinding. Clear ownership still matters."},{"q":"Why use const reference for a read-only vector parameter?","a":"It avoids copying a potentially large container while expressing that the function should not mutate it through that reference. The caller must keep the referenced object alive."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which container is a usual dynamic contiguous sequence?","options":["std::vector","A raw pointer with no length contract","std::mutex","A stream error code"],"answer":0,"explanation":"vector manages a contiguous dynamic sequence with defined size/capacity semantics."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"cpp","code":"#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <numeric>\nint main() {\n    int n; if (!(std::cin >> n) || n < 0) return 1;\n    std::vector<long long> a(n);\n    for (auto &x : a) if (!(std::cin >> x)) return 1;\n    std::cout << std::accumulate(a.begin(), a.end(), 0LL) << \"\\n\";\n}","expected":"42","stdin":"3\n10 12 20\n"}},{"id":"java-basics","title":"Java: classes, collections and the JDK","body":"## The JDK includes development tools\njavac compiles source to class files and java runs a suitable entry point on the JVM. A JRE-only installation may not include the compiler. This app's local runner expects a Main class in Main.java and detects a JDK; it does not download one silently.\n\n## Values and object references\nPrimitive values and object references have different semantics. Strings are immutable and value comparison normally uses equals rather than reference identity with ==. Use generics for typed collections such as List<Integer> and Map<String,Integer>, while remembering boxing and null-related cases.\n\nArrayList supports a dynamic indexed sequence; HashMap/HashSet provide expected hash-based operations. A Queue/Deque implementation can suit BFS/stack use. Do not assume every collection operation is constant time or that a hash container has sorted iteration order.\n\n## Input and errors\nScanner is convenient for small exercises; buffered input/parsing can be appropriate for large contest data. Validate the expected input and choose int/long according to constraints. Handle exceptions at a useful boundary instead of catching everything and printing a fake result.\n\nUse classes/interfaces to model contracts and composition for collaborators. Garbage collection reclaims unreachable memory but does not close every external resource at the correct business time; use try-with-resources for suitable closeable objects.","exercise":"Write a Main program that reads two long values and prints their sum. Then compare two independently created equal strings with == and equals and explain the result.","interviews":[{"q":"How do JDK and a runtime-only installation differ?","a":"A JDK includes development tools such as javac as well as runtime capabilities. Native local judging needs a compiler, not only a java executable."},{"q":"Why use equals for string contents?","a":"== compares reference identity for objects. equals expresses value/content equality according to the class contract, so independently created equal strings can compare equal by contents."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which tool compiles Java source?","options":["javac","Only a CSS bundler","git push","A browser title tag"],"answer":0,"explanation":"javac is the JDK compiler that produces class files from source."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"java","code":"import java.util.Scanner;\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner input = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long a = input.nextLong(), b = input.nextLong();\n        System.out.println(a + b);\n    }\n}","expected":"42","stdin":"20 22\n"}}]},{"id":"mobile-flutter","title":"Flutter & Dart","group":"Mobile development","summary":"Choose one cross-platform path and learn its state, navigation and device boundaries.","duration":"4-6 weeks after programming basics","project":"Build a task app with forms, local storage, API loading/error states and a signed test release.","sources":[{"title":"Learn Flutter","url":"https://docs.flutter.dev/learn"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"flutter-dart","title":"Dart, widgets, layouts and state","body":"## Dart first, then widgets\nLearn variables, types, null safety, functions, collections, classes and async/await before copying a large Flutter screen. A nullable value needs an explicit handling path; suppressing every null warning does not make missing data valid.\n\nFlutter builds UI from widgets. StatelessWidget describes output from inputs, while StatefulWidget associates a state object with a widget lifecycle. The framework rebuilds descriptions as state changes; a rebuild is not automatically a full recreation of every native resource.\n\n## Compose a layout\nUse Row/Column for linear arrangement, Stack for overlap and padding/alignment/sizing widgets for constraints. Parent constraints and child sizing rules explain many overflow errors. A long scrollable list should use an appropriate lazy builder rather than constructing an unbounded giant column.\n\nsetState marks relevant state as changed so UI can rebuild. Keep a small form's state local. Provider/Riverpod can organise broader state/dependency ownership, but choose one policy and understand its lifecycle instead of adding several state libraries at once.\n\nTest text scaling, narrow devices, keyboard appearance and screen-reader labels. A layout that fits one emulator screenshot may overflow when translated or when a user increases font size. The browser study desk displays Dart examples as reading material; a Flutter project requires its SDK/toolchain.","exercise":"Build a two-screen task UI with a list, an add form and a local completed filter. Explain which state is local, which is shared and how layout responds to a large font.","interviews":[{"q":"How do stateless and stateful widgets differ?","a":"A stateless widget describes UI from its inputs. A stateful widget has an associated state lifecycle for values that change over time; state ownership and update rules still need deliberate design."},{"q":"Why does a Row overflow?","a":"Its children may request more horizontal space than the parent constraints allow. Inspect constraints and use appropriate flexible/wrapping/scrolling behaviour rather than shrinking every text blindly."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should guide choosing a state-management tool?","options":["State ownership and lifecycle needs","The largest number of packages","A belief that every value must be global","Only the app icon"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A state tool should clarify who owns updates and how long state lives."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"dart","code":"class Task {\n  final String id;\n  final String title;\n  final bool completed;\n  const Task(this.id, this.title, {this.completed = false});\n}\nvoid main() {\n  const task = Task(\"1\", \"Revise widgets\");\n  print(task.title);\n}","expected":"In a Dart environment, prints Revise widgets.","stdin":""}},{"id":"flutter-data","title":"Navigation, APIs, local storage, Firebase and release","body":"## Navigation preserves a state contract\nPass stable identifiers between screens instead of relying on an object from one previous click. Decide which state survives returning, app backgrounding and process restart. Forms need validation, a submit state and retained data after a failed request.\n\n## Local and remote data\nUse an HTTP client such as http/dio with explicit timeouts, status/payload validation and stale-request handling. Preferences suit small non-secret settings; a local database/store can suit structured offline data. Do not store sensitive credentials in an ordinary unencrypted preferences file.\n\nFirebase can provide authentication, Firestore and notifications, but its security rules are an actual backend boundary. A client configuration file is not permission to read every document. Enforce user/tenant access in rules/server logic and test denied cases. Service plans/quotas and setup requirements should be checked before deployment.\n\nFor offline updates, define conflict/deletion and retry policies. A local success indicator should distinguish saved-on-device from acknowledged-by-server. Notifications require device/platform permissions and an authenticated delivery design; they are not a reliable source of truth for business state.\n\n## Prepare a release\nBuild the correct signed Android/iOS artefact using the relevant SDK/toolchain or supported build service. Keep signing material private, test on real devices and review current store requirements. An Android APK for local testing and an AAB/store distribution workflow are different deliverables.","exercise":"Specify task persistence across app restart, a failed API save and a later retry. Plan authentication/rules for a per-user Firestore collection and a release smoke checklist.","interviews":[{"q":"Why distinguish device-saved from server-synced?","a":"A local write can succeed while the network request has not. The user should know which durability/synchronisation boundary has actually acknowledged the change."},{"q":"Does a Firebase client SDK remove access-rule design?","a":"No. Security rules and server-side validation determine which data a signed-in or anonymous caller may access. Test one user attempting another user's documents."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which belongs in ordinary preferences?","options":["A non-secret theme choice","A private server administrator key","A payment webhook signing secret","An unrestricted database credential"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Preferences are not a server secret store or a substitute for secure credential storage."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"mobile-kotlin","title":"Android & Kotlin","group":"Mobile development","summary":"Build native Android screens with Compose, lifecycle-aware state and tested local data.","duration":"4-6 weeks after basics","project":"Build a Compose task app with Room persistence, an API repository and a signed internal-test build.","sources":[{"title":"Android Basics with Compose","url":"https://developer.android.com/courses/android-basics-compose/course"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"android-state","title":"Kotlin, Compose, ViewModel and navigation","body":"## Learn the language contract\nKotlin has val/var bindings, nullable types, functions, classes and data classes. A nullable reference requires handling; a force-unwrapping operator can still crash when the assumption is wrong. Use immutable data and explicit updates for predictable UI state.\n\nJetpack Compose describes UI with composable functions. State changes can trigger recomposition, so keep composables free of unintended repeated side effects. Remembered UI state, saveable state and persistent database data have different survival lifetimes.\n\n## ViewModel and MVVM\nA ViewModel can own screen-level state and interact with repositories across suitable lifecycle events. MVVM separates presentation state, UI and data responsibilities; it is not a rule to place every function into a class with a particular suffix.\n\nNavigation should use a defined route/argument contract and handle missing/deleted items. Pass an ID and reload through a repository when appropriate rather than keeping a fragile full object forever. Account for process death and configuration changes explicitly.\n\nUse Android Studio/emulators for development and real-device tests for important behaviour. Validate large fonts, orientation/size changes, keyboard focus and accessibility semantics. A screen that recomposes is not by itself a performance bug; measure the actual expensive work.","exercise":"Design Compose UI state for loading/error/ready task details. Explain what survives recomposition, configuration changes and process death, and where the task record is persisted.","interviews":[{"q":"Why use a ViewModel rather than storing everything in a composable?","a":"It can provide an appropriate owner for screen state and repository interaction across relevant lifecycle changes. Persisted data still needs a durable store; a ViewModel is not a backup database."},{"q":"What is recomposition?","a":"Compose reevaluates relevant UI descriptions when observed state changes. Side effects and expensive work need appropriate APIs/ownership so reevaluation remains safe and efficient."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which value should normally be loaded through a stable navigation argument?","options":["A task ID used by a repository","A private backend key","An arbitrary executable string","A global mutable screen object required from a previous click"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A stable identifier supports direct navigation, reload and clear data ownership."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"kotlin","code":"data class Task(val id: String, val title: String, val done: Boolean = false)\nfun main() {\n    val task = Task(\"1\", \"Learn Compose\")\n    val completed = task.copy(done = true)\n    println(completed)\n}","expected":"In a Kotlin environment, creates an updated immutable record.","stdin":""}},{"id":"android-data","title":"Room, Retrofit, coroutines and publishing","body":"## Repository boundaries\nRoom maps structured local data onto SQLite through defined entities/DAOs. Migrations preserve installed users' data as the schema evolves. A repository can coordinate local/remote sources, but it needs an explicit authority, freshness and conflict policy.\n\nRetrofit is a common HTTP API client tool. Configure serialisation, authentication and timeouts, then handle non-success status and malformed/missing payloads. A generated interface does not validate every business invariant automatically.\n\n## Coroutines are structured work\nA suspend function can suspend without blocking its current execution context at supported operations; it does not automatically move every calculation to a background thread. Use appropriate dispatchers and lifecycle scopes, propagate cancellation and avoid retaining a dead screen through a long task.\n\nObserve data through a lifecycle-aware mechanism and render loading, empty, failed and success states. Keep a failed edit locally and show whether retry/sync is pending. Handle app restart and a cancelled request without silently duplicating the operation.\n\n## Platform boundaries\nAsk for permissions only when the related feature needs them and provide a usable denied path. Notifications and background execution follow platform/version policies. For publishing, sign the correct artefact, protect signing credentials and verify current Play requirements, privacy declarations and target SDK rules before release.","exercise":"Design an offline task edit stored in Room and retried through a repository. Explain coroutine cancellation on leaving a screen and the migration test for adding a required field.","interviews":[{"q":"Does suspend mean run on a background thread?","a":"No. It means the function can suspend. The execution context/dispatcher and the operations inside it determine where work runs and whether blocking occurs."},{"q":"Why test a Room migration with existing data?","a":"An empty new database does not expose data-preservation and compatibility failures. A migration must keep previously installed users' records valid under the new schema."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should a denied notification permission do?","options":["Keep the core app usable and explain the optional feature state","Delete local tasks","Retry the permission prompt forever","Assume every notification was delivered"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Permissions are user-controlled capabilities with a required denied/recovery path."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"mobile-react-native","title":"React Native & Expo","group":"Mobile development","summary":"Reuse React concepts while learning native components, storage and device-specific delivery.","duration":"3-5 weeks after React","project":"Build a cross-platform task client with navigation, API access, local drafts and a tested release build.","sources":[{"title":"React Native introduction","url":"https://reactnative.dev/docs/getting-started"},{"title":"React Learn","url":"https://react.dev/learn"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"rn-components","title":"Native components, hooks and screen navigation","body":"## React concepts, different rendering targets\nReact Native uses components such as View, Text, Image, Pressable and scroll/list primitives instead of browser divs and paragraphs. Hooks and composition remain useful, but DOM APIs and CSS files do not transfer unchanged. Learn the supported style/layout model and platform differences.\n\nExpo provides development/build tooling and modules around React Native. The available runtime and native-module workflow depend on the project configuration; do not assume every third-party native package works in every preview client without an appropriate build.\n\n## Screen state and navigation\nUse a navigation library with a clear route/parameter contract. Keep stable IDs and reloadable data, preserve useful local drafts and handle a missing/deleted resource. A mobile Back action and a browser history entry are different platform interactions that need testing.\n\nLarge lists should use an appropriate virtualised list primitive with stable keys. Handle safe areas, keyboard interaction, text scaling and accessible labels. A screenshot on one device does not establish correct layout across Android/iOS sizes.\n\nKeep server state separate from purely local interface state. A repository/API module can centralise request configuration, but the backend still enforces auth, tenant permissions and business rules for the mobile client just as for the web client.","exercise":"Convert a React web task card into a native component using supported primitives. Define navigation parameters, loading/error UI and a stable-key large-list strategy.","interviews":[{"q":"Can a React Native screen use the browser DOM directly?","a":"No. It renders through native-target components and APIs. React state/composition knowledge transfers, while platform UI, permissions and storage contracts differ."},{"q":"Why test real device text scaling?","a":"Longer/larger text and platform accessibility settings can expose clipping, inaccessible controls and layout assumptions that a fixed emulator screenshot misses."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a basic React Native UI primitive?","options":["View","A browser-only document.querySelector call","An HTML table tag as a native layout","A CSS stylesheet link element"],"answer":0,"explanation":"React Native has its own supported component and platform API model."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"jsx","code":"import { View, Text, Pressable } from \"react-native\";\nexport function TaskCard({task, onComplete}) {\n  return <View><Text>{task.title}</Text><Pressable accessibilityRole=\"button\" onPress={() => onComplete(task.id)}><Text>Complete</Text></Pressable></View>;\n}","expected":"In a React Native project, renders a native task card with an accessible action.","stdin":""}},{"id":"rn-release","title":"Async storage, API sync, notifications and EAS builds","body":"## Local data has a sensitivity level\nAsyncStorage is useful for suitable non-secret key/value persistence, not a universal secure credential vault. Use an appropriate platform-backed secure store for credentials, and understand what survives logout, reinstall or device backup. Version structured saved data and test migrations.\n\nFor offline edits, store a local result and an explicit pending operation with an idempotency policy. Show device-saved versus server-acknowledged status. Resolve conflicting remote edits/deletions deliberately; copying one entire JSON blob over another can lose work.\n\n## Connect to the right backend\nOn a physical device, localhost names that device, not your development laptop. Use the intended reachable development/staging endpoint with appropriate transport and auth configuration. Do not expose a privileged local code-execution server to a wider network as a shortcut.\n\nPush notifications require platform credentials, permissions and token lifecycle management. A notification is a delivery signal, not proof of payment/order state. The app should fetch authorised durable state when needed.\n\nExpo EAS can support build/distribution workflows with the required project configuration and signing credentials. Native iOS local builds/simulators require the relevant Apple tooling; cloud-build services have their own requirements. Review current store policies, privacy declarations and account/signing rules before publishing.","exercise":"Specify local draft persistence, secure token handling and a pending-sync indicator. Plan an Android/iOS test release and explain how you verify the backend URL on a real phone.","interviews":[{"q":"Why is AsyncStorage not the default place for a server secret?","a":"It is client-side application storage, not a protected server credential boundary. Users/devices and compromised client code can expose it; keep server secrets on the backend."},{"q":"Why does localhost fail to reach a laptop API from a phone?","a":"The hostname refers to the phone's own loopback interface. Development connectivity needs the correct reachable endpoint and security configuration, not an assumption that every device shares one localhost."}],"quiz":{"question":"What should a push notification trigger for sensitive business state?","options":["An authorised check of durable server state","Blindly marking an invoice paid","Skipping login forever","Publishing the device token to everyone"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Notifications can be missed/duplicated and should not replace server-authoritative state."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"mobile-swift","title":"Swift & SwiftUI","group":"Mobile development","summary":"Learn native Apple UI, optionals, model ownership and async data boundaries.","duration":"4-6 weeks after basics","project":"Build a SwiftUI notes app with persisted data, navigation, API error states and a distribution plan.","sources":[{"title":"Swift language guide","url":"https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"swift-state","title":"Swift optionals, SwiftUI views and MVVM","body":"## Make missing data explicit\nSwift uses value/reference types, functions, structs/classes and optionals. An optional can hold a value or nil. Optional binding and other supported patterns make the missing path explicit; force unwrapping merely asserts that the programmer's assumption is correct at runtime.\n\nSwiftUI describes views from state using layouts such as VStack, HStack and List. State ownership matters: local state, a binding into another owner's value and observable model state have different roles. The exact observation APIs depend on the supported SDK/version; learn the ownership contract rather than memorising one property wrapper for every situation.\n\n## Separate responsibilities\nMVVM can keep view rendering, screen state and data/repository operations understandable. A view model should expose useful loading/error/ready state, not hide every operation behind unrelated mutable flags. Keep persistence outside a temporary view's lifetime.\n\nNavigationStack and a route/data identity strategy help make navigation reproducible. Handle deletion or a missing record when returning to a screen. Test larger accessibility text, keyboard/VoiceOver and device size changes.\n\nNative Apple development uses the appropriate Xcode/macOS tooling for local builds and simulators. This offline lesson is reference material and does not emulate Xcode or package an iOS SDK.","exercise":"Model a note detail screen with an optional selected note, loading/error/ready state and an editable title binding. Explain where the durable note lives when the view disappears.","interviews":[{"q":"Why avoid force unwrapping by default?","a":"It converts a potentially missing value into a runtime crash if the assumption is wrong. Handle the missing case explicitly unless a genuinely enforced invariant justifies the assertion."},{"q":"What is a binding conceptually?","a":"It lets a child view read/update a value owned elsewhere through a controlled relationship. It is not an independent durable copy of that value."}],"quiz":{"question":"What does an optional represent?","options":["A value that may be absent","A guarantee that nil can never occur","Only a UI colour","A background thread"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The missing case is part of the type and needs appropriate handling."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"swift","code":"struct Note { let id: Int; var title: String }\nlet selected: Note? = Note(id: 1, title: \"Learn Swift\")\nif let note = selected {\n    print(note.title)\n} else {\n    print(\"No note selected\")\n}","expected":"In a Swift environment, prints Learn Swift.","stdin":""}},{"id":"swift-data","title":"URLSession, local persistence and App Store delivery","body":"## Networking is a typed failure boundary\nURLSession supports HTTP work and modern async/await interfaces. Check status, decode a defined model and handle transport, decoding and business errors distinctly. Do not assume a response body has the expected shape merely because bytes arrived.\n\nCancel stale work when an operation no longer belongs to the current screen/request. Keep a failed edit locally and make retry state visible. Update UI state on the appropriate execution context and avoid a long blocking calculation on the main actor.\n\n## Persistence and permissions\nCore Data and other current Apple persistence options manage structured local records under their own lifecycle/migration rules. Choose one and test existing data through a schema update. Store credentials using appropriate secure platform facilities, not a plain note database.\n\nNotifications, photos, microphone and other device capabilities need purpose-specific permission handling and a useful denied path. Private media/document uploads require a clear user action and backend access policy.\n\n## Release is more than Build succeeded\nSigning, identifiers, distribution profiles, TestFlight/App Store Connect and current review/privacy requirements form a delivery workflow. Local native iOS tooling requires a suitable Mac/Xcode setup; cloud workflows still need the relevant credentials and supported configuration. Test launch, offline data, login recovery, permissions and an upgrade from a previous installed version.","exercise":"Design a notes API call with cancellation, decoding and retained drafts on failure. Create a release checklist covering signing, privacy/permissions, persistence migration and real-device tests.","interviews":[{"q":"Why distinguish transport and decoding errors?","a":"They indicate different failure boundaries and recovery paths. A reachable server can return an incompatible payload, while a network outage can occur before any valid response exists."},{"q":"Why test an upgrade from an earlier installed app?","a":"Existing users bring saved data, credentials and prior configuration. A clean install cannot expose migration or compatibility failures affecting those users."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must a persistence upgrade protect?","options":["Existing users' valid saved data","Only a new empty demo database","Only the launch icon","Only a screenshot of the newest screen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Migration correctness includes previously stored records and their intended meaning."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"desktop-electron","title":"Electron Desktop","group":"Desktop development","summary":"Combine web UI with a narrow, permission-aware native bridge.","duration":"2-3 weeks after web development","project":"Build a local notes desktop app with scoped file import/export and a signed/reviewed packaging plan.","sources":[{"title":"Electron tutorial","url":"https://www.electronjs.org/docs/latest/tutorial/tutorial-prerequisites"},{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"electron-processes","title":"Main, renderer, preload and IPC","body":"## A desktop window has separate responsibilities\nElectron combines a native main-process application layer with renderer windows using web technology. The main process owns privileged application/window operations. A preload can expose a narrow bridge; the renderer should not receive unrestricted operating-system authority just because it displays your UI.\n\nUse context isolation and keep Node integration disabled for untrusted/ordinary renderer content according to the application's security design. Expose specific operations such as choose-and-read-note rather than a generic execute-command or read-any-path function. Validate IPC arguments and the calling context.\n\n## Native workflows\nMenus, windows and file dialogs belong to a clear process/lifecycle owner. A renderer asks for an operation; the privileged side validates the request and returns a bounded result. Keep source paths scoped to the user's intended selection and handle cancelled dialogs without discarding current work.\n\nTreat loaded remote content, imported Markdown and generated HTML as untrusted. Navigation/new-window policy and resource loading matter in a desktop renderer too. Do not disable security settings merely to make an iframe or package work.\n\nTest malformed IPC, a cancelled file picker and a renderer failure. A desktop wrapper adds capabilities and responsibility; it does not make an unsafe web interface automatically safe.","exercise":"Define a preload API with readSelectedNote and saveCurrentNote operations. Specify input/output limits, cancellation and why arbitrary path/command methods are excluded.","interviews":[{"q":"Why keep a narrow preload bridge?","a":"It limits the authority available to renderer code. A successful renderer injection should not automatically become unrestricted file or command access."},{"q":"Does context isolation replace IPC validation?","a":"No. It separates JavaScript contexts, while each exposed operation still needs argument, caller and resource-scope checks."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which bridge is safer?","options":["A specific validated save-note operation","An unrestricted execute-shell method","A read-any-file method exposed to every page","A private signing key in renderer state"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A narrow operation can enforce the actual user-authorised resource and action."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"electron","code":"// Preload concept: expose a small operation, not Node itself.\ncontextBridge.exposeInMainWorld(\"notes\", {\n  save: (note) => ipcRenderer.invoke(\"notes:save\", note)\n});\n// The main handler must validate note shape, caller and destination.","expected":"Project sketch: the privileged handler owns validation and file access.","stdin":""}},{"id":"electron-packaging","title":"Local data, updates and installers","body":"## Separate application files from user data\nPackaged application assets may be replaced during an update. Store user notes/settings in an appropriate per-user data directory and use versioned migrations. Do not place the only copy of a user's work inside a disposable installation directory.\n\n## File workflows\nUse native dialogs for user-selected import/export, validate content and preserve a current backup before destructive replacement. Atomic replacement can reduce partial writes; concurrency, crash recovery and durability still need their own policy. Bound imported sizes and sanitise rich content.\n\n## Package deliberately\nTools such as electron-builder can create platform deliverables, but signing, notarisation, runtime dependencies and distribution requirements differ. Protect signing credentials. Test the actual installed artefact away from the source repository, including paths with spaces and a user account without administrator privileges.\n\nAn update mechanism must verify artefact origin/integrity under its design and preserve user data. Stage a release, test upgrade/rollback and keep a clear version/history view. A downloaded file being named update does not establish trust.\n\nFor an offline-first tool, distinguish local saving from any optional remote sync. Export/import should remain usable when the provider or network is down. An installer finishing successfully is not proof that the app can open, save and reopen a note.","exercise":"Specify a user-data directory policy, migration backup and installed-app smoke test. Include a path with spaces, a cancelled import and an upgrade retaining existing notes.","interviews":[{"q":"Why keep user data outside application assets?","a":"Updates and reinstalls can replace the application files. User data needs an independent lifecycle, migration and backup policy."},{"q":"Why test the installed package away from source?","a":"The development environment can hide missing files, paths and runtime dependencies. A portable/installed smoke test checks what the user actually receives."}],"quiz":{"question":"What is essential in an app update?","options":["Verified artefact handling and preserved user data","Replacing every user note unconditionally","Publishing signing credentials","Assuming source-tree execution proves packaging"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Updates affect both executable trust and the lifecycle of existing user work."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"desktop-python","title":"Python Desktop","group":"Desktop development","summary":"Build event-driven tools with a clear UI/thread/storage boundary.","duration":"2-3 weeks after Python","project":"Build a local task manager with SQLite, import/export and a tested packaged executable.","sources":[{"title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"gui-python","title":"Tkinter, PySide and event-driven interfaces","body":"## Choose a toolkit and learn its event loop\nTkinter is part of the usual Python GUI ecosystem and is available in many standard Python installations; actual platform packaging can vary. PySide/PyQt provide Qt bindings, while CustomTkinter offers another styled-widget approach. Choose one toolkit and its deployment/license requirements before mixing several UI stacks.\n\nWidgets include windows, buttons, labels, inputs, lists and menus. Layout managers express size/placement relationships; fixed pixel coordinates rarely survive different fonts and window sizes well. Keep callbacks small and move reusable business logic into plain Python functions/classes.\n\n## Keep the interface responsive\nThe GUI event loop must continue processing input/render events. A long blocking network call or CPU loop inside a button callback freezes the window. Use a suitable worker strategy and the toolkit's supported way to deliver results back to the UI thread. Do not mutate widgets from arbitrary worker threads.\n\nShow running, failed and completed states and preserve a draft on errors. Handle window-close while work is active with a bounded cancellation/save policy.\n\nThe browser's Python runtime does not provide an operating-system GUI display, so these toolkit examples belong in a local desktop environment. You can still read the full explanation and practise the non-GUI logic here.","exercise":"Design a desktop task form with add/complete/delete callbacks. Separate validation/storage from widgets and explain how a slow import runs without freezing the window.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does a long callback freeze a GUI?","a":"It occupies the event-loop thread, preventing it from processing input and redraws. Move suitable work away and return updates through the toolkit's supported UI mechanism."},{"q":"Why test business logic outside the GUI?","a":"Pure validation/calculation/storage contracts can be tested deterministically without opening windows. Keep a smaller set of interaction tests for the actual widget workflow."}],"quiz":{"question":"Where should a worker deliver a widget update?","options":["Through the toolkit's supported UI-thread mechanism","By mutating every widget from any thread","Only through a global print statement","By blocking the event loop until it finishes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Most GUI toolkits require coordinated UI-thread access."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python-gui","code":"import tkinter as tk\nroot = tk.Tk()\nroot.title(\"Study tasks\")\nlabel = tk.Label(root, text=\"One useful task today\")\nlabel.pack(padx=24, pady=12)\ntk.Button(root, text=\"Complete\", command=lambda: label.config(text=\"Completed\")).pack(pady=12)\nroot.mainloop()","expected":"Run in a local Python installation with Tk support, not the browser engine.","stdin":""}},{"id":"gui-storage","title":"SQLite persistence and PyInstaller packaging","body":"## Local data still needs a contract\nSQLite is useful for structured single-application storage. Use parameterised SQL, transactions for related changes and a versioned migration policy. A desktop UI should not concatenate a task title into a SQL statement merely because the database is local.\n\nStore the database in an appropriate writable user-data location. The current working directory can differ between a terminal, shortcut and installed executable. Keep temporary files separate from the only durable copy of a user's notes.\n\n## Package the real dependency set\nPyInstaller can bundle a Python application for a target platform, but hidden imports, data files, native libraries and toolkit plugins need validation. Building on one platform does not automatically produce a correct native installer for every other platform.\n\nTest the output on a clean environment without the source folder or development virtual environment. Include a path with spaces/nontrivial characters, read-only install assets, database creation and a retained-data upgrade. Antivirus/code-signing and distribution policies can affect delivery and need a proper release process.\n\nProvide export/import and a recoverable backup before a migration or replacement. A packaged executable starting once is only the first check; saving, closing and reopening actual user data is the important workflow.","exercise":"Define SQLite tables and a migration for a desktop task app. Plan a package test that opens, saves, closes and restores data without access to the source repository.","interviews":[{"q":"Why not rely on the current working directory for user data?","a":"It can change depending on how the app is launched and may be unwritable or disposable. Use a deliberate per-user data location and keep installation assets separate."},{"q":"What can a source-folder test miss in packaging?","a":"Missing bundled resources, native libraries, writable-path assumptions and implicit development dependencies. Test the produced executable in an isolated location."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which SQL approach is correct for a task title?","options":["A bound parameter","String concatenation into SQL","Trust it because the app is local","Execute it as a statement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Local input can still alter query structure if data and SQL are concatenated unsafely."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"python-desktop","code":"import sqlite3\nwith sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\") as db:\n    db.execute(\"CREATE TABLE tasks(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT NOT NULL)\")\n    db.execute(\"INSERT INTO tasks(title) VALUES (?)\", (\"Revise SQL\",))\n    print(db.execute(\"SELECT title FROM tasks\").fetchone()[0])","expected":"Revise SQL","stdin":""}}]},{"id":"desktop-dotnet","title":"C# & .NET Desktop","group":"Desktop development","summary":"Use typed models, native Windows UI and explicit data/service boundaries.","duration":"3-4 weeks after programming basics","project":"Build a Windows task tool with validation, local data, async operations and a tested installer.","sources":[{"title":"Microsoft Learn C#","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/csharp/tour-of-csharp/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"dotnet-ui","title":"C#, .NET, WPF and WinForms","body":"## Language, runtime and UI are separate layers\nC# is the language; .NET supplies runtime/libraries/tooling. WPF and WinForms are Windows UI frameworks with different programming models. A .NET application is not automatically cross-platform when its selected UI stack is Windows-specific.\n\nLearn types, nullable-reference contracts, collections, classes/interfaces, exceptions and async/await. Use composition/dependency injection for services when it clarifies ownership. Value and reference semantics affect copies, equality and mutable shared state.\n\n## UI architecture\nWinForms is event/control-oriented; WPF uses XAML, binding and commonly an MVVM approach. Keep validation and persistence out of a large button-click handler. A view model exposes state/commands while a service/repository handles a clear external boundary.\n\nDo not block the UI thread with long synchronous work. Async I/O needs cancellation/error handling and an explicit loading state. CPU work needs an appropriate worker strategy rather than merely adding async to the method name.\n\nUse accessible labels, keyboard paths and scaling-aware layouts. Test data binding updates and error recovery as behaviour. A successful compile does not prove that a binding points at the intended property or that user data survives closing the window.","exercise":"Choose WPF or WinForms for a small Windows tool and explain the choice. Separate a task model, view model/callback layer and repository with a cancellation-aware save operation.","interviews":[{"q":"Does .NET imply every UI is cross-platform?","a":"No. Portability depends on the selected framework and platform APIs. WPF/WinForms are Windows-specific choices even though other .NET workloads can target different platforms."},{"q":"Why avoid database work directly in a giant click handler?","a":"It mixes UI, validation and persistence lifecycles, making testing and failure recovery difficult. Separate responsibilities and keep the visible loading/error state explicit."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a Windows UI framework choice?","options":["WPF or WinForms","A DNS record","A Git remote","A database index"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Those frameworks provide Windows desktop UI capabilities."},"skills":[],"minutes":15,"example":{"lang":"csharp","code":"public record TaskItem(int Id, string Title, bool Done);\nvar task = new TaskItem(1, \"Learn .NET\", false);\nvar completed = task with { Done = true };\nConsole.WriteLine(completed.Title);","expected":"In a compatible C# project, prints Learn .NET.","stdin":""}},{"id":"dotnet-data","title":"Entity Framework, configuration and installers","body":"## Data access still has database semantics\nEntity Framework Core maps models and queries to a database provider. It can simplify access, but constraints, transactions, indexes and generated query behaviour still need review. Keep a DbContext lifetime appropriate to the unit of work rather than sharing one global mutable context across unrelated operations.\n\nReview migrations against existing data and test the selected provider. An in-memory substitute does not reproduce every relational guarantee. Select explicit public/view models instead of binding an unrestricted persistence entity directly to a privileged edit form.\n\n## Configuration and secrets\nSeparate environment settings from application source. A desktop client distributed to users cannot keep a universal privileged server credential private. Authenticate the user to a backend when access requires server authority, and use suitable OS-backed storage for local credentials.\n\n## Deliver a repeatable artefact\nChoose framework-dependent versus self-contained deployment according to runtime/distribution needs. An installer/update path should preserve user data and apply safe migrations. Signing, runtime dependencies and enterprise/store distribution requirements depend on the target environment.\n\nTest installation under a normal user, first launch, offline local work, save/reopen and an upgrade with prior data. Keep a recovery/export path when a migration fails. Record which produced artefact was tested, not only that the IDE run button worked.","exercise":"Plan an EF Core SQLite task repository and a migration test. Compare framework-dependent/self-contained deployment and specify an installer upgrade that retains existing data.","interviews":[{"q":"Why does DbContext lifetime matter?","a":"It tracks a unit of work and entity state. An inappropriate global/shared lifetime can retain stale state, grow memory or create concurrency issues; scope it to the operation design."},{"q":"Can a distributed desktop client keep a shared admin API key secret?","a":"No reliable client-side boundary makes a universal embedded key private from its users. Use per-user authentication and a backend that enforces authority."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which must an installer upgrade preserve?","options":["The intended user-data lifecycle and migration path","Only the development project file","A private admin key embedded in every copy","Only a new empty screenshot"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Existing saved data and compatibility are part of the delivered product contract."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"projects","title":"Project Architecture","group":"Build and explain","summary":"Connect the skills into a portal or storefront and explain the actual trade-offs with evidence.","duration":"Build incrementally alongside the courses","project":"Deliver a small tested vertical slice, then add permissions, payments, background work and an operational runbook.","sources":[{"title":"React Learn","url":"https://react.dev/learn"},{"title":"Next.js App Router","url":"https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started"},{"title":"Node.js Learn","url":"https://nodejs.org/en/learn/getting-started/introduction-to-nodejs"},{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/"},{"title":"MongoDB data modelling","url":"https://www.mongodb.com/docs/manual/data-modeling/"},{"title":"OWASP Cheat Sheet Series","url":"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"project-portal","title":"Design a multi-tenant client portal","body":"## Begin with one vertical slice\nA portal connects an agency/team to clients, projects, tasks and invoices. Start with one authenticated user creating and reading one tenant-scoped project through UI, API and database. Verify the full path before adding a Kanban board, charts or AI summaries.\n\n## Model the permissions\nWrite an admin/member/client matrix. Associate resources with reliable tenant/relationship data and enforce it in every query, export, upload and realtime room. A protected route in React improves navigation but cannot replace a backend access check.\n\n## Add capabilities by boundary\nReact/Vite can build an interactive dashboard; Router handles client navigation; a state/context policy owns session/UI state. Recharts can display labelled metrics and dnd-kit can support accessible task moves. Node/Express and PostgreSQL/Prisma are one suitable implementation family, with validated inputs and migration evidence.\n\nPayments require server-authoritative amounts, verified/idempotent provider events and transaction-time invoice snapshots. Notifications should not decide whether the core task change succeeded. A report job needs a durable period identity and a deterministic fallback if the chosen model provider fails.\n\n## Interview with proof\nThe supplied project references disagree on some provider/testing details. Treat them as study prompts, not verified claims about a shipped repository. Before naming Claude/Groq, Jest/Vitest or an integration on a resume, check the actual dependency/configuration, code path and test evidence in that project.","exercise":"Build a project creation slice and write its permission tests. Then prepare a two-minute explanation of one real schema/permission decision, citing the actual code/test rather than a planned feature.","interviews":[{"q":"How does a client avoid seeing another client's data?","a":"Verified identity must constrain the actual object/data access across API queries, exports, storage, search and realtime delivery. Test a cross-tenant request through the full boundary instead of relying only on hidden UI."},{"q":"What if the model provider is down?","a":"The optional summary should degrade to a clear deterministic result based on validated task facts while the core portal remains usable. Preserve bounded retries and safe operational evidence."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is a defensible project interview claim?","options":["A feature you can trace through actual code and tests","Every optional service named in an old plan","An invented traffic number","A tool listed only because it is popular"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A project claim should be supported by the actual implementation and evidence."},"skills":[],"minutes":15},{"id":"project-commerce","title":"Design a storefront, inventory and order workflow","body":"## Separate browsing from transaction authority\nA public storefront benefits from discoverable product pages and clear loading/error states. Next.js can support pre-rendered/server-rendered routes, while a client cart provides interaction. The backend remains authoritative for product availability, prices, discounts, tax calculations and final order state.\n\n## Choose data by access patterns\nProducts with varying specifications may suit document modelling; relations and invariants still need explicit design. Index filtering/sorting fields, paginate large collections and avoid loading every product into one response. Redis/CDN caches need freshness and tenant/public scope rules.\n\n## Preserve the transaction\nValidate the cart against current server data, reserve/decrement stock atomically under a defined policy and create an immutable order snapshot. Track pending, paid, fulfilled, cancelled and refunded transitions deliberately. A frontend success callback is not a paid-state authority; verified provider events and reconciliation are required.\n\nRecord base amounts, discounts, applicable tax amounts and totals using a deliberate money/rounding contract. Keep historical invoice/order values stable when current catalog data changes. Address/pincode serviceability and configurable branding are separate validated business features, not reasons to accept arbitrary admin payloads.\n\nOne API can serve web and mobile clients with explicit versioned contracts. Authentication, permissions and rate limits apply equally to each client. Test the last-item race, duplicate payment event, failed notification and a mobile client using an older compatible contract.","exercise":"Draw checkout from validated cart to payment event to fulfilment. Specify atomic stock handling, snapshot fields, duplicate events and a stale-cache scenario.","interviews":[{"q":"How do you stop the last item being sold twice?","a":"Enforce sufficient stock in an atomic database update or suitable transaction/reservation design, then check its result. A prior read or disabled frontend button cannot arbitrate concurrent buyers."},{"q":"Why use server rendering for some storefront routes?","a":"Public product routes may benefit from pre-rendered discoverable content and a controlled caching strategy. A private dashboard can have different requirements; choose per route and verify actual freshness/performance."}],"quiz":{"question":"What must an order snapshot preserve?","options":["The relevant transaction-time item/pricing/address values","Only a pointer to mutable current product details","Only the cart button colour","An unverified paid flag from the browser"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Historical records must remain meaningful after the current catalog changes."},"skills":[],"minutes":15},{"id":"project-interview","title":"Architecture comparisons, testing evidence and scaling","body":"## Compare from requirements\nPERN/MERN labels name some technologies, not the full architecture. Explain why a relational schema fits one set of relationships/transactions and why document modelling fits a particular flexible catalog. State the trade-offs, not that one database is universally superior.\n\n## A useful interview answer\nStart with the concrete user problem, identify the chosen boundary/invariant, explain the implementation and show the evidence. For a webhook, describe raw-body signature verification, duplicate event handling and a negative test. For tenant isolation, show the query condition and a cross-account test.\n\n## Scale the measured bottleneck\nIf traffic grows tenfold, inspect latency breakdown, query plans, cache hit/miss behaviour, connections, queue age and provider quotas. Possible responses include better indexes, pagination, bounded jobs, a correctly scoped cache and horizontal capacity. Do not promise sharding or microservices before identifying the limit.\n\nDocument failure behaviour: invalid input, denied permission, provider outage, a retried request and a cancelled job. Include deployment/migration/restore evidence and a clear list of remaining limitations.\n\nPrepare short answers, then expect a deeper follow-up. A two-sentence explanation that you can expand with a trace/test is stronger than reciting a long list of packages. Keep resume/project claims aligned with the actual repository and release.","exercise":"Prepare answers for database choice, tenant isolation, stock concurrency, provider failure, token storage and tenfold traffic. For each, identify one concrete code/test/measurement you would show.","interviews":[{"q":"What would you change first for ten times the traffic?","a":"Measure the actual saturation and latency path first. Optimise the demonstrated query, connection, queue or provider bottleneck with a testable change rather than choosing a fashionable architecture blindly."},{"q":"How do you explain a technology you planned but did not ship?","a":"Call it planned/optional and distinguish it from implemented work. Explain the design if relevant, but do not claim operational or testing evidence that does not exist."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which answer best demonstrates engineering judgement?","options":["A measured bottleneck, chosen fix and validation","A list of every popular technology","A claim that microservices solve all load","An invented production user count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Requirements, trade-offs and evidence support a useful architecture explanation."},"skills":[],"minutes":15}]},{"id":"placement","title":"Placement Notes","group":"Interview and exam foundations","summary":"Combine formulas, reasoning methods, verbal evidence and CS/output questions with deliberate review.","duration":"Daily short sessions alongside DSA","project":"Use the dashboard, revise due mistakes and compare topic accuracy/time after each short mock.","sources":[{"title":"MIT introduction to algorithms","url":"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/resources/lecture-notes/"},{"title":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"},{"title":"PostgreSQL documentation","url":"https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/"},{"title":"MDN web development","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development"}],"prerequisites":"","lessons":[{"id":"placement-quant","title":"Quantitative aptitude & data interpretation","body":"## Build a method before a timer\nTranslate the question into units and a known relationship. For percentages, keep the reference base explicit; successive changes multiply factors. For ratios, use a common scale. For work, add rates rather than completion times. For speed, convert units before comparing journeys.\n\nSimple interest uses principal, annual rate and time under its stated convention; compound growth multiplies period factors. Profit percentage and discount percentage can use different bases. In mixtures, conserve the actual quantity being mixed. For probability, define the sample space and independence assumptions.\n\n## Read a dataset carefully\nIdentify the population, period and units before calculation. A bar height, table count and percentage share are different quantities. Use the correct denominator for growth/share and compare absolute versus relative change. Do not average percentages from differently sized groups without the appropriate weighting.\n\n## A practical routine\nRead the worked example below, cover its answer and solve it again. Check a boundary or alternative method, then take a topic drill. After a mock, review incorrect and unanswered items and the active time spent on them. Reducing time is useful only while preserving a meaningful accuracy level.\n\nThe topic reference below includes the app's bundled formulas, notes, traps and worked examples, so you can study them here without opening another website.\n\n\n## Bundled topic reference\n\n\n\n\n\n### Percentages\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Percentage = part / whole × 100; new value = old × (1 ± r/100).`\n\nChoose the reference whole before calculating. Successive changes multiply their factors; they do not simply add.\n\n**Shortcut:** Convert 12.5% to 1/8 and 16⅔% to 1/6 when useful.\n\n**Common trap:** A percentage increase and an equal percentage decrease do not cancel.\n\n**Worked example:** A training centre has 400 learners. 5% choose Python. How many choose Python?\n\n**Answer:** 20\n\nUse the full learner count as the base: 400 × 5/100 = 20.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Profit, loss & marked price\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Profit% = (SP − CP)/CP × 100; SP = MP × (1 − discount/100).`\n\nCost price is the base for profit/loss; marked price is the base for discount.\n\n**Shortcut:** Set CP to 100 for percentage-only questions.\n\n**Common trap:** Never divide profit by selling price unless a margin is explicitly requested.\n\n**Worked example:** A keyboard costs Rs 400 and is sold at a 10% profit on cost. Find its selling price in rupees.\n\n**Answer:** 440\n\nProfit = 400×10/100. Add it to cost: 400×110/100 = 440.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Successive discounts\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Equivalent discount = a + b − ab/100.`\n\nApply each discount to the price remaining after the previous one.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use remaining-price multipliers.\n\n**Common trap:** Two 20% discounts produce 36%, not 40%.\n\n**Worked example:** A store applies discounts of 5% and then 10%. What is the equivalent single discount percentage?\n\n**Answer:** 29/2\n\nThe remaining-price factor is 95/100 × 90/100. Discount = 5+10−(5×10/100) = 29/2%.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Simple interest\n\n**Formula / rule:** `SI = PRT/100; amount = P + SI.`\n\nUse consistent time units. Under simple interest, interest is always computed on the original principal.\n\n**Shortcut:** Find interest for one year, then scale.\n\n**Common trap:** Convert months to years before using an annual rate.\n\n**Worked example:** Find simple interest in rupees on Rs 1000 at 4% per year for 2 years.\n\n**Answer:** 80\n\nSI = PRT/100 = 1000×4×2/100 = 80.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Compound interest\n\n**Formula / rule:** `A = P(1+r/100)^n; CI = A−P.`\n\nFor half-yearly compounding, halve the annual rate and double the number of years.\n\n**Shortcut:** For two years, CI−SI = P(r/100)².\n\n**Common trap:** Distinguish compound amount from compound interest.\n\n**Worked example:** Rs 10000 earns 10% per year compounded annually for 2 years. Find the compound interest in rupees.\n\n**Answer:** 2100\n\nAmount = 10000×1.1² = 12100. Subtract principal: CI = 2100.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Ratio & proportion\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Share = total × corresponding ratio / sum of ratio terms.`\n\nRatios compare quantities in the same units; multiply both terms by the same factor.\n\n**Shortcut:** Work in ratio units before substituting the total.\n\n**Common trap:** The denominator for a part of the total is a+b, not the other part.\n\n**Worked example:** A and B divide Rs 180 in the ratio 2:4. How many rupees does A get?\n\n**Answer:** 60\n\nTotal ratio units = 6. One unit = 180/6 = 30. A receives 2×30=60.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Averages & weighted means\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Average = sum/count; combined mean = (n₁m₁+n₂m₂)/(n₁+n₂).`\n\nTreat group size as the weight. Averages of unequal-sized groups cannot be averaged directly.\n\n**Shortcut:** Track the change in the total when a value is replaced.\n\n**Common trap:** A new member changes both sum and count.\n\n**Worked example:** The average of 8 values is 20. A new value 38 is added. Find the new average.\n\n**Answer:** 22\n\nOld sum=160. New sum=198; count=9. Average=22.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Mixtures & alligation\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Quantity at concentration c₁ / quantity at c₂ = (c₂−c)/(c−c₁).`\n\nThe target concentration must lie between the two input concentrations for a positive mixture.\n\n**Shortcut:** Cross differences give the quantity ratio.\n\n**Common trap:** Apply the ratio in the correct low-to-high order.\n\n**Worked example:** A 10% solution and a 50% solution are mixed to get a 30% solution. Find the ratio of low-concentration to high-concentration quantities.\n\n**Answer:** 1:1\n\nAlligation gives (50−30):(30−10) = 20:20 = 1:1.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Partnership\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Profit share ∝ capital × time invested.`\n\nSplit an investment into intervals when capital changes, then add capital-months.\n\n**Shortcut:** Reduce weighted investments by their GCD.\n\n**Common trap:** Equal investments for unequal durations do not get equal profits.\n\n**Worked example:** A invests Rs 2000 for 12 months; B invests Rs 6000 for 6 months. A total profit of Rs 5000 is shared by capital-time. Find A's share.\n\n**Answer:** 2000\n\nWeighted capitals are 24000 and 36000, giving ratio 2:3. A gets 5000×2/5 = 2000.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Problems on ages\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Present ages x,y become x+t,y+t after t years.`\n\nAge differences are constant; ratios change with time.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use ratio units and solve one linear equation.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not add a year count to the ratio terms themselves.\n\n**Worked example:** A is 6 years older than B. Their present ages total 30 years. Find B's age.\n\n**Answer:** 12\n\nLet B=x, A=x+6. Then 2x+6=30, so x=12.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Time & work\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Combined work rate = 1/a + 1/b; time = 1/rate.`\n\nChoose one completed job as the unit of work. Rates add, completion times do not.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use the LCM of completion times as total work units.\n\n**Common trap:** An outlet or undoing worker contributes a negative rate.\n\n**Worked example:** A completes a job in 10 days; B completes it in 20 days. Working together at constant rates, how many days are needed?\n\n**Answer:** 20/3\n\nCombined daily rate = 1/10+1/20 = 3/20. Time is its reciprocal = 20/3 days.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Pipes & cisterns\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Net rate = filling rates − draining rates.`\n\nAssume a constant flow only when stated. An empty tank cannot lose water below zero.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use tank capacity equal to the LCM of the times.\n\n**Common trap:** A net rate of zero or less cannot fill an initially empty tank.\n\n**Worked example:** A pipe fills a tank in 6 hours. A drain empties it in 12 hours. With both open, how many hours fill the initially empty tank?\n\n**Answer:** 12\n\nNet hourly rate = 1/6−1/12=1/12. Time=12 hours.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Time, speed & distance\n\n**Formula / rule:** `d=vt; km/h→m/s multiply by 5/18; equal-distance mean speed=2uv/(u+v).`\n\nUse relative speeds for two moving objects and align distance/time units.\n\n**Shortcut:** For equal distances use the harmonic mean.\n\n**Common trap:** The arithmetic mean applies to equal travel times, not equal distances.\n\n**Worked example:** A car travels the same distance at 30 km/h outward and 60 km/h returning. What is its average speed in km/h?\n\n**Answer:** 40\n\nFor equal distances use 2uv/(u+v) = 2×30×60/90 = 40.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Trains & relative motion\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Time to cross = total length to clear / relative speed.`\n\nFor a pole use train length; for a platform add platform length.\n\n**Shortcut:** Opposite directions add speeds; same direction subtract.\n\n**Common trap:** Convert km/h to m/s when lengths are in metres.\n\n**Worked example:** A 100 m train moving at 36 km/h crosses a 100 m platform. Find the time in seconds.\n\n**Answer:** 20\n\n36 km/h = 10 m/s. Distance to clear = 100+100=200 m. Time=200/10=20 s.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Boats & streams\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Downstream = b+s; upstream = b−s.`\n\nThe still-water speed must exceed the stream speed for upstream travel.\n\n**Shortcut:** b=(down+up)/2; s=(down−up)/2.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not use downstream speed for a return upstream trip.\n\n**Worked example:** A boat moves at 12 km/h in still water. The stream is 2 km/h. How long in hours does it take to travel 30 km upstream?\n\n**Answer:** 3\n\nUpstream speed=12−2=10 km/h. Time=30/10=3 hours.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Divisibility, factors & remainders\n\n**Formula / rule:** `a = dq+r with 0≤r<d; divisibility by 9 uses digit sum.`\n\nUse modular arithmetic for powers and last digits. Factor counts come from prime exponents.\n\n**Shortcut:** Reduce residues at each multiplication.\n\n**Common trap:** A remainder is relative to the given divisor, not a digit by default.\n\n**Worked example:** What is the non-negative remainder when 10² is divided by 7?\n\n**Answer:** 2\n\n10²=100; 100=7×14+2. The remainder is 2.\n\n\n\n\n\n### HCF & LCM\n\n**Formula / rule:** `For two positive integers: gcd(a,b) × lcm(a,b) = ab.`\n\nThe identity applies to two numbers. Euclid repeatedly replaces (a,b) by (b,a mod b).\n\n**Shortcut:** Divide before multiplying to reduce overflow.\n\n**Common trap:** For three numbers use pairwise LCM; do not multiply all numbers and divide by one GCD.\n\n**Worked example:** Find the least common multiple of 12 and 18.\n\n**Answer:** 36\n\ngcd(12,18)=6. lcm=12×18/6=36.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Fractions & simplification\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Apply brackets, powers, multiplication/division, addition/subtraction.`\n\nOperations of equal precedence proceed left to right. Convert mixed numbers carefully.\n\n**Shortcut:** Cancel common factors before multiplication.\n\n**Common trap:** Multiplication does not always precede division; they have equal precedence.\n\n**Worked example:** Evaluate (2 + 5) × 3 − 12 ÷ 4.\n\n**Answer:** 18\n\nBrackets give 7; multiply to get 21; 12÷4=3. Result=18.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Surds, indices & logarithms\n\n**Formula / rule:** `a^m a^n=a^(m+n); logₐ(a^k)=k for a>0,a≠1.`\n\nExponent laws require compatible bases; logarithms require positive arguments.\n\n**Shortcut:** Rewrite all terms using a common base.\n\n**Common trap:** (a+b)² includes the cross term 2ab.\n\n**Worked example:** Evaluate log₂(2^2) + 2³.\n\n**Answer:** 10\n\nlog₂(2^2)=2; 2³=8. Sum=10.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Arithmetic & geometric progressions\n\n**Formula / rule:** `AP: aₙ=a+(n−1)d; Sₙ=n(2a+(n−1)d)/2.`\n\nFirst differences identify an AP; constant ratios identify a GP.\n\n**Shortcut:** For an AP, average of first and last terms times count gives the sum.\n\n**Common trap:** The nth term uses n−1 steps from the first term.\n\n**Worked example:** An arithmetic progression starts at 3 with common difference 2. Find its 8th term.\n\n**Answer:** 17\n\naₙ=a+(n−1)d=3+(8−1)×2=17.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Permutations\n\n**Formula / rule:** `nPr = n!/(n−r)!; repeated letters: n!/(p!q!…).`\n\nOrder matters for arrangements; identical objects do not create new permutations.\n\n**Shortcut:** Fill constrained positions first.\n\n**Common trap:** Treating identical objects as distinct overcounts.\n\n**Worked example:** From 5 distinct runners, in how many ways can ordered first 2 places be assigned?\n\n**Answer:** 20\n\nPositions are distinct, so use nPr=5!/3!=20.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Combinations\n\n**Formula / rule:** `nCr = n!/[r!(n−r)!]; nCr=nC(n−r).`\n\nChoose without ordering when a committee or subset is requested.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use the smaller of r and n−r.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not use permutations for an unordered team.\n\n**Worked example:** A committee of 3 is chosen from 6 people. How many different committees exist?\n\n**Answer:** 20\n\nOrder does not matter: C(6,3)=6×5×4/6=20.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Probability\n\n**Formula / rule:** `P(E)=favourable/equally likely outcomes; P(not E)=1−P(E).`\n\nState replacement and independence assumptions. Without replacement the sample changes.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use the complement for at-least-one questions.\n\n**Common trap:** Mutually exclusive events are generally not independent.\n\n**Worked example:** A bag contains 3 red and 5 blue balls. One ball is drawn uniformly at random. What is the probability it is red?\n\n**Answer:** 3/8\n\nTotal balls=8; favourable=3. Probability=3/8.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Area, perimeter & geometry\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Rectangle A=lw; triangle A=bh/2; circle A=πr²; circumference=2πr.`\n\nKeep length, square units and volume units separate. Similar-area ratios are squares of length ratios.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use π=22/7 only when the question specifies it.\n\n**Common trap:** Area and perimeter do not scale by the same factor.\n\n**Worked example:** A rectangle is 10 cm long and 4 cm wide. Find its area in cm².\n\n**Answer:** 40\n\nArea=length×width=10×4=40 cm².\n\n\n\n\n\n### Volume & surface area\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Cuboid V=lwh; cylinder V=πr²h; cone V=πr²h/3.`\n\nConvert all dimensions before multiplying. One litre is 1000 cubic centimetres.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use dimensional analysis to check cubic units.\n\n**Common trap:** A cone with the same base and height has one third of the cylinder volume.\n\n**Worked example:** Using π=22/7, find the volume in cm³ of a cylinder of radius 7 cm and height 3 cm.\n\n**Answer:** 462\n\nV=πr²h=(22/7)×49×3=462 cm³.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Heights & distances\n\n**Formula / rule:** `tan θ = opposite/adjacent; sin²θ+cos²θ=1.`\n\nDraw the right triangle and identify the angle of elevation before applying a ratio.\n\n**Shortcut:** At 45°, vertical height above eye level equals horizontal distance.\n\n**Common trap:** Add eye height to the triangle height when the observer is above ground.\n\n**Worked example:** An observer's eye is 1 m above ground. A tower top has elevation 45° at horizontal distance 12 m. Find the tower height in metres.\n\n**Answer:** 13\n\ntan45°=1, so the height above eye level equals 12 m. Add eye height 1: 13 m.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Clocks\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Angle = |30h−5.5m| degrees, then use the smaller of θ and 360−θ.`\n\nThe hour hand moves continuously at 0.5° per minute.\n\n**Shortcut:** At a whole hour use 30° times the hour difference.\n\n**Common trap:** The hour hand is not fixed at the hour numeral between whole hours.\n\n**Worked example:** Find the smaller angle in degrees between clock hands at 12:10.\n\n**Answer:** 55\n\nHour-hand angle=5°, minute-hand angle=60°. Difference=55°. The smaller angle is 55°.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Calendars\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Ordinary year shifts weekdays by 1; leap year by 2; Gregorian leap: divisible by 4 except centuries unless divisible by 400.`\n\nUse the Gregorian calendar unless another calendar is stated.\n\n**Shortcut:** Count complete weeks first and keep only the remainder modulo 7.\n\n**Common trap:** 1900 is not a leap year; 2000 is.\n\n**Worked example:** Under the Gregorian calendar, how many days are in 2024?\n\n**Answer:** 366\n\n2024 is divisible by 4 and is not an excluded century, so it is a leap year with 366 days.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Tables & data comparison\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Growth%=(new−old)/old×100; share%=part/total×100.`\n\nRead row/column labels and units first. Distinguish percentage points from relative percentage growth.\n\n**Shortcut:** Compute the denominator before the numerator.\n\n**Common trap:** A higher absolute increase does not necessarily mean higher percentage growth.\n\n**Worked example:** A workshop reports units sold:\nProduct | Monday | Tuesday\nP | 80 | 160\nQ | 100 | 110\nWhat is the percentage increase in sales of P from Monday to Tuesday?\n\n**Answer:** 100\n\nP rises from 80 to 160. Increase=80; percentage=80/80×100=100%.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Caselets & multi-step data\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Weighted average=Σ(value×weight)/Σweights.`\n\nTranslate a caselet into a compact table. Verify totals before answering subquestions.\n\n**Shortcut:** Keep a shared table and annotate units.\n\n**Common trap:** Average price per item is weighted by quantities, not by the number of price categories.\n\n**Worked example:** A shop sells 10 notebooks at Rs 20 each and 20 notebooks at Rs 35 each. Find average revenue per notebook in rupees.\n\n**Answer:** 30\n\nTotal revenue=10×20+20×35=900; total notebooks=30. Divide to get 30.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Conditional probability & sampling\n\n**Formula / rule:** `P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B); without-replacement draws use a changing denominator.`\n\nCount ordered draws or unordered combinations consistently. State the conditioning event explicitly.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use combinations for an unordered sample.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not multiply probabilities as if draws without replacement were independent.\n\n**Worked example:** A bag has 4 red and 6 blue balls. Two are drawn uniformly without replacement. Find the probability that both are red.\n\n**Answer:** 2/15\n\nChoose 2 red balls in C(4,2)=6 ways out of C(10,2)=45 pairs. Probability=2/15.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Repeated mixture replacement\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Amount of original solute remaining = initial amount × (1−removedVolume/totalVolume)^k.`\n\nAfter each replacement, assume thorough mixing before the next draw.\n\n**Shortcut:** Track the original component rather than both components.\n\n**Common trap:** The fraction removed on later rounds applies to the current mixture, not only to the original solute.\n\n**Worked example:** A 100-litre vessel initially contains pure juice. Remove 10 litres and replace with water, mix thoroughly, and repeat for 2 total removal/replacement rounds. How many litres of original juice remain?\n\n**Answer:** 81\n\nEach round keeps 90/100 of the current original juice. Remaining=100×(90/100)^2=81 litres.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Revenue, cost & multi-step interpretation\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Profit=revenue−cost; profit%=profit/cost×100.`\n\nCompute revenue as price times quantity for each category; use total cost as the profit-rate denominator.\n\n**Shortcut:** Build separate revenue and cost columns.\n\n**Common trap:** A revenue-weighted or quantity-weighted average is not necessarily the same as a simple average.\n\n**Worked example:** A shop sells all its stock at the following unit prices:\nItem | Quantity | Cost price | Selling price\nP | 15 | 60 | 80\nQ | 10 | 90 | 120\nAll prices are in rupees. What is the total profit percentage on total cost?\n\n**Answer:** 100/3\n\nTotal revenue=15×80+10×120=2400. Total cost=15×60+10×90=1800. Profit=600; profit%=600/1800×100=100/3%.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Linear equations & quadratic factorisation\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Preserve equality by applying the same operation to both sides; x²−(a+b)x+ab=(x−a)(x−b).`\n\nDefine unknowns before translating a word problem. Check every root against the original conditions.\n\n**Shortcut:** Look for two factors with the required sum and product.\n\n**Common trap:** A root of a transformed equation can violate the original domain.\n\n**Worked example:** If x² - 5x + 6 = 0, find the values of x.\n\n**Answer:** 2 and 3\n\nFactor x²−5x+6 as (x−2)(x−3). Setting either factor to zero gives x=2 or x=3.\n\n\nLibrary books issued by day (books)\nMon: 80; Tue: 100; Wed: 60; Thu: 120; Fri: 140\n\n### Charts, tables and weighted comparisons\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Change % = (new-old)/old x 100; share % = part/total x 100; weighted mean = sum(value x weight)/sum(weight).`\n\nRead units, categories and denominator first. Compute totals explicitly and distinguish counts, shares and growth.\n\n**Shortcut:** Estimate the expected range, then calculate with the correct base.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not average percentages from unequal groups or divide growth by the new value.\n\n**Worked example:** Books chart: What is the percentage increase from Monday to Friday?\n\n**Answer:** 75%\n\nThe increase is 140-80=60 books. Divide by Monday's base 80: 60/80 x 100=75%.","exercise":"Choose two topics below. Solve each worked example with the answer covered, explain the trap and then complete a short focused practice set.","interviews":[{"q":"How should I review a wrong answer in this subject?","a":"State the relevant rule and its assumptions, solve the example without looking, identify why the chosen distractor fails and schedule a focused review instead of memorising an option letter."},{"q":"How should speed and accuracy be balanced?","a":"Begin with a correct method and explicit units/assumptions. Use measured short drills to reduce avoidable time while preserving accuracy, then review skipped and incorrect items by topic."},{"q":"Explain the method for Percentages.","a":"Percentage = part / whole × 100; new value = old × (1 ± r/100).. Choose the reference whole before calculating. Successive changes multiply their factors; they do not simply add. Common trap: A percentage increase and an equal percentage decrease do not cancel."},{"q":"A training centre has 400 learners. 5% choose Python. How many choose Python?","a":"The answer is 20. Use the full learner count as the base: 400 × 5/100 = 20. Method: Percentage = part / whole × 100; new value = old × (1 ± r/100).. Check: A percentage increase and an equal percentage decrease do not cancel."},{"q":"Explain the method for Profit, loss & marked price.","a":"Profit% = (SP − CP)/CP × 100; SP = MP × (1 − discount/100).. Cost price is the base for profit/loss; marked price is the base for discount. Common trap: Never divide profit by selling price unless a margin is explicitly requested."},{"q":"A keyboard costs Rs 400 and is sold at a 10% profit on cost. Find its selling price in rupees.","a":"The answer is 440. Profit = 400×10/100. Add it to cost: 400×110/100 = 440. Method: Profit% = (SP − CP)/CP × 100; SP = MP × (1 − discount/100).. Check: Never divide profit by selling price unless a margin is explicitly requested."},{"q":"Explain the method for Successive discounts.","a":"Equivalent discount = a + b − ab/100.. Apply each discount to the price remaining after the previous one. Common trap: Two 20% discounts produce 36%, not 40%."},{"q":"A store applies discounts of 5% and then 10%. What is the equivalent single discount percentage?","a":"The answer is 29/2. The remaining-price factor is 95/100 × 90/100. Discount = 5+10−(5×10/100) = 29/2%. Method: Equivalent discount = a + b − ab/100.. Check: Two 20% discounts produce 36%, not 40%."},{"q":"Explain the method for Simple interest.","a":"SI = PRT/100; amount = P + SI.. Use consistent time units. Under simple interest, interest is always computed on the original principal. Common trap: Convert months to years before using an annual rate."},{"q":"Find simple interest in rupees on Rs 1000 at 4% per year for 2 years.","a":"The answer is 80. SI = PRT/100 = 1000×4×2/100 = 80. Method: SI = PRT/100; amount = P + SI.. Check: Convert months to years before using an annual rate."},{"q":"Explain the method for Compound interest.","a":"A = P(1+r/100)^n; CI = A−P.. For half-yearly compounding, halve the annual rate and double the number of years. Common trap: Distinguish compound amount from compound interest."},{"q":"Rs 10000 earns 10% per year compounded annually for 2 years. Find the compound interest in rupees.","a":"The answer is 2100. Amount = 10000×1.1² = 12100. Subtract principal: CI = 2100. Method: A = P(1+r/100)^n; CI = A−P.. Check: Distinguish compound amount from compound interest."},{"q":"Explain the method for Ratio & proportion.","a":"Share = total × corresponding ratio / sum of ratio terms.. Ratios compare quantities in the same units; multiply both terms by the same factor. Common trap: The denominator for a part of the total is a+b, not the other part."},{"q":"A and B divide Rs 180 in the ratio 2:4. How many rupees does A get?","a":"The answer is 60. Total ratio units = 6. One unit = 180/6 = 30. A receives 2×30=60. Method: Share = total × corresponding ratio / sum of ratio terms.. Check: The denominator for a part of the total is a+b, not the other part."},{"q":"Explain the method for Averages & weighted means.","a":"Average = sum/count; combined mean = (n₁m₁+n₂m₂)/(n₁+n₂).. Treat group size as the weight. Averages of unequal-sized groups cannot be averaged directly. Common trap: A new member changes both sum and count."},{"q":"The average of 8 values is 20. A new value 38 is added. Find the new average.","a":"The answer is 22. Old sum=160. New sum=198; count=9. Average=22. Method: Average = sum/count; combined mean = (n₁m₁+n₂m₂)/(n₁+n₂).. Check: A new member changes both sum and count."},{"q":"Explain the method for Mixtures & alligation.","a":"Quantity at concentration c₁ / quantity at c₂ = (c₂−c)/(c−c₁).. The target concentration must lie between the two input concentrations for a positive mixture. Common trap: Apply the ratio in the correct low-to-high order."},{"q":"A 10% solution and a 50% solution are mixed to get a 30% solution. Find the ratio of low-concentration to high-concentration quantities.","a":"The answer is 1:1. Alligation gives (50−30):(30−10) = 20:20 = 1:1. Method: Quantity at concentration c₁ / quantity at c₂ = (c₂−c)/(c−c₁).. Check: Apply the ratio in the correct low-to-high order."},{"q":"Explain the method for Partnership.","a":"Profit share ∝ capital × time invested.. Split an investment into intervals when capital changes, then add capital-months. Common trap: Equal investments for unequal durations do not get equal profits."},{"q":"A invests Rs 2000 for 12 months; B invests Rs 6000 for 6 months. A total profit of Rs 5000 is shared by capital-time. Find A's share.","a":"The answer is 2000. Weighted capitals are 24000 and 36000, giving ratio 2:3. A gets 5000×2/5 = 2000. Method: Profit share ∝ capital × time invested.. Check: Equal investments for unequal durations do not get equal profits."},{"q":"Explain the method for Problems on ages.","a":"Present ages x,y become x+t,y+t after t years.. Age differences are constant; ratios change with time. Common trap: Do not add a year count to the ratio terms themselves."},{"q":"A is 6 years older than B. Their present ages total 30 years. Find B's age.","a":"The answer is 12. Let B=x, A=x+6. Then 2x+6=30, so x=12. Method: Present ages x,y become x+t,y+t after t years.. Check: Do not add a year count to the ratio terms themselves."},{"q":"Explain the method for Time & work.","a":"Combined work rate = 1/a + 1/b; time = 1/rate.. Choose one completed job as the unit of work. Rates add, completion times do not. Common trap: An outlet or undoing worker contributes a negative rate."},{"q":"A completes a job in 10 days; B completes it in 20 days. Working together at constant rates, how many days are needed?","a":"The answer is 20/3. Combined daily rate = 1/10+1/20 = 3/20. Time is its reciprocal = 20/3 days. Method: Combined work rate = 1/a + 1/b; time = 1/rate.. Check: An outlet or undoing worker contributes a negative rate."},{"q":"Explain the method for Pipes & cisterns.","a":"Net rate = filling rates − draining rates.. Assume a constant flow only when stated. An empty tank cannot lose water below zero. Common trap: A net rate of zero or less cannot fill an initially empty tank."},{"q":"A pipe fills a tank in 6 hours. A drain empties it in 12 hours. With both open, how many hours fill the initially empty tank?","a":"The answer is 12. Net hourly rate = 1/6−1/12=1/12. Time=12 hours. Method: Net rate = filling rates − draining rates.. Check: A net rate of zero or less cannot fill an initially empty tank."},{"q":"Explain the method for Time, speed & distance.","a":"d=vt; km/h→m/s multiply by 5/18; equal-distance mean speed=2uv/(u+v).. Use relative speeds for two moving objects and align distance/time units. Common trap: The arithmetic mean applies to equal travel times, not equal distances."},{"q":"A car travels the same distance at 30 km/h outward and 60 km/h returning. What is its average speed in km/h?","a":"The answer is 40. For equal distances use 2uv/(u+v) = 2×30×60/90 = 40. Method: d=vt; km/h→m/s multiply by 5/18; equal-distance mean speed=2uv/(u+v).. Check: The arithmetic mean applies to equal travel times, not equal distances."},{"q":"Explain the method for Trains & relative motion.","a":"Time to cross = total length to clear / relative speed.. For a pole use train length; for a platform add platform length. Common trap: Convert km/h to m/s when lengths are in metres."},{"q":"A 100 m train moving at 36 km/h crosses a 100 m platform. Find the time in seconds.","a":"The answer is 20. 36 km/h = 10 m/s. Distance to clear = 100+100=200 m. Time=200/10=20 s. Method: Time to cross = total length to clear / relative speed.. Check: Convert km/h to m/s when lengths are in metres."},{"q":"Explain the method for Boats & streams.","a":"Downstream = b+s; upstream = b−s.. The still-water speed must exceed the stream speed for upstream travel. Common trap: Do not use downstream speed for a return upstream trip."},{"q":"A boat moves at 12 km/h in still water. The stream is 2 km/h. How long in hours does it take to travel 30 km upstream?","a":"The answer is 3. Upstream speed=12−2=10 km/h. Time=30/10=3 hours. Method: Downstream = b+s; upstream = b−s.. Check: Do not use downstream speed for a return upstream trip."},{"q":"Explain the method for Divisibility, factors & remainders.","a":"a = dq+r with 0≤r<d; divisibility by 9 uses digit sum.. Use modular arithmetic for powers and last digits. Factor counts come from prime exponents. Common trap: A remainder is relative to the given divisor, not a digit by default."},{"q":"What is the non-negative remainder when 10² is divided by 7?","a":"The answer is 2. 10²=100; 100=7×14+2. The remainder is 2. Method: a = dq+r with 0≤r<d; divisibility by 9 uses digit sum.. Check: A remainder is relative to the given divisor, not a digit by default."},{"q":"Explain the method for HCF & LCM.","a":"For two positive integers: gcd(a,b) × lcm(a,b) = ab.. The identity applies to two numbers. Euclid repeatedly replaces (a,b) by (b,a mod b). Common trap: For three numbers use pairwise LCM; do not multiply all numbers and divide by one GCD."},{"q":"Find the least common multiple of 12 and 18.","a":"The answer is 36. gcd(12,18)=6. lcm=12×18/6=36. Method: For two positive integers: gcd(a,b) × lcm(a,b) = ab.. Check: For three numbers use pairwise LCM; do not multiply all numbers and divide by one GCD."},{"q":"Explain the method for Fractions & simplification.","a":"Apply brackets, powers, multiplication/division, addition/subtraction.. Operations of equal precedence proceed left to right. Convert mixed numbers carefully. Common trap: Multiplication does not always precede division; they have equal precedence."},{"q":"Evaluate (2 + 5) × 3 − 12 ÷ 4.","a":"The answer is 18. Brackets give 7; multiply to get 21; 12÷4=3. Result=18. Method: Apply brackets, powers, multiplication/division, addition/subtraction.. Check: Multiplication does not always precede division; they have equal precedence."},{"q":"Explain the method for Surds, indices & logarithms.","a":"a^m a^n=a^(m+n); logₐ(a^k)=k for a>0,a≠1.. Exponent laws require compatible bases; logarithms require positive arguments. Common trap: (a+b)² includes the cross term 2ab."},{"q":"Evaluate log₂(2^2) + 2³.","a":"The answer is 10. log₂(2^2)=2; 2³=8. Sum=10. Method: a^m a^n=a^(m+n); logₐ(a^k)=k for a>0,a≠1.. Check: (a+b)² includes the cross term 2ab."},{"q":"Explain the method for Arithmetic & geometric progressions.","a":"AP: aₙ=a+(n−1)d; Sₙ=n(2a+(n−1)d)/2.. First differences identify an AP; constant ratios identify a GP. Common trap: The nth term uses n−1 steps from the first term."},{"q":"An arithmetic progression starts at 3 with common difference 2. Find its 8th term.","a":"The answer is 17. aₙ=a+(n−1)d=3+(8−1)×2=17. Method: AP: aₙ=a+(n−1)d; Sₙ=n(2a+(n−1)d)/2.. Check: The nth term uses n−1 steps from the first term."},{"q":"Explain the method for Permutations.","a":"nPr = n!/(n−r)!; repeated letters: n!/(p!q!…).. Order matters for arrangements; identical objects do not create new permutations. Common trap: Treating identical objects as distinct overcounts."},{"q":"From 5 distinct runners, in how many ways can ordered first 2 places be assigned?","a":"The answer is 20. Positions are distinct, so use nPr=5!/3!=20. Method: nPr = n!/(n−r)!; repeated letters: n!/(p!q!…).. Check: Treating identical objects as distinct overcounts."},{"q":"Explain the method for Combinations.","a":"nCr = n!/[r!(n−r)!]; nCr=nC(n−r).. Choose without ordering when a committee or subset is requested. Common trap: Do not use permutations for an unordered team."},{"q":"A committee of 3 is chosen from 6 people. How many different committees exist?","a":"The answer is 20. Order does not matter: C(6,3)=6×5×4/6=20. Method: nCr = n!/[r!(n−r)!]; nCr=nC(n−r).. Check: Do not use permutations for an unordered team."},{"q":"Explain the method for Probability.","a":"P(E)=favourable/equally likely outcomes; P(not E)=1−P(E).. State replacement and independence assumptions. Without replacement the sample changes. Common trap: Mutually exclusive events are generally not independent."},{"q":"A bag contains 3 red and 5 blue balls. One ball is drawn uniformly at random. What is the probability it is red?","a":"The answer is 3/8. Total balls=8; favourable=3. Probability=3/8. Method: P(E)=favourable/equally likely outcomes; P(not E)=1−P(E).. Check: Mutually exclusive events are generally not independent."},{"q":"Explain the method for Area, perimeter & geometry.","a":"Rectangle A=lw; triangle A=bh/2; circle A=πr²; circumference=2πr.. Keep length, square units and volume units separate. Similar-area ratios are squares of length ratios. Common trap: Area and perimeter do not scale by the same factor."},{"q":"A rectangle is 10 cm long and 4 cm wide. Find its area in cm².","a":"The answer is 40. Area=length×width=10×4=40 cm². Method: Rectangle A=lw; triangle A=bh/2; circle A=πr²; circumference=2πr.. Check: Area and perimeter do not scale by the same factor."},{"q":"Explain the method for Volume & surface area.","a":"Cuboid V=lwh; cylinder V=πr²h; cone V=πr²h/3.. Convert all dimensions before multiplying. One litre is 1000 cubic centimetres. Common trap: A cone with the same base and height has one third of the cylinder volume."},{"q":"Using π=22/7, find the volume in cm³ of a cylinder of radius 7 cm and height 3 cm.","a":"The answer is 462. V=πr²h=(22/7)×49×3=462 cm³. Method: Cuboid V=lwh; cylinder V=πr²h; cone V=πr²h/3.. Check: A cone with the same base and height has one third of the cylinder volume."},{"q":"Explain the method for Heights & distances.","a":"tan θ = opposite/adjacent; sin²θ+cos²θ=1.. Draw the right triangle and identify the angle of elevation before applying a ratio. Common trap: Add eye height to the triangle height when the observer is above ground."},{"q":"An observer's eye is 1 m above ground. A tower top has elevation 45° at horizontal distance 12 m. Find the tower height in metres.","a":"The answer is 13. tan45°=1, so the height above eye level equals 12 m. Add eye height 1: 13 m. Method: tan θ = opposite/adjacent; sin²θ+cos²θ=1.. Check: Add eye height to the triangle height when the observer is above ground."},{"q":"Explain the method for Clocks.","a":"Angle = |30h−5.5m| degrees, then use the smaller of θ and 360−θ.. The hour hand moves continuously at 0.5° per minute. Common trap: The hour hand is not fixed at the hour numeral between whole hours."},{"q":"Find the smaller angle in degrees between clock hands at 12:10.","a":"The answer is 55. Hour-hand angle=5°, minute-hand angle=60°. Difference=55°. The smaller angle is 55°. Method: Angle = |30h−5.5m| degrees, then use the smaller of θ and 360−θ.. Check: The hour hand is not fixed at the hour numeral between whole hours."},{"q":"Explain the method for Calendars.","a":"Ordinary year shifts weekdays by 1; leap year by 2; Gregorian leap: divisible by 4 except centuries unless divisible by 400.. Use the Gregorian calendar unless another calendar is stated. Common trap: 1900 is not a leap year; 2000 is."},{"q":"Under the Gregorian calendar, how many days are in 2024?","a":"The answer is 366. 2024 is divisible by 4 and is not an excluded century, so it is a leap year with 366 days. Method: Ordinary year shifts weekdays by 1; leap year by 2; Gregorian leap: divisible by 4 except centuries unless divisible by 400.. Check: 1900 is not a leap year; 2000 is."},{"q":"Explain the method for Tables & data comparison.","a":"Growth%=(new−old)/old×100; share%=part/total×100.. Read row/column labels and units first. Distinguish percentage points from relative percentage growth. Common trap: A higher absolute increase does not necessarily mean higher percentage growth."},{"q":"A workshop reports units sold:\nProduct | Monday | Tuesday\nP | 80 | 160\nQ | 100 | 110\nWhat is the percentage increase in sales of P from Monday to Tuesday?","a":"The answer is 100. P rises from 80 to 160. Increase=80; percentage=80/80×100=100%. Method: Growth%=(new−old)/old×100; share%=part/total×100.. Check: A higher absolute increase does not necessarily mean higher percentage growth."},{"q":"Explain the method for Caselets & multi-step data.","a":"Weighted average=Σ(value×weight)/Σweights.. Translate a caselet into a compact table. Verify totals before answering subquestions. Common trap: Average price per item is weighted by quantities, not by the number of price categories."},{"q":"A shop sells 10 notebooks at Rs 20 each and 20 notebooks at Rs 35 each. Find average revenue per notebook in rupees.","a":"The answer is 30. Total revenue=10×20+20×35=900; total notebooks=30. Divide to get 30. Method: Weighted average=Σ(value×weight)/Σweights.. Check: Average price per item is weighted by quantities, not by the number of price categories."},{"q":"Explain the method for Conditional probability & sampling.","a":"P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B); without-replacement draws use a changing denominator.. Count ordered draws or unordered combinations consistently. State the conditioning event explicitly. Common trap: Do not multiply probabilities as if draws without replacement were independent."},{"q":"A bag has 4 red and 6 blue balls. Two are drawn uniformly without replacement. Find the probability that both are red.","a":"The answer is 2/15. Choose 2 red balls in C(4,2)=6 ways out of C(10,2)=45 pairs. Probability=2/15. Method: P(A|B)=P(A∩B)/P(B); without-replacement draws use a changing denominator.. Check: Do not multiply probabilities as if draws without replacement were independent."},{"q":"Explain the method for Repeated mixture replacement.","a":"Amount of original solute remaining = initial amount × (1−removedVolume/totalVolume)^k.. After each replacement, assume thorough mixing before the next draw. Common trap: The fraction removed on later rounds applies to the current mixture, not only to the original solute."},{"q":"A 100-litre vessel initially contains pure juice. Remove 10 litres and replace with water, mix thoroughly, and repeat for 2 total removal/replacement rounds. How many litres of original juice remain?","a":"The answer is 81. Each round keeps 90/100 of the current original juice. Remaining=100×(90/100)^2=81 litres. Method: Amount of original solute remaining = initial amount × (1−removedVolume/totalVolume)^k.. Check: The fraction removed on later rounds applies to the current mixture, not only to the original solute."},{"q":"Explain the method for Revenue, cost & multi-step interpretation.","a":"Profit=revenue−cost; profit%=profit/cost×100.. Compute revenue as price times quantity for each category; use total cost as the profit-rate denominator. Common trap: A revenue-weighted or quantity-weighted average is not necessarily the same as a simple average."},{"q":"A shop sells all its stock at the following unit prices:\nItem | Quantity | Cost price | Selling price\nP | 15 | 60 | 80\nQ | 10 | 90 | 120\nAll prices are in rupees. What is the total profit percentage on total cost?","a":"The answer is 100/3. Total revenue=15×80+10×120=2400. Total cost=15×60+10×90=1800. Profit=600; profit%=600/1800×100=100/3%. Method: Profit=revenue−cost; profit%=profit/cost×100.. Check: A revenue-weighted or quantity-weighted average is not necessarily the same as a simple average."},{"q":"Explain the method for Linear equations & quadratic factorisation.","a":"Preserve equality by applying the same operation to both sides; x²−(a+b)x+ab=(x−a)(x−b).. Define unknowns before translating a word problem. Check every root against the original conditions. Common trap: A root of a transformed equation can violate the original domain."},{"q":"If x² - 5x + 6 = 0, find the values of x.","a":"The answer is 2 and 3. Factor x²−5x+6 as (x−2)(x−3). Setting either factor to zero gives x=2 or x=3. Method: Preserve equality by applying the same operation to both sides; x²−(a+b)x+ab=(x−a)(x−b).. Check: A root of a transformed equation can violate the original domain."},{"q":"Explain the method for Charts, tables and weighted comparisons.","a":"Change % = (new-old)/old x 100; share % = part/total x 100; weighted mean = sum(value x weight)/sum(weight).. Read units, categories and denominator first. Compute totals explicitly and distinguish counts, shares and growth. Common trap: Do not average percentages from unequal groups or divide growth by the new value."},{"q":"Books chart: What is the percentage increase from Monday to Friday?","a":"The answer is 75%. The increase is 140-80=60 books. Divide by Monday's base 80: 60/80 x 100=75%. Method: Change % = (new-old)/old x 100; share % = part/total x 100; weighted mean = sum(value x weight)/sum(weight).. Check: Do not average percentages from unequal groups or divide growth by the new value."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the strongest review habit?","options":["Explain the method and why the distractor fails","Memorise only the option letter","Ignore units and assumptions","Reduce time even when accuracy collapses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A reusable explanation exposes the underlying mistake and transfers to new questions."},"skills":[],"minutes":175},{"id":"placement-reasoning","title":"Logical and diagram-based reasoning","body":"## Separate evidence from assumption\nRepresent the given relationships explicitly before choosing an answer. In a syllogism, all/none/some statements have different logical strength, and existence must not be invented. In arrangements, track fixed positions, adjacency, direction and exclusion constraints separately.\n\n## Trace a pattern with a rule\nFor a number/shape sequence, describe the change at every step and test that one rule explains all shown terms. Rotation, reflection, count and fill can be independent dimensions. A plausible next picture is insufficient if it ignores a second changing feature.\n\nFor blood relations and directions, choose a consistent reference and draw a small labelled structure. For coding/decoding, test the transform across every provided example instead of matching one coincidence. In data sufficiency, judge whether the statements determine one answer under the exact combination rules; do not solve a different question.\n\n## Review usefully\nRecord the assumption that caused a mistake and rewrite the valid inference. Use the answer palette to mark a difficult item, move on deliberately and return when time allows. A marked answer still counts if selected; an unanswered item contributes no correct answer.\n\nThe reference below includes topic-specific rules and examples, including the original diagram questions added for this upgrade.\n\n\n## Bundled topic reference\n\n\n\n\n\n### Number & letter series\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Inspect first/second differences, ratios and alternating subsequences.`\n\nUse the simplest consistent rule; a finite list alone never proves a unique mathematical continuation.\n\n**Shortcut:** Write differences underneath the series.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not assume a constant gap without checking it.\n\n**Worked example:** The sequence is arithmetic with a constant difference: 2, 5, 8, 11, ?. Find the next term.\n\n**Answer:** 14\n\nEach gap is 3; add it to 11 to obtain 14.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Coding & decoding\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Alphabet shift: index(new)=(index(old)+k) mod 26.`\n\nApply the same declared encoding rule to every letter.\n\n**Shortcut:** Map A=0,…,Z=25 for cyclic shifts.\n\n**Common trap:** Z wraps around to A.\n\n**Worked example:** Every capital letter is shifted 1 places forward cyclically (Z wraps to A). Encode CODE.\n\n**Answer:** DPEF\n\nApply +1 modulo 26 to each letter: CODE → DPEF.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Directions & displacement\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Final displacement = (sum east−west, sum north−south); distance=√(x²+y²).`\n\nDistinguish path length from shortest distance.\n\n**Shortcut:** Draw a coordinate grid.\n\n**Common trap:** A turn depends on the direction currently faced.\n\n**Worked example:** A person walks 3 m east, then 4 m north. What is the shortest distance from the start in metres?\n\n**Answer:** 5\n\nThe legs are perpendicular. √(3²+4²)=5 m (a scaled 3-4-5 triangle).\n\n\n\n\n\n### Ranking & order\n\n**Formula / rule:** `For one person: total=rank from top+rank from bottom−1.`\n\nFor two people, count the positions strictly between them.\n\n**Shortcut:** Place ranks on a number line.\n\n**Common trap:** The person counted in both directions is counted twice unless you subtract one.\n\n**Worked example:** A learner ranks 4th from the top and 9th from the bottom. How many learners are there?\n\n**Answer:** 12\n\nTotal=4+9−1=12; subtract the learner counted twice.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Blood relations\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Translate each statement into a family-tree edge.`\n\nUse only explicitly stated gender and parentage.\n\n**Shortcut:** Draw generations on separate rows.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not assume a spouse or sibling unless the stem states it.\n\n**Worked example:** Asha is the mother of K. K is the father of M. What is Asha's relation to M?\n\n**Answer:** Grandmother\n\nAsha is one generation above K and two above M; she is M's paternal grandmother.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Linear & circular arrangements\n\n**Formula / rule:** `For a line, fix orientation before translating left/right.`\n\nUse all constraints; test whether the arrangement is unique.\n\n**Shortcut:** Anchor a fixed position and place immediate neighbours first.\n\n**Common trap:** People facing south have their left/right reversed relative to your drawing.\n\n**Worked example:** 6 people face north in a row. P is at position 2 from the left; Q is immediately to P's right. What is Q's position from the right?\n\n**Answer:** 4\n\nQ is 3 from the left. From the right: 6−3+1=4.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Syllogisms & Venn logic\n\n**Formula / rule:** `All A are B means A⊆B; some A are B means A∩B is nonempty.`\n\nUniversal statements do not imply that the class has members.\n\n**Shortcut:** Look for a counterexample to disprove a claimed conclusion.\n\n**Common trap:** All A are B does not imply all B are A.\n\n**Worked example:** All coders are readers. No readers are artists. Which conclusion must follow?\n\n**Answer:** No A are C\n\nEvery member of A lies inside B, while B and C are disjoint. Therefore A and C are disjoint too. Here A=coders, B=readers, C=artists.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Inequalities\n\n**Formula / rule:** `If a>b and b≥c, then a>c.`\n\nChain compatible comparison signs and keep strictness.\n\n**Shortcut:** Draw ordered quantities in a line.\n\n**Common trap:** A>B and C>B alone do not compare A and C.\n\n**Worked example:** If A > B, B ≥ C, and C = 2, which is necessarily true?\n\n**Answer:** A > C\n\nA>B≥C implies A>C. Equality of B and C does not remove the strict inequality between A and B.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Data sufficiency\n\n**Formula / rule:** `A statement is sufficient only if it uniquely determines the requested value.`\n\nTest each statement alone, then together.\n\n**Shortcut:** Try two possible values satisfying the data.\n\n**Common trap:** Enough equations is not sufficient if they are dependent.\n\n**Worked example:** Find positive integers x and y individually. I: x+y=10. II: x−y=-2. Which data suffice?\n\n**Answer:** Both together only\n\nI alone allows multiple pairs; II alone also allows multiple pairs. Together, add the equations to get 2x=8, so x=4, y=6.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Assumptions & arguments\n\n**Formula / rule:** `A necessary assumption is a condition the argument needs.`\n\nDistinguish the author’s claim from external facts.\n\n**Shortcut:** Negate an assumption and see if the reasoning breaks.\n\n**Common trap:** A useful fact is not automatically a necessary assumption.\n\n**Worked example:** An organiser says: \"Opening the bus one hour earlier will help people who can attend only before its current opening time.\" Which assumption is needed?\n\n**Answer:** Those people can use the earlier hour\n\nThe proposal helps the stated group only if at least some of that group can attend during the newly available hour.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Conclusions & deductions\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Accept only conclusions forced by the stated premises.`\n\nDo not add real-world knowledge to formal deduction.\n\n**Shortcut:** Construct a model in which premises hold but the conclusion fails.\n\n**Common trap:** Possibility is weaker than necessity.\n\n**Worked example:** In batch 1, every person who passed module A also passed module B. R did not pass B. What follows?\n\n**Answer:** R did not pass A\n\nA-pass implies B-pass. Its contrapositive is not-B-pass implies not-A-pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Analogies & classification\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Identify the relation before matching a pair.`\n\nA shared superficial feature may not preserve the relation.\n\n**Shortcut:** Describe the relation in one short sentence.\n\n**Common trap:** Reverse direction can change the relation.\n\n**Worked example:** Thermometer : temperature :: Barometer : ? \n\n**Answer:** pressure\n\nThe relation is preserved: Thermometer relates to temperature in the same way that Barometer relates to pressure.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Sets & Venn diagrams\n\n**Formula / rule:** `|A∪B|=|A|+|B|−|A∩B|.`\n\nSubtract overlap once when counting at least one category.\n\n**Shortcut:** Fill the intersection first.\n\n**Common trap:** Neither=total−union; do not subtract the two groups independently.\n\n**Worked example:** Of 80 learners, 35 study Python, 30 study SQL and 10 study both. How many study neither?\n\n**Answer:** 25\n\nAt least one=35+30−10=55. Neither=80−55=25.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Cubes & spatial reasoning\n\n**Formula / rule:** `A painted n×n×n cube has 8 corner cubes; exactly-two-face cubes=12(n−2), n≥2.`\n\nClassify small cubes into corners, edges, faces and interior.\n\n**Shortcut:** Count by location rather than drawing every cube.\n\n**Common trap:** Interior cubes have no painted face.\n\n**Worked example:** A cube is painted on all outer faces, then cut into 3×3×3 equal small cubes. How many have exactly two painted faces?\n\n**Answer:** 12\n\nOnly edge cubes excluding corners have exactly two painted faces. Each of 12 edges contributes 3−2=1; total=12.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Counting arrangements & constraints\n\n**Formula / rule:** `For independent choices multiply; for mutually exclusive cases add.`\n\nState whether repetitions and order are allowed.\n\n**Shortcut:** Count the complement when forbidden cases are simpler.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not multiply choices that are not independent.\n\n**Worked example:** A code has one letter chosen from 3 distinct allowed letters followed by one digit from 4 allowed digits. How many codes are possible?\n\n**Answer:** 12\n\nEach of 3 letter choices pairs with any of 4 digit choices: 3×4=12.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Constraint puzzles & exhaustive reasoning\n\n**Formula / rule:** `List feasible assignments, then eliminate those violating any condition.`\n\nRecord each constraint precisely. A count question may have several feasible arrangements.\n\n**Shortcut:** Place the strongest constraints first.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not choose one plausible arrangement and assume it is the only one.\n\n**Worked example:** Five people A,B,C,D,E occupy positions 1–5, one person per position. A is left of B; C and D are exactly 1 position(s) apart; E is at a position no greater than 4. How many arrangements satisfy all conditions? (Use ordinary distance |pos(C)−pos(D)|.)\n\n**Answer:** 18\n\nStart with 5!=120 distinct position assignments. A<B leaves 60. Enforcing |C−D|=1 leaves 24. Finally E≤4 leaves 18 valid arrangements. Enumerating with these three tests avoids assuming any unstated adjacency or orientation.\n\n\nRight, Down, Left, Up, missing.\n\n\n### Diagram sequences and stated rules\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Track shape, orientation, fill and count separately; apply the stated transformation.`\n\nA diagram can change along several independent dimensions. Describe the rule for each, then verify every shown step.\n\n**Shortcut:** Annotate the direction or count beneath each figure before choosing.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not assume a rotation is a reflection or invent an unstated extra rule.\n\n**Worked example:** D1: The arrow turns 90 degrees clockwise at every step. Which direction replaces the question mark?\n\n**Answer:** Right\n\nRight becomes Down, then Left, then Up. Another 90-degree clockwise turn returns to Right.","exercise":"Choose two topics below. Solve each worked example with the answer covered, explain the trap and then complete a short focused practice set.","interviews":[{"q":"How should I review a wrong answer in this subject?","a":"State the relevant rule and its assumptions, solve the example without looking, identify why the chosen distractor fails and schedule a focused review instead of memorising an option letter."},{"q":"How should speed and accuracy be balanced?","a":"Begin with a correct method and explicit units/assumptions. Use measured short drills to reduce avoidable time while preserving accuracy, then review skipped and incorrect items by topic."},{"q":"Explain the method for Number & letter series.","a":"Inspect first/second differences, ratios and alternating subsequences.. Use the simplest consistent rule; a finite list alone never proves a unique mathematical continuation. Common trap: Do not assume a constant gap without checking it."},{"q":"The sequence is arithmetic with a constant difference: 2, 5, 8, 11, ?. Find the next term.","a":"The answer is 14. Each gap is 3; add it to 11 to obtain 14. Method: Inspect first/second differences, ratios and alternating subsequences.. Check: Do not assume a constant gap without checking it."},{"q":"Explain the method for Coding & decoding.","a":"Alphabet shift: index(new)=(index(old)+k) mod 26.. Apply the same declared encoding rule to every letter. Common trap: Z wraps around to A."},{"q":"Every capital letter is shifted 1 places forward cyclically (Z wraps to A). Encode CODE.","a":"The answer is DPEF. Apply +1 modulo 26 to each letter: CODE → DPEF. Method: Alphabet shift: index(new)=(index(old)+k) mod 26.. Check: Z wraps around to A."},{"q":"Explain the method for Directions & displacement.","a":"Final displacement = (sum east−west, sum north−south); distance=√(x²+y²).. Distinguish path length from shortest distance. Common trap: A turn depends on the direction currently faced."},{"q":"A person walks 3 m east, then 4 m north. What is the shortest distance from the start in metres?","a":"The answer is 5. The legs are perpendicular. √(3²+4²)=5 m (a scaled 3-4-5 triangle). Method: Final displacement = (sum east−west, sum north−south); distance=√(x²+y²).. Check: A turn depends on the direction currently faced."},{"q":"Explain the method for Ranking & order.","a":"For one person: total=rank from top+rank from bottom−1.. For two people, count the positions strictly between them. Common trap: The person counted in both directions is counted twice unless you subtract one."},{"q":"A learner ranks 4th from the top and 9th from the bottom. How many learners are there?","a":"The answer is 12. Total=4+9−1=12; subtract the learner counted twice. Method: For one person: total=rank from top+rank from bottom−1.. Check: The person counted in both directions is counted twice unless you subtract one."},{"q":"Explain the method for Blood relations.","a":"Translate each statement into a family-tree edge.. Use only explicitly stated gender and parentage. Common trap: Do not assume a spouse or sibling unless the stem states it."},{"q":"Asha is the mother of K. K is the father of M. What is Asha's relation to M?","a":"The answer is Grandmother. Asha is one generation above K and two above M; she is M's paternal grandmother. Method: Translate each statement into a family-tree edge.. Check: Do not assume a spouse or sibling unless the stem states it."},{"q":"Explain the method for Linear & circular arrangements.","a":"For a line, fix orientation before translating left/right.. Use all constraints; test whether the arrangement is unique. Common trap: People facing south have their left/right reversed relative to your drawing."},{"q":"6 people face north in a row. P is at position 2 from the left; Q is immediately to P's right. What is Q's position from the right?","a":"The answer is 4. Q is 3 from the left. From the right: 6−3+1=4. Method: For a line, fix orientation before translating left/right.. Check: People facing south have their left/right reversed relative to your drawing."},{"q":"Explain the method for Syllogisms & Venn logic.","a":"All A are B means A⊆B; some A are B means A∩B is nonempty.. Universal statements do not imply that the class has members. Common trap: All A are B does not imply all B are A."},{"q":"All coders are readers. No readers are artists. Which conclusion must follow?","a":"The answer is No A are C. Every member of A lies inside B, while B and C are disjoint. Therefore A and C are disjoint too. Here A=coders, B=readers, C=artists. Method: All A are B means A⊆B; some A are B means A∩B is nonempty.. Check: All A are B does not imply all B are A."},{"q":"Explain the method for Inequalities.","a":"If a>b and b≥c, then a>c.. Chain compatible comparison signs and keep strictness. Common trap: A>B and C>B alone do not compare A and C."},{"q":"If A > B, B ≥ C, and C = 2, which is necessarily true?","a":"The answer is A > C. A>B≥C implies A>C. Equality of B and C does not remove the strict inequality between A and B. Method: If a>b and b≥c, then a>c.. Check: A>B and C>B alone do not compare A and C."},{"q":"Explain the method for Data sufficiency.","a":"A statement is sufficient only if it uniquely determines the requested value.. Test each statement alone, then together. Common trap: Enough equations is not sufficient if they are dependent."},{"q":"Find positive integers x and y individually. I: x+y=10. II: x−y=-2. Which data suffice?","a":"The answer is Both together only. I alone allows multiple pairs; II alone also allows multiple pairs. Together, add the equations to get 2x=8, so x=4, y=6. Method: A statement is sufficient only if it uniquely determines the requested value.. Check: Enough equations is not sufficient if they are dependent."},{"q":"Explain the method for Assumptions & arguments.","a":"A necessary assumption is a condition the argument needs.. Distinguish the author’s claim from external facts. Common trap: A useful fact is not automatically a necessary assumption."},{"q":"An organiser says: \"Opening the bus one hour earlier will help people who can attend only before its current opening time.\" Which assumption is needed?","a":"The answer is Those people can use the earlier hour. The proposal helps the stated group only if at least some of that group can attend during the newly available hour. Method: A necessary assumption is a condition the argument needs.. Check: A useful fact is not automatically a necessary assumption."},{"q":"Explain the method for Conclusions & deductions.","a":"Accept only conclusions forced by the stated premises.. Do not add real-world knowledge to formal deduction. Common trap: Possibility is weaker than necessity."},{"q":"In batch 1, every person who passed module A also passed module B. R did not pass B. What follows?","a":"The answer is R did not pass A. A-pass implies B-pass. Its contrapositive is not-B-pass implies not-A-pass. Method: Accept only conclusions forced by the stated premises.. Check: Possibility is weaker than necessity."},{"q":"Explain the method for Analogies & classification.","a":"Identify the relation before matching a pair.. A shared superficial feature may not preserve the relation. Common trap: Reverse direction can change the relation."},{"q":"Thermometer : temperature :: Barometer : ? ","a":"The answer is pressure. The relation is preserved: Thermometer relates to temperature in the same way that Barometer relates to pressure. Method: Identify the relation before matching a pair.. Check: Reverse direction can change the relation."},{"q":"Explain the method for Sets & Venn diagrams.","a":"|A∪B|=|A|+|B|−|A∩B|.. Subtract overlap once when counting at least one category. Common trap: Neither=total−union; do not subtract the two groups independently."},{"q":"Of 80 learners, 35 study Python, 30 study SQL and 10 study both. How many study neither?","a":"The answer is 25. At least one=35+30−10=55. Neither=80−55=25. Method: |A∪B|=|A|+|B|−|A∩B|.. Check: Neither=total−union; do not subtract the two groups independently."},{"q":"Explain the method for Cubes & spatial reasoning.","a":"A painted n×n×n cube has 8 corner cubes; exactly-two-face cubes=12(n−2), n≥2.. Classify small cubes into corners, edges, faces and interior. Common trap: Interior cubes have no painted face."},{"q":"A cube is painted on all outer faces, then cut into 3×3×3 equal small cubes. How many have exactly two painted faces?","a":"The answer is 12. Only edge cubes excluding corners have exactly two painted faces. Each of 12 edges contributes 3−2=1; total=12. Method: A painted n×n×n cube has 8 corner cubes; exactly-two-face cubes=12(n−2), n≥2.. Check: Interior cubes have no painted face."},{"q":"Explain the method for Counting arrangements & constraints.","a":"For independent choices multiply; for mutually exclusive cases add.. State whether repetitions and order are allowed. Common trap: Do not multiply choices that are not independent."},{"q":"A code has one letter chosen from 3 distinct allowed letters followed by one digit from 4 allowed digits. How many codes are possible?","a":"The answer is 12. Each of 3 letter choices pairs with any of 4 digit choices: 3×4=12. Method: For independent choices multiply; for mutually exclusive cases add.. Check: Do not multiply choices that are not independent."},{"q":"Explain the method for Constraint puzzles & exhaustive reasoning.","a":"List feasible assignments, then eliminate those violating any condition.. Record each constraint precisely. A count question may have several feasible arrangements. Common trap: Do not choose one plausible arrangement and assume it is the only one."},{"q":"Five people A,B,C,D,E occupy positions 1–5, one person per position. A is left of B; C and D are exactly 1 position(s) apart; E is at a position no greater than 4. How many arrangements satisfy all conditions? (Use ordinary distance |pos(C)−pos(D)|.)","a":"The answer is 18. Start with 5!=120 distinct position assignments. A<B leaves 60. Enforcing |C−D|=1 leaves 24. Finally E≤4 leaves 18 valid arrangements. Enumerating with these three tests avoids assuming any unstated adjacency or orientation. Method: List feasible assignments, then eliminate those violating any condition.. Check: Do not choose one plausible arrangement and assume it is the only one."},{"q":"Explain the method for Diagram sequences and stated rules.","a":"Track shape, orientation, fill and count separately; apply the stated transformation.. A diagram can change along several independent dimensions. Describe the rule for each, then verify every shown step. Common trap: Do not assume a rotation is a reflection or invent an unstated extra rule."},{"q":"D1: The arrow turns 90 degrees clockwise at every step. Which direction replaces the question mark?","a":"The answer is Right. Right becomes Down, then Left, then Up. Another 90-degree clockwise turn returns to Right. Method: Track shape, orientation, fill and count separately; apply the stated transformation.. Check: Do not assume a rotation is a reflection or invent an unstated extra rule."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the strongest review habit?","options":["Explain the method and why the distractor fails","Memorise only the option letter","Ignore units and assumptions","Reduce time even when accuracy collapses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A reusable explanation exposes the underlying mistake and transfers to new questions."},"skills":[],"minutes":85},{"id":"placement-verbal","title":"Verbal ability and reading comprehension","body":"## Read for a supported claim\nIdentify the passage's main point, evidence and limits. Distinguish a directly stated fact, a necessary inference and a plausible outside assumption. Choose an answer supported by this passage rather than by personal knowledge about the subject.\n\n## Grammar is a relationship\nCheck subject/verb agreement, tense sequence, pronoun reference, modifiers and parallel structure in context. A familiar phrase can still be wrong for the sentence's intended meaning. For vocabulary, use surrounding evidence and note the tone rather than assuming every synonym is interchangeable.\n\nFor sentence ordering, look for an introduction, references that need an earlier noun, logical connectors and a conclusion. For concise writing, preserve meaning while removing redundancy; do not remove a qualification that changes the claim.\n\n## A timed passage routine\nRead the question, locate the relevant paragraph, state the evidence in your own words and eliminate choices that exaggerate, reverse or go beyond it. Main-idea and inference questions may require the whole passage, not one isolated keyword.\n\nAfter review, write why the distractor was tempting and which exact phrase/relationship rejects it. This develops a reusable reading method more effectively than memorising an answer letter. Worked topic notes and passage questions are bundled below.\n\n\n## Bundled topic reference\n\n\n\n\n\n### Subject–verb agreement\n\n**Formula / rule:** `The finite verb agrees with the grammatical subject.`\n\nIgnore intervening prepositional phrases when finding the subject.\n\n**Shortcut:** Find the subject before selecting the verb.\n\n**Common trap:** Words next to the verb are not always the subject.\n\n**Worked example:** Each of the candidates ___ a registration number.\n\n**Answer:** has\n\nEach is singular, so it takes has.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Tenses & aspect\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Use present perfect for a past event connected to the present; past perfect for an earlier past event.`\n\nChoose tense using time markers and sequence.\n\n**Shortcut:** Mark which event happened first.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not use present perfect with a finished past-time marker such as yesterday.\n\n**Worked example:** By the time the interview began, she ___ the form.\n\n**Answer:** had completed\n\nCompletion happened before a past event, so use past perfect.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Articles & determiners\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Use a/an by the following sound; the marks a specific referent.`\n\nSound, countability and specificity determine the article.\n\n**Shortcut:** Say the phrase aloud.\n\n**Common trap:** A university starts with a consonant sound; an hour begins with a vowel sound.\n\n**Worked example:** She waited for ___ hour.\n\n**Answer:** an\n\nHour starts with a vowel sound because h is silent.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Prepositions & collocations\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Use the preposition required by the adjective/verb and intended relationship.`\n\nMany combinations are conventional and need contextual practice.\n\n**Shortcut:** Learn short chunks: interested in, responsible for.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not translate a preposition mechanically from another language.\n\n**Worked example:** She is interested ___ data science.\n\n**Answer:** in\n\nThe standard collocation is interested in.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Active & passive voice\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Passive = object + suitable be + past participle (+ by agent).`\n\nPreserve tense, meaning and agreement when changing voice.\n\n**Shortcut:** Identify subject, verb and object first.\n\n**Common trap:** Only transitive uses normally admit a direct passive.\n\n**Worked example:** Choose the passive form of: The team completed the project.\n\n**Answer:** The project was completed by the team.\n\nSimple past completed becomes was completed; project becomes the passive subject.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Reported speech\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Report the message with suitable tense, pronoun and time-reference changes.`\n\nBackshift is common after a past reporting verb, but universal truths can retain present tense.\n\n**Shortcut:** Identify who said what and when.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not retain the original speaker’s pronouns without checking the referent.\n\n**Worked example:** Ravi said, \"I am tired.\" Choose the usual reported form.\n\n**Answer:** Ravi said that he was tired.\n\nAfter said, am normally backshifts to was and I refers to Ravi.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Parallelism & sentence correction\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Coordinate grammatical structures should be parallel.`\n\nCompare list items and structures joined by and/or.\n\n**Shortcut:** Read only the coordinated elements.\n\n**Common trap:** Mixing gerunds and infinitives can break a parallel list.\n\n**Worked example:** Choose the sentence with parallel activity forms.\n\n**Answer:** She enjoys reading, writing, and coding.\n\nAll three objects of enjoys are gerunds.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Synonyms & antonyms\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Use the sense required by context, not merely a related word.`\n\nIdentify whether the task asks similarity or opposition.\n\n**Shortcut:** Substitute each candidate into the sentence.\n\n**Common trap:** A synonym may fit one sense of a word but not another.\n\n**Worked example:** Choose the closest synonym of concise.\n\n**Answer:** brief\n\nConcise means expressing much in few words.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Idioms, phrases & usage\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Interpret the conventional figurative meaning in context.`\n\nAn idiom’s meaning is often not a literal sum of its words.\n\n**Shortcut:** Learn an example sentence with each idiom.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not infer a literal physical action from an idiom.\n\n**Worked example:** What does \"get the ball rolling\" mean in a project meeting?\n\n**Answer:** start the activity\n\nThe idiom means initiate an activity.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Spelling & word choice\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Choose the standard spelling and the word whose meaning fits.`\n\nSeparate homophones by their grammatical function.\n\n**Shortcut:** Check the part of speech the sentence needs.\n\n**Common trap:** Similar pronunciation does not imply interchangeable spelling.\n\n**Worked example:** Choose the correct spelling of the word for a place to stay.\n\n**Answer:** accommodation\n\nAccommodation has double c and double m.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Reading comprehension & inference\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Support each answer with passage evidence; inference must follow without extra assumptions.`\n\nRead for the author’s central claim, supporting evidence, contrast and limits.\n\n**Shortcut:** Summarise each paragraph in one line.\n\n**Common trap:** A plausible statement may still be unsupported by the passage.\n\n**Worked example:** The library extended its opening hours for a one-month trial. Visits increased during that month. The manager noted that a holiday occurred in the same period and requested more data before attributing the increase to the longer hours.\nWhich conclusion is best supported?\n\n**Answer:** The trial alone does not establish that longer hours caused the increase.\n\nThe manager explicitly notes another possible influence and asks for more data. A simultaneous increase is not proof of causation.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Paragraph ordering & coherence\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Find the introduction, references, connectors and conclusion.`\n\nPronouns and expressions like this result need an earlier referent.\n\n**Shortcut:** Lock clear sentence pairs before ordering the full paragraph.\n\n**Common trap:** A grammatically correct sentence may still be misplaced in the argument.\n\n**Worked example:** Order these sentences about a library survey:\nA: Therefore, the team revised its plan.\nB: The team first collected visitor feedback.\nC: This feedback revealed a need for quieter spaces.\n\n**Answer:** B-C-A\n\nB introduces the feedback; C refers to this feedback; A states the resulting action.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Cloze tests & contextual completion\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Choose a word that fits both grammar and the passage’s logical relationship.`\n\nRead the full sentence, then surrounding context.\n\n**Shortcut:** Look for contrast, cause or continuation markers.\n\n**Common trap:** A common word may be semantically wrong despite fitting grammar.\n\n**Worked example:** The library received more visitors; ___, its waiting time decreased after the new booking system was introduced.\n\n**Answer:** however\n\nThe second clause contrasts higher demand with lower waiting time, so however fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Parts of speech, plurals & one-word substitutions\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Identify a word’s function in its sentence; distinguish a general category from a precise term.`\n\nAdverbs modify actions or descriptions; adjectives describe nouns. Some borrowed nouns have irregular plurals.\n\n**Shortcut:** Use sentence function and meaning together.\n\n**Common trap:** A broad term can be related without being the most precise answer.\n\n**Worked example:** Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'He runs QUICKLY.'\n\n**Answer:** Adverb\n\nQuickly modifies the action runs, explaining how the action happens, so it functions as an adverb.\n\nA study club compared its members' revision logs over six weeks. Members who used short sessions spread across several days remembered more of the material on a later quiz than members who concentrated their study into one evening. The club did not assign schedules randomly: members chose when and how to study. Several other differences, including prior familiarity and total practice time, were not controlled. The organisers therefore recommended trying spaced sessions and measuring personal results, while cautioning that their logs alone did not prove which factor caused the score difference.\n\n\n\n### Passage evidence and inference\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Separate stated facts, supported inferences and claims beyond the passage.`\n\nUse the exact scope, qualifiers and evidence in the passage. A plausible outside fact is not a supported answer.\n\n**Shortcut:** Reject choices that reverse, exaggerate or introduce a new claim.\n\n**Common trap:** Association alone does not establish causation; a partial result is not a universal guarantee.\n\n**Worked example:** Passage A: Which conclusion best matches the organisers' evidence?\n\n**Answer:** Spaced sessions were associated with higher later scores in these logs.\n\nThe passage reports an observed association and explicitly says schedules were self-selected and other factors uncontrolled.","exercise":"Choose two topics below. Solve each worked example with the answer covered, explain the trap and then complete a short focused practice set.","interviews":[{"q":"How should I review a wrong answer in this subject?","a":"State the relevant rule and its assumptions, solve the example without looking, identify why the chosen distractor fails and schedule a focused review instead of memorising an option letter."},{"q":"How should speed and accuracy be balanced?","a":"Begin with a correct method and explicit units/assumptions. Use measured short drills to reduce avoidable time while preserving accuracy, then review skipped and incorrect items by topic."},{"q":"Explain the method for Subject–verb agreement.","a":"The finite verb agrees with the grammatical subject.. Ignore intervening prepositional phrases when finding the subject. Common trap: Words next to the verb are not always the subject."},{"q":"Each of the candidates ___ a registration number.","a":"The answer is has. Each is singular, so it takes has. Method: The finite verb agrees with the grammatical subject.. Check: Words next to the verb are not always the subject."},{"q":"Explain the method for Tenses & aspect.","a":"Use present perfect for a past event connected to the present; past perfect for an earlier past event.. Choose tense using time markers and sequence. Common trap: Do not use present perfect with a finished past-time marker such as yesterday."},{"q":"By the time the interview began, she ___ the form.","a":"The answer is had completed. Completion happened before a past event, so use past perfect. Method: Use present perfect for a past event connected to the present; past perfect for an earlier past event.. Check: Do not use present perfect with a finished past-time marker such as yesterday."},{"q":"Explain the method for Articles & determiners.","a":"Use a/an by the following sound; the marks a specific referent.. Sound, countability and specificity determine the article. Common trap: A university starts with a consonant sound; an hour begins with a vowel sound."},{"q":"She waited for ___ hour.","a":"The answer is an. Hour starts with a vowel sound because h is silent. Method: Use a/an by the following sound; the marks a specific referent.. Check: A university starts with a consonant sound; an hour begins with a vowel sound."},{"q":"Explain the method for Prepositions & collocations.","a":"Use the preposition required by the adjective/verb and intended relationship.. Many combinations are conventional and need contextual practice. Common trap: Do not translate a preposition mechanically from another language."},{"q":"She is interested ___ data science.","a":"The answer is in. The standard collocation is interested in. Method: Use the preposition required by the adjective/verb and intended relationship.. Check: Do not translate a preposition mechanically from another language."},{"q":"Explain the method for Active & passive voice.","a":"Passive = object + suitable be + past participle (+ by agent).. Preserve tense, meaning and agreement when changing voice. Common trap: Only transitive uses normally admit a direct passive."},{"q":"Choose the passive form of: The team completed the project.","a":"The answer is The project was completed by the team.. Simple past completed becomes was completed; project becomes the passive subject. Method: Passive = object + suitable be + past participle (+ by agent).. Check: Only transitive uses normally admit a direct passive."},{"q":"Explain the method for Reported speech.","a":"Report the message with suitable tense, pronoun and time-reference changes.. Backshift is common after a past reporting verb, but universal truths can retain present tense. Common trap: Do not retain the original speaker’s pronouns without checking the referent."},{"q":"Ravi said, \"I am tired.\" Choose the usual reported form.","a":"The answer is Ravi said that he was tired.. After said, am normally backshifts to was and I refers to Ravi. Method: Report the message with suitable tense, pronoun and time-reference changes.. Check: Do not retain the original speaker’s pronouns without checking the referent."},{"q":"Explain the method for Parallelism & sentence correction.","a":"Coordinate grammatical structures should be parallel.. Compare list items and structures joined by and/or. Common trap: Mixing gerunds and infinitives can break a parallel list."},{"q":"Choose the sentence with parallel activity forms.","a":"The answer is She enjoys reading, writing, and coding.. All three objects of enjoys are gerunds. Method: Coordinate grammatical structures should be parallel.. Check: Mixing gerunds and infinitives can break a parallel list."},{"q":"Explain the method for Synonyms & antonyms.","a":"Use the sense required by context, not merely a related word.. Identify whether the task asks similarity or opposition. Common trap: A synonym may fit one sense of a word but not another."},{"q":"Choose the closest synonym of concise.","a":"The answer is brief. Concise means expressing much in few words. Method: Use the sense required by context, not merely a related word.. Check: A synonym may fit one sense of a word but not another."},{"q":"Explain the method for Idioms, phrases & usage.","a":"Interpret the conventional figurative meaning in context.. An idiom’s meaning is often not a literal sum of its words. Common trap: Do not infer a literal physical action from an idiom."},{"q":"What does \"get the ball rolling\" mean in a project meeting?","a":"The answer is start the activity. The idiom means initiate an activity. Method: Interpret the conventional figurative meaning in context.. Check: Do not infer a literal physical action from an idiom."},{"q":"Explain the method for Spelling & word choice.","a":"Choose the standard spelling and the word whose meaning fits.. Separate homophones by their grammatical function. Common trap: Similar pronunciation does not imply interchangeable spelling."},{"q":"Choose the correct spelling of the word for a place to stay.","a":"The answer is accommodation. Accommodation has double c and double m. Method: Choose the standard spelling and the word whose meaning fits.. Check: Similar pronunciation does not imply interchangeable spelling."},{"q":"Explain the method for Reading comprehension & inference.","a":"Support each answer with passage evidence; inference must follow without extra assumptions.. Read for the author’s central claim, supporting evidence, contrast and limits. Common trap: A plausible statement may still be unsupported by the passage."},{"q":"The library extended its opening hours for a one-month trial. Visits increased during that month. The manager noted that a holiday occurred in the same period and requested more data before attributing the increase to the longer hours.\nWhich conclusion is best supported?","a":"The answer is The trial alone does not establish that longer hours caused the increase.. The manager explicitly notes another possible influence and asks for more data. A simultaneous increase is not proof of causation. Method: Support each answer with passage evidence; inference must follow without extra assumptions.. Check: A plausible statement may still be unsupported by the passage."},{"q":"Explain the method for Paragraph ordering & coherence.","a":"Find the introduction, references, connectors and conclusion.. Pronouns and expressions like this result need an earlier referent. Common trap: A grammatically correct sentence may still be misplaced in the argument."},{"q":"Order these sentences about a library survey:\nA: Therefore, the team revised its plan.\nB: The team first collected visitor feedback.\nC: This feedback revealed a need for quieter spaces.","a":"The answer is B-C-A. B introduces the feedback; C refers to this feedback; A states the resulting action. Method: Find the introduction, references, connectors and conclusion.. Check: A grammatically correct sentence may still be misplaced in the argument."},{"q":"Explain the method for Cloze tests & contextual completion.","a":"Choose a word that fits both grammar and the passage’s logical relationship.. Read the full sentence, then surrounding context. Common trap: A common word may be semantically wrong despite fitting grammar."},{"q":"The library received more visitors; ___, its waiting time decreased after the new booking system was introduced.","a":"The answer is however. The second clause contrasts higher demand with lower waiting time, so however fits. Method: Choose a word that fits both grammar and the passage’s logical relationship.. Check: A common word may be semantically wrong despite fitting grammar."},{"q":"Explain the method for Parts of speech, plurals & one-word substitutions.","a":"Identify a word’s function in its sentence; distinguish a general category from a precise term.. Adverbs modify actions or descriptions; adjectives describe nouns. Some borrowed nouns have irregular plurals. Common trap: A broad term can be related without being the most precise answer."},{"q":"Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'He runs QUICKLY.'","a":"The answer is Adverb. Quickly modifies the action runs, explaining how the action happens, so it functions as an adverb. Method: Identify a word’s function in its sentence; distinguish a general category from a precise term.. Check: A broad term can be related without being the most precise answer."},{"q":"Explain the method for Passage evidence and inference.","a":"Separate stated facts, supported inferences and claims beyond the passage.. Use the exact scope, qualifiers and evidence in the passage. A plausible outside fact is not a supported answer. Common trap: Association alone does not establish causation; a partial result is not a universal guarantee."},{"q":"Passage A: Which conclusion best matches the organisers' evidence?","a":"The answer is Spaced sessions were associated with higher later scores in these logs.. The passage reports an observed association and explicitly says schedules were self-selected and other factors uncontrolled. Method: Separate stated facts, supported inferences and claims beyond the passage.. Check: Association alone does not establish causation; a partial result is not a universal guarantee."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the strongest review habit?","options":["Explain the method and why the distractor fails","Memorise only the option letter","Ignore units and assumptions","Reduce time even when accuracy collapses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A reusable explanation exposes the underlying mistake and transfers to new questions."},"skills":[],"minutes":75},{"id":"placement-cs","title":"CS awareness and coding-output reasoning","body":"## Explain execution and invariants\nFor an output question, trace state in the language actually named. Operator precedence, scope, mutation and async ordering can differ between languages. Do not use a Python rule to answer a JavaScript question or assume every database has identical transaction behaviour.\n\nCS interview preparation connects SQL/DBMS, OS, networks and OOP to a concrete system. Explain a transaction through a payment/stock example, a deadlock through a lock order and a cache through a freshness/access policy. The separate courses provide deeper notes; this reference supports short written drills.\n\n## Awareness has a dated scope\nStatic science, geography, civics and history practice is different from current affairs. This app's static bank does not automatically update itself with every exam's current-affairs syllabus. A practice profile is not a guarantee of a future company's official pattern.\n\n## Review a wrong option\nState the language/database/version assumption, trace a minimal example and identify why each distractor fails. If a question is ambiguous, fix the wording or add the missing assumption rather than pretending the answer is universal.\n\nUse the following topic rules and worked examples for revision, then a short mixed drill and an interview explanation of one related project decision.\n\n\n## Bundled topic reference\n\n\n\n\n\n### DBMS & SQL\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Keys identify rows; joins combine related tables; transactions preserve integrity.`\n\nNULL is unknown/missing and needs IS NULL rather than = NULL.\n\n**Shortcut:** Separate row filtering WHERE from group filtering HAVING.\n\n**Common trap:** COUNT(column) excludes NULL values; COUNT(*) counts rows.\n\n**Worked example:** Which SQL clause filters groups after aggregation?\n\n**Answer:** HAVING\n\nHAVING evaluates a condition on grouped results; WHERE filters input rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Operating systems\n\n**Formula / rule:** `A process has its own address space; threads share a process address space.`\n\nScheduling, memory management, files and synchronization are core OS responsibilities.\n\n**Shortcut:** Identify the shared resource in concurrency questions.\n\n**Common trap:** A mutex is not a substitute for all forms of scheduling.\n\n**Worked example:** Which condition is required for a deadlock?\n\n**Answer:** Circular wait\n\nCircular wait is one of the four necessary Coffman conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Networks & security\n\n**Formula / rule:** `TCP provides an ordered byte stream; DNS maps names to records; TLS protects transport.`\n\nAuthentication checks identity; authorization checks allowed actions.\n\n**Shortcut:** Identify which layer/function the question describes.\n\n**Common trap:** Encryption alone does not decide access permissions.\n\n**Worked example:** Which service resolves a domain name to address records?\n\n**Answer:** DNS\n\nDNS stores and resolves domain records, including address records.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Indian polity & static awareness\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Distinguish constitutional bodies, legislative houses and levels of government.`\n\nThese are static foundational checks, not a current-affairs feed.\n\n**Shortcut:** Make institution-function pairs.\n\n**Common trap:** Office holders and exam notifications can change; use current official sources for them.\n\n**Worked example:** Which is the lower house of the Parliament of India?\n\n**Answer:** Lok Sabha\n\nThe Lok Sabha is the House of the People and lower house of Parliament.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Science, environment & economy basics\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Use units and definitions to distinguish related concepts.`\n\nStatic science and economic concepts support government-exam foundations.\n\n**Shortcut:** Check whether a question asks a stock, flow, rate or unit.\n\n**Common trap:** Nominal growth and inflation-adjusted growth are different.\n\n**Worked example:** What is the SI unit of electric current?\n\n**Answer:** ampere\n\nAmpere measures current; volt measures potential difference.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Python output, aliasing & slicing\n\n**Formula / rule:** `range(stop) excludes stop; a slice [start:stop] excludes stop; assignment can alias a mutable object.`\n\nTrace state line by line, including shared references and loop boundaries.\n\n**Shortcut:** Draw arrows from variable names to list objects.\n\n**Common trap:** A=b does not copy a list; b=a[:] creates a shallow copy.\n\n**Worked example:** What is printed by this Python code?\ns = 0\nfor i in range(3):\n    s += i\nprint(s)\n\n**Answer:** 3\n\nrange(3) is 0 through 2. Sum=3×2/2=3.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Complexity & data structures\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Count input-dependent operations; binary search halves a sorted search space each step.`\n\nState average/worst-case assumptions. Dictionary lookup is expected O(1), not a universal worst-case guarantee.\n\n**Shortcut:** For nested loops multiply counts only when both run their full ranges.\n\n**Common trap:** A loop with a doubling counter has logarithmic iteration count.\n\n**Worked example:** A sorted array contains n values (n ≥ 10). A search halves the remaining interval at each step. What is the asymptotic worst-case search time?\n\n**Answer:** O(log n)\n\nAfter k halvings, at most n/2^k candidates remain; reaching one requires k≈log₂n.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Python state tracing & recursion\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Aliased lists share mutations; list slicing makes a shallow copy; recursion needs a base case.`\n\nTrack object identity separately from variable names and trace return values backward.\n\n**Shortcut:** Write the list state after each mutation.\n\n**Common trap:** A shallow copy still shares nested mutable elements.\n\n**Worked example:** What is printed by this Python code?\na = [2]\nb = a\nc = a[:]\nb.append(3)\nprint(len(a), len(c))\n\n**Answer:** 2 1\n\nb aliases a, so appending through b changes a. c is a separate shallow list copy made before the append. Thus a has two entries and c has one.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Indian geography & history foundations\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Match geographical features and historical events to their defining facts.`\n\nFocus on static locations and chronology before adding current affairs from updated official material.\n\n**Shortcut:** Build a timeline and a map rather than memorising isolated names.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not infer current political boundaries or appointments from an old reference.\n\n**Worked example:** Which Indian state contains the Thar Desert’s largest Indian extent?\n\n**Answer:** Rajasthan\n\nThe Thar Desert lies mainly in western Rajasthan within India.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Web, APIs & version control\n\n**Formula / rule:** `HTTP methods express request semantics; status codes express outcomes; Git records content history.`\n\nPUT and PATCH have different update semantics. CSS controls presentation, while APIs define software interfaces.\n\n**Shortcut:** Learn method–purpose and status-code–meaning pairs.\n\n**Common trap:** Do not confuse creating a Git branch with switching the checkout.\n\n**Worked example:** Which listed HTTP method requests creation or complete replacement of a target resource’s state using the supplied representation?\n\n**Answer:** PUT\n\nPUT requests create or replace the target resource’s state using the enclosed representation. The revised question distinguishes this from a partial update.\n\n\n\n\n\n### Types, coercion & language semantics\n\n**Formula / rule:** `Evaluate operators under the named language’s rules; a const binding is not a deeply immutable object.`\n\nDistinguish strings from numbers, reference types from primitives and invocation receivers from function ownership.\n\n**Shortcut:** Write down the type of each intermediate value.\n\n**Common trap:** JavaScript loose and strict equality perform different checks.\n\n**Worked example:** Which of the following is a primitive data type in Java?\n\n**Answer:** int\n\nJava int is a primitive type. String, arrays and ArrayList are reference types.","exercise":"Choose two topics below. Solve each worked example with the answer covered, explain the trap and then complete a short focused practice set.","interviews":[{"q":"How should I review a wrong answer in this subject?","a":"State the relevant rule and its assumptions, solve the example without looking, identify why the chosen distractor fails and schedule a focused review instead of memorising an option letter."},{"q":"How should speed and accuracy be balanced?","a":"Begin with a correct method and explicit units/assumptions. Use measured short drills to reduce avoidable time while preserving accuracy, then review skipped and incorrect items by topic."},{"q":"Explain the method for DBMS & SQL.","a":"Keys identify rows; joins combine related tables; transactions preserve integrity.. NULL is unknown/missing and needs IS NULL rather than = NULL. Common trap: COUNT(column) excludes NULL values; COUNT(*) counts rows."},{"q":"Which SQL clause filters groups after aggregation?","a":"The answer is HAVING. HAVING evaluates a condition on grouped results; WHERE filters input rows. Method: Keys identify rows; joins combine related tables; transactions preserve integrity.. Check: COUNT(column) excludes NULL values; COUNT(*) counts rows."},{"q":"Explain the method for Operating systems.","a":"A process has its own address space; threads share a process address space.. Scheduling, memory management, files and synchronization are core OS responsibilities. Common trap: A mutex is not a substitute for all forms of scheduling."},{"q":"Which condition is required for a deadlock?","a":"The answer is Circular wait. Circular wait is one of the four necessary Coffman conditions. Method: A process has its own address space; threads share a process address space.. Check: A mutex is not a substitute for all forms of scheduling."},{"q":"Explain the method for Networks & security.","a":"TCP provides an ordered byte stream; DNS maps names to records; TLS protects transport.. Authentication checks identity; authorization checks allowed actions. Common trap: Encryption alone does not decide access permissions."},{"q":"Which service resolves a domain name to address records?","a":"The answer is DNS. DNS stores and resolves domain records, including address records. Method: TCP provides an ordered byte stream; DNS maps names to records; TLS protects transport.. Check: Encryption alone does not decide access permissions."},{"q":"Explain the method for Indian polity & static awareness.","a":"Distinguish constitutional bodies, legislative houses and levels of government.. These are static foundational checks, not a current-affairs feed. Common trap: Office holders and exam notifications can change; use current official sources for them."},{"q":"Which is the lower house of the Parliament of India?","a":"The answer is Lok Sabha. The Lok Sabha is the House of the People and lower house of Parliament. Method: Distinguish constitutional bodies, legislative houses and levels of government.. Check: Office holders and exam notifications can change; use current official sources for them."},{"q":"Explain the method for Science, environment & economy basics.","a":"Use units and definitions to distinguish related concepts.. Static science and economic concepts support government-exam foundations. Common trap: Nominal growth and inflation-adjusted growth are different."},{"q":"What is the SI unit of electric current?","a":"The answer is ampere. Ampere measures current; volt measures potential difference. Method: Use units and definitions to distinguish related concepts.. Check: Nominal growth and inflation-adjusted growth are different."},{"q":"Explain the method for Python output, aliasing & slicing.","a":"range(stop) excludes stop; a slice [start:stop] excludes stop; assignment can alias a mutable object.. Trace state line by line, including shared references and loop boundaries. Common trap: A=b does not copy a list; b=a[:] creates a shallow copy."},{"q":"What is printed by this Python code?\ns = 0\nfor i in range(3):\n    s += i\nprint(s)","a":"The answer is 3. range(3) is 0 through 2. Sum=3×2/2=3. Method: range(stop) excludes stop; a slice [start:stop] excludes stop; assignment can alias a mutable object.. Check: A=b does not copy a list; b=a[:] creates a shallow copy."},{"q":"Explain the method for Complexity & data structures.","a":"Count input-dependent operations; binary search halves a sorted search space each step.. State average/worst-case assumptions. Dictionary lookup is expected O(1), not a universal worst-case guarantee. Common trap: A loop with a doubling counter has logarithmic iteration count."},{"q":"A sorted array contains n values (n ≥ 10). A search halves the remaining interval at each step. What is the asymptotic worst-case search time?","a":"The answer is O(log n). After k halvings, at most n/2^k candidates remain; reaching one requires k≈log₂n. Method: Count input-dependent operations; binary search halves a sorted search space each step.. Check: A loop with a doubling counter has logarithmic iteration count."},{"q":"Explain the method for Python state tracing & recursion.","a":"Aliased lists share mutations; list slicing makes a shallow copy; recursion needs a base case.. Track object identity separately from variable names and trace return values backward. Common trap: A shallow copy still shares nested mutable elements."},{"q":"What is printed by this Python code?\na = [2]\nb = a\nc = a[:]\nb.append(3)\nprint(len(a), len(c))","a":"The answer is 2 1. b aliases a, so appending through b changes a. c is a separate shallow list copy made before the append. Thus a has two entries and c has one. Method: Aliased lists share mutations; list slicing makes a shallow copy; recursion needs a base case.. Check: A shallow copy still shares nested mutable elements."},{"q":"Explain the method for Indian geography & history foundations.","a":"Match geographical features and historical events to their defining facts.. Focus on static locations and chronology before adding current affairs from updated official material. Common trap: Do not infer current political boundaries or appointments from an old reference."},{"q":"Which Indian state contains the Thar Desert’s largest Indian extent?","a":"The answer is Rajasthan. The Thar Desert lies mainly in western Rajasthan within India. Method: Match geographical features and historical events to their defining facts.. Check: Do not infer current political boundaries or appointments from an old reference."},{"q":"Explain the method for Web, APIs & version control.","a":"HTTP methods express request semantics; status codes express outcomes; Git records content history.. PUT and PATCH have different update semantics. CSS controls presentation, while APIs define software interfaces. Common trap: Do not confuse creating a Git branch with switching the checkout."},{"q":"Which listed HTTP method requests creation or complete replacement of a target resource’s state using the supplied representation?","a":"The answer is PUT. PUT requests create or replace the target resource’s state using the enclosed representation. The revised question distinguishes this from a partial update. Method: HTTP methods express request semantics; status codes express outcomes; Git records content history.. Check: Do not confuse creating a Git branch with switching the checkout."},{"q":"Explain the method for Types, coercion & language semantics.","a":"Evaluate operators under the named language’s rules; a const binding is not a deeply immutable object.. Distinguish strings from numbers, reference types from primitives and invocation receivers from function ownership. Common trap: JavaScript loose and strict equality perform different checks."},{"q":"Which of the following is a primitive data type in Java?","a":"The answer is int. Java int is a primitive type. String, arrays and ArrayList are reference types. Method: Evaluate operators under the named language’s rules; a const binding is not a deeply immutable object.. Check: JavaScript loose and strict equality perform different checks."}],"quiz":{"question":"Which is the strongest review habit?","options":["Explain the method and why the distractor fails","Memorise only the option letter","Ignore units and assumptions","Reduce time even when accuracy collapses"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A reusable explanation exposes the underlying mistake and transfers to new questions."},"skills":[],"minutes":55}]}],"paths":[{"title":"Frontend Developer","level":"Start with the browser","description":"Build the pages, interactions and accessible interfaces users see.","courses":["html","css","javascript","git","typescript","react","next"],"project":"Build an accessible portfolio, a weather dashboard and a tested storefront interface."},{"title":"Backend Developer","level":"Choose Python or Node first","description":"Design APIs, model data, validate input and enforce permissions on the server.","courses":["python","node","python-backend","databases","networks","security","devops"],"project":"Build a task API with login, object-level permissions, transactions and deployment."},{"title":"Full-Stack Developer","level":"Connect both halves","description":"Take a product from a form in the browser through an API to a database and a reliable release.","courses":["html","css","javascript","git","react","node","databases","security","projects","devops"],"project":"Build a client portal or commerce application with tests and a documented failure strategy."},{"title":"App Developer","level":"Pick one mobile path","description":"Learn shared programming concepts, then specialise in Flutter, Kotlin, React Native or Swift.","courses":["mobile-flutter","mobile-kotlin","mobile-react-native","mobile-swift","networks","security"],"project":"Build an offline-capable task app with navigation, local storage and a release checklist."},{"title":"Desktop App Developer","level":"Pick a desktop toolkit","description":"Create useful Windows, macOS or Linux applications with native file and window workflows.","courses":["desktop-electron","desktop-python","desktop-dotnet","git","security"],"project":"Build a local notes application with import/export, a database and an installer."},{"title":"Python Developer","level":"Language to applied work","description":"Use Python for automation, APIs, data processing and measured AI features.","courses":["python","dsa","data-science","python-backend","ai-foundations","rag","agents"],"project":"Build a tested report pipeline that validates data, measures model quality and works through provider failures."},{"title":"Placement & CS Foundations","level":"Study alongside projects","description":"Practise DSA, SQL, operating systems, networks and concise interview explanations.","courses":["dsa","databases","os","networks","placement"],"project":"Follow a topic sheet, complete judged drills and explain one project trade-off every day."}],"skills":[{"number":1,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Syntax and semantics Indentation blocks, dynamic typing, truthiness, and the places Python behaves differently from JavaScript","lesson":"py-values"},{"number":2,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Types and mutability int, float, str, bool, None; mutable vs immutable objects and the bugs that come from confusing them","lesson":"py-values"},{"number":3,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Collections list, tuple, dict and set, and choosing the right one for the job","lesson":"py-collections"},{"number":4,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Comprehensions List, dict and set comprehensions and generator expressions as idiomatic transforms","lesson":"py-collections"},{"number":5,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Functions Default and keyword arguments, *args and **kwargs, closures, functions as values","lesson":"py-functions"},{"number":6,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Type hints Annotations, Optional, unions and generics, checked by mypy or pyright the way tsc checks TypeScript","lesson":"py-functions"},{"number":7,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Environments and packaging venv, pip or uv, pyproject.toml: the Python equivalent of package.json","lesson":"py-setup"},{"number":8,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Classes and OOP __init__, inheritance vs composition, properties, the dunder methods worth knowing","lesson":"py-oop"},{"number":9,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Dataclasses Lightweight typed records; the default for internal data structures","lesson":"py-oop"},{"number":10,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Modules and packages Imports, package layout, and the __name__ == '__main__' entry point","lesson":"py-setup"},{"number":11,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Exceptions try / except / else / finally, custom exception classes, raising with context","lesson":"py-errors"},{"number":12,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Context managers with-blocks for files, database sessions and API clients; writing your own","lesson":"py-errors"},{"number":13,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Decorators How they work, and why FastAPI and pytest are built on them","lesson":"py-lazy"},{"number":14,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Iterators and generators yield and lazy pipelines for data too large to hold in memory, and for streaming","lesson":"py-lazy"},{"number":15,"stage":1,"page":5,"text":"Async Python async / await, asyncio.gather and semaphores for calling many model APIs concurrently","lesson":"py-async"},{"number":16,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Logging The logging module, levels and structured output instead of print","lesson":"py-files"},{"number":17,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Concurrency models Threads vs processes vs asyncio, and what the GIL means for real workloads","lesson":"py-async"},{"number":18,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Files and formats Text, JSON, CSV and YAML with pathlib; configuration from environment variables","lesson":"py-files"},{"number":19,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Pydantic Validated models and settings, used throughout FastAPI and modern model SDKs","lesson":"py-validation"},{"number":20,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"HTTP clients httpx or requests: headers, auth, timeouts, retries, streaming responses","lesson":"py-async"},{"number":21,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"NumPy Arrays, shapes and vectorised maths: the vocabulary behind embeddings","lesson":"data-numpy"},{"number":22,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Pandas DataFrames, filtering, groupby, joins and cleaning messy real-world data","lesson":"data-pandas"},{"number":23,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Testing with pytest Fixtures, parametrised tests, mocking external APIs","lesson":"py-tests"},{"number":24,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Lint and format ruff and pre-commit hooks: your ESLint and Prettier habits, carried over","lesson":"py-setup"},{"number":25,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Scripts and CLIs argparse or Typer for automating repetitive work","lesson":"py-files"},{"number":26,"stage":1,"page":6,"text":"Notebooks Jupyter for exploration, and knowing when code should leave the notebook","lesson":"data-notebooks"},{"number":27,"stage":2,"page":6,"text":"Semantic HTML Landmarks, headings, lists, tables and forms: meaning first, divs last","lesson":"html-semantic"},{"number":28,"stage":2,"page":6,"text":"Layout Box model, flexbox, grid, positioning and stacking context","lesson":"css-layout"},{"number":29,"stage":2,"page":6,"text":"Responsive design Mobile-first layouts, media and container queries, fluid sizing","lesson":"css-responsive"},{"number":30,"stage":2,"page":6,"text":"Tailwind CSS Utility-first workflow, design tokens, theming","lesson":"css-utilities"},{"number":31,"stage":2,"page":6,"text":"Accessibility Keyboard paths, focus order, labels, contrast, ARIA only where native HTML falls short, screen-reader testing","lesson":"html-semantic"},{"number":32,"stage":2,"page":6,"text":"Modern CSS Custom properties, :has(), container queries, cascade layers","lesson":"css-modern"},{"number":33,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Language core Scope, closures, this, prototypes, ES modules","lesson":"js-functions"},{"number":34,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Promises and async / await Composing asynchronous work and handling its errors","lesson":"js-async"},{"number":35,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"The event loop in depth Call stack, microtasks vs macrotasks, and why one blocking loop freezes a whole server","lesson":"js-event-loop"},{"number":36,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"TypeScript Generics, narrowing, utility types, discriminated unions","lesson":"ts-types"},{"number":37,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Shared types in a monorepo One source of truth for API contracts across frontend and backend","lesson":"ts-contracts"},{"number":38,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Runtime validation Zod or similar, so types are enforced at the boundaries and not only at compile time","lesson":"ts-contracts"},{"number":39,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Error handling patterns Typed errors and result types instead of swallowed exceptions","lesson":"js-async"},{"number":40,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"How a page loads Parsing, the critical rendering path, render-blocking resources","lesson":"web-browser"},{"number":41,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"DevTools Network waterfall, performance profiles, memory snapshots","lesson":"web-browser"},{"number":42,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Browser storage Cookies vs localStorage vs IndexedDB, and the security consequences of each","lesson":"js-browser"},{"number":43,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Browser APIs fetch, AbortController, streams and Web Workers: the parts a streaming AI interface is built from","lesson":"js-browser"},{"number":44,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Build tooling Vite, bundling, tree-shaking, source maps","lesson":"tooling-build"},{"number":45,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Git Branching, rebase vs merge, conflict resolution, readable history","lesson":"git-branches"},{"number":46,"stage":2,"page":7,"text":"Code quality ESLint, Prettier and shared conventions","lesson":"tooling-build"},{"number":47,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Components and composition Props, children, composition over configuration","lesson":"react-components"},{"number":48,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Hooks State, effects, refs and memoisation, and the effect mistakes that cause bugs","lesson":"react-effects"},{"number":49,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Custom hooks Extracting reusable logic and testing it in isolation","lesson":"react-effects"},{"number":50,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Rendering model Reconciliation, keys, why components re-render, what changed in React 19","lesson":"react-components"},{"number":51,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Error boundaries and Suspense Isolating failures and designing loading states","lesson":"react-forms"},{"number":52,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Forms at scale React Hook Form with schema validation, server errors, optimistic updates","lesson":"react-forms"},{"number":53,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Component testing Testing Library: test behaviour, not implementation","lesson":"react-quality"},{"number":54,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"App Router Layouts, route groups, loading and error files","lesson":"next-routing"},{"number":55,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Server and client components What runs where, and what can cross the serialisation boundary","lesson":"next-routing"},{"number":56,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Rendering strategies SSR, SSG, ISR and client rendering, chosen per route","lesson":"next-rendering"},{"number":57,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Data fetching and caching Fetch caching, revalidation and tags, and avoiding stale-data bugs","lesson":"next-rendering"},{"number":58,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Server actions and route handlers Mutations without a separate API, and when you still want one","lesson":"next-mutations"},{"number":59,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Streaming UI Streaming server components and Suspense boundaries; the base for AI interfaces","lesson":"next-mutations"},{"number":60,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Middleware and the edge Auth redirects, edge runtime limits","lesson":"next-routing"},{"number":61,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Client state useState, context and Redux Toolkit, and when global state is a mistake","lesson":"react-state"},{"number":62,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Server state TanStack Query or SWR: caching, refetching, mutations, invalidation","lesson":"react-data"},{"number":63,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"URL as state Filters, sorting and pagination kept in the query string so views are shareable","lesson":"next-url-state"},{"number":64,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Realtime UI Socket.io client, reconnection, merging live events into cached data","lesson":"react-data"},{"number":65,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Design systems Component variants, tokens, accessible primitives such as Radix or shadcn/ui","lesson":"react-design"},{"number":66,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Data visualisation Recharts dashboards that answer a specific question","lesson":"react-design"},{"number":67,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Performance Core Web Vitals, bundle analysis, code splitting, image optimisation","lesson":"react-quality"},{"number":68,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"End-to-end testing Playwright for critical user journeys, run in CI","lesson":"react-quality"},{"number":69,"stage":3,"page":8,"text":"Internationalisation Locale-aware dates, numbers and currency","lesson":"react-design"},{"number":70,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"REST resource design Resources, nesting, filtering, sorting, pagination","lesson":"api-resources"},{"number":71,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Error contracts One consistent error shape across every endpoint, for example RFC 9457 problem details","lesson":"api-middleware"},{"number":72,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Idempotency Idempotency keys so a retried request never charges or creates twice","lesson":"api-idempotency"},{"number":73,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Contracts and versioning OpenAPI specs, generated typed clients, a breaking-change policy","lesson":"api-resources"},{"number":74,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Cursor pagination Offset vs cursor, and staying consistent while data changes","lesson":"api-resources"},{"number":75,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Application structure Middleware order, routers, controller and service layers","lesson":"api-middleware"},{"number":76,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"API hardening Helmet, CORS policy, two-tier rate limiting","lesson":"api-middleware"},{"number":77,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"API testing Vitest, in-memory databases, end-to-end smoke suites","lesson":"api-tests"},{"number":78,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Centralised error handling Async errors, error middleware, never leaking stack traces","lesson":"api-middleware"},{"number":79,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Node runtime behaviour Blocking the event loop, worker threads, streams, memory limits","lesson":"node-runtime"},{"number":80,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Routing Routers and path, query and body parameters","lesson":"fastapi-routing"},{"number":81,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Pydantic schemas Request and response models, validation errors, serialisation","lesson":"fastapi-routing"},{"number":82,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Dependency injection Depends() for database sessions, auth and settings","lesson":"fastapi-routing"},{"number":83,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Async endpoints Async database drivers, and never blocking the event loop","lesson":"fastapi-routing"},{"number":84,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"SQLAlchemy and Alembic The Python ORM layer and its migrations","lesson":"py-orm"},{"number":85,"stage":4,"page":9,"text":"Generated docs OpenAPI and Swagger UI produced from your code","lesson":"fastapi-routing"},{"number":86,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Authentication JWT, sessions, refresh strategy, httpOnly cookies","lesson":"api-auth"},{"number":87,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect Authorisation-code flow, PKCE, Google sign-in","lesson":"api-auth"},{"number":88,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Authorisation RBAC with checks enforced at the query layer, not only at the route","lesson":"api-auth"},{"number":89,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Payments Razorpay orders and webhook signature verification","lesson":"api-payments"},{"number":90,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Email and notifications Transactional email, and fallbacks when realtime delivery fails","lesson":"api-integrations"},{"number":91,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Document generation Server-side PDF invoices and reports","lesson":"api-integrations"},{"number":92,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Outgoing webhooks Signing, retries and delivery logs for events you send","lesson":"api-payments"},{"number":93,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Job queues BullMQ or Celery: workers, retries, dead-letter queues","lesson":"api-jobs"},{"number":94,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Scheduled work Recurring jobs such as a weekly report, safe against double and missed runs","lesson":"api-jobs"},{"number":95,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"WebSockets Socket.io rooms, authenticating the connection, fallbacks","lesson":"api-realtime"},{"number":96,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"Server-Sent Events One-way streaming over HTTP; how model tokens reach the browser","lesson":"api-realtime"},{"number":97,"stage":4,"page":10,"text":"State machines Explicit, enforced status transitions such as an order lifecycle","lesson":"api-jobs"},{"number":98,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"SQL essentials Joins, aggregates, grouping, subqueries","lesson":"sql-query"},{"number":99,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"Advanced SQL CTEs, window functions, upserts, recursive queries","lesson":"sql-advanced"},{"number":100,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"Schema design Entities, relationships and constraints across a multi-table schema","lesson":"db-schema"},{"number":101,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"Normalisation 1NF to BCNF, functional dependencies, and denormalising on purpose","lesson":"db-schema"},{"number":102,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"ER modelling Cardinality and optionality worked out before any code is written","lesson":"db-schema"},{"number":103,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"Prisma Schema, migrations, relations, and when to drop down to raw SQL","lesson":"db-prisma"},{"number":104,"stage":5,"page":10,"text":"Database-enforced rules Foreign keys, unique and check constraints instead of application-only checks","lesson":"db-schema"},{"number":105,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Storage Pages and heap files: how a row physically lives on disk","lesson":"db-indexes"},{"number":106,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Indexes B-tree structure, composite index order, covering and partial indexes, the write cost","lesson":"db-indexes"},{"number":107,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Query planning EXPLAIN ANALYZE, cost estimates, reading a plan top to bottom","lesson":"db-indexes"},{"number":108,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Transactions and ACID What atomicity, consistency, isolation and durability guarantee in practice","lesson":"db-transactions"},{"number":109,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Atomic updates Race-free conditional updates such as a stock decrement","lesson":"db-transactions"},{"number":110,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Isolation levels Read committed to serialisable; dirty reads, phantoms, write skew","lesson":"db-transactions"},{"number":111,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Locking and deadlock Row and table locks, lock ordering, deadlock detection","lesson":"db-transactions"},{"number":112,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"MVCC Multi-version concurrency, and why readers do not block writers in Postgres","lesson":"db-transactions"},{"number":113,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Join algorithms Nested loop, hash and merge joins, and why the planner picks one","lesson":"db-indexes"},{"number":114,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"WAL, vacuum and statistics Write-ahead logging, table bloat, stale statistics causing bad plans","lesson":"db-indexes"},{"number":115,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Connection pooling Pool sizing, PgBouncer, connection limits on serverless platforms","lesson":"db-operations"},{"number":116,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Zero-downtime migrations Expand and contract, backfills, safe column changes","lesson":"db-operations"},{"number":117,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Backups and restore Logical vs physical backups, point-in-time recovery, rehearsed restores","lesson":"db-operations"},{"number":118,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Replication Primary and replicas, read scaling, replication lag","lesson":"db-operations"},{"number":119,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Partitioning and sharding Range and hash partitioning, and when sharding is worth the pain","lesson":"db-operations"},{"number":120,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Document modelling MongoDB embedding vs referencing, aggregation pipelines","lesson":"db-documents"},{"number":121,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Choosing a data model Relational, document or key-value, decided by access patterns","lesson":"db-documents"},{"number":122,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Redis Cache-aside, TTLs, invalidation, counters for rate limits","lesson":"db-cache-search"},{"number":123,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Search engines Postgres full-text search first, then Meilisearch, Typesense or Elasticsearch","lesson":"db-cache-search"},{"number":124,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"Vector storage pgvector and vector indexes: the bridge into Stage 10","lesson":"db-cache-search"},{"number":125,"stage":5,"page":11,"text":"OLTP vs OLAP Transactional vs analytical workloads, columnar storage at concept level","lesson":"db-cache-search"},{"number":126,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Processes vs threads Address spaces, shared memory, why threads are cheaper","lesson":"os-processes"},{"number":127,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Process lifecycle and signals fork and exec, SIGTERM vs SIGKILL, graceful shutdown","lesson":"os-processes"},{"number":128,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Scheduling Preemption, priorities, CPU-bound vs I/O-bound work","lesson":"os-scheduling"},{"number":129,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Concurrency vs parallelism The difference, and why async is not the same as threads","lesson":"os-processes"},{"number":130,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Race conditions Interleavings, lost updates, and reproducing them reliably","lesson":"os-synchronisation"},{"number":131,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Locks and semaphores Mutexes, semaphores, read-write locks, lock granularity","lesson":"os-synchronisation"},{"number":132,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Deadlock The four conditions, prevention by lock ordering, detection","lesson":"os-synchronisation"},{"number":133,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Context switching What it costs and how it shows up as latency","lesson":"os-processes"},{"number":134,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Virtual memory and paging Address translation, page faults, thrashing","lesson":"os-memory"},{"number":135,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Stack vs heap Allocation, lifetimes, what a stack overflow really is","lesson":"os-memory"},{"number":136,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Memory leaks Heap snapshots, retained objects, common leak patterns in Node and Python","lesson":"os-memory"},{"number":137,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Container memory limits cgroup limits, the OOM killer, sizing the Node heap","lesson":"os-memory"},{"number":138,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Garbage collection Generational GC in V8, reference counting in CPython","lesson":"os-memory"},{"number":139,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"File descriptors The descriptor table, ulimits, 'too many open files'","lesson":"os-io"},{"number":140,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Blocking vs non-blocking I/O Event loops, epoll and kqueue, why Node scales on one thread","lesson":"os-io"},{"number":141,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"File systems inodes, permissions, links, mounts and volumes in containers","lesson":"os-io"},{"number":142,"stage":6,"page":12,"text":"Buffering and fsync Why data is not safely on disk when write() returns","lesson":"os-io"},{"number":143,"stage":6,"page":13,"text":"Shell fluency Pipes, redirection, grep, sed, awk, find, xargs","lesson":"os-linux"},{"number":144,"stage":6,"page":13,"text":"Process inspection ps, top or htop, lsof, strace","lesson":"os-linux"},{"number":145,"stage":6,"page":13,"text":"Users and permissions chmod, chown, service accounts, least privilege","lesson":"os-linux"},{"number":146,"stage":6,"page":13,"text":"What a container is Namespaces and cgroups: a container as an ordinary process with limits","lesson":"os-linux"},{"number":147,"stage":6,"page":13,"text":"Docker Images, layers, multi-stage builds, Compose","lesson":"os-linux"},{"number":148,"stage":6,"page":13,"text":"systemd and logs Service units, restart policies, journalctl","lesson":"os-linux"},{"number":149,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"Layered models OSI and TCP/IP used as a debugging map: which layer is failing?","lesson":"net-models"},{"number":150,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"IP and routing Addresses, subnets, NAT, public vs private networks","lesson":"net-models"},{"number":151,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"TCP Handshake, reliability, congestion control, head-of-line blocking","lesson":"net-transport"},{"number":152,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"UDP Connectionless delivery and where it wins","lesson":"net-transport"},{"number":153,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"DNS The resolution chain, record types, TTLs and propagation","lesson":"net-dns-tls"},{"number":154,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"TLS The handshake, certificate chains, and what the padlock actually proves","lesson":"net-dns-tls"},{"number":155,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"HTTP methods and status codes Semantics you already use every day in your REST APIs","lesson":"net-http"},{"number":156,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"HTTP caching Cache-Control, ETag, conditional requests","lesson":"net-http"},{"number":157,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"CORS Preflight and credentials, and why CORS is a browser rule rather than server security","lesson":"net-http"},{"number":158,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"Cookies on the wire SameSite, Secure, HttpOnly, domain scoping","lesson":"net-http"},{"number":159,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"WebSockets The upgrade handshake, keep-alive, proxies that break long connections","lesson":"net-streams"},{"number":160,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"Server-Sent Events Streaming over plain HTTP; the transport behind token streaming","lesson":"net-streams"},{"number":161,"stage":7,"page":13,"text":"HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 Multiplexing, QUIC, and when they matter","lesson":"net-transport"},{"number":162,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Latency anatomy DNS, connect, TLS, server time and transfer, each measured separately","lesson":"net-resilience"},{"number":163,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Timeouts and retries Exponential backoff, jitter, retry budgets, idempotency","lesson":"net-resilience"},{"number":164,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Circuit breakers Failing fast, half-open probes, graceful degradation","lesson":"net-resilience"},{"number":165,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Rate limits Reading 429 and Retry-After, throttling yourself before a provider does","lesson":"net-resilience"},{"number":166,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Load balancers and proxies Nginx, health checks, layer 4 vs layer 7","lesson":"net-operations"},{"number":167,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Network tooling curl -v, dig, ss, nc, traceroute, the browser network tab","lesson":"net-operations"},{"number":168,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"CDNs and the edge Caching static and dynamic content, invalidation","lesson":"net-operations"},{"number":169,"stage":7,"page":14,"text":"Network security Firewalls, private subnets, DDoS basics","lesson":"net-operations"},{"number":170,"stage":8,"page":14,"text":"Containers Multi-stage builds, small images, Compose for local parity","lesson":"ops-delivery"},{"number":171,"stage":8,"page":14,"text":"Continuous integration Lint, test and build gates on every push","lesson":"ops-delivery"},{"number":172,"stage":8,"page":14,"text":"Environments and deployment Preview, staging and production, promoting the same build","lesson":"ops-delivery"},{"number":173,"stage":8,"page":14,"text":"Infrastructure as code Terraform or Pulumi for reproducible environments","lesson":"ops-iac"},{"number":174,"stage":8,"page":14,"text":"Secrets management Nothing in git, per-environment keys, rotation","lesson":"ops-iac"},{"number":175,"stage":8,"page":14,"text":"Release safety Feature flags, canary releases, fast rollback","lesson":"ops-delivery"},{"number":176,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Cloud fundamentals Compute, managed databases, object storage and IAM on one provider","lesson":"ops-cloud"},{"number":177,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Object storage S3-compatible buckets, presigned URLs, user uploads","lesson":"ops-cloud"},{"number":178,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Serverless vs containers Functions vs long-running services, cold starts, limits","lesson":"ops-cloud"},{"number":179,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Cost awareness Cost per user, and what makes a bill spike","lesson":"ops-cost"},{"number":180,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Cloud networking VPCs, subnets, security groups","lesson":"ops-cloud"},{"number":181,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Kubernetes Pods, deployments and services, at concept level","lesson":"ops-cloud"},{"number":182,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Structured logging JSON logs with correlation IDs across services","lesson":"ops-observability"},{"number":183,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Metrics Latency percentiles, error rates, saturation","lesson":"ops-observability"},{"number":184,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Distributed tracing OpenTelemetry spans across Node, Python and model calls","lesson":"ops-observability"},{"number":185,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Error tracking Sentry, release tagging, source maps","lesson":"ops-observability"},{"number":186,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Alerting Alert on symptoms, and keep every alert actionable","lesson":"ops-observability"},{"number":187,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"OWASP Top 10 Each class of flaw, an example, and the fix in your own stack","lesson":"security-risks"},{"number":188,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Injection SQL and NoSQL injection, command injection, ORM pitfalls","lesson":"security-injection"},{"number":189,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"XSS and CSP Output encoding and a Content-Security-Policy","lesson":"security-injection"},{"number":190,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"CSRF What SameSite defaults solve, and when tokens are still needed","lesson":"security-access"},{"number":191,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Broken access control IDOR and object-level checks: attacking your own RBAC","lesson":"security-access"},{"number":192,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Tenant isolation Guaranteeing one tenant can never read another tenant's data","lesson":"security-access"},{"number":193,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Webhook security Signature verification and replay protection","lesson":"security-events"},{"number":194,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"API hardening Security headers, CORS policy, rate limiting","lesson":"security-events"},{"number":195,"stage":8,"page":15,"text":"Supply chain Lockfiles, dependency audits, pinned versions","lesson":"security-supply"},{"number":196,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"The landscape AI, machine learning, deep learning and generative AI, defined precisely","lesson":"ai-ml"},{"number":197,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Learning paradigms Supervised, unsupervised, self-supervised and reinforcement learning","lesson":"ai-ml"},{"number":198,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Training vs inference What happens at each step, and which one you pay for","lesson":"ai-ml"},{"number":199,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Generalisation Overfitting; train, validation and test splits; data leakage","lesson":"ai-ml"},{"number":200,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Classification metrics Precision, recall, F1 and the confusion matrix, reused directly in evals","lesson":"ai-metrics"},{"number":201,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Neural network intuition Layers, weights, loss and gradient descent, conceptually","lesson":"ai-ml"},{"number":202,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"A classic ML baseline One end-to-end scikit-learn model, so you know when an LLM is overkill","lesson":"ai-metrics"},{"number":203,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Tokens and context windows Tokenisation, and why token count is both cost and latency","lesson":"ai-llms"},{"number":204,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Transformers and attention The intuition, not the maths","lesson":"ai-llms"},{"number":205,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Generation Next-token sampling, temperature, top-p, the limits of determinism","lesson":"ai-llms"},{"number":206,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"From base model to assistant Pre-training, instruction tuning and preference alignment","lesson":"ai-llms"},{"number":207,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Failure modes Hallucination, knowledge cut-offs, sensitivity to phrasing","lesson":"ai-llms"},{"number":208,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Embeddings Text as vectors, semantic similarity, choosing an embedding model","lesson":"ai-llms"},{"number":209,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Model selection Frontier, small and open-weight models compared on quality, latency and cost","lesson":"ai-providers"},{"number":210,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Local models Running open-weight models with Ollama or similar, for privacy and offline work","lesson":"ai-providers"},{"number":211,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Model API integration Messages, system prompts and multi-turn context with the Claude API","lesson":"ai-providers"},{"number":212,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Deterministic fallback A non-AI path that keeps the feature working when the model fails","lesson":"ai-providers"},{"number":213,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Prompt design Clear instructions, examples, delimiters, explicit output formats","lesson":"ai-prompts"},{"number":214,"stage":9,"page":16,"text":"Streaming Tokens streamed end to end: provider to server to browser","lesson":"ai-streaming"},{"number":215,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Structured output JSON schemas, validated parsing with Pydantic or Zod, recovery from bad output","lesson":"ai-prompts"},{"number":216,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Tool use Defining tools, executing calls, feeding results back to the model","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":217,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Prompt management Versioned prompt templates, changes reviewed and tested like code","lesson":"ai-prompts"},{"number":218,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Cost and latency budgets Token accounting, prompt caching, the smallest model that does the job","lesson":"ai-streaming"},{"number":219,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Provider abstraction A thin internal interface so a model can be swapped without a rewrite","lesson":"ai-providers"},{"number":220,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Multimodal input Images and documents as model input","lesson":"ai-providers"},{"number":221,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Coding assistants Using AI tools to plan, write, refactor and debug your own code","lesson":"ai-coding"},{"number":222,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Reviewing generated code Reading every line; catching code that looks right and is not","lesson":"ai-coding"},{"number":223,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Specs and tests first Giving an assistant constraints: types, tests, acceptance criteria","lesson":"ai-coding"},{"number":224,"stage":9,"page":17,"text":"Knowing the limits Security-critical code, secrets, licences, and when to write it yourself","lesson":"ai-coding"},{"number":225,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"The RAG pipeline Ingest, chunk, embed, index, retrieve, rerank, generate, cite","lesson":"rag-ingestion"},{"number":226,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Document ingestion Parsing PDFs, HTML and office files; cleaning; keeping metadata","lesson":"rag-ingestion"},{"number":227,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Chunking Size, overlap, structure-aware splitting; the most common point of failure","lesson":"rag-ingestion"},{"number":228,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Vector databases pgvector or MongoDB Atlas Vector Search first; Qdrant, Pinecone or Weaviate when you outgrow them","lesson":"rag-search"},{"number":229,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Similarity and indexes Cosine vs dot product, approximate nearest neighbour, HNSW and IVF","lesson":"rag-search"},{"number":230,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Hybrid search Keyword (BM25) and vector results, combined","lesson":"rag-search"},{"number":231,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Reranking Cross-encoder rerankers, and the quality gained per millisecond spent","lesson":"rag-search"},{"number":232,"stage":10,"page":17,"text":"Context assembly Fitting passages into a token budget; ordering; lost-in-the-middle","lesson":"rag-grounding"},{"number":233,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Grounding and citations Answers traceable to their sources; refusing when evidence is missing","lesson":"rag-grounding"},{"number":234,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Access-aware retrieval Metadata filters so users only retrieve what they are allowed to see","lesson":"rag-grounding"},{"number":235,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Index freshness Re-indexing on change, re-embedding when the embedding model changes","lesson":"rag-freshness"},{"number":236,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Query transformation Rewriting, decomposing and expanding the user's question","lesson":"rag-freshness"},{"number":237,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Advanced patterns GraphRAG, agentic retrieval, long context vs retrieval trade-offs","lesson":"rag-freshness"},{"number":238,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Evals before features You cannot improve what you do not measure","lesson":"eval-methods"},{"number":239,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Golden datasets Representative questions, edge cases and adversarial inputs, versioned","lesson":"eval-methods"},{"number":240,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Retrieval metrics recall@k, precision@k, MRR: is the right context being found?","lesson":"eval-methods"},{"number":241,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Answer quality Faithfulness, relevance, completeness","lesson":"eval-methods"},{"number":242,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Scoring methods Exact match, rubrics, LLM-as-judge and its biases","lesson":"eval-methods"},{"number":243,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Human review Sampling outputs, labelling, calibrating automated judges","lesson":"eval-operations"},{"number":244,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Regression gates Evals in CI, so a prompt change cannot silently make things worse","lesson":"eval-operations"},{"number":245,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Online signals Feedback, corrections and escalations turned into new eval cases","lesson":"eval-operations"},{"number":246,"stage":10,"page":18,"text":"Eval tooling Langfuse, LangSmith, Ragas or promptfoo, or a small harness of your own","lesson":"eval-operations"},{"number":247,"stage":11,"page":18,"text":"The agent loop The model decides, a tool executes, the result returns; explicit stopping conditions","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":248,"stage":11,"page":18,"text":"Tool design Narrow, well-described tools with validated inputs","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":249,"stage":11,"page":18,"text":"Workflows vs agents Fixed pipelines when the path is known, agents only when it is not","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":250,"stage":11,"page":18,"text":"Memory and state Conversation state, summaries, long-term stores","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":251,"stage":11,"page":18,"text":"Model Context Protocol MCP: the open standard for connecting models to tools and data","lesson":"agent-mcp"},{"number":252,"stage":11,"page":18,"text":"Human in the loop Approval gates before any consequential action","lesson":"agent-mcp"},{"number":253,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Guardrails Step limits, budgets, sandboxed side effects, kill switches","lesson":"agent-mcp"},{"number":254,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Planning Decomposing multi-step tasks and re-planning after failure","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":255,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Agent frameworks LangGraph, LlamaIndex or provider agent SDKs, or none when plain code is simpler","lesson":"agent-loop"},{"number":256,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Prompt injection Direct and indirect; untrusted content reaching a model that has tools","lesson":"agent-security"},{"number":257,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Output handling Never render or execute model output unvalidated; XSS through rendered markdown","lesson":"agent-security"},{"number":258,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Tool abuse and SSRF Agents fetching URLs or taking actions on an attacker's behalf","lesson":"agent-security"},{"number":259,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Data exposure What leaves your system, provider retention terms, PII redaction","lesson":"agent-security"},{"number":260,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Audit trails Prompts, retrieved context, tool calls and decisions, all logged","lesson":"agent-security"},{"number":261,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Bias and fairness Where bias enters: data, retrieval corpus, evaluation blind spots","lesson":"agent-security"},{"number":262,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Assistant interfaces Streaming messages, stop and regenerate, conversation history","lesson":"agent-interface"},{"number":263,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Frontend AI SDKs The Vercel AI SDK or similar: streaming hooks, rendering tool calls","lesson":"agent-interface"},{"number":264,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Perceived latency Skeletons, partial results and optimistic UI around slow models","lesson":"agent-interface"},{"number":265,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Trust UX Visible sources, edit-before-send, easy correction","lesson":"agent-interface"},{"number":266,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Generative UI Models choosing interface components, not only text","lesson":"agent-interface"},{"number":267,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Voice and images Speech-to-text and image input in the browser","lesson":"agent-interface"},{"number":268,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Where AI sits in the system In the request path or behind a queue; sync vs async","lesson":"agent-production"},{"number":269,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Graceful degradation The product still works when the model is down","lesson":"agent-production"},{"number":270,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Cost control Caching, per-tenant budgets, trimmed prompts","lesson":"agent-production"},{"number":271,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"AI observability Traces of prompts, tokens, latency and cost per request","lesson":"agent-production"},{"number":272,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Versioning Prompts, models and indexes as deployed artefacts with changelogs","lesson":"agent-lifecycle"},{"number":273,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Model lifecycle Deprecations and migrations, with evals re-run on every switch","lesson":"agent-lifecycle"},{"number":274,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Model routing A small model first, escalating when confidence is low","lesson":"agent-lifecycle"},{"number":275,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Semantic caching Reusing answers to equivalent questions; hit rate vs correctness","lesson":"agent-lifecycle"},{"number":276,"stage":11,"page":19,"text":"Load testing What ten times the traffic does when every request calls a model","lesson":"agent-lifecycle"}],"stages":[{"id":1,"title":"01 - Python"},{"id":2,"title":"02 - Web Foundations"},{"id":3,"title":"03 - Frontend Engineering"},{"id":4,"title":"04 - Backend Engineering"},{"id":5,"title":"05 - Databases & DBMS"},{"id":6,"title":"06 - Operating Systems"},{"id":7,"title":"07 - Computer Networks"},{"id":8,"title":"08 - DevOps, Cloud & Security"},{"id":9,"title":"09 - AI Foundations & Model APIs"},{"id":10,"title":"10 - Retrieval & Evaluation"},{"id":11,"title":"11 - Agents & AI-Native Products"}],"sources":[{"name":"AI_Full_Stack_Engineer_Path.pdf","sha256":"0a612820cad197dcac7a7b01bafdd2e9bbef8a01e081e348a4d11fc57aba6415"},{"name":"Tech_Stack_ClientHub_and_Ramachandra_Traders.docx","sha256":"3c192bc890063f92053115a77e8d120d72c13fbb33298488ba2cbca9e3b8ba8e"}]};
